window.PsychologyATAR = [{"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-nervous-pathways", "title": "Nervous-system organisation and response pathways", "scope": "Structural and functional labels answer different questions · Reflexes and conscious responses can overlap · Explaining autonomic change with evidence", "parent": "psy11-nervous", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Nervous-system organisation and response pathways</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Structural and functional labels answer different questions</li><li>Reflexes and conscious responses can overlap</li><li>Explaining autonomic change with evidence</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Psychology as an evidence-based science", "kind": "learn", "text": "Psychology investigates behaviour and mental processes using systematic evidence. Behaviour includes observable actions, such as the time taken to respond to a signal. Cognition includes remembering, interpreting and deciding; emotion includes feelings and associated responses. Mental processes can be studied through carefully designed tasks, reports and physiological measurements even though they cannot be directly watched. A theory explains a pattern, a model represents selected relationships and a study tests a question with data. These are connected but are not interchangeable. To evaluate a claim that a brain exercise improves memory, ask how memory was measured, what comparison was made, how participants were selected and what alternative explanations remain. A confident advertisement is not evidence of a controlled improvement.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Central and peripheral organisation", "kind": "learn", "text": "The central nervous system, or CNS, consists of the brain and spinal cord. It integrates information and coordinates responses. The peripheral nervous system, or PNS, comprises neural pathways outside the CNS that connect it to receptors, muscles and glands. A sensory receptor responds to stimulation; sensory signals travel towards the CNS. Motor signals travel from the CNS towards effectors such as muscles. The spinal cord carries information between brain and body and can coordinate spinal reflexes. It is therefore incorrect to describe the PNS as making all decisions or the spinal cord as a passive cable. When tracing a response, identify the starting stimulus, receiving receptor, incoming pathway, processing location and outgoing pathway rather than simply listing the two systems.", "diagram": "nervous"}, {"title": "Somatic sensory and motor functions", "kind": "learn", "text": "The somatic division carries sensory information from the skin, muscles and joints and motor commands to skeletal muscles. Sensory is an incoming function; motor is an outgoing function. Picking up a cup involves information about its position and grip followed by coordinated muscle activity. Somatic motor activity is often voluntary, but skeletal muscles also participate in automatic reflexes. A withdrawal reflex can involve a sensory neuron, spinal interneuron and motor neuron before conscious awareness of pain is complete. The brain also receives information about the event. Saying that every somatic action requires a conscious decision misses this distinction. In a scenario, name the relevant direction of information and the target tissue, then explain how that function contributes to the response.", "diagram": "reflex"}, {"title": "Autonomic regulation", "kind": "learn", "text": "The autonomic nervous system regulates organs and glands, including heart rate and digestive activity, largely outside conscious control. Its sympathetic division supports mobilisation during challenge: heart and breathing rates can rise, pupils dilate and digestion becomes less active. Its parasympathetic division supports maintenance and recovery: heart rate can slow and digestive activity increases. They are coordinating divisions rather than a simple pair of whole-body on/off switches. Organ responses vary and normal functioning requires regulation over time. Before a speech, sympathetic activity can help supply oxygen and energy for action. After the speech, parasympathetic influence can support return towards resting activity. Link each division to a specific physiological change and its function instead of describing sympathetic as bad and parasympathetic as good.", "diagram": "autonomic"}, {"title": "Tracing an integrated response", "kind": "learn", "text": "Imagine a cyclist notices an obstacle, decides to brake and experiences a racing heart. Visual sensory information reaches the CNS, which processes the situation. Somatic motor pathways activate skeletal muscles to operate the brakes. Sympathetic autonomic activity can increase heart rate during the sudden challenge. These are different contributions to the same event. Saying that the sympathetic system moves the hand confuses organ regulation with skeletal muscle control. Saying that the CNS only processes voluntary actions overlooks automatic integration. Explain the route relevant to the action specified in the question. Use the scenario details as evidence: hand movement suggests somatic motor control, whereas changes in cardiac or digestive activity suggest autonomic regulation. Several nervous-system components can operate simultaneously.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evidence and measurement in biological psychology", "kind": "learn", "text": "Researchers can measure response time, heart rate or electrical activity to investigate biological processes. Each measure captures selected features rather than the entire experience. A fast response time may reflect practice, attention or anticipation as well as neural transmission. Heart rate can increase during exercise or excitement as well as stress, so it is not a unique measure of fear. A useful investigation specifies the task and units, controls relevant conditions and compares repeated or group observations. For example, comparing reaction time after different practice periods requires the same device, signal, posture and instructions. A possible mechanism is not proof of cause: the researcher must also consider design and alternative explanations. This reasoning connects biological knowledge to Science Inquiry.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Structural and functional labels answer different questions", "kind": "learn", "text": "CNS and PNS describe where neural tissue is located; somatic and autonomic describe functional organisation within the PNS. A sensory neuron carrying touch information from a fingertip is peripheral on its route into the CNS. Processing can then involve spinal interneurons and the brain. A somatic motor pathway carries commands to skeletal muscle. Sympathetic activity can simultaneously alter heart rate through autonomic pathways. Do not describe the sympathetic division as a separate brain, or assume that only one division works during an event. A response combines sensory input, processing and several outputs.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reflexes and conscious responses can overlap", "kind": "learn", "text": "A withdrawal reflex can begin through a spinal circuit before conscious pain perception. Sensory information also ascends to the brain, where perception and decisions about further movement occur. Saying that the brain is not needed to initiate this particular reflex is different from saying that the brain receives no information. Reflexes do not demonstrate that every rapid action is spinal: a learned sporting response can be quick while requiring brain processing. Trace the specific route requested rather than using speed as the only classification rule.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Explaining autonomic change with evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Sympathetic and parasympathetic influences often have contrasting effects on an organ, but their functions are more precise than on and off. During a frightening event sympathetic influence can increase heart rate; during recovery parasympathetic influence can help reduce it. Breathing, movement, temperature and fitness also affect readings. A raised pulse is consistent with arousal but cannot identify a particular emotion on its own. A strong explanation names the division, the relevant organ change and how that change supports the response, then distinguishes interpretation from direct measurement.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A cyclist’s response", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: A cyclist’s response", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Withdrawal reflex", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Autonomic recovery", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Objective does not mean unique", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: integrated biology", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "A cyclist’s response", "prompt": "A cyclist sees a hazard, moves a hand to brake and experiences a faster heart rate. Explain the roles of the CNS, somatic motor system and sympathetic system. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The CNS processes incoming information and coordinates a response to the hazard.", "[2] Somatic motor pathways carry commands to skeletal muscles in the hand to operate the brake.", "[2] Sympathetic autonomic activity increases heart rate, supporting mobilisation during the challenge."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-nervous-q1"}, {"title": "Withdrawal reflex", "prompt": "A fictional person withdraws a hand from a hot surface before fully interpreting the pain. Explain the roles of sensory, inter- and motor neurons. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Sensory neurons convey information from activated receptors towards the spinal CNS.", "[2] Interneurons process and connect signals within the spinal CNS in this illustrative withdrawal pathway.", "[2] Motor neurons convey commands towards muscles that withdraw the hand; information also reaches the brain."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-nervous-q4"}, {"title": "Autonomic recovery", "prompt": "After a presentation, a student’s heart rate returns towards rest and digestion becomes more active. Identify the relevant autonomic division and compare its role with the division active during mobilisation. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Parasympathetic division is relevant to recovery and maintenance.", "[1] It contributes to slowing heart activity and supporting digestion.", "[1] Sympathetic activity contributes to mobilisation during a challenge.", "[1] It can raise heart/breathing rates and reduce digestive activity; the divisions coordinate organ-specific regulation."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-nervous-q5"}, {"title": "Objective does not mean unique", "prompt": "A researcher concludes that every increase in heart rate proves fear. Evaluate this inference and propose an additional measure. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Heart rate is an objective physiological measure but does not uniquely identify fear.", "[1] Exercise, excitement or other influences can also increase it.", "[1] A suitably designed private self-report of the relevant emotion could provide complementary evidence.", "[1] Standardise context and interpret both measures within a design rather than treating either as complete proof."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-nervous-q6"}, {"title": "Extended response: integrated biology", "prompt": "Using a fictional sport scenario, explain how sensory input, CNS processing, somatic action and autonomic mobilisation work together. Include neuron structures, synaptic transmission and one measurement limitation. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Specifies receptor input and incoming sensory information to the CNS.", "[2] Explains CNS integration and outgoing somatic motor signals to skeletal muscles.", "[2] Connects sympathetic activity with appropriate organ changes and function.", "[2] Accurately explains dendrites/soma/axon/terminals and myelin’s contribution.", "[2] Explains transmitter release, diffusion and receptor action at a synapse.", "[2] Evaluates a relevant measure with a specific alternative influence and consequence."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-nervous-q7"}, {"id": "psy11-nervous-pathways-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A fictional student touches a hot tray, withdraws their hand and then reports pain. Explain the sensory, spinal and motor components of the withdrawal, and why later awareness is compatible with a spinal reflex. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Receptors detect the harmful stimulus; sensory neurons carry information towards the spinal cord/CNS.", "[2] Spinal interneurons connect input to a motor response; motor neurons carry commands to skeletal muscles that withdraw the hand.", "[2] Information also reaches the brain for pain perception; cortical awareness need not precede the initiation of withdrawal."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-nervous-pathways-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "After a false fire alarm, a student’s pulse gradually falls. Explain a relevant autonomic change and evaluate the claim that pulse alone proves the student is no longer anxious. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Parasympathetic influence can reduce heart rate during recovery; contrast this with sympathetic arousal.", "[2] Heart rate is an objective physiological measure but does not uniquely measure anxiety; activity and other factors can alter it.", "[2] Combine a suitable subjective anxiety measure with standardised physiological recording to evaluate the interpretation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which statement correctly separates organisation and function?", "o": ["The PNS includes somatic and autonomic pathways.", "The CNS is the sympathetic division.", "All sensory processing occurs outside the CNS.", "Somatic motor pathways principally target glands."], "a": 0, "why": "CNS/PNS are structural divisions; somatic/autonomic distinguish peripheral functions."}], "subtitle": "21 screens · 7 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-nervous-neurons", "title": "Neuron structure and electrical transmission", "scope": "Structure supports the direction of communication · Myelin changes conduction rather than meaning · From a neuron drawing to a network explanation", "parent": "psy11-nervous", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Neuron structure and electrical transmission</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Structure supports the direction of communication</li><li>Myelin changes conduction rather than meaning</li><li>From a neuron drawing to a network explanation</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Neuron structure and signal direction", "kind": "learn", "text": "A neuron is a specialised cell that receives and transmits information. Dendrites usually receive input from other cells. The soma, or cell body, contains the nucleus and supports cell metabolism and integration of inputs. An axon conducts an electrical impulse away from the soma towards axon terminals. Many axons have a myelin sheath that insulates sections of the axon and enables faster conduction between gaps called nodes. Axon terminals communicate with a target cell at a synapse. In the simplified model, follow the direction dendrites, soma, axon, terminals. Real neurons have varied shapes and many connections, so a textbook drawing is not a literal map of every neuron. Label structures and explain their functions separately when a question requests both.", "diagram": "neuron"}, {"title": "Sensory, motor and interneurons", "kind": "learn", "text": "Sensory neurons transmit information from receptors towards the CNS. Motor neurons transmit commands from the CNS towards muscles or glands. Interneurons connect and process signals within the CNS, often linking sensory and motor pathways. These names describe functional roles, not simply the shape of a cell drawing. Touching a hot surface stimulates receptors, sensory information reaches the spinal cord, interneurons contribute to processing and motor neurons activate muscles that withdraw the hand. The brain receives information allowing conscious interpretation and later decisions. A full response should explain the contribution of each type to the pathway. Do not reverse motor and sensory directions or place all interneurons in the peripheral system. Some reflex circuits differ from this illustrative three-neuron pathway.", "diagram": "reflex"}, {"title": "Electrical activity along the axon", "kind": "learn", "text": "Neural transmission has an electrical component within a neuron and a chemical component at many synapses. An action potential is a brief change in electrical potential across the axon membrane that propagates along the axon after sufficient activation. This is not a stream of neurotransmitter flowing down the axon and is not electricity travelling along a metal wire. Myelin increases conduction speed by supporting transmission between nodes. Individual action potentials are all-or-none events; a stronger effective stimulus can be represented by firing frequency and by the number of neurons recruited, rather than by making each impulse larger. The syllabus requires the electrochemical distinction and direction of transmission. Use those core ideas when explaining how information can move rapidly while still being regulated at connections between cells.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Structure supports the direction of communication", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a typical model dendrites receive input, the soma supports cell functioning, and the axon conducts a signal towards axon terminals. Sensory neurons can have a different arrangement from the illustrated multipolar neuron, so a drawing is a functional guide rather than a universal photograph. Sensory, motor and interneuron labels describe roles in a circuit, not simply their size or shape. Always add an arrow showing the direction of transmission and identify which end provides input to the next cell. A labelled structure without a function gives an incomplete explanation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Myelin changes conduction rather than meaning", "kind": "learn", "text": "Myelin insulates sections of many axons. Action potentials are regenerated at gaps between these sections, supporting faster conduction than in a comparable unmyelinated axon. At this course level the important distinction is electrical activity along the axon versus chemical communication across a typical synapse. Myelin does not carry neurotransmitter through the synaptic cleft and does not determine what a person believes. Reduced conduction can affect timing within pathways, but a particular behavioural consequence depends on which pathway is affected.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "From a neuron drawing to a network explanation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A single neuron is a component of an interconnected circuit. A stronger sensory stimulus does not simply create a larger version of each action potential: neural coding can involve firing frequency and recruitment across cells. These details support the model but the syllabus emphasis is direction and electrochemical communication, not memorising every ion-channel mechanism. To analyse a diagram, first locate the axon, then its terminals, then the next cell. If arrows point from terminals backwards through the same axon in an ordinary transmission diagram, correct the pathway before explaining a response.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Pathway and neuron reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Pathway and neuron reference</h3><p>Use the labels in a connected pathway and explain the role, not just the name.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Structure/type</th><th>Contribution</th><th>Common distinction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dendrites</td><td>Usually receive inputs</td><td>Not the myelin covering</td></tr><tr><td>Soma</td><td>Metabolism and integration</td><td>Contains nucleus</td></tr><tr><td>Axon</td><td>Conducts impulse towards terminals</td><td>Electrical component within neuron</td></tr><tr><td>Myelin</td><td>Insulation supporting faster conduction</td><td>On axon sections</td></tr><tr><td>Axon terminals</td><td>Release messenger at chemical synapses</td><td>Presynaptic side</td></tr><tr><td>Sensory neuron</td><td>Receptors towards CNS</td><td>Incoming information</td></tr><tr><td>Interneuron</td><td>Processing/connectivity within CNS</td><td>Not all PNS</td></tr><tr><td>Motor neuron</td><td>CNS towards effectors</td><td>Outgoing commands</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Diagrams simplify varied neuron shapes and networks. A typical chemical synapse adds transmitter/receptor communication between cells.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Myelin and the pathway", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Myelin and the pathway", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Myelin and the pathway", "prompt": "Draw a simplified neuron and label dendrites, soma, axon, myelin and axon terminals. Explain the direction of transmission and myelin’s contribution. (7 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A connected neuron drawing shows dendrites around a soma and an axon extending towards terminals.", "[2] Labels correctly identify all five requested structures, including myelin on the axon rather than in the synaptic gap.", "[1] Arrows show the simplified direction towards the soma and along the axon to terminals.", "[2] Myelin insulates axon sections and supports faster conduction between nodes."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1, 2], "marks": 7, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-nervous-q3"}, {"id": "psy11-nervous-neurons-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A diagram labels myelin as the chemical messenger that crosses to the next cell. Correct this error and explain how myelin and axon terminals contribute to communication. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Myelin insulates axonal sections and supports efficient/faster electrical conduction; it is not the chemical messenger.", "[2] Axon terminals release neurotransmitter at a typical chemical synapse.", "[2] Neurotransmitter crosses the cleft and interacts with receptors on the target cell, linking two neurons."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-nervous-neurons-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Draw a typical neuron and explain the functions of dendrites, axon and axon terminals. State one limitation of your drawing. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Label dendrites and explain their role in receiving input.", "[2] Label the axon with the ordinary direction of transmission towards terminals; terminals release transmitter to communicate with a target.", "[2] State that real neurons differ in shape and form networks; this simplified drawing does not represent every cell or every connection."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Where does electrical activity travel within a typical neuron?", "o": ["Along the axon towards its terminals.", "Through myelin across the synaptic cleft.", "Only between two different somas.", "From postsynaptic receptors directly into a gland without a pathway."], "a": 0, "why": "An axon conducts activity within the neuron; terminals support communication with another cell."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-nervous-synapses", "title": "Synapses, neurotransmitters and receptor action", "scope": "Release, binding and the target response are separate steps · Excitation and inhibition belong to circuit explanations · Ending a message and testing an interruption", "parent": "psy11-nervous", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Synapses, neurotransmitters and receptor action</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Release, binding and the target response are separate steps</li><li>Excitation and inhibition belong to circuit explanations</li><li>Ending a message and testing an interruption</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Chemical communication at a synapse", "kind": "learn", "text": "A synapse is a communication junction between a neuron and another cell. At a typical chemical synapse, arrival of an action potential at the presynaptic axon terminal leads to release of neurotransmitter from vesicles into the synaptic cleft. Molecules diffuse across the cleft and bind to suitable receptors on the postsynaptic membrane. This changes the likelihood that the receiving cell will become active. Neurotransmitter can then be removed by reuptake, breakdown or diffusion, limiting the duration of the signal. The two neurons do not need to touch directly. A strong answer describes release, movement across the cleft and receptor binding, and connects these events to the receiving cell rather than merely saying that chemicals send a message.", "diagram": "synapse"}, {"title": "Neurotransmitters and receptor specificity", "kind": "learn", "text": "A neurotransmitter is a chemical messenger released by a neuron that influences a target cell through receptors. Receptor compatibility helps determine which cells respond. Effects can increase or decrease the likelihood of activation, depending on the transmitter, receptor and circuit. It is an oversimplification to assign one chemical to one emotion or assume that more of a neurotransmitter always improves functioning. Many interacting circuits contribute to behaviour. If a hypothetical substance blocks receptors, explain that the transmitter may still be released but cannot exert its usual effect at those receptors. This is an application of the communication model, not a diagnosis or medication recommendation. Questions may test structure, sequence and function together, so include both the location of a process and why it matters.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Exam explanations of neural communication", "kind": "learn", "text": "An explanation must connect the structures to the process being asked about. For a four-mark response about a typical chemical synapse, four distinct points might describe arrival of an action potential, neurotransmitter release, diffusion across the cleft and receptor binding that alters postsynaptic activation. Repeating that the signal passes from one neuron to another in four different sentences does not add four scientific points. A comparison of sensory and motor neurons should state both directions and both destinations. Drawings need readable labels and arrows with a clear meaning. Use precise terms such as axon terminal and synaptic cleft rather than vague phrases such as the end and the gap. Check that your answer follows the actual prompt and the suggested marks.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A receptor-blocking scenario", "kind": "learn", "text": "Suppose a hypothetical chemical occupies a postsynaptic receptor without producing the transmitter’s usual effect. The presynaptic neuron may still conduct an action potential and release transmitter, but receptor interaction at the target is disrupted. This illustrates why axonal transmission and synaptic communication are separate processes. The example does not mean every blocked receptor produces one predictable mood or behaviour: effects depend on receptors and circuits. Explain the location of the interruption and what can continue normally. A precise answer is stronger than saying the whole brain stops or that the chemical removes myelin. Keep the scenario hypothetical rather than using the model to choose medication.", "diagram": "synapse"}, {"title": "Release, binding and the target response are separate steps", "kind": "learn", "text": "An action potential arriving at a terminal can trigger neurotransmitter release. Molecules diffuse across the narrow cleft and bind to compatible receptors on the postsynaptic membrane. Binding changes the target cell’s activity; its overall inputs influence whether it subsequently produces an action potential. This distinction prevents the misleading claim that every released molecule guarantees one firing event. The chemical messenger does not become an electrical impulse floating in the cleft. Explain the sequence in the correct locations: terminal, cleft, receptor and postsynaptic response.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Excitation and inhibition belong to circuit explanations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Neurotransmitter effects depend on receptor type and the cell or circuit involved. Excitatory input makes firing more likely; inhibitory input makes it less likely under the relevant conditions. Several inputs can converge, so information processing involves integration rather than a simple relay that is always activated. A neurotransmitter name alone cannot establish a complex emotion. This is why a biological explanation of behaviour should identify a pathway or mechanism and recognise interactions with experience and context, rather than treating one chemical as a complete explanation of a person.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Ending a message and testing an interruption", "kind": "learn", "text": "Transmitter action is limited by processes such as reuptake, breakdown or diffusion, depending on the synapse. Ending one message allows subsequent signals to remain distinguishable. In a hypothetical receptor-blocking question, identify whether the interruption affects conduction, release, binding or removal. If a blocker occupies receptors without activating them, presynaptic conduction and release may continue while the usual target response is reduced. This is a reasoning exercise about communication stages, not a way to infer the effects of a real medicine without evidence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A chemical synapse", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: A chemical synapse", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "A chemical synapse", "prompt": "Explain how an arriving neural impulse can influence another neuron at a typical chemical synapse. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] An action potential reaches the presynaptic axon terminal.", "[1] Neurotransmitter is released from vesicles into the synaptic cleft.", "[1] It diffuses across the cleft and binds to compatible postsynaptic receptors.", "[1] Binding changes the likelihood of postsynaptic activation; the signal is regulated by subsequent transmitter removal."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-nervous-q2"}, {"id": "psy11-nervous-synapses-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "An invented blocker occupies target receptors but does not affect the axon. Explain two events that may continue normally and one event that may be disrupted. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Electrical activity may continue along the presynaptic axon towards its terminals.", "[2] Arrival can still trigger neurotransmitter release and diffusion across the cleft, under the stated conditions.", "[2] Normal transmitter binding/activation at occupied receptors is disrupted, reducing its usual postsynaptic effect."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-nervous-synapses-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain why the statement “a neurotransmitter crosses the cleft so the next neuron must fire” is too strong. Include direction and receptor action in your answer. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Transmitter is released from the presynaptic terminal and crosses towards postsynaptic receptors.", "[2] Compatible receptor interaction affects postsynaptic activity; effects can be excitatory or inhibitory.", "[2] The cell integrates inputs and may or may not reach the conditions for firing; crossing alone does not guarantee an action potential."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is the best description of neurotransmitter action?", "o": ["Its effect depends on interaction with compatible receptors.", "It is an electrical impulse travelling through the cleft.", "It replaces the myelin sheath.", "Every molecule always causes a postsynaptic action potential."], "a": 0, "why": "Chemical communication involves receptor interaction; the resulting response depends on the synapse and integrated inputs."}, {"q": "Which route carries commands to skeletal muscles?", "o": ["Somatic motor pathway", "Sympathetic sensory pathway", "Sensory pathway to CNS", "Synaptic cleft alone"], "a": 0, "why": "Motor signals travel from CNS to skeletal-muscle effectors."}, {"q": "What is released at a typical chemical synapse?", "o": ["Neurotransmitter", "A whole axon", "Myelin", "A new nucleus"], "a": 0, "why": "Transmitters cross the cleft and bind to receptors."}, {"q": "Which structure supports faster axonal conduction?", "o": ["Myelin sheath", "Dendritic nucleus", "Synaptic gap", "Receptor label"], "a": 0, "why": "Myelin insulates axon sections and supports conduction between nodes."}, {"q": "Which is CNS?", "o": ["Brain and spinal cord", "Only limbs", "Somatic and autonomic nerves only", "Only muscles"], "a": 0, "why": "The brain and spinal cord form the central system."}, {"q": "Why cannot heart rate alone identify fear?", "o": ["Several influences can increase it", "It is qualitative", "It never changes", "It directly measures a belief"], "a": 0, "why": "An objective physiological measure can still be nonspecific."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-brain-regions", "title": "Brainstem, forebrain and homeostatic functions", "scope": "Distinguish regulation, coordination and arousal · Thalamus versus hypothalamus · Localisation is a useful but limited model", "parent": "psy11-brain", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Brainstem, forebrain and homeostatic functions</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Distinguish regulation, coordination and arousal</li><li>Thalamus versus hypothalamus</li><li>Localisation is a useful but limited model</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Hindbrain and midbrain functions", "kind": "learn", "text": "The medulla, in the hindbrain, contributes to essential autonomic functions such as regulation of breathing and heart activity. The cerebellum supports coordination, balance and skilled movement, and contributes to some forms of learning. The reticular formation is a network extending through the brainstem, including the midbrain, that contributes to alertness and arousal. These structures cooperate with other areas rather than acting as isolated control boxes. A person can recognise a movement goal yet struggle with smooth coordination if relevant cerebellar systems are impaired. Changes in alertness involve different systems from a simple failure to understand language. For an exam response, connect the named area to the specific function shown in the scenario, and avoid claiming that one region alone controls every example of a complex behaviour.", "diagram": "brainregions"}, {"title": "Thalamus and hypothalamus", "kind": "learn", "text": "The thalamus acts as an important relay and processing station for most sensory information travelling towards the cerebral cortex. The hypothalamus contributes to homeostatic regulation, including temperature, hunger, thirst and coordination of autonomic and hormonal responses. Their similar names do not imply identical functions. Relaying incoming sensory information differs from regulating internal conditions. In a scenario about body-temperature regulation, the hypothalamus is the relevant structure. In a scenario about sensory relay, the thalamus is more directly relevant. Neither description means the structure performs only that one role. Brain diagrams simplify position and scale. Students should learn relative locations and functions, then use the question details to select the relevant structure rather than choosing a name because it sounds familiar.", "diagram": "brainregions"}, {"title": "Distinguish regulation, coordination and arousal", "kind": "learn", "text": "The medulla contributes to vital autonomic functions such as cardiovascular and breathing regulation. The cerebellum contributes to coordination, balance and aspects of learned motor activity. The reticular formation is a network extending through the brainstem, including the midbrain, with roles in arousal and alertness. A person staying awake, maintaining posture and adjusting breathing therefore draws on interacting functions. The labels are useful distinctions, but ordinary behaviour is not allocated to isolated boxes that never communicate. Use the most specific function needed for the scenario.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Thalamus versus hypothalamus", "kind": "learn", "text": "The thalamus relays and processes most sensory information towards relevant cortical systems; olfaction is an important qualification to a universal all-senses statement. The hypothalamus contributes to homeostatic regulation, autonomic control and hormone-related coordination. Hunger, thirst and temperature regulation therefore fit hypothalamic functions more closely than a simple sensory relay. Similar spelling does not imply interchangeable functions. In a comparison answer name both structures, distinguish their roles and apply each role to a different part of an event, instead of providing two unrelated definitions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Localisation is a useful but limited model", "kind": "learn", "text": "A location-function model predicts that disruption to a region can affect functions to which it contributes. It does not guarantee that one observed difficulty proves damage in that region: several pathways, compensation and other causes can influence performance. Brain imaging or clinical evidence would be needed to evaluate a real case. In a fictional examination vignette, infer a plausible contribution from the stated evidence and qualify the inference where asked. Avoid diagnosing a person from a brief symptom list or claiming that one region exclusively controls all complex behaviour.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Locations and functions", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Locations and functions", "prompt": "Compare the primary motor, primary somatosensory, primary auditory and primary visual cortices using their main lobe and contribution. (8 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Primary motor cortex in the frontal lobe contributes to voluntary movement.", "[2] Primary somatosensory cortex in the parietal lobe processes bodily sensation such as touch.", "[2] Primary auditory cortex in the temporal lobe processes auditory information.", "[2] Primary visual cortex in the occipital lobe processes visual information."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 8, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-brain-q4"}, {"id": "psy11-brain-regions-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Compare the likely contributions of the thalamus and hypothalamus when a person notices a bright light while regulating body temperature. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The thalamus contributes to relay/processing of visual sensory information towards cortex.", "[2] The hypothalamus contributes to temperature/homeostatic regulation and coordinated responses.", "[2] Apply the distinction: sensory relay for the light differs from maintaining internal temperature, although systems interact."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-brain-regions-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A fictional patient has poor coordination but normal language comprehension. Identify a relevant brain region, explain its role and evaluate why the observation alone does not establish a diagnosis. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Identify the cerebellum as a relevant contributor to coordination and balance.", "[2] Explain that impaired coordinated movement is consistent with disruption of this contribution, while preserved language reflects distinct contributions.", "[2] Other causes and interacting pathways are possible; assessment and further evidence are needed rather than diagnosis from one observation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which pairing is most accurate?", "o": ["Hypothalamus — homeostatic regulation.", "Thalamus — the only breathing controller.", "Cerebellum — all language comprehension.", "Medulla — visual interpretation in the occipital cortex."], "a": 0, "why": "The hypothalamus contributes to coordinated regulation of internal conditions."}], "subtitle": "12 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-brain-cortex", "title": "Cortical lobes, language and contralateral control", "scope": "Contralateral control is not a personality split · Primary areas and association functions · Language functions and their qualifications", "parent": "psy11-brain", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Cortical lobes, language and contralateral control</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Contralateral control is not a personality split</li><li>Primary areas and association functions</li><li>Language functions and their qualifications</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Cortex, hemispheres and contralateral control", "kind": "learn", "text": "The cerebral cortex is the outer layer of the cerebrum and supports complex sensory processing, voluntary movement, cognition and language. The left and right cerebral hemispheres communicate through the corpus callosum, a large bundle of neural fibres. Many motor and sensory functions show contralateral organisation: a hemisphere primarily processes or controls the opposite side of the body. This does not mean that every pathway crosses or that the hemispheres have completely separate personalities. Language is left-lateralised in many people, but lateralisation varies and everyday tasks involve communication across networks. Explain left hemisphere and right hand relationships using the specified task. Avoid the popular claim that people are either logical left-brain people or creative right-brain people.", "diagram": "lobes"}, {"title": "Frontal and parietal lobes", "kind": "learn", "text": "The frontal lobe includes the primary motor cortex, which contributes to voluntary skeletal-muscle control, and the prefrontal cortex, which supports planning, inhibition, decision-making and aspects of personality and social behaviour. The parietal lobe includes the primary somatosensory cortex, which processes bodily sensations such as touch. A question about difficulty planning despite intact movement is different from one about weakness in voluntary movement. Likewise, impaired touch perception differs from loss of vision. Localisation means that some functions are associated especially strongly with particular regions, not that all thoughts reside in one tiny spot. Connect function to location with a clear example. Damage effects depend on the affected pathways, extent of injury and contributions from other regions.", "diagram": "lobes"}, {"title": "Temporal and occipital lobes", "kind": "learn", "text": "The temporal lobe includes the primary auditory cortex and contributes to hearing, language and memory processes. The occipital lobe includes the primary visual cortex and contributes to visual processing. Hearing a word, understanding its meaning and recalling a related experience engage different but connected processes. A student who can detect a sound may still have difficulty understanding spoken language if relevant language networks are affected. A visual stimulus travels through sensory pathways before cortical interpretation. Do not describe the eyes as the primary visual cortex or place auditory processing in the occipital lobe. In a labelled diagram, learn the approximate lobe positions and distinguish the organ receiving stimulation from the brain region processing that information.", "diagram": "lobes"}, {"title": "Broca’s and Wernicke’s areas", "kind": "learn", "text": "In many people, Broca’s area in the left frontal region contributes to speech production, while Wernicke’s area in the left temporal region contributes to language comprehension. Damage associated with Broca-type aphasia can produce effortful, limited speech, with some comprehension relatively preserved. Wernicke-type aphasia can involve fluent speech with disrupted meaning and comprehension. These classroom descriptions represent broad patterns; language depends on distributed networks and clinical presentations vary. A scenario about difficulty generating speech requires a different explanation from one about fluent but poorly understood language. Specify production or comprehension and the relevant location. Do not assume that every speech problem proves damage to one named area or that these regions function without other neural connections.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Contralateral control is not a personality split", "kind": "learn", "text": "The hemispheres generally contribute to sensory and motor control of the opposite side of the body. This does not imply that creative people use only one hemisphere and logical people only the other. Many tasks recruit distributed systems in both hemispheres, connected by the corpus callosum. Distinguish body side from visual field: information from each visual field is represented primarily in the opposite hemisphere, with contributions from both eyes. Treating one eye as one hemisphere is a common error that produces incorrect split-brain predictions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Primary areas and association functions", "kind": "learn", "text": "The primary motor cortex lies in the frontal lobe and contributes to voluntary movement; the primary somatosensory cortex lies in the parietal lobe and processes body sensations. Auditory processing has important temporal contributions and visual processing important occipital contributions. Prefrontal functions include planning, inhibition and judgement. A lobe contains several functional areas, and naming a whole lobe is less precise than naming the relevant cortical area when the question requests it. Complex actions combine sensory, motor and association processes rather than staying within one lobe.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Language functions and their qualifications", "kind": "learn", "text": "Broca’s and Wernicke’s areas are historically associated with language production and comprehension respectively, often in the left hemisphere. Production includes organised speech and comprehension includes interpretation of language; neither function belongs exclusively to one tiny region. Actual language networks and impairment patterns are more complex, and lateralisation can vary. A course answer should describe the traditional distinction and apply it to a specified difficulty while recognising the model’s limits. Producing fluent speech does not automatically demonstrate understanding of what was heard.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Language scenario", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Language scenario", "prompt": "A fictional patient produces effortful limited speech; another produces fluent speech with disrupted meaning and comprehension. Relate these patterns to Broca’s and Wernicke’s areas and state a limitation. (5 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Broca-associated left frontal networks relate to speech production and the first broad pattern.", "[2] Wernicke-associated left temporal networks relate to comprehension and the second broad pattern.", "[1] Language is distributed and presentations vary; the vignette alone cannot establish a clinical diagnosis."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-brain-q5"}, {"id": "psy11-brain-cortex-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A fictional student hears an instruction, plans a response and moves their right hand. Explain relevant auditory, prefrontal and motor cortical contributions. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Temporal/auditory cortical systems contribute to processing the spoken input.", "[2] Prefrontal systems contribute to planning and choosing the response.", "[2] The left primary motor cortex contributes to contralateral control of right-hand movement; these areas operate in connected systems."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-brain-cortex-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare Broca’s and Wernicke’s areas using a production difficulty and a comprehension difficulty. State one qualification to strict localisation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Broca’s area is traditionally associated with language production, so disrupted production is a relevant example.", "[2] Wernicke’s area is traditionally associated with comprehension, so difficulty interpreting language is a relevant example.", "[2] Language depends on distributed networks; lateralisation and impairment vary, so neither label exclusively determines all language."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What does contralateral motor control mean?", "o": ["A hemisphere generally contributes to movement on the opposite body side.", "Each eye communicates only with its same-side hemisphere.", "Creative tasks use no left hemisphere.", "The corpus callosum prevents hemispheric communication."], "a": 0, "why": "Contralateral refers to opposite-side contributions, not a division into personality types."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-brain-history", "title": "Gage, Sperry and Freeman: evaluating brain evidence", "scope": "A case can reveal a dissociation without isolating every cause · Predict a split-brain result step by step · Historical intervention needs an ethical and methodological judgement", "parent": "psy11-brain", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Gage, Sperry and Freeman: evaluating brain evidence</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>A case can reveal a dissociation without isolating every cause</li><li>Predict a split-brain result step by step</li><li>Historical intervention needs an ethical and methodological judgement</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Phineas Gage and case-study evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Gage survived an 1848 accident in which an iron tamping rod passed through his head, damaging frontal regions. Accounts of subsequent changes in behaviour helped suggest that frontal systems contribute to personality, judgement and social regulation. The case provided unusual evidence that would be unethical to create deliberately. However, the historical reports are incomplete, popular retellings can exaggerate changes, and there were no modern baseline tests or controlled comparison. A single case cannot establish that every frontal injury has the same effect. Explain the contribution and limitation together: changes after injury support a link with frontal function, but multiple influences and uncertain records restrict precise causal conclusions. Avoid turning a complex person and recovery history into a simplistic claim that one rod removed all personality.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Sperry and split-brain investigations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Roger Sperry’s work from 1959–1968 contributed to understanding hemisphere specialisation and communication through the corpus callosum. Research with people whose corpus callosum had been surgically severed to treat severe epilepsy used tightly controlled presentation of stimuli. Information shown briefly to the left visual field initially reaches the right hemisphere; information in the right visual field initially reaches the left. With language predominantly in the left hemisphere, a participant may be unable to verbally name a left-field item but may select it using the left hand. This pattern illustrates restricted transfer between hemispheres. Visual field is not the same as eye: both eyes normally contribute to each visual field. The findings require careful control of presentation and are not evidence of two independent personalities in everyone.", "diagram": "splitbrain"}, {"title": "Limits of split-brain evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Split-brain research used small, unusual clinical samples whose surgery and epilepsy history differed. The laboratory tasks controlled exposure and response, making them useful for investigating lateralisation, but they do not represent all everyday functioning. Intact brains share information through the corpus callosum and other pathways. If a question asks why a participant selects an object but cannot name it, explain where information first arrived, which response route was available and what transfer was restricted. If asked to evaluate, identify a limitation tied to the evidence rather than saying small sample without a consequence. The unusual sample restricts generalisation to people with intact brains. Evidence of specialisation does not justify stereotypes about gender, creativity or intellectual ability.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Freeman and historical lobotomy", "kind": "learn", "text": "Walter Freeman’s frontal-lobotomy work during 1936–1945 is studied as historical evidence about the prefrontal cortex and as an example of serious ethical concerns. Procedures disrupted frontal connections in attempts to manage psychiatric symptoms. Reports of reduced emotional responsiveness and major changes in motivation, judgement or personality contributed to ideas about frontal function, but patients also experienced grave harms. There was inadequate controlled evidence by current standards, and consent and protection were major concerns. Describing the operation as simply a successful cure ignores its history and limitations. Compare the evidence with current ethical expectations: research needs justified methods, independent review, meaningful consent and safeguards against harm. These are historical procedures to evaluate, not treatments for students to consider carrying out.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A case can reveal a dissociation without isolating every cause", "kind": "learn", "text": "Gage’s injury linked frontal damage with reported changes in aspects of behaviour, contributing to discussion of localisation. The evidence was retrospective and lacked a controlled before-and-after psychological assessment. Injury extent, reports and later adaptation complicate interpretation. Therefore a case can challenge a claim that all brain tissue is interchangeable while failing to prove that one small location completely determines personality. The strength is detailed unusual evidence; the limitation is the inability to control competing influences or assume the case represents everyone.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Predict a split-brain result step by step", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a typical experimental arrangement a briefly presented left-visual-field stimulus initially reaches the right hemisphere. If language production is primarily left-sided and callosal communication is disrupted, naming may be difficult even when the left hand can select the matching object. Follow visual field, hemisphere, available communication and response mode in that order. This controlled dissociation supports specialised and connected functions. It is not the claim that the person cannot recognise anything, that the left eye is blind, or that every split-brain patient shows an identical result.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Historical intervention needs an ethical and methodological judgement", "kind": "learn", "text": "Freeman’s frontal lobotomy interventions affected prefrontal connections and were used historically for psychiatric conditions. Reports of reduced agitation cannot automatically count as improved overall wellbeing when autonomy, cognition, emotion or everyday functioning may be harmed. Weak control comparisons and changing procedures make causal evaluation difficult. Consent quality, irreversible harm and vulnerability are major ethical issues. Evaluate historical practices using both the scientific claim and the participant consequences, rather than treating a dramatic intervention as a good experiment solely because it changed behaviour.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Split-brain visual fields", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Split-brain visual fields", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Gage as evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Gage as evidence", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Freeman and ethics", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Split-brain visual fields", "prompt": "In a controlled split-brain task, an item appears briefly in the left visual field. A participant with left-dominant language cannot name it but selects it using the left hand. Explain. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Left visual-field information initially reaches the right hemisphere.", "[1] Restricted corpus-callosum transfer limits access by the language-dominant left hemisphere.", "[1] Verbal naming therefore may fail under these task conditions.", "[1] The right hemisphere can guide the left-hand selection through contralateral motor organisation.", "[1] Visual field is not the same as presenting an item to only the left eye.", "[1] The result illustrates specialised task processing and restricted transfer, not two independent personalities."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-brain-q2"}, {"title": "Gage as evidence", "prompt": "Explain one contribution and two limitations of the Gage case for understanding frontal-lobe function. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Reported post-injury behaviour changes helped link frontal systems to judgement/personality/social regulation.", "[2] Incomplete historical accounts and lack of standardised baseline testing limit precise interpretation.", "[2] A single unusual injury and other influences restrict causal certainty and generalisation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-brain-q3"}, {"title": "Freeman and ethics", "prompt": "Evaluate historical frontal-lobotomy evidence by explaining a functional contribution, an evidential limitation and an ethical concern. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Changes in emotion, motivation and personality contributed to understanding prefrontal function.", "[2] Inadequate controlled comparisons and complex clinical histories limited conclusions about outcomes.", "[2] Serious harms and inadequate consent/protection show major ethical problems; contribution does not justify reproduction."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-brain-q6"}, {"id": "psy11-brain-history-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A typical split-brain participant cannot name a word briefly shown in the left visual field but can select a matching object with the left hand. Explain this pattern and one limit to generalisation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Left-field input primarily reaches the right hemisphere; disrupted callosal transfer can limit access to left-dominant language production.", "[2] Right-hemisphere control of the left hand can support matching even when verbal naming is difficult.", "[2] Small clinical samples, variable surgery and lateralisation limit extension to all patients or ordinary intact brains."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-brain-history-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Evaluate the claim “Gage and Freeman proved that the prefrontal cortex alone determines personality.” Use both evidence and ethics. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Gage provided an unusual case association between frontal injury and reported behavioural change, with limited baseline/control evidence.", "[2] Freeman’s interventions changed connections but uncontrolled reports, damage extent and broader consequences restrict causal/localisation claims.", "[2] Irreversible harm and inadequate informed consent raise major ethical concerns; connected networks and contextual influences rule out the word alone."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why might a split-brain participant match an object without naming it?", "o": ["Visual information and a hand response can be available in a hemisphere without ordinary access to the dominant speech system.", "The participant necessarily has no visual perception.", "Each eye is entirely disconnected from the brain.", "Naming and all hand movements always use the same hemisphere."], "a": 0, "why": "The response mode and hemispheric access matter; inability to name need not mean inability to recognise."}], "subtitle": "18 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-brain-imaging", "title": "EEG, CT, MRI and fMRI: choosing evidence", "scope": "Ask what the instrument actually measures · Resolution involves a trade-off · A colourful activation map is a comparison", "parent": "psy11-brain", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>EEG, CT, MRI and fMRI: choosing evidence</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Ask what the instrument actually measures</li><li>Resolution involves a trade-off</li><li>A colourful activation map is a comparison</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "EEG, CT, MRI and fMRI", "kind": "learn", "text": "EEG records electrical activity detected through electrodes on the scalp. It has good temporal resolution for rapid changes but limited precision for identifying the exact source deep in the brain. CT uses X-rays to create structural cross-sectional images and involves ionising radiation. MRI uses magnetic fields and radio waves to produce detailed structural images without ionising radiation, with practical constraints such as suitability for some implants. fMRI measures blood-oxygenation changes associated with activity, providing spatial information about functional patterns but an indirect, delayed signal. Choose a method based on the question: sleep-stage electrical patterns suggest EEG; detailed anatomy suggests MRI; task-associated functional localisation suggests fMRI. None can simply read a complete thought from a coloured image.", "diagram": "imaging"}, {"title": "Comparing evidence rather than memorising pictures", "kind": "learn", "text": "A brain image or case history becomes scientific evidence when interpreted within a design. A task-associated fMRI change shows a relationship between task performance and an indirect activity measure; it does not show that a region alone caused the whole behaviour. A structural scan can reveal injury but cannot by itself describe every functional consequence. Historical cases provide detailed observations while lacking the control of an experiment. An exam evaluation should state what each method reveals, what it cannot reveal and why that matters to the conclusion. Use specific comparisons such as EEG timing versus fMRI spatial localisation. Colour in a functional image often represents statistical processing, not literal coloured brain tissue. Explain what the model or measurement stands for before drawing a conclusion.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Temporal and spatial resolution", "kind": "learn", "text": "Temporal resolution concerns distinguishing events close together in time, while spatial resolution concerns distinguishing locations close together. EEG is useful for rapid electrical patterns but source localisation is less precise. fMRI has useful spatial information while the blood-oxygenation response is indirect and relatively slow. A question asking which technique is best has no universal answer without a purpose. State what needs to be known, choose a suitable measure and explain the trade-off. CT and structural MRI reveal anatomy rather than the full timing of processing. Combining methods can provide complementary evidence but also requires thoughtful analysis rather than assuming more images automatically prove causation.", "diagram": "imaging"}, {"title": "Ask what the instrument actually measures", "kind": "learn", "text": "EEG records scalp electrical activity arising from populations of neurons, with useful timing information. CT uses X-rays to construct structural images. Structural MRI uses magnetic-field and radiofrequency properties to produce detailed anatomy. fMRI commonly measures activity-associated blood-oxygenation changes, an indirect signal rather than individual neurons firing. These are different observations, not four interchangeable photographs of thoughts. Choose a method according to whether the question concerns anatomy, fast timing or patterns associated with a task, and explain why its measurement is relevant.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Resolution involves a trade-off", "kind": "learn", "text": "Temporal resolution distinguishes events close together in time; spatial resolution distinguishes locations close together. EEG can reveal rapid changes but has limited precision about their sources. fMRI can help locate patterns but the haemodynamic response is relatively slow. More spatial detail does not automatically produce a more accurate answer to a timing question. CT and MRI can identify structural features without demonstrating the sequence of a cognitive operation. Combining complementary evidence can strengthen an interpretation, provided each measurement’s limitations are retained.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A colourful activation map is a comparison", "kind": "learn", "text": "A task-based fMRI result often compares conditions, such as word processing against a baseline. If the conditions also differ in difficulty, attention or movement, those differences can influence the pattern. A region associated with a task is not thereby proven to be its only cause. Researchers need appropriate controls and cautious inference from group results to individuals. In an evaluation answer identify the measurement, the comparison and an alternative explanation. Simply calling an instrument objective does not remove validity problems or make it read a private mental state directly.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Brain-region and technique reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Brain-region and technique reference</h3><p>Choose evidence by the question being investigated.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Region/method</th><th>Key contribution</th><th>Qualification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Medulla</td><td>Essential autonomic regulation</td><td>Part of hindbrain</td></tr><tr><td>Cerebellum</td><td>Coordination and selected implicit learning</td><td>Interacts with other systems</td></tr><tr><td>Reticular formation</td><td>Arousal/alertness</td><td>Network across brainstem including midbrain</td></tr><tr><td>Thalamus</td><td>Most sensory relay to cortex</td><td>Not hypothalamus</td></tr><tr><td>Hypothalamus</td><td>Homeostatic/autonomic/hormonal regulation</td><td>Multiple interacting functions</td></tr><tr><td>Prefrontal cortex</td><td>Planning/inhibition/judgement</td><td>Distributed networks</td></tr><tr><td>Broca/Wernicke</td><td>Production/comprehension contributions</td><td>Often left-lateralised; patterns vary</td></tr><tr><td>EEG</td><td>Electrical timing</td><td>Limited source precision</td></tr><tr><td>CT</td><td>X-ray structural imaging</td><td>Ionising radiation</td></tr><tr><td>MRI</td><td>Detailed structure</td><td>Magnetic-field practical constraints</td></tr><tr><td>fMRI</td><td>Activity-associated blood oxygenation</td><td>Indirect and delayed</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Historical cases and imaging complement one another. Neither a coloured image nor a single injury establishes every cause of behaviour.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Choosing imaging methods", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Choosing imaging methods", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: comparing evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Choosing imaging methods", "prompt": "A study needs the timing of rapid brain electrical changes and a separate detailed view of brain anatomy. Choose suitable methods and justify each choice. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] EEG is suitable for rapid electrical activity.", "[1] It has strong temporal resolution but limited precise spatial localisation.", "[1] MRI is suitable for detailed structural anatomy.", "[1] It produces structural images rather than directly measuring the timing of electrical impulses."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-brain-q1"}, {"title": "Extended response: comparing evidence", "prompt": "Compare Gage, Sperry’s split-brain research and contemporary imaging as evidence about localisation. Explain what each reveals and evaluate its limits. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Accurately describes Gage’s frontal injury and behavioural contribution.", "[2] Connects historical uncertainty/single case to limits of causal inference.", "[2] Describes controlled visual-field/response tasks and restricted callosal transfer in Sperry’s work.", "[2] Explains unusual samples and task-specific generalisation limits.", "[2] Distinguishes structural imaging, EEG timing and fMRI indirect functional patterns.", "[2] Makes an explicit comparison and explains why no method alone establishes every aspect of a complex behaviour."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-brain-q7"}, {"id": "psy11-brain-imaging-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A researcher asks whether a brain response begins 40 or 90 milliseconds after a sound. Choose a suitable method and compare it with fMRI for this question. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] EEG is suitable for tracking rapid electrical changes with relatively strong temporal resolution.", "[2] fMRI measures a delayed blood-oxygenation response, so it is less suitable for directly distinguishing these fast onsets.", "[2] State the trade-off: EEG source localisation is less precise, while fMRI can provide useful spatial information."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-brain-imaging-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A task produces a coloured fMRI map and a headline says “this spot causes happiness.” Evaluate the headline and propose one relevant improvement to the comparison. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] fMRI measures activity-associated blood oxygenation indirectly, not happiness or single-neuron firing directly.", "[2] Association/localisation in a comparison does not prove exclusive causation; task difficulty or attention may differ.", "[2] Match the control task on relevant demands and use an appropriate wellbeing/emotion measure, retaining causal caution."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which technique measures activity-associated blood oxygenation?", "o": ["fMRI.", "Structural CT as a direct thought detector.", "EEG as a magnetic anatomy scan.", "Ordinary structural MRI as a direct neurotransmitter counter."], "a": 0, "why": "fMRI commonly uses a blood-oxygenation-dependent signal; it is indirect and temporally delayed."}, {"q": "Which method best records rapid scalp electrical patterns?", "o": ["EEG", "CT", "Structural MRI", "A personality label"], "a": 0, "why": "EEG has good temporal resolution for electrical activity."}, {"q": "A left visual-field stimulus initially reaches which hemisphere?", "o": ["Right", "Left", "Neither", "Only the left eye"], "a": 0, "why": "Visual field and eye are different; left-field input initially reaches right hemisphere."}, {"q": "What does the corpus callosum principally support?", "o": ["Communication between hemispheres", "Food digestion", "All muscle contraction", "Only hearing"], "a": 0, "why": "Its fibres permit interhemispheric information transfer."}, {"q": "Primary visual cortex is mainly in which lobe?", "o": ["Occipital", "Parietal", "Frontal", "Temporal"], "a": 0, "why": "Occipital cortex contributes to visual processing."}, {"q": "What does fMRI measure indirectly?", "o": ["Activity-associated blood oxygenation", "Complete private thoughts", "Only skull density", "Action potentials at every synapse"], "a": 0, "why": "BOLD changes are indirect and delayed relative to neural firing."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-development-lifespan", "title": "Developmental domains across the lifespan", "scope": "Physical, cognitive and social change are distinct domains · A milestone is a pattern, not a deadline · Separate age from cohort and measurement", "parent": "psy11-development", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Developmental domains across the lifespan</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Physical, cognitive and social change are distinct domains</li><li>A milestone is a pattern, not a deadline</li><li>Separate age from cohort and measurement</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Development across the lifespan", "kind": "learn", "text": "Development describes changes and continuities in physical, cognitive, social and emotional functioning across life. Infancy, childhood, adolescence, early adulthood, middle age and older age are useful broad categories, but boundaries and individual experiences vary. Physical development includes growth and movement; cognitive development includes attention, memory and language; social development includes relationships; emotional development includes expression and regulation of feeling. These domains interact. Learning to speak supports social participation, while supportive relationships can provide opportunities for language learning. A developmental claim should specify the domain and evidence rather than assuming that every change is biological or that development ends in adulthood. Use typical patterns as frameworks, while acknowledging variation linked to experience, health and cultural context.", "diagram": "lifespan"}, {"title": "Gross motor, fine motor and language development", "kind": "learn", "text": "Gross motor skills involve large-muscle movements such as sitting, walking and running. Fine motor skills involve smaller controlled movements such as grasping, writing and using tools. Language development includes understanding and producing communication, expanding vocabulary and combining words, with considerable variation in timing. Childhood usually brings improved coordination and increasingly complex thought and social participation. These categories are distinct but connected: a child can have strong vocabulary while still developing fine motor control. A scenario about fastening a button points to fine motor development, whereas climbing stairs points to gross motor development. Avoid using a single milestone to judge the whole child. Developmental patterns are not a diagnosis, and age estimates are broad descriptions rather than fixed deadlines for every individual.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Adolescence, adulthood and older age", "kind": "learn", "text": "Adolescence includes puberty, changes in identity and peer relationships, and increasing capacity for abstract thinking and self-regulation. Early adulthood often involves independence, work and close relationships, while middle age and older age can involve changing roles, experience and adaptation to physical change. These experiences are not universal. Cognitive expertise and knowledge can remain strong even when some processing or movement becomes slower. Emotional and social development continue throughout life. When explaining a case, identify the observed change and domain rather than describing all older people as forgetful or all adolescents as reckless. Compare developmental influences carefully: biological maturation, opportunities, relationships and culture interact. A broad stage label cannot predict one person’s exact abilities or decisions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Language, experience and developmental designs", "kind": "learn", "text": "Comparing language in different age groups once is cross-sectional and may confound age with cohort experience. Following the same children repeatedly is longitudinal and shows within-person change, but attrition and repeated-task familiarity can affect the pattern. Neither design by itself manipulates early stimulation, and deliberately depriving children would be unethical. Researchers can use ethical observations and relevant existing evidence to investigate developmental associations. When evaluating a task, consider whether memory, language demands or motor skills affect the response in addition to the intended concept. This connects Piaget’s task limitations with Science Inquiry rather than assuming every incorrect answer reveals only developmental stage.", "diagram": "developmentdesign"}, {"title": "Physical, cognitive and social change are distinct domains", "kind": "learn", "text": "Gross motor development involves larger muscle actions such as walking; fine motor development involves precise movements such as manipulating a small object. Language development concerns comprehension and production, while social and emotional development involves relationships and emotion regulation. A single activity can involve several domains: writing a story requires fine motor control and language, and sharing it involves social interaction. Identify the actual change described rather than classifying the whole person. Developmental periods are broad descriptions; individual pathways and cultural expectations vary.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A milestone is a pattern, not a deadline", "kind": "learn", "text": "Infancy often brings rapid sensory-motor and attachment changes; childhood includes language, skills and social expansion. Adolescence involves physical maturation and changing social roles, while adulthood and older age include both gains and challenges. Average differences do not establish what every individual can do. A developmental explanation should consider biology, learning, opportunities and context. Avoid presenting older adulthood as inevitable total cognitive decline or adolescence as universal irrationality. Variation within an age group can be substantial, and experience can support adaptation throughout life.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Separate age from cohort and measurement", "kind": "learn", "text": "A cross-sectional language comparison between current children and adults may reflect different schooling and experiences as well as age. A longitudinal study follows the same individuals, allowing within-person change to be observed, but repeated tasks, attrition and historical changes can matter. Neither design allows deliberate deprivation or proves that age alone caused the pattern. A task requiring complex spoken instructions may measure language comprehension as well as the intended motor skill. Choose accessible instructions and describe what the measurement can establish.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"id": "psy11-development-lifespan-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A child learns to catch a ball, fasten a small button and explain the rules to a friend. Identify and explain three developmental contributions. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Catching uses gross motor coordination involving larger muscles.", "[2] Fastening a small button uses fine motor control and precise movements.", "[2] Explaining rules uses language/cognitive development; interaction with a friend can also involve social development."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-development-lifespan-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Older adults perform worse on a new computer-based language task than teenagers. Explain two alternative interpretations and propose a targeted improvement. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Age-related change is one possible interpretation, but the comparison is not a manipulation of age.", "[2] Computer familiarity, schooling or cohort experience may differ and affect performance.", "[2] Provide comparable practice/accessibility and consider following participants over time; these changes address specific alternatives rather than proving age is the only cause."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which activity principally illustrates fine motor control?", "o": ["Fastening a small button.", "Running across a field.", "Understanding the meaning of a sentence.", "Describing an emotion in an interview."], "a": 0, "why": "Fine motor skills use precise small-muscle movements; developmental domains can interact."}], "subtitle": "13 screens · 2 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-development-plasticity", "title": "Neural plasticity and adolescent development", "scope": "Developmental and adaptive plasticity can coexist · The five processes do not form a simple one-way timetable · Adolescent development is probabilistic", "parent": "psy11-development", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Neural plasticity and adolescent development</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Developmental and adaptive plasticity can coexist</li><li>The five processes do not form a simple one-way timetable</li><li>Adolescent development is probabilistic</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Developmental and adaptive plasticity", "kind": "learn", "text": "Neural plasticity is the nervous system’s capacity to change its structure or functioning in response to development and experience. Developmental plasticity includes changes as the brain matures. Adaptive plasticity includes changes associated with learning, environmental demands or recovery after injury. These categories overlap in real life, but help organise explanations. Repeated practice can strengthen useful connections, while development involves forming and reorganising networks. Plasticity continues across the lifespan but is constrained by the task, location, age and injury. It does not mean that every brain region can replace any other or that recovery is guaranteed. Link the experience to a change in neural connections or function, then explain how that could support the observed skill.", "diagram": "plasticity"}, {"title": "Stages of neural development", "kind": "learn", "text": "Proliferation involves production of neural cells. Migration involves developing cells moving to appropriate locations. Synaptogenesis forms connections between neurons. Synaptic pruning removes or reduces less-used connections as networks become more selective. Myelination adds insulation to axons, supporting more efficient communication. These processes overlap over developmental periods and do not occur as a single brief sequence that ends permanently. A useful explanation connects each process with function: forming connections allows communication; pruning helps refine networks; myelination supports transmission efficiency. More connections are not automatically better than well-organised connections. The model simplifies complex timing and regional differences. Avoid confusing pruning with destruction of all unused brain areas or migration with neurotransmitter travelling between cells.", "diagram": "plasticity"}, {"title": "Adolescent amygdala and prefrontal development", "kind": "learn", "text": "The amygdala contributes to emotional salience and responses to relevant stimuli. Prefrontal systems contribute to planning, inhibition and evaluating consequences. During adolescence, maturation and connections among these systems continue to develop. Emotional, social and rewarding situations can influence behaviour while regulatory capacities are still developing and depend on context. This provides one contributing explanation for differences in decision-making, but cannot justify the claim that adolescents have no self-control or that the amygdala controls all their actions. Peer context, learning, stress and individual differences also matter. In an exam explanation, name the structures, describe their contributions and connect development to the specified behaviour. Treat the account as a developmental tendency, not a judgement about a particular student.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Developmental and adaptive plasticity can coexist", "kind": "learn", "text": "Developmental plasticity describes changes accompanying maturation and experience across development. Adaptive plasticity describes modification in response to changed demands, learning or injury. The distinction concerns the context of change, not a rigid split between childhood and adulthood. A child learning a skill and an adult practising a new skill both involve experience-dependent changes. Recovery after injury is variable and does not imply that any lost function can be completely restored. Describe what is changing, the relevant experience and the limits of the inference.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "The five processes do not form a simple one-way timetable", "kind": "learn", "text": "Proliferation creates cells; migration moves developing cells to locations; synaptogenesis forms connections; synaptic pruning reduces selected connections; myelination supports efficient conduction. Their timing differs across regions and overlaps. Pruning is not the loss of every unused memory, and myelination is not the creation of neurotransmitters. Practice and environmental input influence networks alongside biological development. When explaining plasticity, name the relevant process and connect it to an effect on circuitry rather than listing the five terms without showing their relationships.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Adolescent development is probabilistic", "kind": "learn", "text": "The amygdala contributes to emotional significance and responses; prefrontal systems contribute to planning, inhibition and evaluation. Changes in their development and connectivity are relevant to adolescent decision making, especially in emotionally or socially salient situations. This does not make teenagers incapable of careful decisions. Sleep, peers, experience and the task itself also influence behaviour. Brain development can explain tendencies at a group level while providing an incomplete account of one individual decision. A good application links the context to interacting systems and avoids biological inevitability.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read, apply and qualify the focused model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read each label and distinguish the relationships or levels of explanation. Describe the pattern in your own words, then use the accompanying theory to explain why it matters. Apply the model to the written questions in this lesson and state its limit rather than treating an illustration as proof. Where a diagram uses fictional numbers or arbitrary units, keep those separate from the findings of a designated study.", "diagram": "plasticity-processes"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Plasticity and development", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Plasticity and development", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Adolescent decisions", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Plasticity and development", "prompt": "Compare developmental and adaptive plasticity and explain pruning and myelination. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Developmental plasticity involves maturation-related neural changes.", "[1] Adaptive plasticity involves changes associated with experience/learning or recovery.", "[2] Pruning refines networks by reducing less-used connections, rather than automatically destroying whole regions.", "[2] Myelination insulates axons and supports more efficient transmission, with timing varying across systems."], "allocations": [1, 1, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-development-q3"}, {"title": "Adolescent decisions", "prompt": "Explain how developing prefrontal systems and emotional salience can contribute to context-dependent adolescent decisions. Include one qualification. (5 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Prefrontal systems support planning, inhibition and consequence evaluation while maturation continues.", "[2] Amygdala-related emotional salience and rewarding/social contexts can influence responses.", "[1] Individual experience and context matter; the explanation does not mean all adolescents lack control."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-development-q6"}, {"id": "psy11-development-plasticity-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain how synaptic pruning and myelination can contribute to developmental change. Distinguish each process from synaptogenesis. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Pruning reduces selected connections, contributing to changes in network organisation and efficiency.", "[2] Myelination insulates axonal sections and supports efficient/faster conduction.", "[2] Synaptogenesis creates synaptic connections; it is distinct from selective reduction and axonal insulation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-development-plasticity-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A headline states that teenagers cannot make rational decisions because their prefrontal cortex is still developing. Evaluate it using brain development and one contextual factor. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Prefrontal maturation/connectivity is relevant to planning and inhibition, and emotional systems including the amygdala interact with it.", "[2] Developmental tendencies do not establish that every teenager is incapable; individual differences and task conditions matter.", "[2] Explain a relevant context, such as peer salience or inadequate sleep, that can alter decision making without making behaviour inevitable."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What does adaptive plasticity describe?", "o": ["Modification in response to changed demands, experience or injury.", "A guarantee of complete recovery after any injury.", "Only the prenatal creation of every neuron.", "The chemical diffusion of myelin across a synapse."], "a": 0, "why": "Adaptive plasticity concerns adjustment; its extent and outcomes vary."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-development-schemas", "title": "Piaget: schemas, assimilation and accommodation", "scope": "A schema organises interpretation · Disequilibrium can lead to accommodation · Use evidence of the change, not just the child’s age", "parent": "psy11-development", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Piaget: schemas, assimilation and accommodation</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>A schema organises interpretation</li><li>Disequilibrium can lead to accommodation</li><li>Use evidence of the change, not just the child’s age</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Piaget’s schemas and assimilation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Piaget’s cognitive-development theory (1936) describes children as active constructors of understanding. A schema is an organised mental framework for interpreting experience and acting on it. Assimilation incorporates a new experience into an existing schema without substantially changing that framework. A child who has a dog schema and calls an unfamiliar breed a dog is assimilating. If the child calls every four-legged animal a dog, the same process is being used but the framework is too broad. State the existing schema and show how the new experience was interpreted through it. Assimilation is not simply learning any new fact. The theory connects maturation and experience with changes in thinking, rather than treating children as miniature adults with only less information.", "diagram": "schema"}, {"title": "Accommodation, equilibrium and disequilibrium", "kind": "learn", "text": "Accommodation changes a schema or creates a new one when an experience cannot be adequately explained by the existing framework. Learning that a cat differs from a dog can lead a child to revise the dog schema and form a cat schema. Equilibrium is a state in which current understanding fits experience sufficiently well. Disequilibrium occurs when a mismatch challenges understanding, creating pressure to reorganise knowledge. Assimilation and accommodation contribute to restoring balance through adaptation. An application should explain the mismatch and actual schema change rather than saying the child was confused. These are theoretical processes, not directly visible objects in the brain. A researcher must infer them from patterns of response, which is one issue to consider when evaluating the theory.", "diagram": "schema"}, {"title": "A schema organises interpretation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A schema is an organised framework of knowledge or action used to interpret experience. A child’s dog schema may include four legs, fur and barking. Assimilation incorporates an experience within an existing schema: the child may initially call a new four-legged animal a dog. That classification can be wrong while still illustrating assimilation. Do not define assimilation as learning nothing new, or assume every new object automatically changes the schema. The question is whether the existing organisation is retained or revised.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Disequilibrium can lead to accommodation", "kind": "learn", "text": "If an animal does not fit the child’s expectations, inconsistency can produce disequilibrium. Accommodation modifies a schema or forms a new one to account for the mismatch, such as distinguishing cats from dogs. Equilibrium describes a workable balance between understanding and experience; it is not a claim that a person has learned everything. A clear sequence includes the previous schema, the contradictory experience and the actual revision. Merely becoming upset or seeing an unfamiliar object does not by itself show accommodation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use evidence of the change, not just the child’s age", "kind": "learn", "text": "Piaget’s theory emphasises active construction of understanding. To identify a process in an exam scenario, look at the child’s explanation before and after the experience. If the scenario supplies only a correct answer, it may not establish how the child acquired it. Teacher explanation, language and prior experience can influence performance, so a brief vignette is simplified evidence. The model is useful for explaining conceptual change, but schema processes should not be treated as an automatic age test or a fixed measure of intelligence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Schema change", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Schema change", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Schema change", "prompt": "A child calls a goat a dog because both have four legs. After learning that goats differ, the child forms a separate category. Apply assimilation, disequilibrium and accommodation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Assimilation interprets the goat through the existing broad dog schema.", "[2] Evidence that the label does not fit creates a mismatch or disequilibrium.", "[2] Accommodation revises the original schema or creates a goat schema, improving fit with experience."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-development-q1"}, {"id": "psy11-development-schemas-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A child calls every flying animal a bird. After learning that bats have different features, the child forms a separate bat category. Explain assimilation, disequilibrium and accommodation in this sequence. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Initially classifying a bat within the existing bird schema illustrates assimilation.", "[2] Conflicting bat features create a mismatch with the bird schema, illustrating disequilibrium.", "[2] Revising the categories/forming a bat schema illustrates accommodation and a possible return to equilibrium."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-development-schemas-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A student memorises that a whale is a mammal but still explains that all mammals must live on land. Evaluate whether the statement alone demonstrates a fully accommodated schema. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Accommodation requires revision of the organising schema, not just memorisation of a label.", "[2] The retained land-only rule shows an unresolved inconsistency, so full revision is not established.", "[2] Ask the student to explain and apply mammal characteristics to new examples to gather evidence of conceptual change."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which change most clearly illustrates accommodation?", "o": ["Revising a category after an experience conflicts with its previous rule.", "Using an unchanged schema for another familiar example.", "Repeating a label without changing the underlying rule.", "Assuming no further experiences can challenge understanding."], "a": 0, "why": "Accommodation changes the schema or creates a new one to fit inconsistent evidence."}], "subtitle": "13 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-development-early-stages", "title": "Piaget: sensorimotor and pre-operational thinking", "scope": "Object permanence must be inferred from suitable evidence · Symbolic thought does not remove egocentrism · Evaluate what the task demands", "parent": "psy11-development", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Piaget: sensorimotor and pre-operational thinking</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Object permanence must be inferred from suitable evidence</li><li>Symbolic thought does not remove egocentrism</li><li>Evaluate what the task demands</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Sensorimotor stage and invisible displacement", "kind": "learn", "text": "Piaget’s sensorimotor stage, approximately birth to two years, involves learning through sensory experience and actions. Object permanence is understanding that an object continues to exist when it is out of view. An invisible-displacement task asks a child to infer where an object has moved when part of its transfer was concealed. This requires more than reacting to an object currently visible. A child who searches appropriately shows evidence of representing the object and its movement. A failure can also reflect task demands, memory, attention or motor limitations. Typical ages are broad estimates. Explain the task, the relevant cognitive ability and what the response suggests, while avoiding the assumption that one unsuccessful trial proves a child has no concept of object permanence.", "diagram": "piaget"}, {"title": "Pre-operational thinking and the three mountains", "kind": "learn", "text": "The pre-operational stage, approximately two to seven years, includes symbolic thinking: words, pictures or pretend objects can stand for other things. Egocentrism refers to difficulty representing another person’s perspective, not selfishness. In the three-mountains task, a child may choose the view seen from their own position rather than a doll’s position. Centration is focusing on one prominent feature while overlooking others. Animism attributes lifelike properties to inanimate things. A child who thinks a tall glass necessarily contains more liquid may focus on height and overlook width. Link each term to the actual response. Task complexity and unfamiliar instructions can influence performance, so later evidence sometimes finds capacities earlier than Piaget’s original tasks suggested.", "diagram": "piaget"}, {"title": "Object permanence must be inferred from suitable evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "In Piaget’s sensorimotor stage, understanding develops through perception and action. Object permanence is the understanding that an object continues to exist when it is not seen. Invisible displacement tasks require tracking an object moved while hidden and then searching appropriately. Failure can reflect memory, attention or motor demands as well as incomplete object understanding. Watching an object disappear is not itself proof of object permanence; evidence concerns expectations or search behaviour. The conventional birth-to-about-two age range is a broad theoretical estimate.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Symbolic thought does not remove egocentrism", "kind": "learn", "text": "Pre-operational children can use symbols, language and pretend play, yet may have difficulty representing another person’s visual perspective. Piagetian egocentrism is a cognitive perspective limitation, not selfishness or intentional unkindness. The three-mountains task asks the child to distinguish views from different positions. Animism attributes life-like qualities to inanimate objects, while centration focuses on one salient feature. A child describing a sad toy and judging a taller glass as containing more liquid may illustrate different concepts rather than one general inability to think.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluate what the task demands", "kind": "learn", "text": "The three-mountains procedure also requires comprehension, memory and interpretation of pictures or models. A familiar, simpler perspective task may produce different performance. This supports criticism of rigid stage conclusions based on one task, without proving that every developmental concept is false. Give the concept, the relevant task behaviour and one specific competing demand. An ethical developmental inquiry uses ordinary, accessible activities and avoids embarrassment or assigning a child a permanent ability label based on a brief response.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Three mountains", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Three mountains", "prompt": "A child selects the photograph representing their own view instead of a doll’s view. Explain the Piagetian concept and give a task-related limitation. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] The response can illustrate pre-operational egocentrism.", "[1] It concerns difficulty representing another perspective, not selfishness.", "[1] Unfamiliar instructions or complex spatial/memory demands can affect performance.", "[1] One failure therefore does not conclusively establish absence of perspective-taking in all contexts."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-development-q4"}, {"id": "psy11-development-early-stages-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A child chooses a photograph of their own view when asked what a person on the opposite side of a mountain model sees. Explain the Piagetian interpretation and one task limitation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The response is consistent with egocentrism in pre-operational thinking: difficulty adopting another visual perspective.", "[2] Link the choice of the child’s own view to the three-mountains task rather than calling the child selfish.", "[2] Language, picture interpretation or memory demands may also affect performance; one response is not a complete stage diagnosis."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-development-early-stages-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain the distinction between object permanence and symbolic thinking. Use an appropriate task or behaviour for each. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Object permanence means an unseen object continues to exist; appropriate search after hidden displacement can provide evidence.", "[2] Symbolic thinking uses something to represent something else, such as pretend play or words.", "[2] Distinguish continuing existence from representation; neither example alone proves all abilities within a developmental stage."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Piagetian egocentrism principally refers to what?", "o": ["Difficulty representing another person’s perspective.", "Deliberately refusing to share.", "Understanding that hidden objects cease to exist.", "Having no symbolic language whatsoever."], "a": 0, "why": "The concept concerns perspective taking; it should not be confused with a moral judgement about selfishness."}], "subtitle": "12 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-development-operations", "title": "Piaget: concrete and formal operations", "scope": "Conservation requires more than the right guess · The pendulum task tests systematic reasoning · Compare strengths with limitations", "parent": "psy11-development", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Piaget: concrete and formal operations</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Conservation requires more than the right guess</li><li>The pendulum task tests systematic reasoning</li><li>Compare strengths with limitations</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Concrete operations and conservation", "kind": "learn", "text": "The concrete operational stage, approximately seven to eleven years, involves more logical operations about concrete situations. Conservation is understanding that a quantity remains the same despite changes in appearance when nothing has been added or removed. A child can explain that water poured into a taller glass has the same volume because the transformation is reversible or height is compensated by width. Seriation involves ordering objects along a dimension, such as length. These abilities show coordination of relationships rather than merely remembering a rule. An exam answer about conservation should name the quantity, transformation and reasoning. Success in concrete tasks does not imply that every abstract hypothetical problem will be solved. The stage framework describes a pattern of thinking with variation across tasks and experience.", "diagram": "piaget"}, {"title": "Formal operations and the pendulum problem", "kind": "learn", "text": "The formal operational stage, typically described from around eleven years, includes abstract reasoning and systematic testing of hypotheses. In a pendulum problem, factors such as string length, mass and release conditions can be varied. Systematic reasoning tests one factor at a time while holding others constant to determine what influences swing period. Uncontrolled changes make the outcome difficult to interpret. This links cognitive development with experimental design. Formal reasoning can depend on familiarity, schooling and context, and is not used equally on every task by every adult. Piaget’s theory emphasises qualitative changes and active learning, but rigid stage ages and underestimation of younger children are limitations. Use specific task evidence and distinguish theory claims from universal rules.", "diagram": "piaget"}, {"title": "Conservation requires more than the right guess", "kind": "learn", "text": "Conservation is understanding that a quantity remains the same despite an irrelevant change in appearance. In a liquid task no liquid is added or removed when it is poured into a differently shaped container. Reversibility mentally undoes the transformation; compensation recognises that greater height can be offset by reduced width. A correct explanation supports an inference of concrete operational reasoning more strongly than a chance answer. Seriation involves ordering objects along a dimension. These concepts concern logical operations on concrete information, not abstract mastery of every subject.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "The pendulum task tests systematic reasoning", "kind": "learn", "text": "Formal operational reasoning includes abstract and hypothetical thinking. In the pendulum problem, a systematic approach changes one factor while controlling others to examine what affects the swing period. A learner who changes length and release height together creates ambiguous evidence. The point is the reasoning strategy, not merely finding the correct physical answer. Adults and adolescents can use formal reasoning differently according to familiarity, instruction and context. Piaget’s stages provide a developmental model rather than an inflexible schedule reached by everyone in every task.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Compare strengths with limitations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Piaget provided a coherent account linking active learning and qualitative changes in reasoning, with tasks that make thought processes observable. Limitations include age variation, cultural and educational influences, language demands and evidence that performance can be more uneven than rigid stages predict. Evaluation should identify how a limitation affects the conclusion. Saying the theory is old gives no explanation. A stronger judgement acknowledges useful concepts while questioning whether a single task can establish a universal, whole-person stage.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Piaget stages and task reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Piaget stages and task reference</h3><p>Ages are conventional broad theory estimates, not rigid universal deadlines.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage (approximate ages)</th><th>Concepts</th><th>Task evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sensorimotor, 0–2</td><td>Object permanence</td><td>Invisible displacement/search</td></tr><tr><td>Pre-operational, 2–7</td><td>Symbolic thought, egocentrism, animism, centration</td><td>Three mountains; conservation difficulty</td></tr><tr><td>Concrete operational, 7–11</td><td>Conservation, reversibility/compensation, seriation</td><td>Quantity transformation; ordering</td></tr><tr><td>Formal operational, about 11 onward</td><td>Abstract and systematic hypothetical reasoning</td><td>Pendulum factor testing</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Infer the concept from the task while considering language, memory, familiarity and motor demands. Adults do not necessarily use formal reasoning on every task.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Conservation task", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Conservation task", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Pendulum reasoning", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: Piaget and inquiry", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Conservation task", "prompt": "Equal water volumes start in identical glasses. One is poured into a taller narrower glass. A child says it has more. Explain centration and contrast concrete-operational reasoning. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] The child focuses on the salient height.", "[1] Centration overlooks compensating width and unchanged quantity.", "[1] Concrete-operational conservation recognises no water was added or removed.", "[1] Reasoning can use compensation or reversibility rather than appearance alone."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-development-q2"}, {"title": "Pendulum reasoning", "prompt": "A learner changes string length, mass and release height together. Explain why the design is weak and how systematic formal reasoning could improve it. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Several factors change simultaneously, creating alternative explanations.", "[1] The result cannot isolate the effect of one factor on swing period.", "[1] Vary one factor across trials while holding the others constant.", "[1] Use repeated, consistent measurement to evaluate the predicted relationship."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-development-q5"}, {"title": "Extended response: Piaget and inquiry", "prompt": "Explain Piaget’s stages with one relevant task for each, apply schema change and evaluate the theory using evidence and task limitations. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Sensorimotor object permanence linked to invisible displacement.", "[2] Pre-operational egocentrism/centration linked to three mountains or conservation difficulty.", "[2] Concrete-operational conservation/seriation with relevant reasoning.", "[2] Formal-operational abstraction and systematic pendulum testing.", "[2] Accurate application of assimilation and accommodation to a schema example.", "[2] Evaluates stage rigidity/task demands and recognises age/cultural/experience variation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-development-q7"}, {"id": "psy11-development-operations-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A learner changes only string length across pendulum trials while keeping mass and release conditions constant. Explain what this strategy illustrates and why changing several factors together would weaken the evidence. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Systematic testing of one factor illustrates hypothetical/formal operational reasoning.", "[2] Holding other relevant factors constant isolates the tested relationship more clearly.", "[2] Simultaneous changes introduce alternative explanations/confounding, so the outcome cannot be attributed confidently to length."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-development-operations-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A child says two containers hold equal liquid because “we can pour it back” and “this one is taller but narrower.” Explain two cognitive operations and evaluate what the response establishes. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Pouring it back illustrates reversibility, mentally undoing the transformation.", "[2] Taller but narrower illustrates compensation between dimensions, supporting conservation.", "[2] The response supports concrete operational reasoning on this task, but does not prove identical performance in all contexts or fix the child’s stage absolutely."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is the best evidence of compensation in conservation?", "o": ["Explaining that greater height is offset by reduced width.", "Judging quantity only from the tallest surface.", "Changing all experimental factors at once.", "Assuming age alone proves understanding."], "a": 0, "why": "Compensation coordinates dimensions rather than centring on one feature."}, {"q": "Revising a schema because a new animal does not fit is what?", "o": ["Accommodation", "Assimilation without change", "Synaptic release", "Seriation"], "a": 0, "why": "Accommodation changes or creates schemas."}, {"q": "Understanding unchanged water quantity despite a new glass shape is what?", "o": ["Conservation", "Animism", "Proliferation", "Egocentrism"], "a": 0, "why": "Conservation concerns unchanged quantity despite transformation."}, {"q": "What does the pendulum task test?", "o": ["Systematic control of factors", "Attachment reunion", "Salivation", "Pure word recognition"], "a": 0, "why": "Formal reasoning can vary one factor while holding others constant."}, {"q": "Reducing less-used neural connections is what?", "o": ["Pruning", "Migration", "Transduction", "Internalisation"], "a": 0, "why": "Pruning refines neural networks."}, {"q": "Piagetian egocentrism primarily concerns what?", "o": ["Difficulty representing another perspective", "Deliberate selfishness", "A lack of every emotion", "Being noisy"], "a": 0, "why": "The term concerns cognition rather than a moral judgement."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-attachment-bowlby", "title": "Bowlby: attachment theory and working models", "scope": "Attachment has an evolutionary function · Internal working models can be revised · Deprivation and separation are not interchangeable", "parent": "psy11-attachment", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Bowlby: attachment theory and working models</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Attachment has an evolutionary function</li><li>Internal working models can be revised</li><li>Deprivation and separation are not interchangeable</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Attachment as a relationship", "kind": "learn", "text": "Attachment is an enduring emotional bond in which an infant or child seeks proximity, comfort and security from a familiar caregiver, especially when distressed. A secure base supports exploration because the caregiver is available when needed. Attachment differs from a momentary preference for an interesting object and is not limited to feeding. Children can form meaningful attachments to multiple caregivers. The pattern of a relationship develops through interactions and context; it is not a simple measure of how much a child loves someone. When applying the concept, identify seeking comfort or returning to the caregiver during uncertainty. Use fictional scenarios and study evidence rather than labelling your own family or classmates with attachment categories.", "diagram": "attachment"}, {"title": "Bowlby’s evolutionary perspective", "kind": "learn", "text": "Bowlby’s attachment theory (1969, 1988) proposes that attachment behaviours, such as crying and following, help keep a vulnerable infant near a protective caregiver and therefore support survival. Monotropy refers to the proposed special importance of a primary attachment figure while recognising that other attachments can exist. The caregiver need not be the biological mother. This perspective explains proximity seeking as more than a learned preference for food. It also connects attachment to later expectations of relationships. A strength is its coherent account of why security and responsive care matter. A limitation is that early formulations can overemphasise a single relationship and do not fully account for diverse caregiving arrangements or the capacity for later supportive experiences to influence development.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Critical and sensitive periods", "kind": "learn", "text": "A critical period is a restricted developmental window during which particular experience is required for typical development, with limited later opportunity if it is absent. A sensitive period is a time of heightened responsiveness when relevant experience has a particularly strong influence, but development can still occur later. Bowlby’s early account emphasised a critical attachment period in early childhood, while later interpretation allows a more flexible sensitive period. Avoid treating one birthday as a universal deadline beyond which attachment is impossible. The distinction concerns the degree of developmental flexibility. In a scenario, explain why early experience may be influential and whether the claim allows later development. Evidence of early deprivation does not by itself show that no recovery or new attachment can ever occur.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Maternal deprivation and the internal working model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Bowlby’s maternal-deprivation account proposed that prolonged disruption of a significant early attachment could affect later social, emotional and cognitive development. Modern discussion should recognise the role of responsive care from different caregivers and distinguish temporary separation with good support from severe ongoing deprivation. An internal working model is a set of expectations about the self, others and relationships shaped partly by caregiving experience. Consistently responsive care may support expectations that others are available and that the child is worthy of care. These expectations can guide later interaction, but are not a fixed destiny. Avoid claiming that every hospital stay or non-maternal care damages development. Apply the theoretical mechanism and then recognise contextual influences and opportunities for change.", "diagram": "attachment"}, {"title": "Attachment patterns and later outcomes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Longitudinal associations between early attachment patterns and later relationships can be informative, but do not prove that one early classification fixes a person’s future. Caregiving can change, protective relationships may develop and circumstances can influence both early and later functioning. A study must consider whether the same factors provide alternative explanations. Internal working models are expectations, not immutable biological labels. Ainsworth’s categories describe observed relationship patterns under a procedure. Use that wording when interpreting a vignette and avoid converting it into a diagnosis of parenting quality. Evidence for developmental influence should be expressed alongside the capacity for adaptation and contextual variation.", "diagram": "attachment"}, {"title": "Attachment has an evolutionary function", "kind": "learn", "text": "Attachment is an enduring emotional relationship involving proximity seeking, comfort and a secure base for exploration. Bowlby proposed that proximity to a caregiver supports protection and survival. Monotropy gives a primary attachment particular importance, but does not mean that children cannot form several attachments or that only a biological mother can provide care. Distinguish the theory’s original language from a universal claim about family arrangements. A child exploring and returning for reassurance illustrates the relationship between attachment and independent activity.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Internal working models can be revised", "kind": "learn", "text": "Bowlby’s internal working model describes expectations about self, others and relationships arising from caregiving experience. Reliable comfort may support expectations that others can respond and that help is available. Such expectations can influence later behaviour, but later relationships and circumstances can change them. An association between early attachment and later outcomes does not establish an inevitable individual future. Critical periods imply a narrow time in which an experience is necessary; sensitive periods describe heightened responsiveness without ruling out later development.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Deprivation and separation are not interchangeable", "kind": "learn", "text": "Deprivation concerns losing or lacking sufficiently responsive care after a relationship has developed; privation concerns not having formed an attachment relationship. A brief separation in a supportive context is not equivalent to prolonged deprivation. When applying Bowlby, specify the care, duration and available relationships rather than predicting permanent damage from any separation. The theory directs attention to relationship quality and continuity, but evaluation must consider multiple caregivers, cultural variation and the ethical inability to manipulate severe deprivation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Bowlby’s concepts", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Bowlby’s concepts", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Bowlby’s concepts", "prompt": "Distinguish critical and sensitive periods, and explain an internal working model using a fictional responsive caregiver. (5 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Critical period implies a restricted window with limited later opportunity.", "[1] Sensitive period implies heightened responsiveness with later change still possible.", "[1] An internal working model concerns expectations about self and relationships.", "[1] Responsive care can support expectations that others are available and the self merits care.", "[1] These expectations can guide later interactions but are not fixed destiny."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-attachment-q3"}, {"id": "psy11-attachment-bowlby-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A toddler explores while a familiar caregiver is present and returns to that person for comfort. Explain the secure-base function and how this could relate to an internal working model. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The attachment figure provides a secure base supporting exploration and proximity when reassurance is needed.", "[2] Responsive comfort can support expectations that help is available and relationships are reliable.", "[2] These expectations are an internal working model, which can influence later interactions but remains open to change."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-attachment-bowlby-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare critical and sensitive periods, then evaluate the claim that a brief separation inevitably causes permanent attachment damage. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A critical period proposes a necessary narrow window; a sensitive period proposes heightened responsiveness with later development still possible.", "[2] Brief separation differs from sustained deprivation and must be interpreted with caregiving quality and alternative support.", "[2] Outcomes vary and working models can change, so inevitable permanent damage is unsupported."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is an internal working model?", "o": ["A framework of expectations about self, others and relationships.", "A fixed brain injury caused by every brief separation.", "A requirement that attachment can involve only one biological parent.", "A measure of motor coordination."], "a": 0, "why": "Bowlby’s model concerns relationship expectations, not an unchangeable diagnosis."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-attachment-harlow", "title": "Harlow (1958): contact comfort and research evaluation", "scope": "Separate the research question from the outcome · Explain why contact comfort mattered · Evaluate the specified study, not every later experiment", "parent": "psy11-attachment", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Harlow (1958): contact comfort and research evaluation</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Separate the research question from the outcome</li><li>Explain why contact comfort mattered</li><li>Evaluate the specified study, not every later experiment</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Harlow (1958): aim and experimental comparison", "kind": "learn", "text": "Harlow’s 1958 work investigated the basis of affection and attachment in infant rhesus monkeys, particularly contact comfort compared with feeding. In a key comparison, infants had access to a wire surrogate and a cloth-covered surrogate. For some infants the wire surrogate provided milk; for others the cloth surrogate did. Researchers recorded time spent with each surrogate and responses during frightening or unfamiliar situations. This allowed the role of comfort to be considered separately from which surrogate supplied food. The feeding arrangement was an experimental factor, while contact time and behaviour provided measures. The publication included more than one procedure, so specify the cloth/wire comparison when describing the study rather than mixing it with later isolation research.", "diagram": "harlow"}, {"title": "Harlow (1958): findings and contribution", "kind": "learn", "text": "The infants spent much more time with the cloth-covered surrogate regardless of which surrogate provided milk. They sought the cloth surrogate during frightening situations and used its presence as a base for exploration. These findings challenged an account in which attachment is explained only by feeding and highlighted contact comfort. The results concerned rhesus monkeys under unusual laboratory rearing conditions, so they do not prove that human attachment has no relation to feeding or that all caregiving behaviours reduce to touch. State the result and then the theoretical contribution. A study description needs more than the phrase monkeys liked soft things: explain how the feeding comparison made the evidence relevant to competing explanations of attachment.", "diagram": "harlow"}, {"title": "Harlow (1958): ethics and generalisation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Harlow’s procedures involved separation from normal caregiving and distress, creating serious animal-welfare concerns. Evaluation should identify the harm and link it to the method rather than merely stating that animals were used. The findings contributed to understanding attachment, but scientific value does not erase ethical costs. Human caregiving differs from laboratory surrogate conditions, limiting direct generalisation. Current animal-research reasoning includes replacement with non-animal methods where possible, reduction of animal numbers consistent with valid evidence and refinement to reduce suffering. Do not reproduce deprivation or distress procedures as a class activity. Contrast a controlled comparison’s explanatory strength with the restricted sample and artificial environment. This provides a balanced evaluation rather than an unsupported verdict that the whole study was either perfect or useless.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Separate the research question from the outcome", "kind": "learn", "text": "Harlow’s 1958 work investigated the basis of infant rhesus monkeys’ attachment, challenging an account based only on feeding. In a central comparison infant monkeys had access to wire and cloth surrogate figures, with feeding arranged through different surrogates for different animals. Time spent with each surrogate and behaviour under frightening or unfamiliar conditions provided evidence. The feeding source was therefore distinguished from the comforting surface. State this comparison before the finding, rather than saying that the study simply showed monkeys like mothers.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Explain why contact comfort mattered", "kind": "learn", "text": "Monkeys spent substantially more time with the cloth surrogate, including when the wire surrogate provided milk. They also sought the cloth surrogate under threatening conditions, and its presence supported exploration. The pattern supported contact comfort as an important basis of attachment, beyond food provision alone. It did not show that nutrition is unnecessary or that a cloth object provides all elements of human caregiving. Keep the claim proportional to the comparison and the behaviours that were measured.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluate the specified study, not every later experiment", "kind": "learn", "text": "The designated 1958 surrogate study must not be replaced by details of Harlow’s later severe isolation studies. Separation and artificial rearing nevertheless raise serious welfare issues in the designated work. Controlled comparison helps test a feeding-only explanation, while an animal model, small samples and artificial surrogates limit generalisation to human relationships. A good evaluation explains both the evidential gain and the participant cost. Ethical safeguards are not made irrelevant by a historically influential result.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Harlow’s comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Harlow’s comparison", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Harlow’s comparison", "prompt": "Explain how Harlow’s cloth/wire surrogate comparison tested feeding against contact comfort. State the broad finding and contribution. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Milk was provided by different surrogates across conditions, separating feeding from cloth comfort.", "[2] Infants spent more time with the cloth surrogate regardless of feeding arrangement and sought it under distress.", "[2] Findings challenged feeding-only explanations and highlighted contact comfort, with limited human generalisation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-attachment-q1"}, {"id": "psy11-attachment-harlow-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain how Harlow’s surrogate comparison challenged a feeding-only explanation of attachment. Include method, finding and contribution. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Infant rhesus monkeys had access to wire and cloth surrogates, with the milk source distinguished across feeding arrangements.", "[2] Substantial preference for the cloth surrogate occurred even where the wire surrogate provided milk, with comfort seeking in threatening conditions.", "[2] This supported contact comfort as important beyond feeding alone; it did not make feeding irrelevant."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-attachment-harlow-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Evaluate one methodological strength, one generalisation limit and one ethical issue in Harlow (1958). (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Controlled separation of feeding source and surrogate surface helped test competing explanations.", "[2] Rhesus monkeys and artificial surrogates do not directly represent the full complexity of human caregiving.", "[2] Separation/artificial rearing could cause distress and lasting harm, raising protection-from-harm and animal-welfare concerns."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which finding challenges feeding alone as the basis of attachment?", "o": ["Preference for the cloth surrogate even when milk came from the wire surrogate.", "A conclusion that nutrition has no importance.", "An experiment using human infants randomly deprived of care.", "Every later isolation procedure being part of the 1958 comparison."], "a": 0, "why": "The key evidence separates feeding provision from contact comfort in the designated surrogate study."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-attachment-ainsworth", "title": "Ainsworth (1978): Strange Situation and patterns", "scope": "Reunion is particularly informative · Classifications describe a relationship under a procedure · Reliability and validity ask different questions", "parent": "psy11-attachment", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Ainsworth (1978): Strange Situation and patterns</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Reunion is particularly informative</li><li>Classifications describe a relationship under a procedure</li><li>Reliability and validity ask different questions</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Ainsworth (1978): Strange Situation method", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ainsworth’s 1978 attachment work used the Strange Situation, a structured observation of infants, typically around 12–18 months, with their caregiver and a stranger. A short sequence included exploration, separation and reunion episodes. Researchers observed exploration, responses to separation, reactions to the stranger and especially behaviour when the caregiver returned. The reunion pattern was central to classification. Standardising the episodes helped researchers compare responses, but the situation was unfamiliar and deliberately mildly stressful. It did not measure love with a questionnaire or randomly assign infants to attachment styles. Describe the observation method and the behaviours recorded. A category reflects a pattern observed in that relationship and procedure, rather than a permanent label for the child across every setting.", "diagram": "strangesituation"}, {"title": "Type A, B and C attachment patterns", "kind": "learn", "text": "Type B, secure attachment, typically involves exploration with the caregiver as a secure base and effective comfort on reunion after distress. Type A, insecure avoidant attachment, involves comparatively little overt proximity seeking or avoidance on reunion, although limited visible distress does not prove absence of physiological stress. Type C, insecure resistant attachment, involves distress with difficulty settling, often combining proximity seeking with resistance on reunion. These are patterns in Ainsworth’s classification, not personality diagnoses or rankings of love. Separate the observable reunion response from any explanation about prior caregiving. A vignette must supply enough evidence to support a classification. One behaviour alone, such as crying at separation, does not distinguish every type, since secure infants may also become distressed.", "diagram": "attachmentstyles"}, {"title": "Ainsworth: contribution and limitations", "kind": "learn", "text": "The Strange Situation provided a systematic way to describe differences in infant-caregiver attachment and generated research connecting early relationships to exploration and comfort. Standardised episodes and trained observation can support consistent classification. However, infants’ temperament, familiarity with separation and cultural caregiving practices can influence responses. The brief laboratory setting may not represent everyday relationships, and classification can vary across caregivers. Ethical concerns include distress during separation, requiring monitoring and protection. Evaluation should connect a specific feature to its consequence: a culture in which infants rarely separate may show greater distress in this unfamiliar procedure, affecting interpretation. The method is useful evidence within limits, rather than a universal test of parenting quality or an instruction to diagnose classmates.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reunion is particularly informative", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ainsworth’s Strange Situation used a structured sequence involving an infant, caregiver and stranger, with separations and reunions. Exploration, distress and responses to reunion were observed. Secure attachment is not defined as never crying: a securely attached infant may be distressed on separation but seek and use comfort effectively at reunion, then resume exploration. Avoidant behaviour involves avoidance or limited overt contact, while resistant behaviour combines proximity seeking with resistance and difficulty settling. Interpret the overall pattern rather than one isolated action.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Classifications describe a relationship under a procedure", "kind": "learn", "text": "Type A, B and C are the specified avoidant, secure and resistant patterns. They are not intelligence scores, immutable personality types or a complete diagnosis of a caregiver. Behaviour can depend on familiarity with separation, the setting and cultural practices. Some infants who show little overt distress can still experience physiological arousal. This is why observation and interpretation should be distinguished. The structured procedure supports comparison, but a single laboratory observation cannot establish every feature of a family relationship.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reliability and validity ask different questions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Observers can use operational categories and independent coding to improve inter-rater reliability. Agreement between raters does not itself establish that the categories mean the same thing across cultural settings or fully represent everyday attachment. Validity concerns the inference from observed behaviour to the intended relationship construct. Ethical evaluation includes monitoring distress and limiting unnecessary separation. Explain how a proposed change addresses the actual issue: training coders improves consistency, while culturally informed additional evidence addresses interpretation and external validity.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Strange Situation episode sequence", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Strange Situation episode sequence</h3><p>The classic procedure includes a brief introductory episode and seven subsequent episodes, often described as eight in total. Distress requires monitoring; it is historical research to analyse.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Episode</th><th>Main arrangement</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Caregiver and infant enter unfamiliar room</td><td>Introduction/orientation</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Caregiver present while infant explores</td><td>Exploration/secure-base behaviour</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Stranger enters and interacts</td><td>Response to unfamiliar adult</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Caregiver leaves; stranger remains</td><td>First separation</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Caregiver returns; stranger leaves</td><td>First reunion and comfort</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Caregiver leaves infant briefly alone</td><td>Second separation</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Stranger returns</td><td>Response without caregiver</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Caregiver returns; stranger leaves</td><td>Second reunion</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Observe the whole pattern, especially reunion. Type A avoidance, Type B effective comfort/secure base and Type C distress with resistant proximity seeking are relationship patterns under the procedure, not diagnoses.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Reunion behaviour", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Reunion behaviour", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Ainsworth method and limits", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Reunion behaviour", "prompt": "An infant explores with the caregiver present, is distressed at separation and settles after seeking comfort on reunion. Identify the pattern and explain why crying alone is insufficient. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] This is consistent with Type B secure attachment in the described procedure.", "[1] Exploration and effective comfort on reunion are relevant evidence.", "[1] Secure and insecure-resistant infants can both show separation distress.", "[1] Reunion behaviour and the wider pattern are needed rather than crying alone."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-attachment-q2"}, {"title": "Ainsworth method and limits", "prompt": "Describe the Strange Situation’s method and evaluate one ethical and one cultural limitation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Standardised observation includes exploration, stranger/separation and reunion episodes, recording relevant infant behaviour.", "[2] Separation distress requires protection and monitoring; consent/debriefing do not erase harm.", "[2] Familiarity with separation and caregiving norms can change behaviour, limiting cultural equivalence of interpretations."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-attachment-q4"}, {"id": "psy11-attachment-ainsworth-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "An infant is upset on separation, seeks the returning caregiver, settles with comfort and resumes exploration. Identify the likely pattern and explain why separation crying alone is insufficient. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The overall pattern is consistent with Type B secure attachment.", "[2] Effective reunion comfort and renewed exploration indicate secure-base use, despite separation distress.", "[2] Crying alone occurs in more than one pattern; the classification uses the full sequence, especially reunion."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-attachment-ainsworth-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Two observers agree on every Strange Situation classification. Evaluate whether this proves the procedure is valid across all cultures. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Agreement supports inter-rater reliability when coding was independent and suitable.", "[2] Reliability does not prove validity; cultural meanings and experience of separation may affect the observed behaviour.", "[2] Add culturally informed evidence and relevant naturalistic/longitudinal observations rather than assuming identical interpretation everywhere."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which pattern best supports secure attachment?", "o": ["Distress followed by effective comfort and renewed exploration at reunion.", "Any crying proves insecure attachment.", "No visible distress always proves security.", "A classification permanently fixes adult relationships."], "a": 0, "why": "Reunion behaviour and secure-base use matter more than a single separation response."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-attachment-culture", "title": "Attachment across cultures, deprivation and enrichment", "scope": "Within-culture variation matters · A common procedure may not have a common meaning · Deprivation evidence has multiple possible causes", "parent": "psy11-attachment", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Attachment across cultures, deprivation and enrichment</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Within-culture variation matters</li><li>A common procedure may not have a common meaning</li><li>Deprivation evidence has multiple possible causes</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Cross-cultural patterns: van Ijzendoorn and Kroonenberg (1988)", "kind": "learn", "text": "Van Ijzendoorn and Kroonenberg’s 1988 meta-analysis combined Strange Situation findings from multiple samples across countries. Secure attachment was the most common classification overall. There were differences between samples and countries, and variation within countries was substantial, exceeding the between-country variation in their analysis. This supports the importance of considering cultural context and within-culture diversity. It does not justify assigning a fixed attachment type to a nationality. The included samples were not a perfectly representative census of every culture, and using the same laboratory procedure across settings does not guarantee equivalent meaning. Explain the broad pattern, then evaluate sample coverage and cultural validity. A meta-analysis draws together previous studies; it does not directly observe every participant again under new conditions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Enrichment, deprivation and the Genie case", "kind": "learn", "text": "An enriched environment offers responsive relationships, language, varied stimulation and opportunities for learning. Severe deprivation can restrict development across physical, cognitive, social and emotional domains. Genie’s case involved extreme isolation and maltreatment before discovery in childhood, followed by attempts to support learning and language. She acquired some language and skills but had continuing difficulties, including complex grammatical development. The case illustrates potential consequences of deprivation and the importance of experience, but cannot isolate one cause: trauma, nutrition, unknown early development and later care also matter. Research and care raised ethical concerns about privacy, consent and participant welfare. Avoid claiming that the case proves a precise universal language deadline or that an individual’s development can be explained entirely by one attachment theory.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Within-culture variation matters", "kind": "learn", "text": "Van Ijzendoorn and Kroonenberg’s 1988 meta-analysis compared Strange Situation findings across studies and countries. Secure attachment was the most common pattern overall; the distributions of insecure patterns varied. Variation within countries was substantial and exceeded between-country variation in the reported comparison. A country is not a uniform culture, and study samples are not necessarily representative of every family there. Do not turn an aggregate pattern into a stereotype about an individual parent or assume that a higher proportion of one category proves inferior caregiving.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A common procedure may not have a common meaning", "kind": "learn", "text": "A standardised separation has different familiarity where infants experience different caregiving and separation practices. This can alter distress or stranger responses independently of the intended attachment interpretation. Meta-analysis combines existing studies, so sampling and methodological variation remain relevant. Its strength is comparing a broader evidence base than one laboratory sample. Its limits include uneven representation and whether measures are culturally equivalent. Evaluation should connect the measurement issue to the claim being made, not simply state that culture matters.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Deprivation evidence has multiple possible causes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Genie’s case provided unusual evidence about severe deprivation, language and social development. Abuse, limited stimulation, health, unknown prior difficulties and later caregiving were intertwined, so no controlled single-cause conclusion follows. Enrichment provides opportunities for social, cognitive and emotional development but does not guarantee that every earlier effect disappears. A student resource should examine the case scientifically and respectfully, rather than treating a person’s experience as a dramatic demonstration. It would be unethical to create deprivation experimentally.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read, apply and qualify the focused model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read each label and distinguish the relationships or levels of explanation. Describe the pattern in your own words, then use the accompanying theory to explain why it matters. Apply the model to the written questions in this lesson and state its limit rather than treating an illustration as proof. Where a diagram uses fictional numbers or arbitrary units, keep those separate from the findings of a designated study.", "diagram": "attachment-variation"}, {"title": "Check the research and authoritative explanations", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Further evidence</h3><p>The lesson explanations provide the required student account. These links allow study details and educational health information to be checked. Read historical methods critically; they are not activities to reproduce.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Harlow/love.htm\">Harlow (1958), original report</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.routledge.com/Patterns-of-Attachment-A-Psychological-Study-of-the-Strange-Situation/Ainsworth-Blehar-Waters-Wall/p/book/9780898594614\">Ainsworth et al. (1978), publication details</a></li></ul>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Cross-cultural evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Genie and causation", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: theory and studies", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Cross-cultural evidence", "prompt": "Explain two key conclusions from van Ijzendoorn and Kroonenberg (1988), then state one limit. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Secure attachment was most common overall in the combined studies.", "[1] Substantial variation occurred within countries, exceeding between-country variation in the analysis.", "[1] Countries cannot be assigned one fixed attachment type.", "[1] Sample coverage and applying one procedure across cultures limit universal interpretation."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-attachment-q5"}, {"title": "Genie and causation", "prompt": "A headline claims Genie proves that deprivation alone determines all later development. Evaluate using two alternatives and a methodological limitation. (5 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Trauma, nutrition, unknown early development or later care provide relevant alternative influences.", "[1] The case lacks random assignment and a controlled comparison.", "[1] A single unusual case limits generalisation.", "[1] It illustrates severe deprivation’s possible consequences without proving one universal causal rule."], "allocations": [2, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-attachment-q6"}, {"title": "Extended response: theory and studies", "prompt": "Explain Bowlby’s account and evaluate Harlow and Ainsworth as supporting evidence. Include cultural patterns and the distinction between findings and inference. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Explains attachment, evolution/monotropy and early developmental influence accurately.", "[2] Explains internal working model without deterministic claims.", "[2] Describes Harlow’s comparison and comfort finding.", "[2] Describes Ainsworth’s method and reunion-based A/B/C patterns.", "[2] Evaluates welfare/child distress and limits of animal/cultural generalisation.", "[2] Uses cross-cultural variation and distinguishes observed behaviour from inferred caregiving explanations."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-attachment-q7"}, {"id": "psy11-attachment-culture-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain why the 1988 cross-cultural meta-analysis does not justify describing an entire country as having one attachment style. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] It combined study samples rather than measuring every family in each country.", "[2] Substantial within-country variation means an aggregate distribution does not represent each individual or a uniform culture.", "[2] Cultural caregiving/separation practices and measurement interpretation can affect classifications, limiting simple country labels."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-attachment-culture-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Evaluate the claim that Genie’s case proves one precise critical-period boundary for all human language learning. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The case involved severe deprivation alongside abuse and other uncertain or interacting influences.", "[2] A single uncontrolled case cannot isolate one cause or establish a universal precise age boundary.", "[2] It provides important evidence about developmental experience and limitations, but broader ethical evidence and cautious interpretation are needed."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which conclusion about cross-cultural attachment is most defensible?", "o": ["Aggregate patterns must be interpreted alongside within-country variation and measurement context.", "Every family in a country has the same attachment type.", "A meta-analysis removes all original sampling limitations.", "Strange Situation categories automatically rank whole cultures."], "a": 0, "why": "Country-level summaries cannot replace individual and culturally informed interpretation."}, {"q": "Most informative for Strange Situation classification?", "o": ["Pattern especially at reunion", "Eye colour", "One cry alone", "A later exam grade"], "a": 0, "why": "Reunion response is central and the whole pattern matters."}, {"q": "Harlow’s key comparison challenged what?", "o": ["Feeding-only attachment explanations", "All contact comfort", "All learning", "Existence of sensory neurons"], "a": 0, "why": "Cloth preference regardless of milk source highlighted comfort."}, {"q": "A sensitive period allows what?", "o": ["Heightened early responsiveness with later change possible", "No later development", "Instant maturity", "No environmental effect"], "a": 0, "why": "Sensitive differs from a strictly restricted critical window."}, {"q": "What is an internal working model?", "o": ["Expectations about self and relationships", "A visible brain compartment", "A fixed IQ score", "A feeding schedule"], "a": 0, "why": "Theoretical expectations can guide later interaction and can change."}, {"q": "Cross-cultural attachment findings justify which claim?", "o": ["Substantial within-culture variation must be considered", "One type per nationality", "Every separation is harmful", "No secure attachment existed"], "a": 0, "why": "Meta-analytic patterns do not support national stereotypes."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-inquiry-ethics", "title": "Research ethics: human participants and animal research", "scope": "Consent is an ongoing process · Privacy, confidentiality and anonymity differ · Deception and animal welfare require justification", "parent": "psy11-inquiry", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Research ethics: human participants and animal research</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Consent is an ongoing process</li><li>Privacy, confidentiality and anonymity differ</li><li>Deception and animal welfare require justification</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Ethics review and protection from harm", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ethical guidelines protect participants and support trustworthy research. An independent ethics committee considers whether the question, methods and risks are justified and monitors approved conduct. Protection from harm covers physical and psychological risks, not only obvious injury. An apparently simple memory task can cause embarrassment if scores are public or participation is pressured. A low-risk design uses neutral material, makes responses private and avoids judgement about ability. Ethical approval is a process, not a sentence added after data collection. Historical studies can be analysed without copying harmful methods. Students should practise inquiry with provided fictional or published data unless a school-approved activity has appropriate safeguards. Scientific interest does not make every method acceptable.", "diagram": "ethics"}, {"title": "Consent, participation and withdrawal", "kind": "learn", "text": "Informed consent means receiving understandable information about what participation involves, relevant risks, data handling and rights before agreeing. Voluntary participation means the decision is free from coercion. Withdrawal rights allow stopping participation without penalty and require clarity about whether and until when identifiable data can be removed. A reward or a teacher’s authority can create pressure, so participation should not determine grades or treatment. Children generally require age-appropriate assent and appropriate parent or guardian consent according to the approved procedure. Signing a form alone does not guarantee informed or voluntary agreement. In a question, identify the actual problem, explain the affected right and propose a practical improvement such as an alternative activity and a clear confidential withdrawal process.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Privacy, confidentiality, deception and debriefing", "kind": "learn", "text": "Privacy concerns control over access to personal information and participation. Confidentiality concerns how researchers protect information they obtain from disclosure. Anonymous data contain no identifying link; confidential data may contain a protected identifying link. Deception involves withholding or misleading participants about aspects of a study and requires strong justification, independent approval and safeguards where permitted. Debriefing explains the true purpose, procedures and deception, addresses questions and checks wellbeing. It does not retroactively erase distress or make all deception acceptable. A public list of names and memory scores threatens confidentiality and privacy. Removing names after posting is inadequate prevention. Use fictional scenarios to reason about ethics, and avoid collecting sensitive personal disclosures for student activities.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Animal research and the three Rs", "kind": "learn", "text": "Replacement means using alternatives to animals when they can answer the research question, such as appropriate existing evidence or other methods. Reduction means using the minimum animal numbers compatible with valid research rather than an arbitrarily tiny sample that wastes lives through unusable evidence. Refinement means modifying housing, care and procedures to reduce distress and suffering. These principles guide evaluation of historical animal studies such as Harlow’s work and learning experiments. A strong answer connects a proposed change with the relevant R and its effect. Merely saying use fewer animals cannot address a question about replacing animals entirely. Scientific contribution should be discussed alongside welfare costs and the limits of generalising animal findings to humans.", "diagram": "ethics"}, {"title": "Consent is an ongoing process", "kind": "learn", "text": "Informed consent requires understandable information about participation, relevant risks and how data will be used. Voluntary participation and withdrawal rights must remain meaningful: a person should not feel pressured by grades, authority or a reward they cannot reasonably refuse. For minors, appropriate caregiver permission and the young person’s assent are important within approved procedures. Signing a form does not make subsequent harmful or deceptive conduct acceptable. An ethics committee reviews the plan and monitors conditions; approval does not remove the researcher’s continuing responsibilities.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Privacy, confidentiality and anonymity differ", "kind": "learn", "text": "Privacy concerns access to a person and their information. Confidentiality concerns protecting information entrusted to researchers. Anonymity means responses cannot be linked to identities, which may not be possible in an interview where the researcher knows the participant. A coded identifier supports protection but a separately held identity key means data are not fully anonymous to the research team. Use accurate descriptions when explaining a design, and limit access, collect only necessary information and explain retention or withdrawal arrangements.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Deception and animal welfare require justification", "kind": "learn", "text": "Deception can affect informed choice and should never be treated as an automatic solution to demand characteristics. Where ethically justified, it needs review, minimised risk and appropriate debriefing, with clear consideration of participants’ rights. Animal research uses replacement where suitable alternatives exist, reduction to avoid unnecessary use and refinement to minimise distress and improve welfare. Historical distressing studies are material to evaluate, not classroom activities to reproduce. Scientific usefulness and ethical acceptability are separate judgements that both need evidence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read, apply and qualify the focused model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read each label and distinguish the relationships or levels of explanation. Describe the pattern in your own words, then use the accompanying theory to explain why it matters. Apply the model to the written questions in this lesson and state its limit rather than treating an illustration as proof. Where a diagram uses fictional numbers or arbitrary units, keep those separate from the findings of a designated study.", "diagram": "privacy-distinctions"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"id": "psy11-inquiry-ethics-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A researcher calls recorded interviews anonymous because names will not appear in the report. Evaluate this description and explain two relevant protections. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Interviews known or identifiable to the researcher are not fully anonymous merely because published names are removed.", "[2] Confidentiality requires restricted access, secure handling and removal of identifying details where possible.", "[2] Informed consent should explain recording, use and withdrawal arrangements; privacy requires collecting only appropriate information."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-inquiry-ethics-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A teacher proposes giving extra marks only to students who complete a stress questionnaire about their own health. Identify two ethical concerns and suggest a safer design. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Grade-linked participation can undermine voluntariness and meaningful withdrawal.", "[2] Personal health disclosure raises privacy and potential psychological harm concerns.", "[2] Use approved low-risk fictional data or an optional appropriate task with an equivalent alternative, avoiding personal disclosure and grade pressure."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which statement accurately describes confidentiality?", "o": ["Protecting information entrusted to the research team.", "Guaranteeing that researchers never know an interviewee’s identity.", "Permitting any harm after a form is signed.", "Removing the need for voluntary participation."], "a": 0, "why": "Confidentiality protects entrusted data; it differs from complete anonymity."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 2 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-inquiry-variables", "title": "Research aims, hypotheses, variables and confounds", "scope": "Translate a construct into a measurement · Extraneous becomes confounding when it tracks the condition · Operational precision supports evaluation", "parent": "psy11-inquiry", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Research aims, hypotheses, variables and confounds</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Translate a construct into a measurement</li><li>Extraneous becomes confounding when it tracks the condition</li><li>Operational precision supports evaluation</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Aims, hypotheses and operational variables", "kind": "learn", "text": "An aim states the purpose of an investigation. A quantitative hypothesis predicts a relationship or difference between measurable variables. A directional hypothesis predicts a direction, while a non-directional hypothesis predicts a difference or association without specifying which way. An inquiry question can explore qualitative experience without a numerical prediction. An independent variable is deliberately varied in an experiment; a dependent variable is measured as the outcome. Operationalisation states exactly how each variable is manipulated or measured. For example, compare five versus fifteen minutes of revision and measure the number of correctly recalled words from the same twenty-word list. A hypothesis should identify the population or participants, the conditions and the measurable outcome. Non-experimental measured variables are not necessarily manipulated independent variables.", "diagram": "variables"}, {"title": "Controls, extraneous variables and confounding", "kind": "learn", "text": "A control variable is deliberately held constant to reduce alternative explanations. An extraneous variable is any other factor that could affect the outcome, such as prior knowledge, room noise or the researcher’s instructions. A confounding variable systematically differs with the independent variable and can therefore provide an alternative explanation for a group difference. If one revision group works in silence and the other beside loud music, noise is confounded with revision condition. Random individual variation adds noise but is not automatically a confound. Explain the direction of the alternative influence when possible. Standardising the word list, testing delay and instructions addresses specific problems. Saying control everything is not a realistic plan. Good evaluation identifies the uncontrolled factor, possible effect and targeted improvement.", "diagram": "variables"}, {"title": "Translate a construct into a measurement", "kind": "learn", "text": "A construct such as memory cannot be manipulated or measured clearly by saying more memory. A study might vary semantic versus structural encoding instructions and measure the number of target words correctly recognised later. The independent variable has defined conditions; the dependent variable has an observable scoring rule and time. A directional hypothesis predicts which condition will score higher, while a non-directional hypothesis predicts a difference without specifying its direction. An inquiry question can guide qualitative exploration without forcing experiences into a numerical prediction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Extraneous becomes confounding when it tracks the condition", "kind": "learn", "text": "Room noise is extraneous if it can affect memory independently of the encoding instructions. If every semantic participant is tested quietly and every structural participant is tested noisily, noise varies systematically with the independent variable and is a confound. The outcome could be attributed to either difference. Controls should address this exact problem, such as using comparable rooms or balancing conditions. Saying all variables must be controlled is less useful than naming a plausible variable and explaining its influence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Operational precision supports evaluation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Define what counts as a correct response, when testing occurs and how instructions are delivered. This enables another researcher to understand the measurement and identify alternative explanations. Precision alone does not guarantee validity: a word recognition score may represent the studied task better than all memory in everyday life. A hypothesis must match the actual manipulation and outcome, and a conclusion must match the data rather than expanding a narrow effect into a claim that one method improves everything.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Operational hypothesis", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Operational hypothesis", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Confounded rooms", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Operational hypothesis", "prompt": "A fictional experiment compares five and fifteen minutes of revision using the same twenty-word list, then counts free-recalled words after one minute. Write a directional hypothesis and identify IV, DV and two controls. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Predicts that the specified participants revising fifteen minutes will free-recall more words than those revising five minutes after the fixed delay.", "[1] IV is allocated revision duration, five versus fifteen minutes.", "[1] DV is the number of correctly free-recalled words from twenty after one minute.", "[2] Controls include the word list, delay, instructions, equipment or environment, with specific examples."], "allocations": [2, 1, 1, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-inquiry-q1"}, {"title": "Confounded rooms", "prompt": "One learning group works in a quiet room and another under loud construction noise. Explain the confound and propose an improvement. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Noise systematically differs with learning condition.", "[1] Noise may affect attention and performance, offering an alternative to the intended IV.", "[1] Test groups under equivalent controlled sound conditions.", "[1] Standardise timing/instructions and avoid changing another condition with the intended IV."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-inquiry-q4"}, {"id": "psy11-inquiry-variables-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A study compares semantic and structural word tasks and counts correct recognition after ten minutes. Write a directional hypothesis and identify operational independent and dependent variables. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Predict higher correct recognition after ten minutes for semantic than structural encoding, clearly naming both conditions.", "[2] The IV is the encoding instruction/task, operationalised as semantic versus structural processing.", "[2] The DV is the number or proportion of target words correctly recognised at the specified ten-minute test."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-inquiry-variables-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "The semantic group is tested in silence and the structural group beside a noisy corridor. Explain why noise is a confound and propose a targeted improvement. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Noise can influence attention/encoding independently of processing condition.", "[2] It differs systematically between conditions, so it offers an alternative explanation of recognition differences.", "[2] Use equivalent quiet conditions or balance noise across groups, retaining a clear scoring and timing procedure."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "When is room noise a confound?", "o": ["When it systematically differs with the tested condition and can affect the outcome.", "Whenever it is mentioned in a study, regardless of variation.", "Only when it is the measured dependent variable.", "When random sampling guarantees every room is identical."], "a": 0, "why": "A confound provides an alternative explanation because it covaries with the condition."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-inquiry-designs", "title": "Experiments, observations and developmental designs", "scope": "Choose the design from the question · Control and realism can conflict · Improvement must address the named limitation", "parent": "psy11-inquiry", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Experiments, observations and developmental designs</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Choose the design from the question</li><li>Control and realism can conflict</li><li>Improvement must address the named limitation</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Experimental and non-experimental designs", "kind": "learn", "text": "An experiment manipulates a variable and compares outcomes under controlled conditions, often with experimental and control groups. Well-designed experiments support causal inference by reducing alternative explanations. Observational research records behaviour without manipulating the relevant conditions. A case study investigates a person or small case in depth using multiple sources. Correlational research measures variables and examines their relationship without establishing causation. Each can answer useful questions. It is often unethical or impractical to manipulate harmful experiences or rare injuries, so non-experimental evidence is important. A study can also combine approaches. Identify what the researchers actually did before naming the design. The presence of numbers does not make a study experimental, and a detailed case study is not scientifically useless merely because generalisation is limited.", "diagram": "designs"}, {"title": "Longitudinal and cross-sectional comparisons", "kind": "learn", "text": "A longitudinal study follows the same participants across time, providing evidence about within-person change. It can be slow, costly and affected by attrition, practice effects or changes in circumstances. A cross-sectional study compares different groups at one time, often different ages, providing a faster snapshot. Age differences may instead reflect cohort differences such as schooling or historical experience. Following people over time can show sequence and association, but does not automatically establish causation without control of alternative influences. A study of attachment and later outcomes can be longitudinal without randomly assigning attachment. Choose the method based on the question, then evaluate its specific advantage and limitation. Time structure and experimental status are separate features of a design.", "diagram": "developmentdesign"}, {"title": "Choose the design from the question", "kind": "learn", "text": "An experiment manipulates an independent variable and measures an outcome with a suitable comparison. An observation records behaviour without necessarily manipulating it; a case study investigates one person or situation in depth; a correlation measures how variables vary together. Longitudinal and cross-sectional describe time and participant structure and can occur alongside other design features. Do not assume every study collecting numbers is experimental or every study following people is causal. The actual manipulation and controls determine the strength of a causal inference.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Control and realism can conflict", "kind": "learn", "text": "A controlled word-learning experiment standardises exposure and testing, helping isolate a variable but potentially simplifying everyday study. Naturalistic observation captures behaviour in context but provides less control and can be affected by observer interpretation or reactivity. A case study may reveal an unusual pattern inaccessible through ordinary sampling, while limiting generalisation and causal isolation. Evaluate according to the research purpose: more control is useful for one question, richer context for another. A strength is not universal superiority.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Improvement must address the named limitation", "kind": "learn", "text": "If observers disagree, clearer categories and independent coding training address reliability. If an artificial task does not represent everyday learning, a relevant follow-up in a more natural setting addresses external validity. If attrition changes a longitudinal sample, tracking who leaves and their relevant characteristics helps assess bias. No improvement solves everything: natural settings reduce control and longer follow-up increases cost. State the limitation, its consequence and the proposed change, then qualify the resulting inference.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"id": "psy11-inquiry-designs-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A researcher records naturally occurring classroom helping without assigning conditions. Identify the design and explain one strength and one limitation for understanding everyday behaviour. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] It is observational/non-experimental; no helping condition is manipulated.", "[2] The everyday setting can provide realistic contextual evidence about observed helping.", "[2] Confounds, observer interpretation or reactivity restrict causal inference and require clear categories/ethical recording."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-inquiry-designs-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare longitudinal and cross-sectional approaches to language development, including one distinct limitation of each. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Longitudinal follows the same people across time and can reveal within-person change.", "[2] Cross-sectional compares age groups at one time and is faster but may confound age with cohort experience.", "[2] Longitudinal attrition or repeated-task practice can bias change estimates; neither time structure alone proves a single developmental cause."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What makes a study experimental?", "o": ["Manipulating an independent variable with an appropriate comparison and outcome measurement.", "Collecting any numerical data.", "Interviewing someone in depth.", "Measuring two variables without assigning conditions."], "a": 0, "why": "Experimental status concerns manipulation and comparison, not whether data are numerical."}], "subtitle": "11 screens · 2 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-inquiry-samples", "title": "Sampling, allocation and control of expectations", "scope": "Sampling and allocation act at different stages · Representativeness needs a relevant frame · Control expectations without claiming perfection", "parent": "psy11-inquiry", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Sampling, allocation and control of expectations</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Sampling and allocation act at different stages</li><li>Representativeness needs a relevant frame</li><li>Control expectations without claiming perfection</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Sampling and populations", "kind": "learn", "text": "The population is the group the researcher wants to understand; the sample is the group actually studied. Convenience sampling recruits readily available people, making access easy but potentially biased. Snowball sampling asks existing participants to refer others, helping reach some networks but overrepresenting connected people. Random sampling gives each member of a defined sampling frame a known equal chance in a simple random sample, reducing selection bias when the frame is suitable. Stratified sampling divides the population into relevant subgroups and samples from each, often proportionately, to improve subgroup representation. None guarantees representativeness if the frame is incomplete or response rates differ. Describe the selection steps. A sample of friends is convenience sampling even if a coin is later used to allocate conditions.", "diagram": "sampling"}, {"title": "Random allocation and expectation controls", "kind": "learn", "text": "Random allocation assigns recruited participants to conditions by chance. It differs from random sampling, which selects people from the population. Allocation helps distribute participant characteristics across conditions on average, but small samples can still differ by chance. Standardised instructions and procedures reduce researcher and environmental variation. A placebo is a comparison intervention designed to resemble treatment without its active component, where appropriate. A placebo effect arises from expectations rather than the specific treatment. Demand characteristics are cues about a study’s purpose that influence participants’ responses; experimenter effects arise when researcher expectations affect conduct or assessment. Single-blind and double-blind procedures conceal condition information from specified participants or relevant researchers. Explain who is unaware and which bias this reduces rather than using blind as a magic guarantee.", "diagram": "allocation"}, {"title": "Sampling and allocation act at different stages", "kind": "learn", "text": "Sampling selects participants from a population; allocation assigns selected participants to study conditions. Random sampling gives population members a known random selection opportunity within a suitable sampling frame. Random allocation uses a chance process to assign the sample to groups, reducing systematic group differences on average. One does not guarantee the other. A convenience sample randomly allocated to two tasks may support a controlled comparison while still representing the wider population poorly. Explain separately what each step addresses.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Representativeness needs a relevant frame", "kind": "learn", "text": "Convenience sampling is accessible but can overrepresent available volunteers. Snowball sampling uses participant referrals and can reach difficult-to-access networks, yet may reproduce network similarities. Stratified sampling uses relevant population subgroups and samples within them, often in proportions reflecting the population. A larger biased sample is not necessarily more representative than a smaller well-selected one. Identify the intended population and the characteristics relevant to the research question before judging whether the sample supports generalisation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Control expectations without claiming perfection", "kind": "learn", "text": "A participant’s beliefs about a condition can influence responses through expectations or demand characteristics; researcher expectations can affect instructions or scoring. Standardised instructions and blinding where feasible reduce these influences. A double-blind procedure keeps both participants and relevant researchers unaware of condition assignment; it is not possible for every intervention. Random allocation reduces baseline differences probabilistically and does not guarantee identical groups. Explain the specific protection and its limits rather than treating the words random or blind as a proof of validity.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Sampling versus allocation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Sampling versus allocation", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Sampling versus allocation", "prompt": "Volunteers from one club are assigned to conditions using a random-number generator. Identify selection and allocation, and explain two different consequences. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Volunteer/convenience recruitment from an accessible club restricts the sample.", "[1] Random assignment to conditions is random allocation, not random population sampling.", "[1] Allocation helps distribute participant differences between conditions on average.", "[1] Narrow recruitment still limits generalisation outside the club."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-inquiry-q3"}, {"id": "psy11-inquiry-samples-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A researcher recruits volunteers from one school and uses a random number generator to assign them to two memory tasks. Distinguish the sampling and allocation and evaluate generalisation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Recruitment is a volunteer/convenience approach from an accessible school, not random selection of the whole population.", "[2] Chance assignment is random allocation, helping reduce systematic differences between the two selected groups.", "[2] The school/volunteer sample may differ from the wider population, so random allocation alone does not establish representativeness."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-inquiry-samples-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare convenience and stratified sampling for a study intended to represent Year 11 students across different school settings. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Convenience uses accessible students, making recruitment efficient but potentially overrepresenting one setting.", "[2] Stratified sampling identifies relevant school-setting subgroups and samples within them, with suitable population proportions.", "[2] A sound frame and random selection within strata can improve representation, but non-response and practical limits remain."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What does random allocation primarily support?", "o": ["Reducing systematic baseline differences between study conditions on average.", "Making every convenience sample representative of the country.", "Guaranteeing identical scores before treatment.", "Replacing the need for a dependent variable."], "a": 0, "why": "Allocation addresses group assignment; population selection is a separate issue."}], "subtitle": "13 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Biological and lifespan psychology", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-inquiry-data", "title": "Measurement, data displays and justified conclusions", "scope": "A measurement must fit the construct · Select the display and denominator · A supported conclusion stays within the evidence", "parent": "psy11-inquiry", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Measurement, data displays and justified conclusions</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>A measurement must fit the construct</li><li>Select the display and denominator</li><li>A supported conclusion stays within the evidence</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Qualitative, quantitative and mixed measures", "kind": "learn", "text": "Quantitative data are numerical counts or measurements; qualitative data capture meanings and descriptions. An open-ended survey or interview can provide detailed explanations, while a rating scale such as a Likert response can provide numerical self-report. Structured interviews use set questions; semi-structured interviews retain guiding questions while allowing follow-up. Individual interviews can give privacy and depth; focus groups reveal interaction but can discourage dissent or sensitive disclosure. Physiological measurements such as heart rate, breathing rate and galvanic skin response are more objective indicators of bodily changes, though they do not uniquely identify a feeling. Mixed methods combine data types to examine different aspects. Match the measure to the construct and evaluate self-report bias, interpretation and measurement consistency.", "diagram": "measures"}, {"title": "Tables, graphs, mean, median and correlation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A frequency table shows how often each value or category occurs; a summary table presents quantities such as sample size and group means. Use a column chart for separate categories, a line graph for a meaningful continuous or time sequence, a histogram for frequencies in numerical intervals and a scatterplot for paired measurements of two variables. Label axes, units and a meaningful scale. Mean equals the sum divided by the number of scores; median is the middle ordered score or average of the two middle scores. Outliers can distort the mean. Pearson’s r describes direction and strength of a linear association between −1 and +1. Its sign is direction, not strength. Correlation cannot by itself establish cause or exclude a third variable.", "diagram": "data"}, {"title": "Validity, reliability and justified conclusions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Internal validity concerns whether the design supports the claimed relationship, especially whether alternative explanations for an experimental effect have been controlled. External validity concerns generalisation to other people, settings or times. Test-retest reliability is consistency of measurement across occasions when the underlying construct is stable. Inter-rater reliability is agreement between observers assessing the same behaviour. Reliability does not guarantee validity: a biased scale can repeatedly give the same wrong measurement. Draw conclusions from the actual data and question, using supports rather than proves when uncertainty remains. Identify a limitation and explain its impact, then propose a specific improvement. Evaluate scientific sources by design, sample, methods, evidence and conflicts rather than judging credibility solely by confident wording or website appearance. Reference sources so claims can be checked.", "diagram": "validity"}, {"title": "Subjective and objective do not mean weak and strong", "kind": "learn", "text": "A subjective judgement can be the appropriate evidence when the research question concerns a person’s evaluation of life. An objective heart-rate reading may be reliable yet fail to represent that judgement. Conversely, self-report of a reaction time may be less suitable than a calibrated timer. Choose measures according to the construct, then evaluate reliability and validity separately. Interviews can reveal meaning and rating scales enable numerical comparison, while physiological recordings describe selected bodily changes. Mixed methods are valuable when they answer complementary questions, not because adding every possible measure automatically makes a study correct. State the inference each measure can actually support.", "diagram": "measures"}, {"title": "A measurement must fit the construct", "kind": "learn", "text": "Quantitative data are numerical; qualitative data describe meaning and experience. A Likert rating can be quantitative yet subjective, while recorded heart rate is quantitative and more directly physiological. An interview may provide qualitative evidence with structured or semi-structured questions. Subjective does not mean useless: reported satisfaction is relevant when the question concerns a person’s judgement. Objective does not mean a unique measure of anxiety: heart rate has several influences. Mixed methods can combine complementary evidence without automatically resolving either method’s limitations.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Select the display and denominator", "kind": "learn", "text": "A column graph compares categories; a line graph can show ordered change over time; a histogram displays frequencies across numerical intervals; a scatterplot shows paired values for two variables. Label axes, units, conditions and frequencies clearly. Percentages need a stated denominator: people, responses and groups are different units. Mean is the total divided by the number of scores; median is the middle of ordered scores. An outlier can shift the mean strongly while leaving the median relatively stable.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A supported conclusion stays within the evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "A difference in sample means supports a description of those measured groups, not automatic statistical significance or universal causation. Correlation describes association and requires consideration of direction and third variables. Reliability concerns consistency; validity concerns the inference or measurement purpose. An ethical, reliable task can still have limited external validity. In an exam response quote the relevant value or pattern, connect it to the hypothesis and state the most important qualification, instead of listing every possible weakness without application.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Design and inference reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Design and inference reference</h3><p>Time structure and experimental status are separate features.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Strength</th><th>Limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Experiment</td><td>Tests manipulation with comparison</td><td>Confounds/ethics/artificial conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Observation</td><td>Records behaviour in context</td><td>No manipulation; interpretation/reactivity</td></tr><tr><td>Case study</td><td>Detailed unusual evidence</td><td>Alternative causes/generalisation</td></tr><tr><td>Correlation</td><td>Measures relationship</td><td>Direction/third variables</td></tr><tr><td>Longitudinal</td><td>Within-person change</td><td>Attrition/time/practice</td></tr><tr><td>Cross-sectional</td><td>Faster group comparison</td><td>Cohort differences</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For every evaluation: identify the feature, explain its consequence, propose a feasible targeted improvement and recognise any new trade-off.</p>"}, {"title": "Interactive data: outlier and central tendency", "kind": "interactive", "model": "statistics"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Mean and median", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Mean and median", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Reliability comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Qualitative and quantitative", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: ethical inquiry", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Mean and median", "prompt": "Fictional scores are 2, 4, 4, 5 and 15 words. Calculate mean and median and explain the difference. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Sum is 30 and n is 5.", "[1] Mean is 30/5 = 6 words.", "[1] Ordered middle score is 4, so median = 4 words.", "[1] The high outlier 15 pulls the mean upward more than the median."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-inquiry-q2"}, {"title": "Reliability comparison", "prompt": "Two observers disagree about aggression codes. A scale also gives unstable scores a day later when the construct is assumed stable. Identify both reliability problems and a targeted improvement for each. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Observer disagreement concerns inter-rater reliability.", "[2] Define categories and train/calibrate observers using the same material.", "[1] Instability on repeated measurement concerns test-retest reliability.", "[2] Standardise administration and evaluate item stability with a suitable repeated measurement, considering real change."], "allocations": [1, 2, 1, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-inquiry-q5"}, {"title": "Qualitative and quantitative", "prompt": "Design two complementary measures for understanding a fictional support programme and evaluate one limitation of each. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[1] A private semi-structured interview provides qualitative accounts of experience.", "[2] It permits depth/follow-up but interpretation and interviewer influence need care.", "[1] A specified rating scale provides quantitative self-report of the relevant construct.", "[2] It supports comparison but may reflect social desirability or wording rather than the full experience."], "allocations": [1, 2, 1, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-inquiry-q6"}, {"title": "Extended response: ethical inquiry", "prompt": "Design a low-risk revision investigation with a hypothesis, operational variables, sampling and allocation, ethical safeguards, data display and evaluation. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Gives a testable prediction with specified conditions and numerical outcome.", "[2] Explains recruitment and random allocation separately.", "[2] Standardises relevant procedures and addresses a specific extraneous influence.", "[2] Explains consent/withdrawal, privacy and protection without coercive participation.", "[2] Chooses an appropriate table/graph and mean/median interpretation.", "[2] Gives a sample/design limitation, its consequence and a targeted improvement."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-inquiry-q7"}, {"id": "psy11-inquiry-data-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Fictional questionnaire scores are 2, 4, 4, 5 and 20. Calculate the mean and median, and explain why they differ. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Sum 35 divided by five gives mean 7.", "[2] The ordered middle score is 4, so median 4.", "[2] The high score 20 raises the mean substantially while the central ordered position remains 4; neither measure alone describes the full distribution."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-inquiry-data-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Students reporting more practice also obtain higher recall scores. Explain a supported conclusion, one alternative explanation and a suitable display. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] There is a positive association in the sample; the measured variables vary together.", "[2] Prior knowledge or motivation could influence both practice and recall, and causal direction is not established.", "[2] A labelled scatterplot of paired practice and recall values displays the relationship; Pearson r concerns its linear strength/direction if calculated."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is the median of 2, 4, 4, 5 and 20?", "o": ["4.", "7.", "20.", "35."], "a": 0, "why": "The median is the middle ordered score; the mean is 35/5 = 7."}, {"q": "A coin assigns recruited people to conditions. This is what?", "o": ["Random allocation", "Random population sampling", "Snowball recruitment", "A longitudinal design"], "a": 0, "why": "Allocation concerns conditions after selection."}, {"q": "Another factor systematically differs with the IV. It may be what?", "o": ["Confounding variable", "A proof of causation", "Only a control", "Always irrelevant"], "a": 0, "why": "A confound provides an alternative explanation."}, {"q": "Agreement between observers concerns what?", "o": ["Inter-rater reliability", "Only external validity", "Test-retest only", "Affect balance"], "a": 0, "why": "It concerns consistency of coding the same material."}, {"q": "Mean of 2, 4, 4, 5, 15?", "o": ["6", "4", "15", "30"], "a": 0, "why": "The sum is 30 and there are five scores."}, {"q": "r = −0.8 describes what?", "o": ["Strong negative linear association", "Weak because negative", "Proof of causation", "Eighty percent agreement"], "a": 0, "why": "Magnitude reflects strength and sign reflects direction."}], "subtitle": "20 screens · 6 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Attitudes, stereotypes and social influence", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-attitudes-components", "title": "Attitudes: components, functions and behaviour", "scope": "Components can point in different directions · Functions explain why an attitude is useful to a person · Prediction depends on specificity and context", "parent": "psy11-attitudes", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Attitudes: components, functions and behaviour</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Components can point in different directions</li><li>Functions explain why an attitude is useful to a person</li><li>Prediction depends on specificity and context</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Attitudes and their functions", "kind": "learn", "text": "An attitude is an evaluation of a person, object, issue or group that can influence thoughts, feelings and behaviour. Attitudes help organise experience, guide choices, express values and support social identity, but may also simplify information in biased ways. An explicit attitude is consciously endorsed and can be reported, while an implicit attitude is an automatic association that may influence responses without conscious endorsement. A person’s stated view and automatic response can differ. Neither a questionnaire nor an indirect task perfectly captures every attitude. In an application, identify what is being evaluated and the evidence for that evaluation. Avoid treating an attitude as a single observable action, since behaviour can also reflect rules, opportunity and situational pressure.", "diagram": "attitudes"}, {"title": "The tripartite attitude model", "kind": "learn", "text": "The tripartite model distinguishes affective, behavioural and cognitive components. Affective refers to feelings, such as enjoyment or anxiety about public speaking. Behavioural refers to actions or tendencies to act, such as volunteering for a speech or avoiding it. Cognitive refers to beliefs, such as believing that practice improves confidence. An exam answer should give a different scenario detail for each component. Saying I think public speaking is frightening may contain a belief about danger, whereas feeling fear is affective; attend to the wording. The three components can be consistent or inconsistent. A student may believe recycling matters but fail to recycle because no bin is available. The model organises aspects of evaluation without claiming that they always predict identical behaviour.", "diagram": "attitudes"}, {"title": "Attitude-behaviour consistency", "kind": "learn", "text": "Attitudes can influence behaviour, but behaviour also depends on situational demands, habits, norms, perceived control and competing goals. A positive attitude to exercise does not guarantee exercising every day when injury or time constraints intervene. Conversely, a student may participate in a task because it is required while privately disliking it. This distinction matters when interpreting an experiment: measured behaviour is not always a direct reading of private attitude. An explanation should name the attitude, observed behaviour and relevant contextual influence. Avoid concluding that a person is dishonest whenever behaviour and attitude differ. A carefully designed study may measure both and examine their relationship. Psychological explanations account for patterns and evidence rather than assuming a single cause for every action.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Implicit attitudes and responsible inference", "kind": "learn", "text": "An indirect task can investigate automatic associations, but interpreting one person’s score requires caution about reliability, task demands and context. Explicit reports can differ because conscious values, social desirability or awareness vary. A difference between reported and automatic associations does not justify declaring someone permanently prejudiced from one classroom test. Psychological evidence is strongest when operational definitions and measurement limits are clear. In an exam scenario, explain that implicit refers to an automatic association and explicit to consciously endorsed evaluation. Distinguish that conceptual contrast from the claim that any particular instrument perfectly measures both. Use fictional evidence and avoid personal screening of classmates.", "diagram": "attitudes"}, {"title": "Components can point in different directions", "kind": "learn", "text": "An attitude is an evaluation of a person, object or issue. The tripartite model distinguishes affective feelings, behavioural tendencies/actions and cognitive beliefs. A student may believe recycling is useful, feel annoyed about sorting waste and nevertheless use the correct bin. These are different components of an evaluation, not three separate attitudes that must always agree. Identify the actual evidence for each component, and avoid converting a belief into an observed behaviour when the scenario only states what the person thinks.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Functions explain why an attitude is useful to a person", "kind": "learn", "text": "Attitudes can help organise knowledge, express values, protect self-image and support useful outcomes. The same expressed attitude can serve more than one function in different contexts. Supporting a team may express belonging and personal values, while a practical preference may help obtain a desired result. A function explains the role the attitude serves; a component describes its form. Do not use affective as a function or assume that all attitudes arise from fully rational evidence. Their social and emotional purposes can matter.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Prediction depends on specificity and context", "kind": "learn", "text": "A general positive attitude towards exercise may predict one particular gym visit poorly when time, access and social pressure intervene. A specific intention measured close to a matching behaviour can provide a more relevant prediction. Public statements can also reflect social desirability. Therefore inconsistent action does not automatically prove the person never held the attitude. A good application identifies the attitude, the opportunity or competing demand and the resulting behaviour, then evaluates whether the measurement actually matches what was predicted.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Three components", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Three components", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Three components", "prompt": "A student enjoys volunteering, believes it improves the community and regularly signs up. Apply the tripartite model. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Affective component is enjoyment, an emotional evaluation.", "[2] Cognitive component is the belief about community benefit.", "[2] Behavioural component is signing up, the action or tendency to act."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-attitudes-q1"}, {"id": "psy11-attitudes-components-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student says recycling reduces waste, feels frustrated by sorting rules and usually uses the recycling bin. Identify the three attitude components with evidence. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Cognitive: the belief that recycling reduces waste.", "[2] Affective: frustration about the sorting rules.", "[2] Behavioural: using the bin; the components need not all have identical emotional direction."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-attitudes-components-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A survey finds favourable attitudes to exercise but many respondents miss a later gym session. Explain two reasons for the mismatch and one measurement improvement. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Time, access, illness or competing obligations can constrain behaviour despite a favourable attitude.", "[2] A general exercise attitude differs in specificity from attending this particular session; public reports may reflect desirability.", "[2] Measure a specific intention close to the session and record relevant opportunity/context, without assuming an intention guarantees action."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which is a cognitive attitude component?", "o": ["A belief that recycling reduces waste.", "Feeling annoyed while sorting.", "Placing an item in the bin.", "A recorded heart-rate increase without an expressed belief."], "a": 0, "why": "Cognitive refers to beliefs; affective concerns feelings and behavioural concerns tendencies or actions."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Attitudes, stereotypes and social influence", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-attitudes-dissonance", "title": "Cognitive dissonance and Festinger–Carlsmith (1959)", "scope": "Dissonance concerns an experienced inconsistency · Small external justification can leave more pressure to change · Evaluate the mechanism and measurement", "parent": "psy11-attitudes", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Cognitive dissonance and Festinger–Carlsmith (1959)</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Dissonance concerns an experienced inconsistency</li><li>Small external justification can leave more pressure to change</li><li>Evaluate the mechanism and measurement</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Festinger’s cognitive dissonance", "kind": "learn", "text": "Cognitive dissonance is psychological discomfort associated with perceived inconsistency between relevant cognitions or between beliefs and behaviour. Festinger’s theory proposes that this discomfort can motivate efforts to reduce the inconsistency. A student who believes honesty is important but cheats in a game may experience conflict between that belief and action. The magnitude depends partly on how important the cognitions are and how much inconsistency the person perceives. The same event may therefore produce different discomfort for different people. Dissonance is not simply any sadness or uncertainty. A good scenario explanation identifies the two conflicting elements and explains why the conflict motivates change. The theory is useful for explaining some attitude change, but cognitive discomfort is inferred from evidence rather than directly visible.", "diagram": "dissonance"}, {"title": "Avoidance, rationalisation and change", "kind": "learn", "text": "A person may reduce dissonance by changing behaviour, changing a belief or changing the interpretation of the action. The student might stop cheating, decide that the game rule is unfair, or rationalise that everyone else cheats. Avoidance can involve steering away from information that highlights the inconsistency. These are explanations of possible responses, not recommendations to justify harmful behaviour. Match the response to what actually changes. Saying I will behave honestly next time changes behaviour; saying this was only a joke changes perception of the action. Magnitude can increase motivation to resolve the conflict, but does not determine one inevitable response. Apply the theory using specific conflicting cognitions rather than simply labelling the person dissonant.", "diagram": "dissonance"}, {"title": "Festinger and Carlsmith (1959): aim and method", "kind": "learn", "text": "The 1959 forced-compliance study tested whether different amounts of external justification for expressing a view contrary to experience would affect later private evaluation. Male students completed repetitive, dull tasks. Some were paid one dollar and others twenty dollars to tell a supposed waiting participant that the tasks were interesting. A control condition did not perform the same persuasive role. Researchers later obtained ratings of the task from an interviewer. The key manipulated comparison was payment for saying something inconsistent with the dull experience; the outcome was later evaluation. The study therefore tests more than whether people like money. Describe the task, induced statement, payment conditions and attitude measure, keeping this study distinct from other dissonance experiments.", "diagram": "forcedcompliance"}, {"title": "Festinger and Carlsmith (1959): findings and reasoning", "kind": "learn", "text": "The one-dollar condition rated the task as more enjoyable than the twenty-dollar condition and control. In dissonance terms, a small payment offered less external justification for praising a dull task, creating pressure to alter the private evaluation. A larger payment supplied a clearer external reason for making the statement, reducing the need for attitude change. The result should not be summarised as low pay always makes people enjoy work. It concerns a particular forced-compliance context with an inconsistency between experience and statement. Explain the pattern, the external justification and the proposed internal change. Ratings are self-reports, so alternative interpretations and participants’ awareness must be considered. Findings can support a theory without demonstrating that it explains all attitude change.", "diagram": "forcedcompliance"}, {"title": "Forced-compliance study: contribution and limits", "kind": "learn", "text": "The study provided an experimental test of a prediction from cognitive-dissonance theory and showed how behaviour can influence reported attitudes. Standardised dull tasks and specified payment conditions allowed a controlled comparison. However, the narrow student sample and artificial task restrict generalisation. Deception and the induced false statement raise questions about informed consent and potential discomfort. Ratings may also reflect demand characteristics or alternative processes, including how people infer their own attitude. Evaluation should connect each limitation to the interpretation. For example, participants who guess that the researcher expects a changed rating might alter their report, making it uncertain how much private belief changed. Discuss the strength of the experimental evidence and its limitations rather than calling self-report automatically worthless.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Dissonance concerns an experienced inconsistency", "kind": "learn", "text": "Festinger proposed that inconsistent cognitions or a mismatch between belief and action can create psychological discomfort. A person who values honesty but lies may experience dissonance and be motivated to reduce it. Reduction can involve changing behaviour, changing an attitude, adding a justification or avoiding conflicting information. The original action may remain unchanged while the explanation shifts. In a vignette identify the inconsistency before naming the reduction strategy; unpleasant feelings alone do not establish cognitive dissonance.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Small external justification can leave more pressure to change", "kind": "learn", "text": "In Festinger and Carlsmith (1959), participants completed dull tasks and some were paid either $1 or $20 to tell another person they were enjoyable. Compared with larger external justification, the small payment left less sufficient external explanation for the inconsistent behaviour. Subsequent more favourable evaluations in the small-payment condition supported an attitude-change interpretation. The result is not that smaller rewards always make every activity enjoyable. It is a specific forced-compliance comparison involving justification and reported evaluation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluate the mechanism and measurement", "kind": "learn", "text": "A controlled comparison supports testing differences in justification, but self-reported enjoyment can reflect expectations and other interpretations. Deception and pressure affect informed choice, and the student sample and artificial task restrict generalisation. Strong answers distinguish the hypothesis from the finding and explain why the finding fits the mechanism. Do not replace the study with a vague statement that money changes minds. Apply the exact payment conditions, behaviour and later evaluation, and avoid claiming that one study excludes every alternative explanation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Low versus high external justification", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Low versus high external justification", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Reducing dissonance", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Forced compliance and self-report", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: attitude and action", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Low versus high external justification", "prompt": "Explain why the one-dollar condition in Festinger and Carlsmith (1959) was expected to show greater attitude change than the twenty-dollar condition. Include the result. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Praising a dull task conflicts with the person’s experience, creating potential dissonance.", "[2] One dollar gives relatively weak external justification; changing private evaluation can reduce the inconsistency, while twenty dollars offers a stronger external reason.", "[2] The one-dollar condition rated the task as more enjoyable, supporting the predicted pattern in that context."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-attitudes-q2"}, {"title": "Reducing dissonance", "prompt": "A fictional student values fair play but cheats. Give three distinct dissonance-reduction responses and explain what changes. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Stops cheating: changes behaviour to fit the fairness belief.", "[2] Rejects or weakens the belief that fairness matters: changes belief.", "[2] Describes the action as only practice or an exception: changes perception/rationalises the action; these are possible mechanisms, not endorsements."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-attitudes-q4"}, {"title": "Forced compliance and self-report", "prompt": "Evaluate one methodological strength and two limitations of Festinger and Carlsmith’s study. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Standardised task and payment comparison allowed a controlled test of the prediction.", "[2] Narrow sample/artificial tasks restrict generalisation.", "[2] Deception or demand characteristics/self-report affect consent or confidence that reported ratings reflect private attitude change."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-attitudes-q6"}, {"title": "Extended response: attitude and action", "prompt": "Explain tripartite attitudes, dissonance and attribution in a fictional case. Use the 1959 study accurately and evaluate the evidence. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Separates affective, cognitive and behavioural evidence.", "[2] Identifies conflicting cognitions and explains reduction through a specified change.", "[2] Accurately describes dull tasks, induced praise and payment conditions.", "[2] States the rating pattern and links external justification with the theory.", "[2] Applies a distinct attribution process with relevant internal/external causes.", "[2] Evaluates a specific evidence limitation and avoids treating visible behaviour as proof of private belief."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-attitudes-q7"}, {"id": "psy11-attitudes-dissonance-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A person values honest advertising but promotes a product they distrust, then says “it probably helps most people.” Explain dissonance and the apparent reduction strategy. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Belief in honesty conflicts with promoting a distrusted product, creating possible dissonance.", "[2] The revised positive interpretation adds a justification/changes a cognition, reducing the inconsistency.", "[2] Apply the mechanism without assuming the claim is objectively true or that discomfort alone proves every detail of the theory."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-attitudes-dissonance-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain why the $1 condition in Festinger and Carlsmith (1959) could produce more favourable task evaluations than the $20 condition. Include a limitation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Both conditions involved saying dull tasks were enjoyable, conflicting with the initial experience.", "[2] $1 provided less external justification than $20, leaving greater pressure for attitude change to reduce dissonance.", "[2] Self-report, deception, demand characteristics or an artificial sample/task limits the strength/generalisation of the interpretation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is the key insufficient-justification explanation?", "o": ["Small external justification can leave greater pressure to change a conflicting attitude.", "A large payment can never influence behaviour.", "Every unpleasant feeling is automatically dissonance.", "The 1959 study manipulated attachment security."], "a": 0, "why": "The explanation concerns justification for an inconsistent act in the designated forced-compliance study."}], "subtitle": "19 screens · 6 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Attitudes, stereotypes and social influence", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-attitudes-attribution", "title": "Attribution: situations, dispositions and biases", "scope": "Distinguish cause from description · Biases concern a systematic pattern · Attribution can influence social responses", "parent": "psy11-attitudes", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Attribution: situations, dispositions and biases</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Distinguish cause from description</li><li>Biases concern a systematic pattern</li><li>Attribution can influence social responses</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Situational and dispositional attribution", "kind": "learn", "text": "Attribution is an explanation of the cause of behaviour. A dispositional attribution refers to relatively internal characteristics, such as a person’s traits, intentions or effort. A situational attribution refers to external circumstances, such as task difficulty, social pressure or a transport delay. If a student arrives late, calling the student careless is dispositional; explaining a cancelled bus is situational. Both can be plausible, and evidence is needed before choosing. Attribution is about the explanation made, not whether that explanation is correct. A scenario may show people assigning different causes to the same behaviour. State the proposed cause and why it belongs to the category. Avoid automatically treating negative explanations as dispositional and positive explanations as situational.", "diagram": "attribution"}, {"title": "Fundamental attribution error", "kind": "learn", "text": "Fundamental attribution error is a tendency to overemphasise dispositional explanations and underestimate situational influences when explaining others’ behaviour. A spectator who concludes a player is lazy without considering injury or unclear instructions may illustrate the error. The definition requires the imbalance between the two sources, not merely making any internal explanation. Cultural context and available information can affect the tendency. An internal attribution is not automatically an error if strong evidence supports it. In an exam application, identify the observer’s dispositional explanation, the overlooked situational factor and the resulting bias. Do not confuse this with self-serving bias, which concerns how outcomes are explained in relation to one’s own success and failure.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Self-serving and group-serving bias", "kind": "learn", "text": "Self-serving bias involves attributing one’s successes more to internal factors and failures more to external factors, protecting self-evaluation. A student may credit a high score to ability but explain a poor score entirely by unfair questions. Group-serving bias similarly favours the ingroup: positive ingroup actions are explained internally and negative actions externally, while explanations for outgroups can reverse. These are tendencies, not inevitable rules. A team supporter may explain their own team’s foul as accidental pressure and an opposing team’s foul as evidence of bad character. Identify the outcome, who is being evaluated and the explanation used. Bias does not mean every external explanation is false; it concerns a systematic asymmetry that should be judged against evidence.", "diagram": "attribution"}, {"title": "Applying and evaluating attitude explanations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Compare the explanations rather than forcing every scenario into one theory. The tripartite model describes feelings, beliefs and actions. Dissonance explains possible discomfort and change when important elements conflict. Attribution explains how people assign causes to behaviour. A person who feels uncomfortable after acting against a belief may show dissonance, while an observer blaming their personality makes an attribution. Both processes can occur in one event. An extended response should separate definitions, apply each to the specific evidence and evaluate what the evidence supports. Give a limitation with a consequence, such as self-report failing to distinguish actual belief change from a socially desirable answer. Using accurate terminology and connected reasoning is more valuable than listing several theory names without application.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Distinguish cause from description", "kind": "learn", "text": "A dispositional attribution explains behaviour through characteristics of the person, such as effort or reliability. A situational attribution explains it through circumstances, such as transport disruption or unclear instructions. Observing lateness describes an outcome; saying the person is lazy attributes a cause. One incident usually leaves several possible explanations. A balanced answer separates the observed behaviour from the inference, then uses scenario evidence to explain why one attribution is more or less justified.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Biases concern a systematic pattern", "kind": "learn", "text": "The fundamental attribution error involves overemphasising dispositions and underemphasising situations when explaining others’ behaviour. Self-serving bias involves attributing one’s successes internally and failures externally; group-serving bias applies a favourable pattern to an ingroup. These are tendencies, not proof that every internal explanation is wrong. A student crediting effort for success and blaming an unfair test for failure fits self-serving bias if evidence supports the paired pattern. Merely mentioning a difficult test once does not establish a general bias.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Attribution can influence social responses", "kind": "learn", "text": "If a team interprets a member’s missed meeting as laziness, it may exclude them; recognising a caregiving emergency could lead to support. The attribution affects feelings and decisions, but does not change the original cause by itself. To reduce error, seek relevant contextual evidence and compare behaviour across situations before making a trait conclusion. Evaluation should not claim that all responsibility belongs to the situation. Both personal and contextual factors can contribute, and the task is to assess the evidence rather than choose a universally correct side.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Attribution and dissonance distinctions", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Attribution and dissonance distinctions</h3><p>Terms explain different processes and may coexist.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Core pattern</th><th>Evidence needed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Situational attribution</td><td>External circumstance explains action</td><td>Which circumstance?</td></tr><tr><td>Dispositional attribution</td><td>Internal feature explains action</td><td>Which feature?</td></tr><tr><td>Fundamental attribution error</td><td>Others: disposition overweighted</td><td>Which situation overlooked?</td></tr><tr><td>Self-serving bias</td><td>Own success internal/failure external</td><td>Contrast outcomes/explanations</td></tr><tr><td>Group-serving bias</td><td>Ingroup favoured asymmetrically</td><td>Ingroup/outgroup pattern</td></tr><tr><td>Dissonance</td><td>Perceived relevant inconsistency</td><td>Conflicting elements and reduction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>An internal explanation is not automatically erroneous. Bias concerns a systematic imbalance that should be checked against available evidence.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the research and authoritative explanations", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Further evidence</h3><p>The lesson explanations provide the required student account. These links allow study details and educational health information to be checked. Read historical methods critically; they are not activities to reproduce.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Festinger/\">Festinger and Carlsmith (1959), original report</a></li></ul>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Attribution bias", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Attribution bias", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Fundamental attribution error", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Attribution bias", "prompt": "A supporter calls their own team’s win skill and its loss bad luck, but calls the rival’s win luck and its loss poor ability. Explain the bias. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] This illustrates group-serving bias.", "[1] Positive ingroup outcomes receive dispositional explanations and negative outcomes situational ones.", "[1] The outgroup receives the opposite pattern.", "[1] The asymmetry protects the ingroup’s evaluation and should be checked against actual evidence."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-attitudes-q3"}, {"title": "Fundamental attribution error", "prompt": "An observer labels a late student irresponsible without considering a cancelled bus. Explain the error and contrast self-serving bias. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The observer overweights a dispositional cause and underweights the situational bus disruption.", "[2] Self-serving bias instead concerns favouring internal explanations for one’s own successes and external explanations for failures."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-attitudes-q5"}, {"id": "psy11-attitudes-attribution-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A teacher calls a late student irresponsible without considering a cancelled bus. Explain the attribution and relevant bias, then suggest an evidence-based response. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Irresponsible is a dispositional attribution of the lateness.", "[2] Ignoring the cancelled bus overemphasises disposition and underweights context, consistent with fundamental attribution error.", "[2] Check transport/context and the pattern across occasions before forming a trait conclusion; personal factors may still matter."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-attitudes-attribution-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A team credits its wins to skill but attributes its losses to biased referees, while treating opponents’ wins as luck. Explain group-serving bias and one limitation to this interpretation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Favourable ingroup outcomes are credited internally to skill while unfavourable outcomes are blamed externally.", "[2] Discounting opponents’ success also protects the ingroup’s positive evaluation.", "[2] Evidence about actual skill and refereeing is still needed; one statement or a genuinely unfair decision does not automatically prove a systematic bias."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which attribution is situational?", "o": ["The student was late because the bus was cancelled.", "The student is naturally unreliable.", "The student lacks all motivation.", "The student has a lazy personality."], "a": 0, "why": "A situational attribution locates the explanation in circumstances rather than a personal disposition."}, {"q": "Feeling enjoyment is which attitude component?", "o": ["Affective", "Behavioural", "Only cognitive", "Allocation"], "a": 0, "why": "Affective concerns emotion."}, {"q": "Dissonance requires what?", "o": ["Perceived inconsistency between relevant cognitions/action", "Any fatigue", "Any reward", "Only a group"], "a": 0, "why": "Important conflict can motivate reducing inconsistency."}, {"q": "Why did small payment favour attitude change in the theory?", "o": ["Less external justification", "It was a larger reward", "No statement was made", "All money was removed"], "a": 0, "why": "Weak external justification increases pressure for internal resolution."}, {"q": "Overlooking a bus delay and blaming personality illustrates what?", "o": ["Fundamental attribution error", "Conservation", "Purely self-serving success", "Rehearsal"], "a": 0, "why": "It overweights disposition and underweights situation when judging another."}, {"q": "Crediting own success to ability and failure to luck illustrates what?", "o": ["Self-serving bias", "Reinforcement schedule", "Internal working model", "Sensory memory"], "a": 0, "why": "Explanations favour self-evaluation asymmetrically."}], "subtitle": "18 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Attitudes, stereotypes and social influence", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-identity-groups", "title": "Social identity: categorisation, identification and comparison", "scope": "The same person has several social identities · Positive distinctiveness can support ingroup favouritism · Evaluate prediction without making it inevitable", "parent": "psy11-identity", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Social identity: categorisation, identification and comparison</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>The same person has several social identities</li><li>Positive distinctiveness can support ingroup favouritism</li><li>Evaluate prediction without making it inevitable</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Social identity and group membership", "kind": "learn", "text": "Social identity theory, associated with Tajfel and Turner (1979), explains how part of self-concept comes from belonging to groups. People can belong to many groups, including teams, families, communities and school groups, with different identities becoming important in different situations. A positive group identity can support belonging and esteem. An ingroup is a group perceived as including the self; an outgroup is perceived as outside that membership. These are relational categories, not permanent moral descriptions. The theory helps explain some favouritism and intergroup comparison, but membership does not inevitably produce prejudice or conflict. An application should identify the relevant group, how the person identifies with it and what comparison is being made.", "diagram": "identity"}, {"title": "Categorisation, identification and comparison", "kind": "learn", "text": "Social categorisation organises people into groups based on perceived shared features. Social identification involves taking on aspects of a group’s identity and norms. Social comparison evaluates the ingroup against relevant outgroups, potentially supporting positive distinctiveness. A student may categorise classmates into sporting teams, adopt their team’s colours and then describe their team as more committed than another. The processes are linked but need not follow a rigid one-time sequence. Categorising people can simplify information while overlooking individual variation. A comparison can encourage cooperation or competition depending on context. In an exam response, use separate details for each process and explain how the comparison relates to self-concept rather than saying only that people join groups.", "diagram": "identity"}, {"title": "Strengths and limitations of social identity theory", "kind": "learn", "text": "The theory offers an explanation for ingroup favouritism even when group boundaries are relatively arbitrary and helps connect group membership to esteem and behaviour. It can guide analysis of team rivalry and discrimination without assuming fixed individual hostility. However, group members do not always discriminate and groups can cooperate. Competition over resources, power differences and individual histories may require additional explanation. Evaluate with a specified consequence: a theory focusing on comparison may not fully explain conflict where material scarcity or institutional inequality is central. Avoid claiming that social identity always increases self-esteem or that all outgroup attitudes are negative. Theory evaluation should describe what the account explains well and where predictions depend on context.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "The same person has several social identities", "kind": "learn", "text": "Social identity concerns the part of self-concept associated with group membership. A person can identify as a student, musician, family member and team supporter, with different identities salient in different settings. Social categorisation organises people into groups; identification involves adopting membership as relevant to the self; comparison evaluates groups relative to one another. These processes can support belonging and positive distinctiveness, but do not imply that every group difference produces hostility.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Positive distinctiveness can support ingroup favouritism", "kind": "learn", "text": "Favourable comparisons can support a positive social identity. A student may emphasise the strengths of their house or club while overlooking comparable strengths elsewhere. This helps explain ingroup favouritism without requiring a long personal history of conflict. Group boundaries and status influence the process, alongside personality, resources and norms. In an application show how membership becomes self-relevant and how comparison affects a judgement, rather than merely stating that people like their own group.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluate prediction without making it inevitable", "kind": "learn", "text": "Social identity theory provides an account of group-based self-concept and intergroup responses. It does not alone explain every form of prejudice, nor does categorisation guarantee discrimination. Cooperative norms, shared identities and meaningful contact can alter behaviour. A study of group labels must consider whether competition, authority or expectations are also manipulated. Explain the theory’s contribution and then the missing or interacting factors. This produces a reasoned judgement rather than a blanket claim that group identity is either always good or always harmful.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Group processes", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Group processes", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Group processes", "prompt": "A student divides classmates into teams, adopts one team’s colours and argues their team is more committed. Apply social categorisation, identification and comparison. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Dividing classmates into perceived groups illustrates categorisation.", "[2] Taking on team symbols and membership illustrates identification.", "[2] Evaluating the ingroup against another team illustrates comparison and potential positive distinctiveness."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-identity-q1"}, {"id": "psy11-identity-groups-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student labels pupils by house, wears their own house colours and claims their house is more cooperative than others. Apply categorisation, identification and comparison. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Sorting pupils into house groups illustrates categorisation.", "[2] Wearing colours and treating membership as part of the self illustrates identification.", "[2] Favourably evaluating their house relative to others illustrates comparison and possible positive distinctiveness."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-identity-groups-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Evaluate the claim that creating two named groups necessarily causes discrimination. Use social identity theory and one contextual factor. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Categorisation/identification can support ingroup favouritism and favourable comparisons.", "[2] The process does not guarantee harmful treatment; norms, shared goals and other identities can alter responses.", "[2] Explain a relevant factor such as cooperative goals or competitive reward structure, and distinguish belief/evaluation from actual discriminatory behaviour."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is social identification?", "o": ["Adopting a group membership as relevant to one’s self-concept.", "Only noticing that two groups have different labels.", "Any numerical comparison without personal membership.", "A guarantee of discrimination."], "a": 0, "why": "Identification makes membership self-relevant; categorisation and comparison are related but distinct processes."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Attitudes, stereotypes and social influence", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-identity-prejudice", "title": "Stereotypes, prejudice and discrimination", "scope": "Separate belief, evaluation and treatment · Indirect disadvantage can arise from a uniform rule · Beliefs about fairness can maintain inequality", "parent": "psy11-identity", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Stereotypes, prejudice and discrimination</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Separate belief, evaluation and treatment</li><li>Indirect disadvantage can arise from a uniform rule</li><li>Beliefs about fairness can maintain inequality</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Stereotypes and their cognitive function", "kind": "learn", "text": "A stereotype is a generalised belief about members of a social category. It can reduce the effort of processing social information but may exaggerate similarity, ignore differences and distort interpretation of individuals. Stereotypes can be apparently favourable as well as unfavourable and still impose restrictive expectations. Assuming every member of a group has the same ability is an example, even if described as praise. A stereotype is a cognition; prejudice is an evaluative attitude; discrimination is behaviour. These categories can relate but are not identical. Use respectful fictional examples rather than repeating degrading claims about real communities. Explain how categorisation leads someone to judge an individual before considering specific evidence about that person.", "diagram": "stereotypes"}, {"title": "Prejudice and direct discrimination", "kind": "learn", "text": "Prejudice is a preconceived evaluative attitude towards someone based on group membership, often involving negative affect and unsupported beliefs. Discrimination is unequal treatment based on group membership. Direct discrimination involves an explicit difference in treatment, such as denying someone an opportunity because of their age or disability. An attitude can exist without being acted on, and discriminatory behaviour can occur through rules even when no individual openly reports prejudice. In a scenario, separate what is believed or felt from what is done. Examples can concern gender, race, ethnicity, age, disability or mental illness, but should avoid portraying any group as inherently deficient. A scientific explanation identifies mechanisms and effects without endorsing the prejudice.", "diagram": "stereotypes"}, {"title": "Indirect discrimination", "kind": "learn", "text": "Indirect discrimination can occur when an apparently neutral rule disproportionately disadvantages a group without a relevant justification. A venue that requires every applicant to use an inaccessible upstairs office may disadvantage people with mobility impairments even though the instruction is worded identically for everyone. This differs from openly refusing disabled applicants, which is direct discrimination. Identical wording does not necessarily produce equal opportunity. An exam answer should identify the rule, the group disadvantage and why it is indirect, using the actual scenario. This is a psychological distinction, not a legal judgement about a real case. Avoid assuming that indirect means secret or intentional; the issue is the effect of a general condition rather than whether someone admits hostile intent.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Social influence, competition and just-world beliefs", "kind": "learn", "text": "Prejudice can develop through learned group norms, social categorisation and competition over resources. People may adopt attitudes expressed by valued peers or authorities. Intergroup competition can make differences more salient and encourage hostility when one group’s gain is perceived as another’s loss. The just-world phenomenon involves a belief that people generally get what they deserve, which can lead to blaming disadvantaged people and ignoring situational barriers. These explanations can overlap, but each has a different mechanism. A response should connect the observed prejudice to a relevant process, such as a group norm being repeated or a limited reward creating rivalry. Avoid suggesting that one explanation accounts for every form of discrimination or that disadvantage proves personal fault.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Institutional rules and individual attitudes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Discrimination can arise through procedures even without an openly prejudiced statement. A selection requirement can disadvantage a group through access barriers, while individual attitudes may be positive or mixed. Conversely, a biased belief does not guarantee discriminatory action in every context. This shows why stereotype, prejudice and discrimination are analytically distinct. Researchers can examine outcomes and rules alongside confidential attitude measures. A reduction in hostile questionnaire responses does not demonstrate that an inaccessible procedure has changed. Evaluate the level at which an intervention acts and measure the corresponding outcome. Psychological categories help explain patterns while actual legal conclusions require a separate appropriate process.", "diagram": "stereotypes"}, {"title": "Separate belief, evaluation and treatment", "kind": "learn", "text": "A stereotype is a generalised belief about a group. Prejudice is an evaluation or attitude towards people based on group membership. Discrimination is unequal treatment, which may be direct or indirect. A person can express a stereotype without the scenario showing a discriminatory action; an institutional rule can disadvantage a group without an openly hostile statement. Use the evidence given for each level. These distinctions allow a precise response rather than labelling every uncomfortable interaction with the same term.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Indirect disadvantage can arise from a uniform rule", "kind": "learn", "text": "Direct discrimination involves explicit differential treatment based on group membership in the relevant context. Indirect discrimination can arise from a seemingly uniform requirement that disadvantages a group, especially where it is not appropriately justified or adjusted. A compulsory meeting time may prevent participation for people with specific care or observance commitments. This is a social-psychological explanation, not a legal determination from a short vignette. Identify the rule, the unequal effect and a relevant alternative before judging the situation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Beliefs about fairness can maintain inequality", "kind": "learn", "text": "A just-world belief assumes that people generally get what they deserve, which can encourage blaming those experiencing disadvantage instead of examining conditions. Competition over scarce resources and social learning can also support prejudice. A stereotype simplifies information but can resist contradictory evidence and obscure within-group variation. Evaluation should consider both individual beliefs and institutional opportunities. Changing an expressed attitude is useful but does not automatically remove barriers in procedures or guarantee equal treatment.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Belief, attitude and treatment", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Belief, attitude and treatment", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Indirect discrimination", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Just-world reasoning", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Belief, attitude and treatment", "prompt": "Distinguish a stereotype, prejudice and discrimination using a fictional selection process. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A generalised belief about a category’s ability is a stereotype.", "[2] A preconceived negative evaluation of category members is prejudice.", "[2] Unequal exclusion based on membership is discriminatory behaviour; categories are related but distinct."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-identity-q2"}, {"title": "Indirect discrimination", "prompt": "An activity requires every applicant to attend an inaccessible upstairs venue. Explain why identical wording can still produce indirect discrimination. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] A general condition appears to apply equally.", "[1] It disadvantages people with mobility limitations.", "[1] The unequal effect is linked to group membership/access rather than explicit individual refusal.", "[1] Equal wording does not ensure equal opportunity; a relevant accessible arrangement addresses the barrier."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-identity-q4"}, {"title": "Just-world reasoning", "prompt": "Someone blames a disadvantaged group entirely for its difficulties because everyone gets what they deserve. Explain the phenomenon and an evidential limitation. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] This illustrates a just-world belief.", "[1] It can support victim blaming and prejudice by treating outcomes as deserved.", "[1] It overlooks barriers and situational influences.", "[1] Evidence about actual circumstances is needed; the belief itself cannot establish personal cause."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-identity-q6"}, {"id": "psy11-identity-prejudice-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A club says one group is unreliable, expresses dislike and refuses its members entry. Distinguish stereotype, prejudice and direct discrimination with evidence. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The generalised unreliable belief is a stereotype.", "[2] Expressed dislike based on membership is prejudice.", "[2] Refusal of entry based on membership is discriminatory treatment; distinguish the action from belief and feeling."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-identity-prejudice-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A compulsory participation time disadvantages students with caregiving duties, although everyone receives the same rule. Explain possible indirect discrimination and propose a targeted change. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A uniform rule can have unequal effects on a group; identical wording does not guarantee equal opportunity.", "[2] Specify the caregiving conflict and evaluate whether the requirement is necessary and appropriately accommodated.", "[2] Offer equivalent participation times or an accessible alternative, then evaluate actual participation rather than relying only on favourable attitudes."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which example is discrimination rather than only a belief?", "o": ["Refusing someone entry because of their group membership.", "Believing a group shares a trait without an action described.", "Privately evaluating a group without treatment described.", "Recording an individual’s own chosen identity."], "a": 0, "why": "Discrimination concerns treatment; stereotypes concern beliefs and prejudice concerns evaluations."}], "subtitle": "18 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Attitudes, stereotypes and social influence", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-identity-contact", "title": "Contact, superordinate goals and Sherif (1954)", "scope": "Contact quality matters more than physical proximity · A superordinate goal requires joint effort · Conflict reduction still needs ethical evaluation", "parent": "psy11-identity", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Contact, superordinate goals and Sherif (1954)</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Contact quality matters more than physical proximity</li><li>A superordinate goal requires joint effort</li><li>Conflict reduction still needs ethical evaluation</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Contact hypothesis and equal status", "kind": "learn", "text": "Contact can reduce prejudice under supportive conditions, but mere proximity does not guarantee improvement. Equal-status contact reduces a hierarchy in which one group is treated as inferior. Mutual interdependence means groups need each other to complete a shared task. A superordinate goal is a meaningful goal that neither group can achieve alone. Cooperation, institutional support and opportunities for individual understanding can help challenge stereotypes. Seating rival groups together while making them compete for one prize may intensify hostility instead. Explain why the particular contact arrangement changes relationships: a shared repair task requires cooperative contribution and makes group members useful partners. Distinguish a common interest from genuine interdependence.", "diagram": "contact"}, {"title": "Sherif et al. (1954): aim and group formation", "kind": "learn", "text": "The Robbers Cave study investigated how intergroup conflict could develop and be reduced among boys at a summer camp. The sample consisted of twenty-two boys around eleven years old from a restricted background. Researchers formed two groups that initially developed their own activities, identities and norms without contact with the other group. Later competitive activities with prizes created conditions for rivalry. Staff made observations of attitudes and interactions in a naturalistic camp context. This provides an example of a field investigation with deliberate manipulation of group conditions. Describe the stages and the outcome measures rather than saying simply that boys went camping. The selected sample and researcher-created conditions are important when evaluating the evidence.", "diagram": "robberscave"}, {"title": "Sherif et al. (1954): conflict and superordinate goals", "kind": "learn", "text": "Competition between the groups was associated with hostility, negative labels and antagonistic behaviour. Simply bringing them together in some situations did not immediately remove the conflict. Tasks involving superordinate goals, such as addressing a shared camp problem and coordinating effort, increased cooperation and helped reduce hostility. The study contributed to understanding resource competition and the role of mutual interdependence in improving intergroup relations. Keep the mechanism explicit: both groups needed to contribute to an outcome valuable to everyone, changing the incentive to cooperate. The finding does not mean every conflict can be solved by one shared task or that a reward automatically produces prejudice. Explain the sequence of conditions and the observed changes in relations.", "diagram": "robberscave"}, {"title": "Robbers Cave: evaluation and ethical issues", "kind": "learn", "text": "The camp setting provided realistic interaction and opportunities to observe group processes over time. Deliberately introduced competition and shared goals helped investigate the development and reduction of conflict. However, the narrow sample limits generalisation across genders, ages, cultural backgrounds and existing conflicts. Researchers influenced events and interpreted behaviour, creating possible bias. Children did not fully understand the research purpose, and induced hostility raised concerns about deception, consent and protection from psychological harm. A modern school activity should not deliberately create bullying to copy the study. Evaluation should link the method to both evidence and risk. Observing group processes outside a laboratory can improve realism while making some variables harder to control.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Designing prejudice reduction in a scenario", "kind": "learn", "text": "Suppose two clubs stereotype one another and compete for access to a facility. A useful proposal could involve members working with equal roles to achieve a shared outcome that genuinely needs both groups, supported by fair rules and staff. Explain equal status, mutual interdependence and the superordinate goal with concrete features. Include a way to evaluate change, such as confidential attitude measures and behavioural indicators collected before and after with an appropriate comparison. A fall in reported prejudice alone may reflect socially desirable responding. Look for corroborating behaviour and acknowledge limits of causal inference. Avoid using a simple contact plan that retains unequal power or competitive incentives. Psychological knowledge and research design should both appear in the answer.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Contact quality matters more than physical proximity", "kind": "learn", "text": "The contact hypothesis proposes that contact can reduce prejudice under supportive conditions, including equal status in the situation, common goals, cooperation and institutional support. Placing groups in the same room while rewarding competition may intensify tension rather than reduce it. Equal status means meaningful participation and influence in the task, not merely identical uniforms. Explain how the setting supports a new interpretation of outgroup members and whether both groups have a fair opportunity to contribute.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A superordinate goal requires joint effort", "kind": "learn", "text": "Sherif and colleagues’ 1954 Robbers Cave study involved boys in a camp setting, with group formation followed by intergroup competition and then efforts to reduce conflict. Shared activities alone were less effective than tasks requiring cooperation for a goal neither group could readily achieve independently. Superordinate goals change the interdependence between groups; they are not simply prizes offered to the winning side. Describe the stages and their purpose without mixing the study with unrelated group experiments.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Conflict reduction still needs ethical evaluation", "kind": "learn", "text": "The staged camp design provided rich evidence about group processes but involved a narrow sample and researcher involvement in creating competition. Deception, informed choice and deliberately induced hostility raise ethical concerns. A school application should create genuinely cooperative, low-risk tasks rather than reproduce manipulation of conflict. Assess outcomes with behaviour and attitudes over time, and consider power differences. One successful joint task may reduce immediate tension without proving that all prejudice or structural inequality has disappeared.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Contact and inequality reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Contact and inequality reference</h3><p>Use the actual scenario to identify conditions and effects.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Relevant condition</th><th>Weak substitute</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Equal status</td><td>Comparable respected roles</td><td>One group treated as inferior</td></tr><tr><td>Superordinate goal</td><td>Both need a valued shared outcome</td><td>Only same room</td></tr><tr><td>Mutual interdependence</td><td>Contributions require cooperation</td><td>Competing for one winner</td></tr><tr><td>Direct discrimination</td><td>Explicit unequal treatment</td><td>Not simply a private belief</td></tr><tr><td>Indirect discrimination</td><td>General rule has unequal group effect</td><td>Equal wording assumed sufficient</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A plan needs an appropriate outcome measure: attitude change and accessibility change are not interchangeable.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the research and authoritative explanations", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Further evidence</h3><p>The lesson explanations provide the required student account. These links allow study details and educational health information to be checked. Read historical methods critically; they are not activities to reproduce.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Sherif/chap1.htm\">Sherif et al., original Robbers Cave report</a></li></ul>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Robbers Cave stages", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Robbers Cave stages", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Prejudice-reduction plan", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: intergroup relations", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Robbers Cave stages", "prompt": "Describe the main sequence of Sherif et al. (1954) and explain why superordinate goals mattered. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Separate groups developed identities/norms in the camp.", "[2] Competitive activities with rewards were associated with hostility.", "[2] Shared goals requiring both groups’ contribution created mutual interdependence and increased cooperation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-identity-q3"}, {"title": "Prejudice-reduction plan", "prompt": "Propose three features of contact between rival clubs and explain their mechanisms. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Equal-status roles reduce hierarchy and unequal treatment.", "[2] A meaningful superordinate goal offers an outcome both value and neither can achieve alone.", "[2] Mutual interdependence requires cooperative contributions, encouraging constructive interaction rather than competitive contact."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-identity-q5"}, {"title": "Extended response: intergroup relations", "prompt": "Compare social identity and resource-competition explanations, apply the Robbers Cave study and evaluate a contact-based response. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Explains identity processes and ingroup/outgroup comparison.", "[2] Distinguishes competition over resources as an additional mechanism.", "[2] Describes the designated camp stages and observed hostility/cooperation.", "[2] Proposes equal status and genuine superordinate/interdependent goals.", "[2] Evaluates narrow sample, researcher influence or child ethics with consequences.", "[2] Suggests a valid measure/comparison and recognises that contact alone does not guarantee lasting change."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-identity-q7"}, {"id": "psy11-identity-contact-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Design a low-risk school task using a superordinate goal to improve relations between two clubs. Explain two contact conditions. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Use a shared outcome requiring both clubs’ contributions, such as jointly producing an accessible event, rather than competing for one prize.", "[2] Provide equal status/meaningful roles and cooperative interdependence within the task.", "[2] Institutional support and fair procedures reinforce the shared goal; assess outcomes without assuming one task eliminates prejudice."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-identity-contact-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain how Robbers Cave (1954) linked competition and cooperation to intergroup relations, then evaluate one methodological and one ethical limitation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Group formation preceded competition associated with hostility; later superordinate goals required cooperation and supported reduced conflict.", "[2] A narrow boys’ camp sample and researcher-shaped setting restrict generalisation/control interpretation.", "[2] Deliberately induced hostility and deception raise harm and informed-consent concerns; influence does not justify recreating the procedure."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What makes a goal superordinate?", "o": ["Its achievement requires cooperation across the groups.", "Only one group can win the reward.", "Groups sit together without needing to interact.", "An authority labels one group superior."], "a": 0, "why": "Shared interdependence distinguishes a superordinate goal from competitive or merely parallel activity."}, {"q": "Adopting a group’s norms concerns what?", "o": ["Social identification", "Only reception", "Extinction", "Refinement"], "a": 0, "why": "Identification incorporates membership into self-concept."}, {"q": "Unequal treatment based on membership is what?", "o": ["Discrimination", "A belief alone", "Affect alone", "Only memory loss"], "a": 0, "why": "Discrimination concerns behaviour, distinct from stereotype and prejudice."}, {"q": "A neutral-looking inaccessible rule can be what?", "o": ["Indirect discrimination", "Always equal opportunity", "Only internalisation", "Always direct refusal"], "a": 0, "why": "A general rule can disproportionately disadvantage a group."}, {"q": "A superordinate goal requires what?", "o": ["Valued cooperation that neither group can achieve alone", "Competition for one prize", "Only proximity", "Unequal roles"], "a": 0, "why": "Shared interdependence is central."}, {"q": "Robbers Cave generalisation is limited by what?", "o": ["Restricted sample and created conditions", "Its absence of any groups", "Its brain scans", "Its recognition words"], "a": 0, "why": "Selected boys and camp manipulation limit universal claims."}], "subtitle": "20 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Attitudes, stereotypes and social influence", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-influence-kelman", "title": "Kelman: compliance, identification and internalisation", "scope": "Public agreement and private acceptance differ · Persistence is evidence, not a complete mind reading · Apply the model to change over time", "parent": "psy11-influence", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Kelman: compliance, identification and internalisation</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Public agreement and private acceptance differ</li><li>Persistence is evidence, not a complete mind reading</li><li>Apply the model to change over time</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Kelman’s levels of social influence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Kelman (1958) distinguished compliance, identification and internalisation as processes of social influence. Compliance involves changing public behaviour to obtain approval or avoid disapproval without necessarily changing private beliefs. Identification involves adopting behaviour or views because of a valued relationship or group identity. Internalisation involves accepting a view as consistent with one’s values and continuing it without the original social pressure. These processes can produce similar visible behaviour for different reasons. A student who follows a rule only while supervised illustrates compliance; one who follows it because a respected team models it may show identification; one who endorses its fairness may show internalisation. Explain the reason for the change, not only that the person copied someone.", "diagram": "kelman"}, {"title": "Compliance and private belief", "kind": "learn", "text": "Compliance is an observable change made in response to social pressure or incentives while private acceptance may be absent. A person may publicly agree with friends to avoid ridicule while privately disagreeing. This differs from obedience when the defining feature is a direct order from an authority, and from internalisation when the belief is genuinely accepted. In an exam scenario, show the evidence for temporary public agreement and the absence of private change. A visible action alone cannot prove which process occurred, so a researcher may need confidential attitude measures or behaviour when pressure is removed. Do not use compliance to mean every form of cooperation. The term has a specific psychological meaning in this theoretical framework.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Public agreement after pressure is removed", "kind": "learn", "text": "To distinguish compliance from internalisation, examine what happens when the audience or incentive is absent and, where ethical, ask about private acceptance. A public answer under unanimous pressure is evidence of conformity in that setting; it does not alone show that the person’s belief changed permanently. Follow-up behaviour and confidential reports can help, but demand characteristics and memory still require evaluation. A good research question operationalises the relevant change rather than assuming all agreement has the same motive. This connects Kelman’s account to the limits of Asch’s public-answer measure and demonstrates why theories and measurement choices must be considered together.", "diagram": "kelman"}, {"title": "Public agreement and private acceptance differ", "kind": "learn", "text": "Compliance involves outward conformity to obtain a favourable response or avoid disapproval, without necessarily changing private belief. Identification involves adopting behaviour or attitudes to maintain a valued relationship or group connection. Internalisation involves accepting an attitude because it fits one’s values or understanding, so it can persist without the immediate audience. These are explanations of the basis of influence, not a simple sequence that everyone must follow. Look at why the person agrees and what happens when the social source is absent.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Persistence is evidence, not a complete mind reading", "kind": "learn", "text": "A student agrees with a rule only while an authority watches, which is consistent with compliance. Continuing privately may support internalisation, but could also reflect habit, ongoing rewards or identification with a valued group. A vignette often supplies the motive explicitly; in research the motive must be assessed rather than assumed from one action. Ask what information distinguishes the levels and avoid treating visible agreement as proof of private belief. Several influences can coexist in everyday decisions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Apply the model to change over time", "kind": "learn", "text": "A student may initially follow a safety practice to avoid criticism, later adopt it to fit a valued team, and eventually accept its rationale. The model helps describe changes in the source of agreement but does not guarantee this progression. To evaluate an intervention, examine behaviour and reasons when the audience changes. Respectful explanation and evidence may support internal acceptance, while public pressure may secure only temporary action. A successful public campaign is therefore not automatically evidence of stable attitude change.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Kelman in a vignette", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Kelman in a vignette", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Kelman in a vignette", "prompt": "Three students follow a rule: one only to avoid disapproval, one to resemble a valued group and one because they believe it is fair. Apply Kelman’s processes. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] First illustrates compliance: public action under pressure without necessary private acceptance.", "[2] Second illustrates identification with a valued relationship/group.", "[2] Third illustrates internalisation because the rule fits personal values and can persist without pressure."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-influence-q1"}, {"id": "psy11-influence-kelman-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student publicly supports a policy to avoid ridicule but privately disagrees and stops supporting it when peers leave. Identify Kelman’s level and contrast it with internalisation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] This is consistent with compliance: outward agreement motivated by avoiding social costs.", "[2] Private disagreement and disappearance of support with the audience fit limited private acceptance.", "[2] Internalisation would involve accepting the policy as consistent with values/understanding and potentially maintaining it without the audience."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-influence-kelman-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A student adopts a musician’s environmental views because they admire that person. Later the student explains that the evidence independently fits their values. Explain two possible levels and one evidential caution. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Adoption to maintain connection with an admired person is consistent with identification.", "[2] Independent acceptance based on evidence/values is consistent with internalisation.", "[2] Reported reasons and persistence provide evidence but motives can coexist; a single action does not directly reveal private belief."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which example best fits internalisation?", "o": ["Accepting a position because it fits one’s values and reasoning.", "Agreeing only to avoid immediate ridicule.", "Copying a valued person solely to preserve the relationship.", "Never reconsidering a belief regardless of evidence."], "a": 0, "why": "Internalisation concerns private acceptance grounded in values or understanding, not merely public pressure."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Attitudes, stereotypes and social influence", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-influence-obedience", "title": "Obedience and Milgram (1963): methods and ethics", "scope": "Obedience concerns an authority’s instruction · Keep Milgram’s 1963 baseline distinct · Observed obedience does not identify a fixed personality", "parent": "psy11-influence", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Obedience and Milgram (1963): methods and ethics</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Obedience concerns an authority’s instruction</li><li>Keep Milgram’s 1963 baseline distinct</li><li>Observed obedience does not identify a fixed personality</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Obedience and authority", "kind": "learn", "text": "Obedience involves changing behaviour in response to a direct command from a perceived authority. Authority can arise from an institution, role or accepted expertise; it is not the same as the numerical size of a group. A referee giving a player an instruction is a different influence from teammates expressing a shared preference. Obedience can support coordinated and protective behaviour, but may also lead to harmful acts under inappropriate orders. A psychological account explains conditions and responses rather than excusing responsibility. In a question, identify the authority, the order and the resulting action. Distinguish this from conformity to an implied group norm without a direct command. Some situations can involve both mechanisms.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Milgram (1963): aim and recruitment", "kind": "learn", "text": "Milgram’s 1963 study investigated how far people would obey an authority who instructed them to deliver increasing apparent electric shocks to another person. Forty male volunteers from the New Haven area, aged twenty to fifty, attended a Yale setting for what they believed was a study of learning and memory. The participant was assigned the teacher role through a rigged procedure, while the learner was a confederate. The shock generator had labelled switches up to 450 volts. This was a particular published baseline study, not every later variation. Explain the deceptive cover story and authority context, because they shaped participants’ understanding. The learner did not actually receive the escalating shocks the participants believed they were administering.", "diagram": "milgram"}, {"title": "Milgram (1963): method and findings", "kind": "learn", "text": "Participants delivered an apparent shock for each incorrect response, increasing the level as instructed by the experimenter. When participants hesitated, standardised prompts encouraged continuation. The learner’s scripted responses and eventual lack of response contributed to the situation. The main behavioural outcome was the maximum level reached. Twenty-six of the forty participants, or 65%, continued to the highest level of 450 volts. Many showed visible distress, including sweating, tension or nervous laughter. This result demonstrates substantial obedience in that setting, not that 65% of all people will obey every harmful order. Avoid interpreting nervous laughter as enjoyment. Describe the procedure and outcome separately, then connect them to the role of authority and situational pressure.", "diagram": "milgram"}, {"title": "Milgram: contribution, ethics and generalisation", "kind": "learn", "text": "The study challenged assumptions that harmful obedience requires only unusual personal dispositions and showed the influence of an authoritative situation. Standardised apparatus and prompts supported consistent conditions. However, the restricted volunteer sample, artificial cover story and Yale context limit broad generalisation. Deception prevented fully informed understanding, visible distress challenged protection from harm and repeated prompts could undermine perceived withdrawal rights. Debriefing can clarify deception but cannot erase what occurred during participation. A balanced evaluation recognises both the contribution and these serious concerns. Do not suggest recreating the procedure at school. Distinguish the 1963 study from later variations involving other locations or proximity conditions, since mixing methods can lose accuracy in an exam response.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Obedience concerns an authority’s instruction", "kind": "learn", "text": "Obedience involves responding to an instruction from someone perceived as an authority. Conformity concerns alignment with group behaviour or norms, while compliance can concern outward agreement. These processes can overlap, but the source of influence matters. A student following a teacher’s direct instruction is not the same evidence as copying peers’ line answers. Authority can be legitimate and helpful; explaining obedience scientifically does not imply that every instruction is harmful or that all responsibility disappears.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Keep Milgram’s 1963 baseline distinct", "kind": "learn", "text": "The designated baseline involved adult male volunteers who believed they were participating in a learning-and-memory study. They were assigned the teacher role through a rigged arrangement while a confederate acted as learner. The teacher was prompted to increase apparent shock levels following errors; the learner received no real shocks. In the reported baseline 26 of 40 participants, or 65%, reached the maximum level. Later proximity and setting variations are separate studies/conditions and should not replace the designated method.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Observed obedience does not identify a fixed personality", "kind": "learn", "text": "The result showed powerful situational influence under that experimental arrangement, with participants also showing distress and conflict. It did not prove that 65% of all people would harm someone in any context. Recruitment, period, sample and the laboratory situation limit generalisation. Deception, psychological distress and pressure around withdrawal are central ethical concerns. Evaluation should connect each concern to a procedural detail and explain the consequence. A nominal right to leave is inadequate if the practical interaction strongly undermines it.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Milgram’s outcome", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Milgram’s outcome", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Milgram ethics", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Milgram’s outcome", "prompt": "In the designated 1963 study, 26 of 40 participants reached the maximum switch. Calculate the percentage and explain what it does and does not show. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] 26/40 × 100 = 65%.", "[1] It describes the proportion reaching 450 volts in that studied condition.", "[1] It supports substantial obedience under the specified authority context.", "[1] It does not show that 65% of all people obey every harmful order or enjoyed harming the learner."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-influence-q2"}, {"title": "Milgram ethics", "prompt": "Explain two ethical concerns in the 1963 study and why debriefing alone cannot remove them. (5 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Deception prevented fully informed understanding of roles and apparent shocks.", "[2] Distress and continued prompts challenged protection from harm and perceived withdrawal rights.", "[1] Later explanation cannot undo the distress or guarantee the earlier choice was fully voluntary."], "allocations": [2, 2, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-influence-q6"}, {"id": "psy11-influence-obedience-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain the meaning and limits of the 65% result in Milgram (1963), including what participants believed and what actually happened. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] In the baseline 26/40 reached the maximum apparent shock level under authority prompts.", "[2] Participants believed shocks were being administered, while the confederate received no real shocks.", "[2] The percentage describes this sample/condition, not all people or every authority context; distress and methodological limits matter."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-influence-obedience-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare obedience with conformity using a teacher instruction and a peer-group judgement. Evaluate one ethical issue in Milgram’s method. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Following a teacher’s direct instruction illustrates influence from perceived authority/obedience.", "[2] Aligning an answer with peers illustrates conformity to a group rather than necessarily an authority command.", "[2] Deception, distress or prods affecting meaningful withdrawal created participant-protection concerns; specify the method detail and consequence."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What did 65% describe in the designated Milgram baseline?", "o": ["26 of 40 participants reached the maximum apparent shock level.", "65% of all humans always obey every harmful command.", "65% of learners received real severe shocks.", "A finding from the Bobo-doll study."], "a": 0, "why": "The result belongs to a specific sample and condition, with apparent rather than actual shocks."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Attitudes, stereotypes and social influence", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-influence-conformity", "title": "Conformity and Asch (1951): evidence and mechanisms", "scope": "Normative and informational influence have different motives · Asch’s line task separated judgement from social pressure · Explain the contribution and the limits", "parent": "psy11-influence", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Conformity and Asch (1951): evidence and mechanisms</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Normative and informational influence have different motives</li><li>Asch’s line task separated judgement from social pressure</li><li>Explain the contribution and the limits</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Normative and informational conformity", "kind": "learn", "text": "Conformity involves adjusting behaviour or beliefs towards a group norm. Normative influence arises from wanting acceptance or avoiding social disapproval, even if private judgement differs. Informational influence arises from using others as evidence about what is correct, especially when the situation is ambiguous or unfamiliar. A person copying a group’s answer despite knowing it is wrong may show normative influence. A new visitor following others towards an exit because they seem knowledgeable may show informational influence. The two can operate together. Explain the motive and uncertainty shown in the scenario. Conformity need not involve a direct order and is not automatically harmful; learning appropriate local behaviour can be useful when the information is reliable.", "diagram": "conformity"}, {"title": "Asch (1951): line-judgement method", "kind": "learn", "text": "Asch’s 1951 line-judgement investigation examined the influence of a unanimous majority on an individual’s response to a clear perceptual task. Participants judged which comparison line matched a standard line while seated with others who were actually confederates. Responses were spoken publicly, and confederates deliberately gave the same wrong answer on critical trials. The real participant responded after most of the group. A comparison without the misleading majority helped show that the task itself was straightforward. The measured outcome was conformity to the incorrect majority on critical trials, not a general intelligence score. Explain the public-answer arrangement and unanimous error because these features created the social pressure. The study is distinct from later work manipulating specific conformity factors.", "diagram": "asch"}, {"title": "Asch: findings and interpretation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Participants sometimes gave the majority’s incorrect answer even though the visual comparison was clear and errors without group pressure were rare. The result shows that public judgement can be influenced by a unanimous group. Interviews and later interpretations suggest normative pressure was important for many participants, while some doubted their own perception. Do not say every participant conformed on every trial or that a public answer always reflected private belief. Precise rates differ across reports and variations, so identify what a stated percentage measures if using it: critical-trial responses differ from the percentage of people conforming at least once. Apply the study to a scenario by describing pressure and the response rather than merely writing that people follow crowds.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Normative and informational influence have different motives", "kind": "learn", "text": "Normative influence involves alignment to gain acceptance or avoid rejection; informational influence involves treating others as evidence about what is correct, especially under uncertainty. The same public agreement can result from either. A participant who knows an answer but agrees to avoid standing out illustrates a normative explanation. A participant who changes their judgement because the group appears better informed illustrates an informational explanation. Use the stated motive or evidence rather than inferring a motive from agreement alone.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Asch’s line task separated judgement from social pressure", "kind": "learn", "text": "In the designated line-judgement procedure a participant responded alongside confederates who gave predetermined answers on critical trials. The objectively straightforward task allowed researchers to compare responses with group errors. Often-quoted findings distinguish the proportion of conforming critical-trial responses from the proportion of people conforming at least once. These are different denominators. A student answer should explain what was counted and avoid saying everyone conformed or that most answers were wrong when the finding concerns only a subset of trials.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Explain the contribution and the limits", "kind": "learn", "text": "The procedure showed that group pressure can influence public judgement even on a clear task, while many responses remained independent. Laboratory artificiality, deception and the original participant context limit broad conclusions. Public response formats may increase pressure and do not always reveal private acceptance. An evaluation should consider why the clear task helps test social influence and why it differs from consequential everyday decisions. Avoid treating one experiment as proof that people have no independent judgement.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read, apply and qualify the focused model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read each label and distinguish the relationships or levels of explanation. Describe the pattern in your own words, then use the accompanying theory to explain why it matters. Apply the model to the written questions in this lesson and state its limit rather than treating an illustration as proof. Where a diagram uses fictional numbers or arbitrary units, keep those separate from the findings of a designated study.", "diagram": "conformity-denominators"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Two conformity motives", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Two conformity motives", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Asch’s design", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Two conformity motives", "prompt": "A student copies peers to avoid ridicule; another follows peers because the task is unfamiliar. Compare normative and informational influence. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Avoiding ridicule reflects a motive for acceptance or avoiding disapproval: normative influence.", "[2] Using others as evidence when uncertain reflects informational influence; private acceptance may be more likely but is not guaranteed."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-influence-q3"}, {"title": "Asch’s design", "prompt": "Describe Asch’s 1951 method and identify a limitation of interpreting public answers as private beliefs. (5 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A real participant judges matching lines publicly after confederates on critical trials.", "[1] Confederates give the same wrong answer, creating unanimous majority pressure on a clear task.", "[1] Measured conformity is an incorrect public response.", "[1] It may reflect compliance rather than changed private perception or belief."], "allocations": [2, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-influence-q4"}, {"id": "psy11-influence-conformity-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student is confident their answer is correct but gives the group’s answer to avoid ridicule. Explain the relevant conformity mechanism and contrast it with informational influence. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Avoiding ridicule/obtaining acceptance fits normative social influence.", "[2] Public agreement can occur without private belief change, as in the stated confidence.", "[2] Informational influence would involve treating others’ answers as evidence of correctness, especially under uncertainty."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-influence-conformity-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "In fictional line-task data, 12 of 20 people conform at least once but 18 of 100 critical responses conform. Calculate both percentages and explain why they differ. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] People conforming at least once: 12/20 × 100 = 60%.", "[2] Conforming critical responses: 18/100 × 100 = 18%.", "[2] The denominators are people versus responses; occasional conformity by many people can coexist with mostly independent responses."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which mechanism uses others as evidence of the correct answer?", "o": ["Informational social influence.", "Normative influence solely to avoid rejection.", "Contralateral motor control.", "A reinforcement schedule defined by elapsed time."], "a": 0, "why": "Informational influence concerns perceived information, whereas normative influence concerns social acceptance."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Attitudes, stereotypes and social influence", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-influence-context", "title": "Group size, unanimity, culture and deindividuation", "scope": "Unanimity and group size are distinct variables · Culture and identity can alter a situation’s meaning · Deindividuation is a proposed process, not an excuse", "parent": "psy11-influence", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Group size, unanimity, culture and deindividuation</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Unanimity and group size are distinct variables</li><li>Culture and identity can alter a situation’s meaning</li><li>Deindividuation is a proposed process, not an excuse</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Conformity factors: size and unanimity", "kind": "learn", "text": "Group size can increase conformity when a small majority becomes more persuasive, but adding more people does not produce an unlimited linear increase. Unanimity matters because a dissenter can reduce the impression that everyone else agrees and provide social support for an independent response. The dissenter need not always give the correct answer to weaken unanimity. A larger group with several competing views may exert different pressure from a smaller unanimous group. Explain the actual social information: one dissenting classmate can make disagreement feel more acceptable. Do not confuse majority size with authority status. Research on these factors includes variations beyond the designated 1951 study, so keep baseline method and later factor evidence distinct.", "diagram": "conformity"}, {"title": "Culture, deindividuation and social context", "kind": "learn", "text": "Cultural norms concerning group obligation, independence and agreement can influence conformity, but broad cultural labels do not predict every individual. Deindividuation involves reduced salience of personal identity or accountability within a group, which can alter behaviour in relation to group norms. It does not inevitably cause violence and can support prosocial behaviour when those norms are helpful. An anonymous crowd may make a person feel less individually accountable, while visible individual responsibility can change the response. Explain anonymity, accountability or group norm in the specific situation rather than assuming every group produces the same effect. Context, task importance and expertise can also influence informational and normative processes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluating social-influence evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Controlled studies isolate selected pressures but often use artificial tasks and restricted samples. Real-life conformity or obedience can involve enduring relationships, cultural meanings and consequences that a short laboratory task does not reproduce. A strong evaluation explains this consequence for external validity while recognising the value of a controlled comparison. If a study records public agreement, its findings may not establish lasting private acceptance. An improvement could include confidential follow-up measures, with ethical safeguards, rather than exposing participants to more pressure. Extended responses should identify theory, describe the designated study accurately, apply it and assess limitations. Conformity, obedience and Kelman’s processes are related explanations with different defining features, so explicitly distinguish them.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Unanimity and group size are distinct variables", "kind": "learn", "text": "Increasing group size can increase pressure over some ranges, but the relationship is not unlimited or necessarily linear. A dissenting ally can reduce unanimity and support independent responding even if the majority remains large. To isolate unanimity, compare groups of similar size with and without a dissenter; changing both size and unanimity makes the cause less clear. Consider whether the ally gives the correct answer or simply breaks agreement, and avoid importing one variation’s results into the designated baseline method.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Culture and identity can alter a situation’s meaning", "kind": "learn", "text": "Group norms, social relationships and expectations of independence or obligation influence how social pressure is experienced. Culture is not a uniform personality shared by every member of a country. A social-identity perspective can explain why a valued ingroup’s norm is more influential than an irrelevant outgroup’s statement. Context therefore matters alongside task uncertainty and consequences. Evidence of cultural differences needs suitable sampling and comparable measures, rather than assuming any result reflects national character.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Deindividuation is a proposed process, not an excuse", "kind": "learn", "text": "Reduced individual visibility or accountability in a group can contribute to deindividuation explanations of behaviour. Outcomes also depend on salient group norms: anonymity need not produce antisocial action when cooperative norms are strong. A masked crowd and a private online group may differ in identity, authority and expectations. Explain the proposed mechanism and the relevant norm, then retain responsibility and alternative influences. Interventions can increase accountability and support constructive norms without assuming that naming individuals solves every group problem.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Influence comparison reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Influence comparison reference</h3><p>Kelman processes and behavioural influence categories describe different aspects.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Process</th><th>Defining feature</th><th>Private acceptance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Compliance</td><td>Public change for approval/avoid disapproval</td><td>Not necessary</td></tr><tr><td>Identification</td><td>Valued relationship/group identity</td><td>Linked to relationship</td></tr><tr><td>Internalisation</td><td>Accepted as consistent with values</td><td>Central</td></tr><tr><td>Obedience</td><td>Direct command by perceived authority</td><td>Varies</td></tr><tr><td>Conformity</td><td>Adjustment towards a norm</td><td>Varies; normative/informational</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>One visible action cannot establish every motive. Explain authority, group information, social acceptance and value endorsement using concrete evidence.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the research and authoritative explanations", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Further evidence</h3><p>The lesson explanations provide the required student account. These links allow study details and educational health information to be checked. Read historical methods critically; they are not activities to reproduce.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://irbmember.web.unc.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/7878/2017/01/1963_Milgram_BehavioralStudy.pdf\">Milgram (1963), original report</a></li></ul>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Unanimity and dissent", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: social influence evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Unanimity and dissent", "prompt": "Explain why one dissenter may reduce conformity and why adding more people is not an unlimited linear rule. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A dissenter breaks unanimity and can provide support for an independent response.", "[2] Majority-size effects depend on context and often plateau; size alone does not determine pressure."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-influence-q5"}, {"title": "Extended response: social influence evidence", "prompt": "Compare obedience and conformity, apply Milgram (1963) and Asch (1951), and evaluate their evidence and generalisation. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Distinguishes direct authority commands from adjustment to group norms.", "[2] Describes the 1963 teacher/learner deception, increasing switches and 65% outcome.", "[2] Describes Asch’s public line judgements and unanimous incorrect majority.", "[2] Applies normative/informational or authority processes to the stated evidence.", "[2] Evaluates ethical concerns and narrow/artificial samples with consequences.", "[2] Distinguishes public performance from private belief and avoids mixing later study variations into baseline methods."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-influence-q7"}, {"id": "psy11-influence-context-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A researcher compares a large unanimous group with a small group containing a dissenter and attributes the difference solely to unanimity. Evaluate the design and propose a targeted improvement. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Both group size and unanimity change, so either may contribute to the response difference.", "[2] This confounding weakens attribution solely to unanimity.", "[2] Hold group size and other relevant conditions comparable while varying the presence of a dissenter."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-influence-context-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Evaluate the claim that anonymity inevitably makes groups antisocial. Use deindividuation and group norms. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Reduced accountability/individual visibility can support a deindividuation explanation.", "[2] Behaviour also depends on salient norms; anonymous groups can act cooperatively when those norms support helping.", "[2] Context, identity and consequences vary, so inevitability is unsupported; evaluate specific evidence and support constructive accountability/norms."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why is a dissenter important in conformity research?", "o": ["The dissenter breaks unanimity and can support independent responding.", "The dissenter necessarily makes the group larger.", "A dissenter guarantees that no participant ever conforms.", "Unanimity is identical to the number of group members."], "a": 0, "why": "Unanimity concerns agreement; size concerns the number of people, so they must be distinguished."}, {"q": "Private value-based acceptance is what in Kelman’s account?", "o": ["Internalisation", "Compliance only", "Pruning", "Shock"], "a": 0, "why": "It persists because the view fits values."}, {"q": "A direct command by authority defines what?", "o": ["Obedience", "Every conformity case", "Memory encoding", "Altruism"], "a": 0, "why": "Authority and an explicit order distinguish obedience."}, {"q": "Following others to be accepted illustrates what?", "o": ["Normative influence", "Only transduction", "Pure informational uncertainty", "A placebo measure"], "a": 0, "why": "The motive is social acceptance or avoiding disapproval."}, {"q": "Milgram’s 26/40 highest-level result equals what?", "o": ["65%", "26%", "40%", "75%"], "a": 0, "why": "26 divided by 40 times 100 equals 65, specific to that condition."}, {"q": "What does one dissenter change?", "o": ["Unanimity and support for independence", "The learner’s brain surgery", "Every cultural norm", "The physical line lengths"], "a": 0, "why": "Breaking unanimity can weaken conformity pressure."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Attitudes, stereotypes and social influence", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-helping-bystanders", "title": "Bystander explanations and Latané–Darley (1968)", "scope": "Several processes can delay action · Use the smoke-filled-room study’s unit of analysis · Apply the explanation without creating an emergency", "parent": "psy11-helping", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Bystander explanations and Latané–Darley (1968)</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Several processes can delay action</li><li>Use the smoke-filled-room study’s unit of analysis</li><li>Apply the explanation without creating an emergency</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "The bystander effect", "kind": "learn", "text": "The bystander effect describes a tendency for the presence of other people to reduce the likelihood of an individual intervening in some situations. It is a tendency, not an absolute law: others can also encourage coordinated help. Several processes can contribute. People may assume someone else will take responsibility, look to passive others to judge whether the event is serious, or fear embarrassment from an unnecessary response. Identify the actual mechanism in the scenario rather than using bystander effect as a complete explanation. A witnessed emergency may be clearer or more ambiguous, and personal competence or perceived danger can influence decisions. The number of observers is only one feature of the social situation.", "diagram": "bystander"}, {"title": "Diffusion of responsibility", "kind": "learn", "text": "Diffusion of responsibility occurs when each observer feels less personally responsible because responsibility seems shared among others. A student may assume that another bystander has already reported a problem and therefore do nothing. The key is reduced individual obligation, not inability to notice the event. Assigning a clear role to a named person can reduce diffusion, because responsibility becomes identifiable. In an exam explanation, state the number or presence of others, the assumption about who will act and the effect on the individual’s response. Do not confuse this with audience inhibition, which concerns fear of other people’s judgement, or with informational influence, which concerns interpreting the situation through others’ reactions.", "diagram": "bystander"}, {"title": "Audience inhibition and social influence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Audience inhibition is reluctance to act because of concern about embarrassment or negative evaluation by others. A bystander may fear appearing foolish if a situation turns out not to be serious. Social influence can also lead people to use others’ calm behaviour as information that no intervention is needed. When everyone privately feels concern but publicly remains calm, observers can mistakenly infer that concern is unnecessary. This is often discussed as pluralistic ignorance. A vignette about fear of ridicule points to audience inhibition; one about interpreting others’ calmness points to informational social influence. Explain these as separate mechanisms even when they interact. Merely saying that a crowd was present does not distinguish them.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Latané and Darley (1968): designated method", "kind": "learn", "text": "The designated 1968 smoke-filled-room study investigated how the presence and behaviour of other people influenced reporting a potential emergency. Male students completed questionnaires in a room while smoke gradually entered. Conditions included being alone, being with two passive confederates and being in groups of three genuine participants. Researchers observed whether the smoke was reported within the study period and the timing of responses. The passive confederates were instructed not to respond. This is not the separate seizure/intercom bystander study, which used a different emergency and method. The distinction matters because the syllabus specifies the smoke-filled-room investigation. Explain what changed between conditions and what counted as intervention.", "diagram": "smoke"}, {"title": "Smoke-filled room: findings and contribution", "kind": "learn", "text": "About 75% of participants alone reported smoke, compared with 10% when accompanied by two passive confederates. In groups of three genuine participants, a report occurred in 38% of groups. Note the denominator: the group result concerns whether a group produced a report, not simply 38% of individual participants. The pattern supports the influence of passive others on interpreting an ambiguous situation and deciding to act. It does not establish that all emergencies produce identical rates or that participants had no concern. The study helped explain group inhibition of intervention. Discuss social information and reluctance to react, and avoid substituting the different numerical findings from the seizure study.", "diagram": "smoke"}, {"title": "Smoke-filled room: evaluation and ethics", "kind": "learn", "text": "The controlled comparison of social conditions allowed investigation of the effect of passive others, while smoke offered an ambiguous situation that required interpretation. The student sample and artificial setting limit generalisation to different populations and emergencies. Participants were deceived about the purpose and could experience anxiety, raising informed-consent and protection-from-harm concerns. The researcher-created smoke should not be copied as a student experiment. A low-risk alternative could use hypothetical vignettes, although that changes what is measured: stated intention to help may differ from real behaviour. A useful evaluation connects the improvement to its limitation rather than claiming a questionnaire fully replaces evidence about actual action.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Several processes can delay action", "kind": "learn", "text": "Diffusion of responsibility reduces each person’s perceived obligation when others are present. Audience inhibition involves concern about embarrassment or judgement while responding. Social influence can lead people to interpret an ambiguous event through others’ apparent inaction. These mechanisms can coexist, but they are not synonyms. Name the scenario evidence: expecting someone else to act suggests diffusion; fear of looking foolish suggests inhibition; interpreting a calm group as evidence of no emergency suggests informational influence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use the smoke-filled-room study’s unit of analysis", "kind": "learn", "text": "Latané and Darley’s 1968 designated study exposed participants completing a questionnaire to smoke entering a room. Conditions included being alone, being with other naive participants and being with passive confederates. Reporting differed across these settings, supporting the role of others’ responses in interpreting an ambiguous emergency. Some reported proportions concern groups rather than each individual, so check the denominator before comparing. Do not substitute the separate seizure/intercom study when the syllabus asks about the smoke-filled room.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Apply the explanation without creating an emergency", "kind": "learn", "text": "An ethical application can clarify responsibility and reduce ambiguity, for example assigning a named person a specific safe task and identifying an appropriate authority to contact. It should not stage frightening emergencies without consent and review. Real emergencies vary in danger, competence and group relationships, and some crowds mobilise effective help. The bystander effect is a tendency in relevant situations, not proof that nobody helps when others are present. Evaluate both interpretation and practical ability to act.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Bystander and prosocial reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Bystander and prosocial reference</h3><p>Keep mechanisms distinct even when several influence one event.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Scenario clue</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Diffusion</td><td>Reduced personal responsibility</td><td>Someone else will act</td></tr><tr><td>Audience inhibition</td><td>Fear of judgement</td><td>I might look foolish</td></tr><tr><td>Social influence</td><td>Others inform interpretation</td><td>They are calm, so no problem</td></tr><tr><td>Cost–benefit</td><td>Perceived costs/benefits</td><td>Danger or embarrassment</td></tr><tr><td>Competence</td><td>Perceived ability to help</td><td>Relevant training</td></tr><tr><td>Reciprocity</td><td>Return/exchange of help</td><td>Returning favour</td></tr><tr><td>Responsibility</td><td>Obligation to assist need</td><td>No repayment needed</td></tr><tr><td>Empathy/altruism</td><td>Concern/motive without reward</td><td>Do not infer all motives from action</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In the smoke study, 75%/10% refer to individuals in the relevant conditions and 38% to whether a genuine three-person group reported.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Different bystander mechanisms", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Different bystander mechanisms", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Smoke-study denominators", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Smoke-study denominators", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Named responsibility", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Different bystander mechanisms", "prompt": "One observer assumes someone else will act; another fears looking foolish; another takes calm peers as evidence nothing is wrong. Identify and explain each process. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Diffusion of responsibility reduces personal obligation when others seem responsible.", "[2] Audience inhibition concerns embarrassment or negative evaluation.", "[2] Informational social influence uses others’ passivity to interpret the ambiguous event as non-serious."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-helping-q1"}, {"title": "Smoke-study denominators", "prompt": "Explain the 75%, 10% and 38% results in the designated smoke-filled-room study and one interpretation. (5 marks)", "steps": ["[1] About 75% of participants alone reported smoke.", "[1] About 10% with two passive confederates reported.", "[1] A report occurred in 38% of genuine three-person groups, a group rather than individual rate.", "[2] Passive others inhibited reporting and supported social interpretation/accountability explanations within the studied context."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 2], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-helping-q2"}, {"title": "Named responsibility", "prompt": "A fictional bystander is given a clear individual role rather than hearing a general request. Explain why this could influence helping and propose a low-risk test. (5 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Clear responsibility can reduce diffusion and increase perceived personal obligation.", "[1] Compare fictional general versus individually assigned request scenarios.", "[1] Measure privately stated helping intention with standardised wording.", "[1] Acknowledge that intention is not real emergency behaviour and protect voluntary participation."], "allocations": [2, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-helping-q4"}, {"id": "psy11-helping-bystanders-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student sees an ambiguous problem, assumes another bystander will act and fears looking foolish. Explain two distinct bystander mechanisms. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Expecting another person to act illustrates diffusion of responsibility.", "[2] Concern about looking foolish illustrates audience inhibition.", "[2] Distinguish reduced personal obligation from fear of judgement; they can coexist and are not interchangeable labels."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-helping-bystanders-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain the contribution of the 1968 smoke-filled-room study and evaluate one methodological and one ethical limitation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Responses across alone, naive-group and passive-confederate conditions showed that others’ inaction can affect interpretation/reporting of an ambiguous event.", "[2] Artificial smoke/questionnaire conditions and group-versus-person denominators limit simple generalisation/comparison.", "[2] Deception and potential distress require ethical evaluation; scientific influence does not justify staging unsafe classroom emergencies."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which statement illustrates diffusion of responsibility?", "o": ["Someone else here will probably handle it.", "I will look foolish if I act.", "Their calm response means there is no problem.", "I know exactly what to do and have accepted the task."], "a": 0, "why": "Diffusion concerns reduced personal obligation, distinct from embarrassment or interpretation of others’ reactions."}], "subtitle": "21 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Attitudes, stereotypes and social influence", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-helping-prosocial", "title": "Prosocial behaviour: norms, empathy and costs", "scope": "Prosocial behaviour and altruism are not identical · Costs include ability and risk · Measure helping without forcing a motive", "parent": "psy11-helping", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Prosocial behaviour: norms, empathy and costs</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Prosocial behaviour and altruism are not identical</li><li>Costs include ability and risk</li><li>Measure helping without forcing a motive</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Prosocial and antisocial behaviour", "kind": "learn", "text": "Prosocial behaviour is action intended to benefit others, such as helping, sharing or cooperating. Antisocial behaviour harms others or violates important social norms, such as bullying or deliberate intimidation. A behaviour’s context and consequences matter. Helping may arise from empathy, responsibility, expected reciprocity or a mixture of motives, so visible helping does not prove pure altruism. Altruism involves benefiting another without expectation of personal reward. A scientific account examines factors that make helping or harm more likely rather than labelling a whole person permanently good or bad. Use fictional situations and respectful descriptions. Avoid confusing antisocial behaviour with simply preferring solitude; social withdrawal and intentional harm are different concepts.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Cost-benefit analysis and competence", "kind": "learn", "text": "A bystander may weigh perceived costs of helping, such as personal danger or social embarrassment, against benefits for the other person and costs of not acting. This need not involve a conscious numerical calculation. Perceived competence also matters: someone trained in first aid may feel more able to respond appropriately than someone unsure what to do. Context can change these perceptions. A person may choose to seek qualified help rather than intervene directly in a dangerous situation. Explain how the perceived cost or competence changes the likelihood or form of helping. Do not equate declining direct physical intervention with total absence of prosocial behaviour when reporting or obtaining help is possible.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reciprocity, responsibility, empathy and mood", "kind": "learn", "text": "The reciprocity principle is a norm of returning help received or expecting cooperative exchange. Social responsibility is a norm of helping people who need assistance, even when direct repayment is unlikely. Empathy involves understanding or sharing another person’s perspective and feelings and can motivate concern for their welfare. Positive mood can sometimes promote helping, while effects depend on context. Perceived competence affects whether someone thinks their help will be effective. These factors are distinct: returning a favour illustrates reciprocity, whereas helping a vulnerable stranger because of obligation illustrates responsibility. Explain the scenario evidence and mechanism. Prosocial action can have mixed motives, so do not infer altruism merely because a person did something helpful.", "diagram": "helping"}, {"title": "Designing an ethical helping investigation", "kind": "learn", "text": "An ethical helping inquiry could ask how a clear request affects responses to fictional low-risk scenarios, with voluntary participation and anonymous answers. Specify the independent condition, such as a general request versus one directed to a named fictional observer, and the dependent measure, such as the stated likelihood of reporting a problem. Standardise scenario length and seriousness. Such a design tests reported intention, not real emergency behaviour, so external validity is limited. A good conclusion refers to that measure and sample. Incorporate the theory by predicting reduced diffusion when responsibility is clear. Evaluation should acknowledge social-desirability responding and propose a suitable improvement without creating a real dangerous emergency or placing students under distress.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Empathy and altruism are not interchangeable", "kind": "learn", "text": "Empathy can motivate helping by making another person’s experience salient. Altruism concerns the absence of expected personal reward in the helping motive. A person can empathise yet decide they lack the capacity to help, and another can help partly for recognition. The visible act does not reveal every motive. Reciprocity involves exchanged obligations and should not be defined as always having no expectation of return. Distinguish mechanism, motive and action in an explanation. Prosocial behaviour includes a range of beneficial actions, including seeking appropriate help rather than attempting a dangerous intervention personally. Context and perceived competence influence the form of response.", "diagram": "helping"}, {"title": "Prosocial behaviour and altruism are not identical", "kind": "learn", "text": "Prosocial behaviour benefits others, while altruism concerns helping motivated principally by concern for others rather than personal gain. The same helpful action can arise from empathy, duty, reciprocity, reputation or several motives. Observing the action alone does not identify its motive conclusively. Empathy involves understanding or sharing another person’s perspective or feelings; it can support helping without proving that every empathic person acts or that all helping is purely altruistic.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Costs include ability and risk", "kind": "learn", "text": "A cost-benefit account considers perceived costs of helping and not helping, including time, danger, effort and emotional consequences. Competence affects whether a person sees action as feasible. Someone may care strongly but lack the ability to perform a risky rescue; seeking suitable assistance can be a prosocial response. Reciprocity concerns returning benefits, while social responsibility concerns helping those who need support. Explain which norm or cost is present rather than assuming all help is a reward-seeking transaction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Measure helping without forcing a motive", "kind": "learn", "text": "An ethical study might offer a voluntary low-risk opportunity to help with a neutral task and record a defined action. A follow-up report can provide information about reasons, but social desirability and retrospective explanation remain. If participants know that generosity is being assessed, demand characteristics may affect the action. Consent, privacy and withdrawal still matter. Combine behaviour and relevant reports where useful, and restrict conclusions to the specific opportunity rather than ranking participants as good or bad people.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Reciprocity and altruism", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Reciprocity and altruism", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Helping measures and validity", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Reciprocity and altruism", "prompt": "A person returns a favour to a friend; another helps anonymously with no expected reward. Compare reciprocity and altruism. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Returning a favour fits the reciprocity norm of exchanged help.", "[2] Anonymous benefit without expected reward can illustrate altruism, though actual motives cannot always be inferred from behaviour alone."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy11-helping-q3"}, {"title": "Helping measures and validity", "prompt": "Researchers use anonymous intention ratings to claim people will definitely act in danger. Evaluate and give a cautious conclusion. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Intention ratings measure reported likelihood, not actual emergency action.", "[1] Social desirability and missing real costs can affect answers.", "[1] External/construct validity for dangerous behaviour is therefore limited.", "[1] Conclude only that the scenario influenced stated intention in the sampled participants, with further ethical evidence needed."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-helping-q6"}, {"id": "psy11-helping-prosocial-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student helps a classmate who previously helped them, while another assists an unfamiliar person because they need support. Explain reciprocity and social responsibility. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Returning earlier help fits the reciprocity norm.", "[2] Helping someone because they need support fits the social-responsibility norm.", "[2] Both are prosocial actions, but their observed form does not establish purely altruistic motives or exclude mixed reasons."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-helping-prosocial-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A person does not enter dangerous water but immediately alerts trained rescuers. Evaluate the claim that this proves a lack of empathy. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Competence and danger affect the perceived cost and feasibility of direct intervention.", "[2] Alerting appropriate rescuers can itself be a prosocial action.", "[2] Empathy/motives cannot be inferred conclusively from refusal of one unsafe action; distinguish concern, ability and behaviour."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which statement best distinguishes prosocial behaviour from altruism?", "o": ["Prosocial describes benefit to others; altruism concerns the motive for helping.", "Every visible helping act proves a purely selfless motive.", "Empathy guarantees immediate helping in every setting.", "Requesting competent assistance can never be prosocial."], "a": 0, "why": "Behaviour and motivation are related but different levels of explanation."}], "subtitle": "17 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Attitudes, stereotypes and social influence", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy11-helping-harm", "title": "Groupthink, bullying and antisocial group decisions", "scope": "Groupthink is about decision processes · Bullying can be socially reinforced · Improve the process and evaluate outcomes", "parent": "psy11-helping", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Groupthink, bullying and antisocial group decisions</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Groupthink is about decision processes</li><li>Bullying can be socially reinforced</li><li>Improve the process and evaluate outcomes</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Groupthink and antisocial group decisions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Groupthink involves pressure for agreement within a cohesive decision-making group that weakens critical evaluation of alternatives. Members may suppress doubts, assume unanimity or dismiss outside criticism to maintain harmony. This can contribute to poor or harmful decisions but is different from simply any group discussion or disagreement. A group planning an unkind prank may ignore one member’s concerns because dissent is treated as disloyal. An improvement is a process that invites independent views and evaluates risks, rather than merely making the group larger. Explain the pressure for agreement and its effect on judgement. Groupthink is a decision process; diffusion of responsibility concerns personal obligation and deindividuation concerns identity or accountability.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Bullying and social reinforcement", "kind": "learn", "text": "Bullying involves harmful behaviour associated with a power imbalance and repetition or potential repetition. Social processes can maintain it when observers laugh, remain passive or treat it as normal. Group norms, pressure for acceptance and reduced personal responsibility may discourage intervention. A psychological explanation should describe those processes without blaming the targeted person or implying that every bystander endorses harm. A prosocial response can include supporting the target and seeking appropriate adult help, using a safe and clear reporting route. For research, surveys need privacy and protection because identifiable disclosures can expose students to further harm. Course activities should use fictional cases or approved evidence rather than asking classmates to disclose personal bullying experiences.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Groupthink is about decision processes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Groupthink involves impaired critical evaluation in a cohesive decision-making group, especially when agreement is prioritised and dissent is discouraged. Symptoms can include pressure on dissenters, self-censorship, illusions of unanimity and overconfidence. Cohesion alone does not guarantee groupthink: a supportive group can encourage scrutiny. The key evidence concerns how alternatives, risks and disagreements are handled. A unanimous correct decision is not automatically groupthink, and a mistaken decision can have causes other than the group process.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Bullying can be socially reinforced", "kind": "learn", "text": "Antisocial behaviour can be supported when peers reward it through laughter, attention or status, and when bystanders fear exclusion. Explaining these influences does not excuse harm or imply that every bystander has the same motive. Distinguish active reinforcement, passive observation and efforts to seek safe support. Effective responses can change norms, remove rewards and provide clear reporting/support pathways. A student lesson uses fictional cases and does not require disclosure of personal experiences to demonstrate understanding.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Improve the process and evaluate outcomes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Inviting independent views before a discussion, appointing a genuine critical reviewer and considering disconfirming evidence can reduce pressures towards premature consensus. In a school scenario these should be respectful roles rather than permission to target people. Anti-bullying outcomes need evidence of safety and behaviour, not just a public promise. Changes in reporting can reflect increased willingness to disclose as well as changed incidence. Explain what is measured and use several appropriate indicators before concluding that a policy has eliminated the problem.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read, apply and qualify the focused model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read each label and distinguish the relationships or levels of explanation. Describe the pattern in your own words, then use the accompanying theory to explain why it matters. Apply the model to the written questions in this lesson and state its limit rather than treating an illustration as proof. Where a diagram uses fictional numbers or arbitrary units, keep those separate from the findings of a designated study.", "diagram": "groupthink-loop"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Groupthink", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: helping and harm", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Groupthink", "prompt": "A cohesive group dismisses all objections to a harmful plan to avoid disagreement. Explain groupthink and one improvement. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Pressure for consensus suppresses critical appraisal and alternatives, producing an appearance of unanimity.", "[2] Invite independent views and structured risk evaluation, so dissent is considered rather than treated as disloyalty."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-helping-q5"}, {"title": "Extended response: helping and harm", "prompt": "Explain bystander mechanisms, the exact 1968 smoke study and prosocial factors. Evaluate evidence and propose a safe intervention scenario. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Separates diffusion, audience inhibition and social interpretation.", "[2] Describes smoke, questionnaires and alone/passive-confederate/genuine-group conditions.", "[2] Gives findings with appropriate individual/group denominators.", "[2] Applies empathy, responsibility, reciprocity or competence to helping.", "[2] Evaluates deception/distress and sample/context limits.", "[2] Proposes clear responsibility or competence support and a safe measure without creating an emergency."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy11-helping-q7"}, {"id": "psy11-helping-harm-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A committee dismisses warnings, pressures a dissenting member and claims silence proves unanimous support. Explain two groupthink processes and one prevention strategy. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Pressure on the dissenter discourages critical evaluation; silence may reflect self-censorship.", "[2] Treating silence as support creates an illusion of unanimity and hides alternatives.", "[2] Gather independent views and explicitly examine risks/contrary evidence before deciding, supporting respectful dissent."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy11-helping-harm-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Reports of bullying rise after a new confidential reporting system. Evaluate the conclusion that bullying itself must have increased. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] More reports may reflect improved trust/accessibility and disclosure rather than only more incidents.", "[2] Reports are one measure and can be affected by awareness, definitions and participation.", "[2] Compare suitable independent indicators and consistent periods/definitions while maintaining privacy and support; avoid a certain causal claim from reports alone."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What most strongly indicates groupthink?", "o": ["Suppressing dissent while treating silence as unanimous agreement.", "A cohesive group openly evaluates contrary evidence.", "A group reaches a correct decision after independent review.", "Every decision made by more than one person."], "a": 0, "why": "Groupthink concerns impaired scrutiny and pressure for agreement, not group membership alone."}, {"q": "Assuming another observer will act concerns what?", "o": ["Diffusion of responsibility", "Audience inhibition alone", "Seriation", "Transduction"], "a": 0, "why": "Personal obligation is diluted."}, {"q": "Fear of looking foolish concerns what?", "o": ["Audience inhibition", "Pure motor inability", "Relearning", "A ratio schedule"], "a": 0, "why": "The concern is others’ evaluation."}, {"q": "Which designated 1968 study belongs here?", "o": ["Smoke-filled room", "Only seizure intercom", "Bobo modelling", "Forced compliance payment"], "a": 0, "why": "The syllabus specifies smoke conditions, not the separate emergency study."}, {"q": "Returning a favour illustrates what?", "o": ["Reciprocity", "All altruism by definition", "Conservation", "Myelination"], "a": 0, "why": "The norm concerns exchanged helping."}, {"q": "Groupthink weakens what?", "o": ["Critical evaluation under pressure for consensus", "All memory storage", "Only hearing", "Every cooperative action"], "a": 0, "why": "Harmony pressure can suppress alternatives and concerns."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-memory-models-senses", "title": "Sensation, perception and attention", "scope": "Sensation provides input; perception gives it meaning · Attention limits what is processed · Interpretation uses context and prior experience", "parent": "psy12-memory-models", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Sensation, perception and attention</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Sensation provides input; perception gives it meaning</li><li>Attention limits what is processed</li><li>Interpretation uses context and prior experience</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Sensation: reception, transduction and transmission", "kind": "learn", "text": "Sensation begins when receptors detect stimulation. Reception is detection of physical energy or chemical stimulation by a suitable receptor. Transduction converts that stimulation into neural activity. Transmission carries information along neural pathways towards the CNS. For hearing, sound vibrations contribute to activation of receptors in the inner ear, which convert the stimulation into neural signals travelling through auditory pathways. Hearing the sound is not yet the same as understanding a spoken sentence. Keep each process distinct and connect it to the scenario. These terms describe a useful sequence within an interacting system rather than three independent conscious decisions. A person can receive stimulation but fail to interpret it in the intended way because attention, experience or context differs.", "diagram": "sensation"}, {"title": "Perception: selection, organisation and interpretation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Perception involves processing sensory information into meaningful experience. Selection prioritises aspects of the available information. Organisation arranges features into patterns or relationships. Interpretation assigns meaning using incoming information and prior knowledge. A student may select a voice in a busy room, organise its sounds into a word and interpret it as their name. The process is affected by context and expectations, so people can interpret similar stimulation differently. Perception is not a camera-like copy of the environment. In an exam response, distinguish sensory transduction from giving meaning to the signal. State the specific feature selected, pattern organised and meaning interpreted, rather than repeating that the brain notices something for all three terms.", "diagram": "sensation"}, {"title": "Attention and what reaches memory", "kind": "learn", "text": "Attention allocates processing resources to selected information and therefore influences what is encoded. Being exposed to a page does not mean every detail is stored. Dividing attention between messages and revision can reduce effective processing and later memory. Attention is included here to explain the models and everyday application; the 2026 Year 12 syllabus no longer lists the former separate Cherry cocktail-party study dot point. Students should focus on the current specified content. An ethical study of attention might compare performance on a low-risk task with and without a secondary task, holding exposure constant. Remember that a lower memory score can reflect disrupted encoding or retrieval, so the design and timing matter to interpretation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Sensation provides input; perception gives it meaning", "kind": "learn", "text": "Reception detects stimulus energy through sensory receptors; transduction converts it into neural activity; transmission carries information through relevant pathways. Perception selects, organises and interprets information. A person can receive a visual stimulus yet fail to attend to it or interpret it correctly. Sensation and perception therefore contribute different processes rather than describing two unrelated events. In an example, identify the stimulus, the receptor response and the interpretation instead of calling the whole experience sensation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Attention limits what is processed", "kind": "learn", "text": "Selective attention prioritises some information amid competing input. Dividing attention between a conversation and a demanding visual task can reduce what is encoded, even when the sensory organs function normally. A later failure to remember may therefore reflect inadequate encoding rather than loss of a fully stored memory. Task demands, expectations and salience affect selection. A useful answer traces attention into memory rather than assuming every stimulus reaching a receptor enters long-term storage automatically.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Interpretation uses context and prior experience", "kind": "learn", "text": "Perception organises features into meaningful patterns and interprets them using context and knowledge. Ambiguous marks may be read differently when surrounded by different letters or numbers. This illustrates interpretation, not evidence that receptors detect entirely different physical energy. A controlled task can vary context while holding the target constant, with clear measurement of the reported judgement. Interpretation data still require consideration of demand characteristics and task familiarity, and one perceptual effect does not prove that all memory is inaccurate.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Sensation versus perception", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Sensation versus perception", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Sensation versus perception", "prompt": "A student detects a sound, hears their name and understands that a friend is calling. Explain sensation and perception using their three processes. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Reception detects sound-related stimulation at auditory receptors.", "[1] Transduction converts stimulation to neural activity.", "[1] Transmission carries signals along auditory pathways.", "[1] Selection prioritises the relevant voice.", "[1] Organisation arranges sound features into a word.", "[1] Interpretation assigns meaning as the student’s name/call in context."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-memory-models-q1"}, {"id": "psy12-memory-models-senses-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student looks directly at a diagram while listening to a difficult conversation and later cannot describe the diagram. Explain sensation, attention and a possible memory consequence. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Light may be received/transduced/transmitted normally even though the diagram is not prioritised.", "[2] Attention to the demanding conversation can reduce selection/processing of diagram information.", "[2] Poor subsequent description may reflect inadequate encoding, not necessarily deterioration of a previously stored long-term memory."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-memory-models-senses-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Design a fictional low-risk comparison testing whether surrounding letters or numbers change interpretation of an identical ambiguous symbol. Identify an IV, DV and control. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] IV: surrounding context, operationalised as letters versus numbers while retaining the same target symbol.", "[2] DV: recorded interpretation/category of the target under a defined response procedure.", "[2] Control target appearance, viewing time and instructions so that a context explanation is clearer; random allocation/counterbalancing may address participant/order differences."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which statement best describes perception?", "o": ["Selecting, organising and interpreting sensory information.", "Only detecting stimulus energy at receptors.", "Automatically placing every stimulus in long-term memory.", "A guarantee that attention never affects recall."], "a": 0, "why": "Perception adds organisation and meaning to sensory input; attention helps determine what is processed."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-memory-models-stores", "title": "Encoding and the multi-store memory model", "scope": "The model separates stores from control processes · Capacity, duration and encoding require qualifications · Rehearsal is not a universal transfer guarantee", "parent": "psy12-memory-models", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Encoding and the multi-store memory model</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>The model separates stores from control processes</li><li>Capacity, duration and encoding require qualifications</li><li>Rehearsal is not a universal transfer guarantee</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Encoding, storage and retrieval", "kind": "learn", "text": "Encoding transforms information into a form that can be retained, such as a representation of sound, meaning or visual features. Storage is retention of information over time. Retrieval brings stored information into usable awareness or performance. Forgetting can arise from difficulty at any of these processes: a student may not attend during encoding, may have weak retention or may lack a useful cue at retrieval. Correct recognition after an earlier recall failure suggests the information may still be accessible with support. These are functional processes, not literal filing cabinets. Explain the stage relevant to the scenario and the evidence supporting it. Reading repeatedly without testing retrieval can create familiarity without demonstrating that the answer can be produced independently.", "diagram": "memoryprocess"}, {"title": "Atkinson and Shiffrin (1968): multi-store model", "kind": "learn", "text": "The multi-store model represents memory through a sensory register, short-term memory and long-term memory, with control processes such as attention and rehearsal influencing movement and retention. The sensory register briefly holds information from sensory input. Selected information can enter short-term memory. Rehearsal and other processing can support long-term retention, while retrieval brings stored material back into active use. The model helped organise evidence about different durations and capacities. Arrows represent processes rather than anatomical tubes. Its simple sequence does not fully explain the different components of working memory or why meaning can matter more than repetition. A strong explanation describes each store’s features and the process connecting stores, then evaluates where the model oversimplifies.", "diagram": "multistore"}, {"title": "Sensory register: iconic and echoic memory", "kind": "learn", "text": "Sensory memory has a large capacity for a very short period and preserves modality-specific information. Iconic memory concerns visual information and typically lasts a fraction of a second. Echoic memory concerns auditory information and can persist for a few seconds. These are approximate laboratory descriptions, not fixed countdowns for every situation. Most information is rapidly lost unless selected for further processing. A brief visual after-effect does not mean the entire scene is a permanent photograph. Hearing the last words of a sentence after attention shifts can illustrate short auditory persistence. Compare encoding, capacity and duration for both forms. When using a number, state its approximate nature and avoid confusing seconds of sensory persistence with minutes of short-term rehearsal.", "diagram": "stores"}, {"title": "Short-term memory and chunking", "kind": "learn", "text": "Short-term memory has limited capacity and brief duration, often described in classic accounts as around fifteen to thirty seconds without rehearsal and roughly seven plus or minus two items. These classic estimates depend on task and chunking; modern capacity estimates can be smaller. Encoding is often acoustic for verbal material, but is not exclusively auditory. Chunking groups elements into meaningful units, increasing how much information a person can handle within a limited number of units. Remembering a familiar date as one unit differs from treating each digit as unrelated. Do not claim that everyone can always hold exactly seven words. Explain how limited capacity and brief duration affect the scenario and why rehearsal or meaningful grouping changes performance.", "diagram": "stores"}, {"title": "The model separates stores from control processes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Atkinson and Shiffrin’s 1968 multi-store model distinguishes sensory, short-term and long-term stores, with attention, rehearsal and retrieval influencing information flow. Encoding changes information into a usable representation; storage retains it; retrieval accesses it. These are processes, not three additional physical boxes. The model makes relationships visible but does not establish that the brain literally contains three isolated containers. Explain the movement and relevant process for the scenario rather than copying arrows without their functions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Capacity, duration and encoding require qualifications", "kind": "learn", "text": "Sensory memory is brief and high-capacity, with iconic visual traces usually shorter than echoic auditory traces. Conventional classroom descriptions give STM roughly 7±2 items and around 18–30 seconds without rehearsal, while newer task-dependent estimates and chunking illustrate why these are not fixed universal limits. LTM has a very large capacity and potentially long duration, with multiple forms of encoding. When using estimates identify them as approximate model descriptions and connect attention or rehearsal to the conditions under which performance is measured.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Rehearsal is not a universal transfer guarantee", "kind": "learn", "text": "Maintenance rehearsal can keep information active, but repeating it does not always establish rich long-term learning. Meaning, organisation and existing knowledge influence encoding and retrieval. A person who repeats a temporary code may use it briefly without remembering it tomorrow. This is a limitation of a simplistic rehearsal-equals-LTM account, not proof that repetition is never useful. Compare what the model explains with evidence for distinct memory types and active working-memory processes when evaluating its explanatory breadth.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Three stores", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Three stores", "prompt": "Compare sensory, short-term and long-term memory in duration, capacity and encoding, acknowledging approximate estimates. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Sensory: large brief modality-specific trace, iconic fraction of a second and echoic a few seconds.", "[2] Short-term: limited capacity and brief duration without rehearsal, often acoustic for verbal tasks but not exclusive.", "[2] Long-term: very large capacity and potentially years of retention, with varied including semantic encoding; estimates are not universal fixed limits."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-memory-models-q4"}, {"id": "psy12-memory-models-stores-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student repeats a short code until entering it, then cannot recall it the next day. Explain the multi-store interpretation and why repetition did not guarantee durable learning. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Attention brings the code into short-term processing, and maintenance rehearsal can keep it active temporarily.", "[2] Use during a short delay does not establish lasting long-term storage accessible the next day.", "[2] Richer encoding and retrieval conditions matter; maintenance repetition alone does not guarantee durable learning."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-memory-models-stores-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare sensory memory and short-term memory in duration, capacity and the role of attention, with suitable qualifications. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Sensory traces are very brief and relatively high-capacity; iconic and echoic duration differ.", "[2] STM is limited in duration without rehearsal and capacity, with estimates depending on task and chunking.", "[2] Attention selects information for further processing; the model is a simplification rather than literal isolated brain containers."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What does rehearsal most directly support in a brief code task?", "o": ["Keeping information active for immediate use.", "Guaranteed permanent storage after any repetition.", "Unlimited short-term capacity.", "Conversion of the whole sensory environment into memory."], "a": 0, "why": "Maintenance rehearsal can sustain immediate information but does not ensure rich durable long-term learning."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-memory-models-long-term", "title": "Long-term memory: implicit and explicit systems", "scope": "Explicit memory can be reported consciously · Implicit memory is expressed through performance · Multiple systems improve the explanation", "parent": "psy12-memory-models", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Long-term memory: implicit and explicit systems</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Explicit memory can be reported consciously</li><li>Implicit memory is expressed through performance</li><li>Multiple systems improve the explanation</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Long-term memory and its types", "kind": "learn", "text": "Long-term memory has a very large capacity and can retain information from minutes to many years. It supports different forms of knowledge and experience, with varied encoding including semantic, visual and acoustic aspects. Explicit, or declarative, memory involves consciously accessible information. Semantic memory includes facts and general knowledge; episodic memory includes personally experienced events in a context. Implicit memory influences behaviour without requiring conscious recollection; procedural memory concerns learned skills and routines. Knowing a definition is semantic, recalling learning it in a particular lesson is episodic and performing a practised movement is procedural. These examples can overlap in one activity. Distinguish the type of information retrieved rather than classifying solely by whether the person remembers something for a long time.", "diagram": "memorytypes"}, {"title": "Explicit memory can be reported consciously", "kind": "learn", "text": "Semantic memory concerns facts and general knowledge, such as knowing a capital city. Episodic memory concerns personally experienced events situated in time and place, such as remembering a visit there. Both are forms of explicit or declarative memory. The same topic can involve both: knowing a fact is different from remembering the occasion on which it was learned. Identify the content and kind of access rather than classifying every personal topic as episodic automatically.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Implicit memory is expressed through performance", "kind": "learn", "text": "Procedural memory involves learned skills and routines, often demonstrated without a detailed conscious explanation of each step. Riding a bicycle can draw on procedural learning even when a person cannot describe every movement. Conscious attention can still interact with a skill, so implicit does not mean completely independent of all awareness. A participant may improve on a task while failing to remember having practised it, demonstrating a dissociation between performance-based learning and explicit recollection rather than a total absence of memory.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Multiple systems improve the explanation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Separate long-term forms help explain why some abilities remain when others are impaired, challenging treatment of LTM as a single uniform store. However, ordinary tasks combine systems: following a familiar route may involve procedural actions, factual knowledge and an episodic memory of a previous trip. Brain networks interact, and no one behavioural example identifies every neural source. Compare the kind of information, how retention is demonstrated and the relevant evidence before making claims about a separate system.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Types of memory", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Types of memory", "prompt": "Classify knowing a fact, recalling a specific picnic and performing a practised skill. Explain why the categories differ. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Fact knowledge is explicit semantic memory.", "[2] A particular experienced picnic is explicit episodic memory.", "[2] A practised skill is procedural implicit memory; categories differ by content/access rather than simply duration."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-memory-models-q5"}, {"id": "psy12-memory-models-long-term-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A person knows the rules of tennis, remembers their first match and performs a well-practised serve. Classify the three examples and explain each. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Knowing rules is semantic explicit memory for facts/general knowledge.", "[2] Remembering a particular first match is episodic explicit memory for a personally experienced event.", "[2] A practised serve draws on procedural implicit memory expressed through skilled performance; systems can interact."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-memory-models-long-term-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A participant improves a practiced skill but cannot remember the practice sessions. Explain the memory dissociation and why “no memory exists” is an incorrect conclusion. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Improved performance provides evidence of procedural/implicit learning.", "[2] Lack of remembered sessions concerns episodic/explicit recollection, which can be affected separately.", "[2] Distinct systems can show different retention, so failure of conscious recall does not establish absence of all memory."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Knowing a scientific definition principally illustrates what?", "o": ["Semantic explicit memory.", "A specific personal episode by definition.", "Only an involuntary reflex.", "Procedural skill with no factual content."], "a": 0, "why": "Semantic memory concerns facts and meanings; episodic memory concerns particular experiences."}], "subtitle": "11 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-memory-models-working", "title": "Working memory: four components and task competition", "scope": "Working memory involves processing as well as holding · Predict competition from shared resources · Integration helps explain meaningful temporary episodes", "parent": "psy12-memory-models", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Working memory: four components and task competition</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Working memory involves processing as well as holding</li><li>Predict competition from shared resources</li><li>Integration helps explain meaningful temporary episodes</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Baddeley and Hitch (1974): working memory", "kind": "learn", "text": "The working memory model explains active short-term maintenance and manipulation through interacting components. The central executive coordinates attention and processing rather than serving as a large storage box. The phonological loop maintains and rehearses verbal or sound-based information. The visuospatial sketchpad maintains and manipulates visual and spatial information. Tasks can compete when they place demands on the same component, while some simultaneous tasks are easier when demands differ. Mentally repeating a phone number relies strongly on verbal maintenance; imagining a route relies more on visuospatial processing. The model explains active task performance better than a single undifferentiated short-term store, but component boundaries and precise executive operations can be difficult to measure directly.", "diagram": "workingmemory"}, {"title": "Baddeley (2000): episodic buffer", "kind": "learn", "text": "The episodic buffer was added to the working memory model in 2000. It is a limited-capacity temporary system that binds information from different sources into integrated representations and links working memory with long-term knowledge. Remembering a short scene can combine visual layout, spoken words and prior understanding. This integration is different from the phonological loop rehearsing sound or the visuospatial sketchpad maintaining spatial features. The central executive helps coordinate use of the components. The buffer is a theoretical component, not a separate visible brain chamber. An application should state what information is being combined and how long-term knowledge contributes. Compare the original 1974 model with the later addition rather than incorrectly assigning all four components to the first version.", "diagram": "workingmemory"}, {"title": "Interference between working-memory tasks", "kind": "learn", "text": "Two simultaneous tasks can compete when they heavily demand the same component. Repeating a verbal sequence while processing unrelated spoken material may strain phonological maintenance. Maintaining a spatial layout while imagining a moving route may strain visuospatial processing. Some mixed-modality tasks interfere less, though the central executive still has limited coordination resources. This is a model-based prediction, not proof that the components are entirely separate anatomical organs. A test needs matched difficulty and appropriate controls, because one task may simply be harder. Describe the demands of both tasks and the expected performance outcome rather than assuming any two different activities never interfere.", "diagram": "workingmemory"}, {"title": "Working memory involves processing as well as holding", "kind": "learn", "text": "Baddeley and Hitch’s 1974 model describes a central executive coordinating attention, a phonological loop for speech-related material and a visuospatial sketchpad for visual-spatial information. Baddeley’s 2000 episodic buffer addition integrates information into temporary, multidimensional representations and links with long-term memory. The central executive is not an unlimited storage box, and the episodic buffer is not identical to episodic long-term memory. Keep the model versions and the components’ functions distinct in exam answers.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Predict competition from shared resources", "kind": "learn", "text": "Remembering spoken words while repeating an irrelevant syllable can interfere with speech-based rehearsal. A demanding visual tracking task can compete with visual-spatial maintenance. Two tasks sharing relevant resources may interfere more than a suitably matched pair using different subsystems, though executive attention and task difficulty still matter. The model does not predict that any two different modalities can be performed perfectly. Explain the task demand and proposed component before predicting performance, then identify the control needed for a fair comparison.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Integration helps explain meaningful temporary episodes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Following a story can involve phonological information, imagined settings and stored knowledge, brought together into a coherent temporary representation. The episodic buffer supports the integration account, while executive control directs attention and selects relevant processing. These are functional explanations inferred from evidence, not directly photographed containers. Evaluation can recognise better explanations of active short-term cognition than a single passive store, alongside uncertainty about detailed mechanisms and limits of highly controlled tasks.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Memory model comparison reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Memory model comparison reference</h3><p>Store estimates are approximate and task-dependent; working memory concerns active processing.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Capacity/duration</th><th>Processing example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Iconic register</td><td>Large, fraction of second</td><td>Brief visual trace</td></tr><tr><td>Echoic register</td><td>Large, a few seconds</td><td>Brief sound trace</td></tr><tr><td>Short-term store</td><td>Limited, seconds without rehearsal</td><td>Active verbal retention</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term systems</td><td>Very large, potentially years</td><td>Facts/events/skills</td></tr><tr><td>Central executive</td><td>Limited coordination</td><td>Allocates attention</td></tr><tr><td>Phonological loop</td><td>Limited verbal maintenance</td><td>Repeated directions</td></tr><tr><td>Visuospatial sketchpad</td><td>Limited visual/spatial</td><td>Mental route</td></tr><tr><td>Episodic buffer</td><td>Limited integration</td><td>Binds sources with long-term knowledge</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A theoretical component is not a literal box in the brain. Distinguish semantic facts, episodic events and procedural skills.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Working-memory components", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Working-memory components", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Working-memory components", "prompt": "A learner mentally repeats directions, imagines a route and combines them with a known map. Apply the four components and identify the later addition. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Phonological loop maintains/rehearses verbal directions.", "[1] Visuospatial sketchpad maintains/manipulates the imagined route.", "[1] Central executive coordinates attention and task processing.", "[2] Episodic buffer binds sources with long-term map knowledge into an integrated representation.", "[1] The buffer was added by Baddeley in 2000 to the 1974 model."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 2, 1], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-memory-models-q2"}, {"id": "psy12-memory-models-working-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student holds a route map in mind while hearing spoken directions and relating them to a familiar street. Apply the sketchpad, loop, buffer and central executive. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The visuospatial sketchpad supports visual-spatial route information; the phonological loop supports speech-related directions.", "[2] The episodic buffer integrates this temporary information with relevant long-term knowledge.", "[2] The central executive coordinates attention and processing rather than acting as unlimited storage."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-memory-models-working-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Predict and evaluate the effect of an articulatory task on remembering spoken word sequences. Include one control and one limitation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Repeating irrelevant speech can compete with phonological rehearsal and reduce retention of speech-based sequences.", "[2] Match exposure, sequence difficulty and timing, with a suitable comparison task/condition.", "[2] General attention and task difficulty may also contribute; interference is evidence for a functional account, not direct observation of a physical storage box."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which component was added in Baddeley’s 2000 model?", "o": ["The episodic buffer.", "The original sensory register from the multi-store model.", "A second cerebellum.", "An unlimited central-executive store."], "a": 0, "why": "The buffer integrates temporary multidimensional information and connects with long-term knowledge."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-memory-models-brain", "title": "Hippocampus, cerebellum, amygdala and Henry Molaison", "scope": "The hippocampus supports formation and consolidation · Cerebellar and amygdala contributions are different · A dissociation is stronger than a total-loss claim", "parent": "psy12-memory-models", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Hippocampus, cerebellum, amygdala and Henry Molaison</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>The hippocampus supports formation and consolidation</li><li>Cerebellar and amygdala contributions are different</li><li>A dissociation is stronger than a total-loss claim</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Hippocampus, cerebellum and amygdala", "kind": "learn", "text": "The hippocampus contributes to forming and consolidating new explicit memories and linking features of experience. Long-term memories depend on distributed systems, so it is misleading to call the hippocampus the permanent store of every memory. The cerebellum contributes to some implicit learning, including aspects of skilled movement and conditioned motor responses, while other implicit skills involve additional systems. The amygdala processes emotional significance and can modulate the strength of memory for emotionally important events. Emotional intensity does not guarantee perfect accuracy. In an application, identify the memory process and relevant structure, then recognise that they interact. Knowing where a contribution occurs is different from showing that one structure alone explains the full behaviour.", "diagram": "memorybrain"}, {"title": "Henry Molaison: evidence for distinct memory systems", "kind": "learn", "text": "Henry Molaison, known historically as H.M., underwent surgery in 1953 that removed parts of his medial temporal lobes, including major hippocampal regions, to treat severe epilepsy. Afterwards he had profound difficulty forming new long-term explicit memories, while short-term retention under some conditions and improvement on some procedural tasks were relatively preserved. His improvement at a mirror-drawing task without remembering prior practice helped distinguish skill learning from conscious recollection. This case contributed to understanding memory systems and consolidation. It was a clinical case rather than a controlled experiment on hippocampal removal alone, so the extent of surgery and unusual history limit precise generalisation. Avoid claiming that he had no memory at all or that all old memories were equally intact.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "The hippocampus supports formation and consolidation", "kind": "learn", "text": "The hippocampus contributes to formation and consolidation of new explicit memories and their relationship to distributed cortical systems. It is not a permanent container for every lifelong memory. Henry Molaison’s surgery involving medial temporal structures was followed by severe difficulty forming new explicit memories, while some immediate memory and skill learning were relatively preserved. This dissociation supported distinctions between systems. The operation involved more than a perfectly isolated single structure, so a complete causal map cannot be inferred from the case alone.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Cerebellar and amygdala contributions are different", "kind": "learn", "text": "The cerebellum contributes to aspects of motor/procedural learning and conditioned responses. The amygdala contributes to emotional significance and modulation of memory, including effects of arousal. Neither structure works alone or stores every memory of its associated type. Emotion can influence encoding and consolidation but does not guarantee accurate recollection of every detail. In a scenario distinguish learning a skill, forming an explicit episode and the emotional salience of that episode, then link the relevant contribution to the observed evidence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A dissociation is stronger than a total-loss claim", "kind": "learn", "text": "Molaison could improve on some skill tasks despite limited explicit recollection of practising them. This suggests that memory is not a single all-or-nothing ability. Intensive case observation provides valuable unusual evidence but has limits in generalisation and control. An appropriate answer states the preserved and affected functions, explains their theoretical significance and qualifies anatomical or population claims. Avoid asserting that hippocampal damage removes every memory, that procedural improvement proves intact episodic recall or that one case perfectly represents all patients.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Henry Molaison", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Henry Molaison", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Emotional memory", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: model and evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Henry Molaison", "prompt": "Explain how Molaison’s explicit-memory difficulty and improved mirror drawing contributed to memory theory, and give a limitation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Difficulty forming new long-term explicit memories after medial-temporal surgery supported hippocampal contributions to formation/consolidation.", "[2] Procedural improvement without recollection showed that skill learning and conscious memory can be dissociated.", "[2] A single clinical case with broader surgical/history influences limits exact localisation and generalisation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-memory-models-q3"}, {"title": "Emotional memory", "prompt": "Explain the roles of amygdala, hippocampus and cerebellum without claiming one store contains every memory. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Amygdala processes emotional significance and modulates memory, without guaranteeing accuracy.", "[2] Hippocampus contributes to forming/consolidating explicit memories across distributed systems.", "[2] Cerebellum contributes to selected implicit/motor learning, with other structures also involved."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-memory-models-q6"}, {"title": "Extended response: model and evidence", "prompt": "Compare multi-store and working memory accounts, apply a complex task and use Molaison’s case to evaluate a simple single-store account. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Explains sensory/short-term/long-term stores and attention/rehearsal/retrieval processes.", "[2] Describes central executive, loop and sketchpad functions.", "[2] Adds buffer integration and the correct 2000 distinction.", "[2] Applies separate component demands to a coherent task.", "[2] Uses explicit/procedural dissociation in Molaison as evidence.", "[2] Evaluates oversimplification and case limits rather than treating diagrams as literal brain compartments."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-memory-models-q7"}, {"id": "psy12-memory-models-brain-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain how improved skill performance without remembered practice in Molaison’s case supported distinct memory systems. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Skill improvement indicated retained aspects of procedural/implicit learning.", "[2] Failure to remember practice reflected marked impairment of new explicit memory formation.", "[2] This dissociation supports distinct interacting systems rather than one uniform memory ability, with case/anatomical limits."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-memory-models-brain-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare hippocampal, cerebellar and amygdala contributions to remembering a frightening first driving lesson and later mastering vehicle control. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Hippocampal systems contribute to formation/consolidation of the explicit episode of the lesson.", "[2] Cerebellar and related systems contribute to aspects of learned coordinated/procedural control.", "[2] The amygdala contributes emotional significance/modulation; the systems interact and fear does not guarantee perfect accuracy."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What did Molaison’s pattern help demonstrate?", "o": ["Explicit and procedural memory can be affected differently.", "All memory was absent in every form.", "The hippocampus stores every old memory permanently.", "Skill improvement necessarily proves remembered practice sessions."], "a": 0, "why": "Preserved skill learning alongside severe new explicit-memory difficulty supports a dissociation."}, {"q": "Converting stimulation into neural activity is what?", "o": ["Transduction", "Interpretation only", "Storage", "Discrimination"], "a": 0, "why": "Transduction is a sensation process."}, {"q": "Which buffer was added in 2000?", "o": ["Episodic buffer", "Hippocampal store", "Only sensory register", "Primary motor loop"], "a": 0, "why": "Baddeley added integration with sources and long-term memory."}, {"q": "A general fact is what type?", "o": ["Semantic explicit memory", "Procedural only", "Always episodic", "Only echoic"], "a": 0, "why": "Facts differ from personal events and skills."}, {"q": "Molaison’s skill improvement without recollection supports what?", "o": ["Distinct procedural and explicit processes", "No learning at all", "Perfect episodic recall", "One universal permanent store"], "a": 0, "why": "Different memory functions can be dissociated."}, {"q": "The central executive primarily does what?", "o": ["Coordinates attention and processing", "Stores every old fact", "Transduces sound", "Produces all REM"], "a": 0, "why": "It is a coordinating component, not a large storage box."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-remembering-retrieval", "title": "Recall, recognition, relearning and rehearsal", "scope": "Recall and recognition supply different cues · Relearning needs an explicit calculation · Rehearsal strategy should match durable understanding", "parent": "psy12-remembering", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Recall, recognition, relearning and rehearsal</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Recall and recognition supply different cues</li><li>Relearning needs an explicit calculation</li><li>Rehearsal strategy should match durable understanding</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Recall, recognition and relearning", "kind": "learn", "text": "Recall requires producing information from memory. Free recall allows any order, serial recall requires the original order and cued recall supplies prompts. Recognition requires identifying previously encountered material among options. Relearning measures how much easier or faster material is learned again compared with the initial learning, often described as savings. These measures can reveal different access to stored information. A student who cannot list all terms but selects them from alternatives demonstrates recognition rather than free recall. A successful multiple-choice response may involve cues or guessing, so it does not guarantee independent recall. Match the retrieval process to the task, and interpret the actual measure used in a study.", "diagram": "retrieval"}, {"title": "Maintenance and elaborative rehearsal", "kind": "learn", "text": "Maintenance rehearsal repeats information to keep it active, such as silently repeating a short number until it is entered. Elaborative rehearsal connects information to meaning, existing knowledge or a relevant example, supporting richer retrieval routes. Explaining how a theory applies to a new scenario is elaborative, while reciting its name repeatedly is maintenance. Repetition can still contribute to learning, especially when distributed and followed by retrieval, but repetition alone does not ensure flexible application. A study method should match the assessment: written Psychology answers require definitions, accurate study details and scenario reasoning. Familiarity from rereading can be mistaken for recall. Test yourself without looking, then correct errors with specific feedback rather than relying only on the feeling that the page is familiar.", "diagram": "rehearsal"}, {"title": "Recall and recognition supply different cues", "kind": "learn", "text": "Free recall asks for information without a specified order or supplied alternatives; serial recall requires an order; cued recall provides a prompt linked to the target. Recognition asks whether information has been encountered among supplied options. Recognition can sometimes appear easier because the test supplies cues, but accuracy also depends on distractors and guessing. Relearning measures improvement or savings when learning material again. These are different demonstrations of retention, not a universal ranking that guarantees one always gives the highest score.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Relearning needs an explicit calculation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A conventional savings calculation is (original learning effort minus relearning effort) divided by original effort, multiplied by 100. If a fictional list needs 20 trials initially and 8 later, savings are 12/20 × 100 = 60%. State what effort means in the task—trials or time—and use comparable criteria. Savings can reveal retention even when free recall is poor, but practice differences or changed conditions can affect interpretation. Show the denominator and units rather than reporting a bare percentage.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Rehearsal strategy should match durable understanding", "kind": "learn", "text": "Maintenance rehearsal repeats information to keep it active; elaborative rehearsal connects it to meaning, existing knowledge and organisation. Explaining a concept and applying it to a new example adds relationships that can support later retrieval. Repetition is still useful for some material, but it does not automatically create understanding. Exam preparation should include retrieval and application as well as familiarity. Being able to recognise a phrase on a page is different from producing a precise mechanism in an extended written answer.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Savings in relearning", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Savings in relearning", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Retrieval measures", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Rehearsal and exam preparation", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Savings in relearning", "prompt": "A fictional list takes 12 trials to learn initially and 8 to relearn. Calculate percentage savings and interpret cautiously. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Saved trials = 12 − 8 = 4.", "[1] Percentage savings = 4/12 × 100 ≈ 33.3%.", "[1] Less relearning effort indicates an influence of earlier learning under these conditions.", "[1] It does not mean exactly 33.3% of every memory remained or generalise directly to all meaningful material."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-remembering-q3"}, {"title": "Retrieval measures", "prompt": "Compare free recall, serial recall, cued recall, recognition and relearning with one suitable example each. (5 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Free recall: produce learnt words in any order.", "[1] Serial recall: produce them in the original sequence.", "[1] Cued recall: produce a response prompted by a relevant cue.", "[1] Recognition: select earlier words among distractors.", "[1] Relearning: compare effort/time to learn again with the original learning."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-remembering-q4"}, {"title": "Rehearsal and exam preparation", "prompt": "Compare maintenance and elaborative rehearsal and apply each to a theory definition. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Maintenance repeats the wording to keep it active, such as reciting the definition.", "[2] Elaborative rehearsal links meaning to prior knowledge or a new scenario, supporting richer retrieval and application."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-remembering-q5"}, {"id": "psy12-remembering-retrieval-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A fictional participant needs 30 trials to learn a list and 12 to relearn it to the same criterion. Calculate savings and explain what this can show. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Savings = (30−12)/30 × 100.", "[2] Result = 60%, with learning effort defined as trials to the same criterion.", "[2] Reduced relearning effort provides evidence of retention, even if unaided recall was limited; comparable conditions remain important."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-remembering-retrieval-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare recognising a psychology definition from four options with explaining it without options, then propose an elaborative study strategy. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Recognition supplies possible targets/cues, whereas unaided explanation requires recall and organised production.", "[2] Recognising a familiar phrase does not establish a precise mechanism or scenario application.", "[2] Link the definition to prior knowledge and explain an original example, then retrieve it without notes; this goes beyond maintenance repetition."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "If initial learning takes 20 trials and relearning 8, what is savings?", "o": ["60%.", "40%.", "150%.", "12%."], "a": 0, "why": "Saved effort is 12 of the original 20 trials: 12/20 × 100 = 60%."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-remembering-processing", "title": "Levels of processing and Craik–Tulving experiment two", "scope": "Processing depth concerns the kind of engagement · Keep experiment two and its outcome measure clear · Evaluation links procedure to interpretation", "parent": "psy12-remembering", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Levels of processing and Craik–Tulving experiment two</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Processing depth concerns the kind of engagement</li><li>Keep experiment two and its outcome measure clear</li><li>Evaluation links procedure to interpretation</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Levels of processing: Craik and Lockhart (1972)", "kind": "learn", "text": "The levels-of-processing account focuses on how information is processed rather than only how long it remains in a store. Structural processing attends to appearance, such as capital letters. Phonemic processing attends to sound, such as rhyme. Semantic processing attends to meaning, such as whether a word fits a sentence. Meaningful processing often produces more durable retention for suitable tasks. Depth is a theoretical description and must be operationalised through an actual task. It is not a literal location deeper in the brain or simply spending more seconds on a word. Meaningful elaboration connects information to relevant knowledge. The benefit depends on encoding and retrieval demands, so saying semantic processing always guarantees perfect memory overstates the model.", "diagram": "processing"}, {"title": "Craik and Tulving (1975): experiment two method", "kind": "learn", "text": "Experiment two examined how orienting questions affected later recognition of words. Twenty-four paid student participants completed sixty trials, with questions about letter case, rhyme or sentence fit and both yes and no answers. A question was heard and a five-letter noun briefly appeared in a tachistoscope; participants responded with keys. They later received an unexpected recognition list containing the sixty original words and 120 distractors, for 180 words in total. Each participant encountered the processing conditions, with formats and ordering controlled. The key outcome was recognition of the previously presented words, not free recall. Keep this experiment distinct from the other nine experiments in the paper, which used different arrangements or later tests.", "diagram": "craik"}, {"title": "Craik and Tulving (1975): findings and contribution", "kind": "learn", "text": "Recognition was greater after meaning-based sentence decisions than after structural case decisions. For words receiving yes responses, reported recognition increased from approximately 15% for case decisions to 81% for sentence decisions. The pattern also differed for yes and no responses, showing that the quality of the encoding relation mattered. Semantic decisions took longer on average, which raised a possible time-based alternative explanation; later analyses and experiments in the paper examined that issue. Do not describe those later manipulations as part of experiment two’s procedure. The result supported the role of encoding operations in retention. Explain both the manipulation and measured outcome, and use the term recognition accurately rather than assuming every memory test is recall.", "diagram": "craik"}, {"title": "Craik and Tulving: evaluating the evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Controlled questions and brief presentation allowed comparison of different encoding operations using the same participants, reducing individual-difference variation. However, a small paid student sample and isolated words limit generalisation to real study tasks and populations. The unexpected test reduces deliberate memory strategy but raises questions about what participants understood. Response time differed with processing task, so a complete evaluation should recognise the time alternative and distinguish experiment-two evidence from the paper’s later tests. The concept of depth can become circular if good memory is used as the only proof of deep processing. State the independent operation and the outcome separately. Original paper links allow study details and dates to be checked rather than mixing general summaries of multiple experiments.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Recognition percentages and what they count", "kind": "learn", "text": "A recognition hit rate concerns correctly identifying old items and is not the same as the percentage of all displayed words a participant selected. Distractor responses can also matter when evaluating performance. In the experiment-two reference here, the 15% and 81% figures refer to original words from particular yes-response processing conditions. They do not represent percentages of participants who remembered anything, and should not be combined with results from a different experiment. A graph must specify processing condition and measure. If a question supplies new fictional data, calculate and interpret those data instead of replacing them with memorised rates. Accurate denominators protect the meaning of the evidence.", "diagram": "craik"}, {"title": "Processing depth concerns the kind of engagement", "kind": "learn", "text": "Craik and Lockhart’s 1972 account emphasises processing rather than only storage location. Structural processing concerns appearance, phonemic processing concerns sound and semantic processing concerns meaning. Meaningful processing often supports more durable retention, but depth cannot be defined solely as whatever produces better memory without becoming circular. Time and effort do not always equal semantic depth. Explain the actual task and the relationships created, then evaluate memory under the stated retrieval conditions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Keep experiment two and its outcome measure clear", "kind": "learn", "text": "Craik and Tulving’s 1975 experiment two used orienting questions about words, comparing structural, phonemic and semantic processing. Participants answered yes/no questions and later received an unexpected recognition test. Differences in subsequent recognition supported the importance of the processing task, with semantic conditions generally producing stronger retention. Recognition percentages concern words correctly recognised under defined conditions, not the percentage of participants with perfect memory. The named experiment is not interchangeable with every later experiment in the same publication.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluation links procedure to interpretation", "kind": "learn", "text": "The orienting tasks provided a controlled comparison and incidental learning reduced an explicit memorisation strategy. However, task duration, elaboration, yes/no congruity and retrieval compatibility can complicate a simple depth-only account. Word lists and laboratory recognition do not fully represent everyday learning. State a concrete alternative and how a comparison could address it, instead of saying only that there was bias. A contribution can be strong while the exact mechanism and generalisation still require qualification.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Forgetting and health-example reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Forgetting and health-example reference</h3><p>Compare mechanisms and use cautious educational language.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Account/example</th><th>Proposed mechanism</th><th>Inference limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Retrieval failure</td><td>Cues/access insufficient</td><td>Trace may remain</td></tr><tr><td>Proactive</td><td>Old interferes with new</td><td>Specify target/intruder</td></tr><tr><td>Retroactive</td><td>New interferes with old</td><td>Specify direction</td></tr><tr><td>Decay</td><td>Trace weakens with time</td><td>Time confounded with other events</td></tr><tr><td>Motivated forgetting</td><td>Motivation affects access/avoidance</td><td>No event proved from a gap</td></tr><tr><td>CTE</td><td>Repeated impacts/pathology</td><td>Exposure not deterministic</td></tr><tr><td>Alzheimer’s</td><td>Progressive degeneration</td><td>Not normal ageing</td></tr><tr><td>WKS</td><td>Severe thiamine deficiency</td><td>Not exclusive to alcohol</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Memory symptoms are not diagnoses. For experiment two, distinguish recognition from recall and identify the processing condition represented by each number.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Experiment two, not a blended study", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Experiment two, not a blended study", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: encoding and forgetting", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Experiment two, not a blended study", "prompt": "Describe the method and key finding of Craik and Tulving (1975) experiment two. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Twenty-four participants encountered sixty case/rhyme/sentence questions with briefly presented nouns and yes/no responses.", "[2] Unexpected recognition used 180 words, sixty original plus 120 distractors; this was recognition, not free recall.", "[2] Semantic sentence processing produced higher recognition than case processing, supporting encoding-quality explanations."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-remembering-q1"}, {"title": "Extended response: encoding and forgetting", "prompt": "Explain levels of processing, the exact experiment-two evidence, rehearsal and two forgetting mechanisms. Evaluate one methodological alternative. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Defines structural, phonemic and semantic operations accurately.", "[2] Describes the sixty-trial/180-word recognition procedure and processing conditions.", "[2] Gives greater semantic recognition and distinguishes later experiments/time analyses.", "[2] Compares maintenance with meaningful elaborative links.", "[2] Applies two mechanisms such as retrieval failure and interference with correct directions.", "[2] Evaluates processing-time/self-report/sample or task-generalisation issues with a consequence."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-remembering-q7"}, {"id": "psy12-remembering-processing-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain why semantic orienting questions in Craik and Tulving’s experiment two support a processing explanation of retention. Include the later test and one limit. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Words were processed through structural, phonemic or semantic orienting questions rather than simply assigned to different physical stores.", "[2] The unexpected recognition test generally showed stronger retention for semantic processing, linking processing type with later recognition.", "[2] Differences in task demands/elaboration or laboratory word-list validity limit a simple universal depth-only conclusion."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-remembering-processing-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A student says “thinking for longer always means deeper processing.” Evaluate the claim and propose a suitable operational comparison. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Processing depth refers to appearance/sound/meaning and meaningful relationships, not merely elapsed time.", "[2] A long structural task can take more time without becoming semantic; time and processing kind can vary separately.", "[2] Compare clearly defined structural and semantic tasks while recording/matching relevant exposure and time, then assess recognition with cautious interpretation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which task is semantic processing?", "o": ["Judging whether a word fits the meaning of a sentence.", "Judging whether the word is printed in capitals.", "Judging only whether it rhymes with another word.", "Assuming any longer viewing time must be semantic."], "a": 0, "why": "Semantic processing concerns meaning; structural and phonemic tasks concern appearance and sound."}], "subtitle": "18 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-remembering-forgetting", "title": "Ebbinghaus, retrieval failure and interference", "scope": "A forgetting curve describes a pattern under conditions · Interference has a direction · Failure to retrieve does not identify one cause", "parent": "psy12-remembering", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Ebbinghaus, retrieval failure and interference</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>A forgetting curve describes a pattern under conditions</li><li>Interference has a direction</li><li>Failure to retrieve does not identify one cause</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Ebbinghaus (1885) and the forgetting curve", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ebbinghaus used repeated learning and relearning of material such as nonsense syllables to investigate retention. Savings in relearning showed that previous learning could influence performance even when direct recall was limited. The classic forgetting curve describes relatively rapid initial loss followed by a slower decline, under the studied conditions. Its shape is not a universal percentage schedule for every learner or meaningful topic. Repetition and review can improve retention, but spacing and meaningfulness affect real learning. A diagram should identify what is measured, such as retention or savings, and label time. Do not infer from a generic curve that everyone forgets exactly half of every lesson after a fixed period. Apply the broad pattern while respecting the method’s limitations.", "diagram": "forgettingcurve"}, {"title": "Retrieval failure and interference", "kind": "learn", "text": "Retrieval failure occurs when stored information is difficult to access with the available cues or conditions. A better cue may restore access. Proactive interference occurs when older information disrupts learning or retrieval of newer information, such as an old password intruding when a new one is needed. Retroactive interference occurs when newer information disrupts retrieval of older information. The names describe the direction of disruption, not whether the interference happens before or after an examination. Specify which information is old, which is new and which is being recalled. Similar material can create competition, but a forgotten answer is not automatically evidence of interference. The task and cues must support the proposed explanation.", "diagram": "interference"}, {"title": "Decay and motivated forgetting", "kind": "learn", "text": "Decay theory proposes that a memory trace weakens over time when it is not used or maintained. It is difficult to isolate pure passage of time because new learning and changing context also occur. Motivated forgetting refers to reduced access or deliberate avoidance associated with a motive to keep some information out of awareness. Conscious suppression and theoretical unconscious repression should not be treated as identical or as explanations proven by a single memory gap. Memories can be reconstructed and errors occur, so inability to remember does not establish that a traumatic event happened or did not happen. For exams, describe the proposed mechanism and evidence limits. Use fictional neutral examples and avoid inviting personal disclosures about distressing experiences.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A forgetting curve describes a pattern under conditions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ebbinghaus studied retention using material such as nonsense syllables and relearning savings. The familiar curve illustrates a relatively rapid initial decrease followed by slower change under the measured conditions. It is not a fixed timetable for every person or every kind of learning. Meaning, prior knowledge, rehearsal and retrieval alter retention. A schematic curve without actual values is a model, not a dataset from which precise percentages should be invented. Explain the axes and measurement before interpreting the shape.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Interference has a direction", "kind": "learn", "text": "Proactive interference occurs when earlier learning disrupts newer learning, such as an old password intruding when recalling a new one. Retroactive interference occurs when newer learning disrupts earlier material, such as a new number making the previous number harder to recall. Define which target is being tested and which competing learning comes first. Similarity and practice affect interference. A vague statement that two memories get confused loses the temporal distinction needed for an exam answer.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Failure to retrieve does not identify one cause", "kind": "learn", "text": "Retrieval failure concerns inadequate access or cues for stored information; decay proposes weakening over time; motivated forgetting concerns motivational influences on avoiding or limiting access. A single failed recall cannot separate these accounts. Time commonly brings intervening activity, making pure decay difficult to isolate. A later appropriate cue may restore recall, supporting an access interpretation in that case. Evaluate the evidence and avoid treating every failure as proof of erasure, repression or a neurological disorder.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Old and new passwords", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Old and new passwords", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Old and new passwords", "prompt": "An old password intrudes when recalling a new one; later the new password makes the old one difficult to recall. Explain both directions. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Old information disrupting newer retrieval is proactive interference.", "[2] New information disrupting older retrieval is retroactive interference; each answer identifies the target and intruding memory."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-remembering-q2"}, {"id": "psy12-remembering-forgetting-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A person repeatedly enters an old password when asked for a new one. Identify the interference and contrast it with retroactive interference. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Earlier password learning disrupts the newer target, illustrating proactive interference.", "[2] Apply the old-to-new direction explicitly rather than only saying confusion.", "[2] Retroactive interference would involve the newer password making the earlier password harder to retrieve."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-remembering-forgetting-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A student cannot recall a definition freely but remembers it after a related cue. Explain a relevant forgetting account and why the first failure does not prove decay. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Retrieval failure is a relevant account: the cue supports access to information not initially retrieved.", "[2] Successful cued recall is inconsistent with assuming the first failure demonstrated complete erasure.", "[2] A single failure does not distinguish decay, interference and access problems; timing, intervening learning and suitable comparisons matter."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which example illustrates retroactive interference?", "o": ["A new number makes an earlier number harder to recall.", "An old password intrudes when recalling a new password.", "A relevant cue helps retrieve a temporarily inaccessible word.", "Every unremembered item is proven permanently erased."], "a": 0, "why": "Retroactive refers to later learning disrupting earlier material."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-remembering-memory-loss", "title": "CTE, Alzheimer’s disease and Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome", "scope": "Memory loss can have different biological pathways · Thiamine deficiency is central to Wernicke–Korsakoff explanations · Explanations should include everyday consequences and limits", "parent": "psy12-remembering", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>CTE, Alzheimer’s disease and Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Memory loss can have different biological pathways</li><li>Thiamine deficiency is central to Wernicke–Korsakoff explanations</li><li>Explanations should include everyday consequences and limits</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "CTE and repeated head impacts", "kind": "learn", "text": "Chronic traumatic encephalopathy, or CTE, is a neurodegenerative condition associated with repeated head impacts over time and characteristic brain pathology involving abnormal tau. It can be linked with cognitive, behavioural and emotional changes, but symptoms are not unique to CTE. Not everyone with concussion or repeated impacts develops it, and one forgotten event does not indicate the condition. Confirmation of CTE pathology currently relies on examination of brain tissue after death; research on clinical syndromes and markers continues. The syllabus uses it as an example of trauma-related memory loss. Explain the exposure and potential consequences with uncertainty, rather than diagnosing a fictional sportsperson solely from mood or memory changes. The course’s health examples are educational explanations, not personal medical assessments.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Alzheimer’s disease and degeneration", "kind": "learn", "text": "Alzheimer’s disease involves progressive changes in brain systems, including disrupted neuronal communication and loss of function, and is a common cause of dementia. Difficulty learning new information can be an early feature; later effects can involve language, judgement, orientation and daily functioning. Emotional and behavioural changes may occur as abilities and independence change. The condition is not a normal or inevitable feature of ageing, and occasional forgetfulness has many possible explanations. In a scenario, explain how progressive degeneration can interfere with encoding, retention or retrieval rather than saying the person simply forgot everything. Distinguish a disease example from a memory model. Appropriate diagnosis requires qualified assessment and cannot be established by a classroom vignette or a concept-check score.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome and careful explanation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome is associated with severe thiamine, or vitamin B1, deficiency, commonly in the context of long-term heavy alcohol use but also in other conditions affecting nutrition. The syllabus categorises it under drug-induced memory loss, yet the mechanism should not be reduced to alcohol directly erasing memories. Wernicke’s encephalopathy is an acute serious condition; Korsakoff syndrome can involve persistent difficulty forming new memories and other memory problems. Confabulation may fill gaps without deliberate deception. Behaviour and emotions can be affected by disorientation and dependence on support. Explain the deficiency and relevant memory consequences without stigmatising people or claiming that every alcohol user develops the syndrome. These examples connect biological disruption with cognitive effects and illustrate why precise mechanisms matter.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Memory loss can have different biological pathways", "kind": "learn", "text": "CTE is a neuropathological condition associated with repeated head impacts; clinical symptoms during life are not specific enough to establish a confirmed diagnosis from memory complaints alone. Alzheimer’s disease involves progressive neurodegeneration and can affect memory alongside other cognition and daily functioning. These are distinct mechanisms and patterns, not interchangeable labels for forgetfulness. An educational explanation links the relevant biological process with possible behavioural and emotional effects, while recognising variability and avoiding diagnosis from a classroom vignette.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Thiamine deficiency is central to Wernicke–Korsakoff explanations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome involves thiamine deficiency, commonly associated with severe alcohol-related nutritional and absorption problems but also possible in other contexts. It is inaccurate to describe every case as alcohol directly erasing memories. Wernicke encephalopathy and Korsakoff syndrome involve different but related features; marked new-learning difficulty and confabulation can be relevant to Korsakoff descriptions. Confabulation is not necessarily deliberate lying. Keep the cause, affected functioning and possible emotional/social consequences distinct.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Explanations should include everyday consequences and limits", "kind": "learn", "text": "Difficulties forming new memories can affect appointments and independent routines, and uncertainty can affect emotion and relationships. The exact outcome depends on condition, severity and support. A short scenario does not establish one diagnosis because ordinary stress, sleep and other conditions can also affect memory. For an exam comparison describe the specified cause and pattern rather than listing unsupported symptoms. Clinical questions require professional assessment; these cases are for understanding syllabus distinctions, not personal screening.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read, apply and qualify the focused model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read each label and distinguish the relationships or levels of explanation. Describe the pattern in your own words, then use the accompanying theory to explain why it matters. Apply the model to the written questions in this lesson and state its limit rather than treating an illustration as proof. Where a diagram uses fictional numbers or arbitrary units, keep those separate from the findings of a designated study.", "diagram": "memory-loss-pathways"}, {"title": "Check the research and authoritative explanations", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Further evidence</h3><p>The lesson explanations provide the required student account. These links allow study details and educational health information to be checked. Read historical methods critically; they are not activities to reproduce.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://doi.org/10.1037/0096-3445.104.3.268\">Craik and Tulving (1975), original paper DOI</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.nia.nih.gov/health/what-alzheimers-disease\">NIH: Alzheimer’s information</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/traumatic-brain-injury/about/repeated-head-impacts.html\">CDC: repeated impacts and CTE</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.niaaa.nih.gov/publications/brochures-and-fact-sheets/wernicke-korsakoff-syndrome\">NIAAA: Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome</a></li></ul>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Three causes of memory loss", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Three causes of memory loss", "prompt": "Compare trauma-related CTE, degenerative Alzheimer’s and WKS with one memory consequence and one qualification. (7 marks)", "steps": ["[2] CTE is associated with repeated impacts and characteristic pathology, potentially affecting cognition/emotion; not everyone exposed develops it.", "[2] Alzheimer’s involves progressive degeneration affecting memory and functioning, not inevitable normal ageing.", "[2] WKS involves severe thiamine deficiency, commonly related to chronic alcohol misuse, with new-memory difficulties; alcohol is not the only context.", "[1] A vignette cannot diagnose a person or establish one cause from nonspecific symptoms."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 1], "marks": 7, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-remembering-q6"}, {"id": "psy12-remembering-memory-loss-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Compare the relevant causes of Alzheimer’s disease and Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome, with one possible effect on behaviour for each. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Alzheimer’s involves progressive neurodegeneration, which can affect memory and everyday cognitive functioning.", "[2] WKS centrally involves thiamine deficiency, often in severe alcohol-related nutritional contexts but not only there.", "[2] Explain a relevant consequence such as missed routines/new-learning difficulty, distinguishing causes without assuming identical symptoms in every case."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-remembering-memory-loss-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Evaluate the claim that a forgetful athlete must have CTE. Include the kind of evidence and an alternative explanation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Repeated head impacts are relevant to CTE risk/association, but forgetfulness alone is nonspecific.", "[2] Confirmed CTE is neuropathological; a short symptom vignette cannot establish it during life.", "[2] Sleep, stress or other conditions can affect memory, so professional assessment and appropriate evidence are needed."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is central to the biological explanation of WKS?", "o": ["Thiamine deficiency.", "Everyday forgetting always proves alcohol damage.", "The same mechanism as a temporary retrieval cue failure.", "A guarantee that confabulation is deliberate deception."], "a": 0, "why": "WKS involves thiamine deficiency; cause and symptoms require careful, non-stereotyped explanation."}, {"q": "Experiment two tested which retrieval measure?", "o": ["Recognition", "Only free recall", "Only serial recall", "Only relearning trials"], "a": 0, "why": "The 180-word list contained targets and distractors."}, {"q": "Older information disrupting newer retrieval is what?", "o": ["Proactive interference", "Retroactive interference", "Always decay", "Internalisation"], "a": 0, "why": "Direction is old interfering with new."}, {"q": "Connecting meaning with prior knowledge is what?", "o": ["Elaborative rehearsal", "Maintenance only", "Punishment", "Synaptic pruning"], "a": 0, "why": "Meaningful links support richer encoding."}, {"q": "WKS involves what key mechanism?", "o": ["Severe thiamine deficiency", "Every head impact", "Normal ageing alone", "A universal happiness chemical"], "a": 0, "why": "Alcohol misuse is common context but deficiency is central and not exclusive to it."}, {"q": "A generic forgetting curve implies what?", "o": ["A broad pattern under specified conditions", "One exact schedule for every learner", "No value of review", "Permanent erasure after a day"], "a": 0, "why": "Material, learning and measures affect the pattern."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-classical-acquisition", "title": "Classical conditioning: components and acquisition", "scope": "Find the reflex before assigning labels · Timing and prediction matter in acquisition · Apply labels to the actual response", "parent": "psy12-classical", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Classical conditioning: components and acquisition</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Find the reflex before assigning labels</li><li>Timing and prediction matter in acquisition</li><li>Apply labels to the actual response</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Learning and reflex responses", "kind": "learn", "text": "Learning is a relatively enduring change in behaviour or knowledge associated with experience, distinguished from temporary fatigue or maturation alone. Classical conditioning concerns learning an association between stimuli, so a previously neutral stimulus comes to elicit a response. A reflex response occurs without needing the particular conditioning history, such as salivation in response to food. Conditioning is not the same as choosing an action to obtain a reward. Identify whether the scenario concerns a stimulus eliciting a response or an action shaped by consequences. Both can contribute to one behaviour. An exam answer should name the learning process and trace the association using specific stimuli and responses rather than simply saying the person learned.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Before conditioning: NS, UCS and UCR", "kind": "learn", "text": "An unconditioned stimulus, or UCS, elicits an unconditioned response, or UCR, without requiring the particular learning procedure. Food can elicit salivation in a dog. A neutral stimulus, or NS, does not initially elicit the target response in the relevant context; a tone might not initially cause the salivation being studied. Neutral does not mean the dog cannot hear the tone or that it produces no response whatsoever. Identify a stimulus as an event or object and a response as an action or physiological change. Food is not the unconditioned response and salivation is not the stimulus. These distinctions prevent errors when a question presents an everyday example with several events.", "diagram": "classical"}, {"title": "Acquisition and stimulus pairing", "kind": "learn", "text": "Acquisition is the period in which an association develops between the neutral stimulus and unconditioned stimulus. Pairing a tone with food can lead the tone to predict food and elicit salivation. Timing and predictive relation matter, so not every arbitrary repeated pairing is equally effective. The neutral stimulus becomes a conditioned stimulus, or CS, once it elicits the learned target response. Explain what is paired and why the formerly neutral event changes its role. Do not call the tone unconditioned simply because it is presented first. The status depends on the learning history and target response. Diagrams of the process should separate before, during and after conditioning.", "diagram": "classical"}, {"title": "After conditioning: CS and CR", "kind": "learn", "text": "A conditioned stimulus elicits a conditioned response, or CR, because of the learned association. After acquisition, the tone is the CS and salivation to that tone is the CR. Salivation to food remains an unconditioned response in this example. The UCR and CR may resemble each other but arise in response to different stimuli and can differ in strength or form. Name both the response and what elicits it. A student who feels anxious on seeing a place linked with an unpleasant experience may show a conditioned response, but a real fear can have multiple influences. A complete exam mapping should keep the five terms tied to one coherent sequence.", "diagram": "classical"}, {"title": "Applying classical conditioning without mixing terms", "kind": "learn", "text": "A fictional student hears a warning tone repeatedly before a distressing simulation and later feels tense on hearing that tone alone. To apply the model, identify the initially neutral tone, the event eliciting unlearned distress, that response, the later conditioned tone and the response to it. Explain the pairing rather than treating the tone as both an action and a consequence. If a question asks about generalisation, introduce a similar tone; if it asks about extinction, describe repeated tone presentation without the distressing event. Keep the response specified throughout. This exercise uses a fictional scenario, not an instruction to create distress. Compare the mechanism with operant conditioning when behaviour changes because of its consequences.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Find the reflex before assigning labels", "kind": "learn", "text": "An unconditioned stimulus elicits an unconditioned response without the relevant learning. Before acquisition, a neutral stimulus does not elicit the target reflex response. Repeated or otherwise effective pairing can establish a conditioned stimulus that elicits a conditioned response. The same physical stimulus changes its functional label after learning: it is not necessary to invent a second object. The unconditioned and conditioned responses can resemble each other but arise to different stimuli and need not be identical in every feature.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Timing and prediction matter in acquisition", "kind": "learn", "text": "A cue that reliably precedes a relevant event can become predictive of it. Merely presenting two things somewhere in the same week does not specify an effective pairing procedure. Draw before, during and after learning with arrows and correct stimuli, then explain the association. Classical conditioning concerns learning about stimulus relationships and elicited responses; it is not defined by a reward following a chosen action. A scenario can involve both classical and operant processes, so identify the particular target response asked about.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Apply labels to the actual response", "kind": "learn", "text": "If a previously neutral clinic cue comes to elicit anticipatory anxiety after painful experiences, the cue is the CS and anticipatory anxiety the CR in the stated example. Attending the clinic or avoiding it is an action and may need a different explanation. Calling the unpleasant event negative reinforcement confuses a stimulus with the function of a consequence. A precise answer states the unlearned relation, the pairing and the new cue response while qualifying a simplified vignette’s evidence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Conditioning sequence reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Conditioning sequence reference</h3><p>This neutral illustrative dog model separates stimulus and response.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Relation</th><th>Term roles</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Before</td><td>Food produces salivation</td><td>UCS gives UCR</td></tr><tr><td>Before</td><td>Tone does not give target salivation</td><td>Tone is NS</td></tr><tr><td>During</td><td>Tone associated with food</td><td>Acquisition</td></tr><tr><td>After</td><td>Tone alone produces learned salivation</td><td>CS gives CR</td></tr><tr><td>Extinction</td><td>Tone repeatedly without food</td><td>CR declines</td></tr><tr><td>After rest</td><td>Tone again, no new pairing</td><td>Possible spontaneous recovery</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Generalisation extends response to similar stimuli; discrimination develops selective responding. Historical fear studies should not be reproduced.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Five components", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Five components", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: classical learning", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Five components", "prompt": "In a fictional dog example, food naturally elicits salivation. A tone initially does not elicit it. After pairings, the tone alone elicits salivation. Identify NS, UCS, UCR, CS and CR and explain acquisition. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Initial tone is NS for the target salivation response.", "[1] Food is UCS.", "[1] Salivation to food is UCR.", "[1] Tone after learning is CS.", "[1] Salivation to tone is CR.", "[1] Pairing establishes the predictive association during acquisition."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-classical-q1"}, {"title": "Extended response: classical learning", "prompt": "Explain acquisition and three further principles, use Pavlov and Little Albert accurately and evaluate evidence and ethics. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Maps NS/UCS/UCR and CS/CR with correct stimulus-response roles.", "[2] Explains pairing/acquisition and a coherent example.", "[2] Explains three requested principles with appropriate conditions.", "[2] Describes Pavlov’s salivary evidence and contribution.", "[2] Describes rat/noise pairing, fear and generalisation in Little Albert.", "[2] Evaluates animal/child welfare and artificial/single-case generalisation limits."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-classical-q7"}, {"id": "psy12-classical-acquisition-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "In a fictional example a tone is repeatedly followed by food that elicits salivation. The tone later elicits salivation alone. Identify NS, UCS, UCR, CS and CR. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Before learning the tone is NS; food is UCS and salivation to food is UCR.", "[2] During acquisition the tone and food are paired, establishing a predictive association.", "[2] After learning the tone is CS and salivation to the tone is CR; distinguish the eliciting stimulus for each response."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-classical-acquisition-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A student labels avoiding a feared place as the CR and a removed unpleasant feeling as the UCS. Explain why this account may mix learning processes. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A CR in a classical account is the learned elicited response to a CS, such as anticipatory fear.", "[2] Avoidance is an action whose consequences may affect future behaviour in an operant account.", "[2] Removal of an unpleasant feeling after avoidance may negatively reinforce that action if it increases; it is not automatically the original UCS."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What changes from NS to CS?", "o": ["The cue’s learned relationship to the target response.", "The unconditioned response becomes a new object.", "Any chosen behaviour automatically becomes the UCS.", "A consequence must follow a voluntary action in every classical example."], "a": 0, "why": "The same cue can receive a different functional label after acquisition."}], "subtitle": "18 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-classical-principles", "title": "Generalisation, discrimination, extinction and recovery", "scope": "Generalisation and discrimination describe cue boundaries · Extinction requires the relevant presentation · Distinguish changes in cue, time and consequence", "parent": "psy12-classical", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Generalisation, discrimination, extinction and recovery</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Generalisation and discrimination describe cue boundaries</li><li>Extinction requires the relevant presentation</li><li>Distinguish changes in cue, time and consequence</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Generalisation and discrimination", "kind": "learn", "text": "Stimulus generalisation occurs when stimuli similar to the conditioned stimulus also elicit the conditioned response. A dog conditioned to one tone may salivate to a similar tone. Stimulus discrimination occurs when learning or experience leads the organism to respond differently to the conditioned stimulus and other stimuli. If only one tone predicts food, the response can become more selective. Generalisation concerns responding to related stimuli, not spreading every type of behaviour. Discrimination in this learning context is different from social discrimination studied in Year 11. Explain the similarity and the response, or the distinction learned. These principles help explain why learned responses can extend to new settings and why they may also become more specific.", "diagram": "generalisation"}, {"title": "Extinction and spontaneous recovery", "kind": "learn", "text": "Extinction occurs when repeated presentation of the conditioned stimulus without the unconditioned stimulus reduces the conditioned response. If the tone no longer predicts food, salivation to it may decline. Extinction is new learning about the changed relation rather than guaranteed deletion of all previous learning. Spontaneous recovery is the reappearance of an extinguished response after a rest period when the conditioned stimulus returns, often at a reduced level. Reconditioning can also occur if pairing resumes, but that is different from spontaneous recovery without renewed pairing. A diagram should show response reduction and later return with the conditions labelled. Do not explain extinction as removing the stimulus permanently; the organism must encounter it without the expected UCS in the basic procedure.", "diagram": "extinction"}, {"title": "Distinguishing extinction from avoidance", "kind": "learn", "text": "Extinction involves experiencing the conditioned stimulus without the unconditioned stimulus so the old predictive relationship is revised. Avoiding the stimulus entirely may prevent that learning opportunity, even if immediate distress is reduced. This conceptual distinction helps explain why absence of a response while a cue is never encountered does not demonstrate extinction. In a fictional neutral example, a tone repeatedly presented without food differs from never hearing the tone again. The response can also return after rest or context change, showing that learning is not a simple erase button. These mechanisms are theoretical explanations and should not be used to conduct unsupervised fear exposure.", "diagram": "extinction"}, {"title": "Generalisation and discrimination describe cue boundaries", "kind": "learn", "text": "Stimulus generalisation occurs when similar cues elicit the learned response beyond the original CS. Discrimination involves responding differently to cues according to learned relevance. If several similar tones elicit salivation but a clearly different signal does not, both patterns can be described at different points. Generalisation is not spontaneous recovery: the former changes the cue, the latter concerns a return after extinction and a rest interval. State what differs in the comparison before selecting the term.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Extinction requires the relevant presentation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Presenting the CS without the UCS across trials can reduce the conditioned response. Simply avoiding the CS does not demonstrate this extinction process because the relevant nonreinforced exposure has not been observed. Extinction should not be equated with deleting the original learning: spontaneous recovery can occur after a rest interval. A returned response may be weaker, and context can influence the pattern. A clear explanation includes the sequence of trials and the target response rather than only saying the fear went away.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Distinguish changes in cue, time and consequence", "kind": "learn", "text": "To classify a scenario ask whether a similar stimulus is introduced, a formerly predictive cue is presented without the event, or time passes after reduction of the response. These features separate generalisation, extinction and recovery. A reward for approaching a cue would add an operant component, while the elicited response remains a classical target. Student applications should use safe neutral examples or provided data, not attempt to treat phobias or expose classmates to distressing stimuli.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Extinction and recovery", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Extinction and recovery", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Generalisation versus discrimination", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Extinction and recovery", "prompt": "A learned response declines when the signal repeatedly occurs without the original event, then briefly returns after a rest. Explain both processes. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Extinction is response reduction through CS presentations without UCS.", "[2] Spontaneous recovery is reappearance after a rest without renewed pairing; it shows reduction need not erase earlier learning."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-classical-q2"}, {"title": "Generalisation versus discrimination", "prompt": "Explain how responding to a similar tone differs from responding only to the trained tone after differential experience. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Responding to a similar stimulus illustrates generalisation of the CR.", "[2] Selective responding to the trained predictive stimulus illustrates discrimination, with the contrasting stimulus distinguished."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-classical-q4"}, {"id": "psy12-classical-principles-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A conditioned response decreases when a cue is repeatedly presented without its original event, then returns after a break. Explain both processes and one implication. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] CS-alone presentations reduce the CR through extinction.", "[2] Reappearance after a rest interval illustrates spontaneous recovery.", "[2] Recovery means extinction should not be assumed to erase all original learning; conditions and context matter."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-classical-principles-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A student says not entering a feared room for a month proves classical extinction. Evaluate the claim and describe the relevant evidence. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Avoidance alone does not show CS presentations without the UCS, so the specified extinction process is not established.", "[2] Relevant evidence would concern changes in the elicited response during suitable nonreinforced cue presentations, not mere elapsed time.", "[2] Avoidance can have an operant consequence, and clinical exposure belongs to appropriate professional practice rather than unsupervised classroom treatment."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What distinguishes spontaneous recovery?", "o": ["A previously reduced conditioned response reappears after a rest interval.", "A new similar stimulus elicits the response for the first time.", "The CS has never been encountered.", "Avoidance alone proves erased learning."], "a": 0, "why": "Recovery concerns return following extinction and rest; generalisation concerns similar cues."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-classical-studies", "title": "Pavlov (1902) and Watson–Rayner (1920): study evidence", "scope": "Pavlov’s contribution concerned predictive cues · Little Albert tested an emotional response association · Ethics and inference must be evaluated separately", "parent": "psy12-classical", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Pavlov (1902) and Watson–Rayner (1920): study evidence</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Pavlov’s contribution concerned predictive cues</li><li>Little Albert tested an emotional response association</li><li>Ethics and inference must be evaluated separately</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Pavlov (1902): aim and measurement", "kind": "learn", "text": "Pavlov’s early work, represented by the syllabus date 1902, investigated conditioned salivary responses in dogs within a broader programme on digestion and reflexes. Salivation was measured using apparatus that collected or recorded secretions. Food elicited salivation, and initially neutral signals associated with food could later elicit a salivary response. The controlled setting allowed researchers to study stimulus relations and reduce distracting cues. There was not one universal bell-only experiment that contained every later conditioning result. A tone or bell is a useful teaching example, but describe the principle and measured response accurately. The designated study contributes to understanding learned associations, not evidence that all complex human learning consists solely of salivation-like reflexes.", "diagram": "pavlov"}, {"title": "Pavlov: findings, contribution and evaluation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Signals associated with food acquired the ability to elicit salivation before food was delivered. This provided evidence of learned anticipatory responses and supported systematic investigation of acquisition, generalisation and extinction. Objective recording and controlled presentation strengthened measurement. However, animal procedures and artificial conditions limit direct generalisation to human cognition, and surgical or restrictive procedures raise welfare concerns. Current evaluation should consider replacement, reduction and refinement. The findings show a learning mechanism, not that dogs consciously understood the researcher’s intention. In an exam response, state the aim, pairing procedure, salivary outcome and contribution. Keep later principles and demonstrations distinguishable from the specific account requested by the question.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Watson and Rayner (1920): Little Albert method", "kind": "learn", "text": "Watson and Rayner investigated whether fear could be conditioned in an infant and generalise to related stimuli. In the 1920 Little Albert study, a young child initially showed little fear of a white rat but reacted fearfully to a sudden loud noise produced behind him. The rat was then presented in association with the noise. Researchers observed crying, withdrawal and other distress responses when the rat was later presented. The rat began as a neutral stimulus, the noise as an unconditioned stimulus and distress to the noise as an unconditioned response. Identify the actual method and measures rather than describing electric shocks or confusing the study with Pavlov’s salivation research.", "diagram": "albert"}, {"title": "Little Albert: findings and stimulus generalisation", "kind": "learn", "text": "After pairings, Albert showed fear-related responses to the rat without the noise, making the rat a conditioned stimulus and the learned fear response a conditioned response. Responses to some related furry stimuli suggested generalisation, but they were not identical for every object or occasion. The study contributed to the claim that emotional responses could be learned through stimulus associations. It did not show that every human fear originates this way or demonstrate a fully controlled permanent adult phobia. The small case, limited measurement and changing circumstances constrain conclusions. An application must distinguish the original response to noise from the learned response to the rat, even when both include crying or withdrawal.", "diagram": "albert"}, {"title": "Little Albert: major ethical and methodological limitations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Deliberately inducing fear in a child raises serious protection-from-harm concerns. Consent, vulnerability and withdrawal arrangements would require much stronger safeguards under current expectations. The investigators did not document a successful removal of the conditioned fear before the child left the research. A single infant and limited standardised measurement restrict generalisation and reliability, while absence of a robust comparison makes alternative explanations difficult to exclude. Historical uncertainty about aspects of the child’s identity should not be presented as settled proof of later outcomes. Evaluate the actual procedure and evidence. Never copy fear-conditioning with children or animals for course activities. Scientific contribution can be acknowledged while clearly describing why the methods are ethically unacceptable to reproduce.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Pavlov’s contribution concerned predictive cues", "kind": "learn", "text": "Pavlov’s salivary research used food-related reflex responses and controlled cues, demonstrating that a previously neutral signal could come to elicit salivation. The historical work used different stimuli and procedures, so a bell is a simplified teaching example rather than the only method. Record the UCS–UCR relation, cue pairing and later cue response. The contribution was a measurable account of learned stimulus relationships, not a conclusion that every human behaviour is a salivary reflex.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Little Albert tested an emotional response association", "kind": "learn", "text": "Watson and Rayner’s 1920 study paired exposure to a white rat with a loud sound that produced distress in a young child. Subsequent responses to the rat and some similar furry objects were interpreted as conditioned fear and generalisation. This was an uncontrolled single-child procedure with variable observations, not proof of permanent fear or a universal effect. The study’s exact sequence and limitations matter more than dramatic claims about its outcome or later unsupported stories.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Ethics and inference must be evaluated separately", "kind": "learn", "text": "Inducing distress in a vulnerable child, consent issues and lack of adequate removal of the conditioned response raise major ethical concerns. A small uncontrolled case also limits causal precision and generalisation. Pavlov’s animal work raises welfare and species-transfer questions alongside its controlled measurements. Explain how a specific feature affects welfare or inference. Current classroom learning should use records, models and low-risk examples, not reproduce harmful historical procedures. Influence on theory does not imply ethical acceptability.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Little Albert mapping", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Little Albert mapping", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Pavlov’s study", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Ethical alternative", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Little Albert mapping", "prompt": "Map the rat/noise/distress sequence in Watson and Rayner (1920), then explain two limitations. (7 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Rat initially NS; loud noise UCS; distress to noise UCR.", "[1] Pairing associates rat and noise.", "[1] Rat becomes CS eliciting learned fear/distress as CR.", "[2] Single infant/limited controls and measurement restrict generalisation and causal precision.", "[2] Deliberate distress and lack of documented successful fear removal raise protection and consent concerns."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 2, 2], "marks": 7, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-classical-q3"}, {"title": "Pavlov’s study", "prompt": "Describe the designated Pavlov work’s aim, method, finding and contribution without relying on a bell-only myth. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Investigated conditioned salivary responding in dogs.", "[1] Measured salivation while signals were associated with food under controlled conditions.", "[1] A previously neutral signal later elicited salivation.", "[1] Supported a learned stimulus-association mechanism; the programme used different signals/procedures."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-classical-q5"}, {"title": "Ethical alternative", "prompt": "Why should students analyse Little Albert rather than reproduce it, and what safe alternative can practise the theory? (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Deliberately inducing child fear would threaten protection from harm and meaningful consent.", "[2] Analyse a fictional neutral vignette or provided historical data, mapping terms without exposing someone to distress."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-classical-q6"}, {"id": "psy12-classical-studies-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Compare Pavlov’s contribution with Watson and Rayner’s 1920 emotional-learning example, using the learned cue and response in each. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Pavlov showed a previously neutral cue could elicit salivation after association with food.", "[2] Watson/Rayner paired a rat with a distressing sound and interpreted later rat-related distress as conditioned fear.", "[2] Both illustrate stimulus-association accounts, but outcomes, participants and evidence differ; neither establishes every form of learning."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-classical-studies-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Evaluate two limitations of Little Albert, one ethical and one methodological, and explain why the findings do not prove lifelong fear. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Deliberately induced child distress and inadequate protection/response removal raise harm and consent concerns.", "[2] An uncontrolled single-child study with variable observations limits causal isolation and generalisation.", "[2] The observed record does not establish a lifetime course; later outcome claims require evidence beyond the procedure."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which conclusion fits the designated Little Albert evidence most cautiously?", "o": ["Observed fear-related responses were interpreted through conditioning, with major ethical and methodological limits.", "The study proved permanent fear in every infant.", "It used a large randomly sampled adult population.", "It measured token-economy reinforcement schedules."], "a": 0, "why": "The evidence is a limited single-child historical study, not a universal or lifelong demonstration."}, {"q": "Food eliciting unlearned salivation is what?", "o": ["UCS", "CR", "CS after tone training", "Only NS"], "a": 0, "why": "Food is stimulus, salivation to it is response."}, {"q": "Tone eliciting learned salivation becomes what?", "o": ["CS", "UCR", "Always UCS", "A motor consequence"], "a": 0, "why": "Its status changes through association."}, {"q": "Extinction uses what arrangement?", "o": ["CS repeatedly without UCS", "CS removed forever only", "New direct punishment only", "More UCS pairing"], "a": 0, "why": "The predictive relationship changes."}, {"q": "Response returns after a rest without new pairing: what?", "o": ["Spontaneous recovery", "Acquisition only", "Reinforcement schedule", "Accommodation"], "a": 0, "why": "Prior learning can reappear after extinction."}, {"q": "Little Albert used which pairing?", "o": ["Rat with loud noise", "Food with lever", "Doll with token", "Visual field with surgery"], "a": 0, "why": "The study examined learned fear and generalisation."}], "subtitle": "18 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-operant-consequences", "title": "Operant conditioning: antecedents and consequences", "scope": "Classify the function using future behaviour · Negative reinforcement is not punishment · Antecedent, behaviour and consequence form a sequence", "parent": "psy12-operant", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Operant conditioning: antecedents and consequences</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Classify the function using future behaviour</li><li>Negative reinforcement is not punishment</li><li>Antecedent, behaviour and consequence form a sequence</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Consequences and the three-phase model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Operant conditioning concerns changes in the likelihood of behaviour because of its consequences. The three-phase model distinguishes antecedent, behaviour and consequence. The antecedent is the preceding cue or context in which the behaviour occurs; the behaviour is the action; the consequence follows the action and influences future responding. A displayed instruction, completing a task and receiving feedback are separate phases. A consequence is identified by its effect on later behaviour, not simply by the researcher’s intention. Reinforcement increases future behaviour; punishment decreases it. This account differs from classical conditioning’s association between stimuli eliciting a response, though the processes can interact. Apply all three phases to the same target behaviour rather than changing the action halfway through the explanation.", "diagram": "abc"}, {"title": "Positive reinforcement", "kind": "learn", "text": "Positive reinforcement increases the future likelihood of a behaviour through addition of a consequence. Positive means added, not morally good. If completing practice leads to valued feedback and completion becomes more frequent, the feedback functions as positive reinforcement. Calling something a reward does not prove it reinforced behaviour; the effect must be considered. Different people may value different consequences, and timing or consistency can matter. A question may ask you to predict a reinforcing effect, in which case make that prediction explicit. State the behaviour, added consequence and expected increase. Do not label praise as reinforcement merely because it follows a task if there is no evidence or stated assumption that the task becomes more likely.", "diagram": "consequences"}, {"title": "Negative reinforcement", "kind": "learn", "text": "Negative reinforcement increases behaviour through removal or avoidance of an aversive condition. Negative means removed. A person fastening a seatbelt to stop an irritating warning sound may become more likely to fasten it promptly. The target behaviour increases, so this is reinforcement rather than punishment. Escaping a pressure-producing situation can also reinforce avoidance, even if that pattern creates longer-term difficulties. Specify what is removed and which action increases. Confusing negative reinforcement with a penalty is one of the most common exam errors. Use a two-question check: does the consequence increase or decrease the action, and is something added or removed? The intended moral value of the action does not determine the classification.", "diagram": "consequences"}, {"title": "Positive and negative punishment", "kind": "learn", "text": "Positive punishment reduces a behaviour by adding an aversive consequence. Negative punishment reduces a behaviour by removing something valued, sometimes described as response cost. For example, if an added penalty reduces rule-breaking, it is positive punishment; if loss of a valued privilege reduces it, it is negative punishment. The classification concerns the target behaviour and future effect. A response can function differently for different people, so use the scenario evidence. Punishment may suppress behaviour without teaching a suitable alternative and can have unwanted effects. These are concepts to evaluate, not recommendations for harsh discipline. A good explanation includes the added or removed event and decreased action rather than simply stating that the person was punished.", "diagram": "consequences"}, {"title": "Classify the function using future behaviour", "kind": "learn", "text": "Reinforcement increases the future probability or frequency of the target behaviour; punishment decreases it. Positive means a stimulus is added, while negative means a stimulus is removed. These terms do not mean good and bad. A consequence intended as punishment can reinforce attention-seeking if the behaviour increases. Identify the target action, the immediate change and the measured later effect before naming the quadrant. Without the later effect, a vignette may describe an intended reward rather than demonstrated reinforcement.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Negative reinforcement is not punishment", "kind": "learn", "text": "A driver fastens a seat belt, the aversive warning stops and fastening becomes more likely. Removal of the warning reinforces fastening, so this is negative reinforcement. Removing access to a game after an action, followed by less of that action, is negative punishment. Both remove something but have different effects on the target behaviour. Specify whose behaviour is being analysed: parent and child actions can be influenced differently in the same exchange. A complete answer avoids switching targets midway.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Antecedent, behaviour and consequence form a sequence", "kind": "learn", "text": "The antecedent is the relevant situation or cue before the action; behaviour is the target action; consequence is what follows and affects later responding. An antecedent can signal that a consequence is available without eliciting a reflex in the classical sense. The three-phase model supports a functional analysis rather than moral judgement. Evidence should include repeated behaviour under comparable opportunities, not just a stated intention or one coincidence. Ethical applications favour appropriate positive support and respect for basic rights.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Interactive consequence classification", "kind": "interactive", "model": "consequence"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Four consequences", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Four consequences", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A seatbelt warning", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: A seatbelt warning", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Intent versus function", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Four consequences", "prompt": "Classify a valued addition that increases an action, an aversive removal that increases it, an aversive addition that decreases it and a valued removal that decreases it. Explain positive/negative. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Valued addition with increase: positive reinforcement.", "[1] Aversive removal with increase: negative reinforcement.", "[1] Aversive addition with decrease: positive punishment.", "[1] Valued removal with decrease: negative punishment.", "[2] Positive/negative mean added/removed, while reinforcement/punishment mean increased/decreased future target behaviour."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-operant-q1"}, {"title": "A seatbelt warning", "prompt": "The warning tone stops after a person fastens a seatbelt, and fastening becomes more likely. Apply antecedent, behaviour and consequence, then classify. (5 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Antecedent is the warning/context before fastening.", "[1] Behaviour is fastening the seatbelt.", "[1] Consequence is removal of the warning sound.", "[2] Negative reinforcement because removal increases the target action, not punishment."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 2], "marks": 5, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-operant-q2"}, {"title": "Intent versus function", "prompt": "A researcher calls praise reinforcement although the target action did not increase. Evaluate the claim. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Reinforcement is defined by increasing future target behaviour.", "[1] Intended reward or praise is not enough to establish that function.", "[1] Relevant repeated behavioural measures/baseline are needed.", "[1] The consequence may have had no reinforcing effect for that person or under that arrangement."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-operant-q6"}, {"id": "psy12-operant-consequences-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student completes a task, an annoying reminder stops and later completes tasks more promptly. Apply the three-phase model and classify the consequence. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Antecedent includes the task/reminder; target behaviour is completing the task.", "[2] The consequence removes an aversive reminder, and later prompt completion increases.", "[2] This is negative reinforcement, not punishment, because removal strengthens the target behaviour."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-operant-consequences-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A teacher intends a reprimand to reduce calling out, but calling out increases after the attention. Classify its observed function and explain the role of intention. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The reprimand/attention is an added stimulus following calling out.", "[2] If it increases future calling out, it functions as positive reinforcement of that behaviour.", "[2] Intended punishment is not the same as demonstrated punishment; classification follows the observed effect and requires adequate repeated evidence."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which combination defines negative reinforcement?", "o": ["A stimulus is removed and the target behaviour increases.", "A stimulus is removed and the target behaviour decreases.", "A stimulus is added and the target behaviour decreases.", "Any consequence that someone regards as unpleasant."], "a": 0, "why": "Negative means removed; reinforcement means strengthened behaviour."}], "subtitle": "19 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-operant-studies", "title": "Thorndike (1898) and Skinner (1938): learning evidence", "scope": "Thorndike measured learning through repeated performance · Skinner studied consequences and response patterns · Evaluate control and transfer", "parent": "psy12-operant", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Thorndike (1898) and Skinner (1938): learning evidence</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Thorndike measured learning through repeated performance</li><li>Skinner studied consequences and response patterns</li><li>Evaluate control and transfer</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Thorndike (1898): puzzle-box method", "kind": "learn", "text": "Thorndike investigated learning through consequences using cats placed in puzzle boxes. A cat could escape by performing an appropriate action, such as operating a mechanism, with food available outside. Repeated trials allowed researchers to record the time taken to escape and changes in behaviour. Early responses were varied, while successful actions became more efficient across trials. The study did not involve a cat observing an expert cat demonstrating the solution. The outcome was performance across repeated opportunities to act. Describe the apparatus, escape response, consequence and measurement. The 1898 work contributed to the law of effect and should be distinguished from Skinner’s later operant chambers and from Pavlov’s stimulus-pairing procedures.", "diagram": "thorndike"}, {"title": "Thorndike: findings and law of effect", "kind": "learn", "text": "Escape times generally reduced over repeated trials as effective responses became more likely. Thorndike’s law of effect proposed that responses followed by satisfying outcomes become strengthened, while less successful responses are weakened. The improvement was gradual and supported trial-and-error learning rather than a sudden human-like insight account for the studied behaviour. This contributed to later theories of learning through consequences. However, restricted animal situations, motivation and confinement limit generalisation, and welfare concerns must be evaluated. A graph of escape time should show decreasing time, not automatically increasing speed without labels. Explain how the consequence connects to future response probability and acknowledge that human learning can also involve instructions, planning and observation.", "diagram": "thorndike"}, {"title": "Skinner (1938): operant chambers", "kind": "learn", "text": "Skinner’s 1938 work, The Behaviour of Organisms, used controlled operant procedures, commonly illustrated by a rat pressing a lever that produced food. Equipment could record responses and arrange consequences under specified conditions. Researchers studied how consequences and reinforcement arrangements affected responding. The measured behaviour was an action emitted by the organism, rather than a reflex elicited solely by a preceding stimulus. Controlled recording enabled precise investigation of response patterns. The designated 1938 work is distinct from Skinner’s 1948 superstition-in-pigeons paper, so do not substitute accidental feeding and circling for the requested lever-pressing account. Name the date, relevant procedure and contribution, and evaluate artificial conditions and animal welfare.", "diagram": "skinner"}, {"title": "Thorndike measured learning through repeated performance", "kind": "learn", "text": "In Thorndike’s 1898 puzzle-box work cats initially made varied actions, with escape and access to food following an effective response. Across trials escape latency generally decreased. This supported trial-and-error learning and the law of effect: responses followed by satisfying outcomes become more likely. The gradual pattern is different from assuming the animal suddenly understood a verbal rule. Describe the measured latency and repeated opportunities before explaining the learning inference.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Skinner studied consequences and response patterns", "kind": "learn", "text": "Skinner’s 1938 work used operant chambers to study actions such as lever pressing and their consequences under controlled arrangements. Operational response recording supported analysis of how reinforcement conditions affected behaviour. The apparatus itself is not the theory; the relation between responding and consequences is the explanatory focus. Later complex schedule findings should be distinguished from whatever specific procedure a question supplies. A controlled animal response can illuminate learning processes without directly representing every human motive.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluate control and transfer", "kind": "learn", "text": "Standardised apparatus and repeated quantitative measures help examine response-consequence relations. Restricted settings, animal samples and welfare issues limit simple transfer to complex human learning. Decreased escape latency can be consistent with learning but interpretation must consider opportunity, motor ability and conditions. Explain the advantage of the operational measure and the boundary of the conclusion. The historical work contributes to behavioural accounts without excluding cognition, social influence or biological constraints.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Thorndike and Skinner", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Thorndike and Skinner", "prompt": "Compare the designated 1898 puzzle-box and 1938 operant-chamber work in method and contribution. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Thorndike recorded cats’ escape learning/time through repeated puzzle-box trials and satisfying outcomes.", "[2] Skinner controlled consequences for operant responses such as rat lever pressing, recording response patterns.", "[2] Both informed consequence-based learning; 1938 should not be replaced by the 1948 pigeon-superstition procedure."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-operant-q4"}, {"id": "psy12-operant-studies-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain how declining escape latency in Thorndike’s puzzle-box trials supports the law of effect. Include the behaviour and consequence. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Varied initial actions included an effective escape response followed by escape/access to food.", "[2] Repeated trials showed shorter latency, consistent with the effective response becoming more likely.", "[2] Satisfying consequences strengthen the response, supporting law-of-effect/trial-and-error interpretation rather than assuming sudden insight."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-operant-studies-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare one methodological strength and one limitation shared by controlled puzzle-box or operant-chamber research. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Standardised conditions and defined response/latency measures support repeatable examination of learning.", "[2] Restricted animal tasks do not directly represent the full complexity of human motivation and social settings.", "[2] Species, apparatus and welfare issues require cautious transfer; control helps inference but does not make generalisation unlimited."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What was a key quantitative measure in Thorndike’s puzzle-box work?", "o": ["Escape latency across trials.", "The participant’s life-satisfaction rating.", "The number of words recognised after semantic questions.", "The proportion of infants using a secure base."], "a": 0, "why": "Declining escape time provided evidence of changing response performance across learning trials."}], "subtitle": "13 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-operant-schedules", "title": "Continuous, fixed, variable, ratio and interval schedules", "scope": "Ratio counts responses; interval concerns availability after time · Describe patterns without treating them as guarantees · Acquisition and persistence answer different questions", "parent": "psy12-operant", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Continuous, fixed, variable, ratio and interval schedules</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Ratio counts responses; interval concerns availability after time</li><li>Describe patterns without treating them as guarantees</li><li>Acquisition and persistence answer different questions</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Continuous and intermittent reinforcement", "kind": "learn", "text": "Continuous reinforcement provides a reinforcing consequence after each target response, often supporting initial acquisition. Intermittent reinforcement provides it only on some occasions according to a schedule. Behaviour maintained intermittently can be more resistant to extinction than behaviour reinforced every time, although outcomes depend on history and arrangement. Fixed means predictable; variable means varying around a pattern or average. Ratio schedules concern the number of responses; interval schedules concern elapsed time and reinforcement of an eligible response. A schedule is not defined merely by the reward’s value. Analyse what has to happen before reinforcement becomes available. This distinction is essential when classifying everyday examples that include both actions and periods of waiting.", "diagram": "schedules"}, {"title": "Fixed and variable ratio schedules", "kind": "learn", "text": "A fixed-ratio schedule reinforces after a set number of responses, such as a token after every fifth completed item. A variable-ratio schedule reinforces after a varying number of responses around a specified average. Ratio is about response count. Variable ratio can sustain high, persistent responding because the next response might be reinforced, while fixed ratio often includes a pause after reinforcement. These are tendencies under studied conditions, not exact guarantees for every learner. An example involving a monthly payment is not ratio merely because work is performed; examine what controls availability. Do not encourage gambling as a classroom activity. Use neutral fictional response-count examples and explain how the schedule differs from time-based reinforcement.", "diagram": "schedules"}, {"title": "Fixed and variable interval schedules", "kind": "learn", "text": "A fixed-interval schedule makes reinforcement available for the first eligible response after a set period has elapsed. A variable-interval schedule makes it available after varying periods. Time passing alone is not necessarily the reinforced behaviour: there is usually an eligible response once the interval is met. Checking for a message at unpredictable availability times can illustrate variable interval, whereas a reward after every ten checks is ratio. Fixed-interval patterns can show increasing response rates as the interval approaches, while variable interval often produces steadier responding. Describe the time rule and the response that obtains reinforcement. Avoid treating every regular payment as a perfect operant example unless the relation between response and consequence is specified.", "diagram": "schedules"}, {"title": "Interval availability requires an eligible response", "kind": "learn", "text": "In an interval schedule, elapsed time makes reinforcement available and an eligible response obtains it. If an event occurs automatically regardless of any behaviour, calling it a reinforcement schedule may be misleading. Ratio schedules instead specify response count. For example, a token after ten completed trials is fixed ratio; a token for the first completed trial after ten minutes is fixed interval. The actions may look similar, but the availability rule differs. Explain the rule in your answer and avoid relying only on everyday names such as salary or reward. This precision makes classification more reliable when an exam scenario includes both repeated actions and passing time.", "diagram": "schedules"}, {"title": "Ratio counts responses; interval concerns availability after time", "kind": "learn", "text": "A fixed-ratio schedule reinforces after a set number of responses; variable ratio uses a changing number around a specified average. Fixed interval makes reinforcement available for the first eligible response after a set time; variable interval changes that waiting period. Interval schedules do not normally deliver reinforcement solely because time passes without an eligible response. State the unit—responses or time—and whether it is fixed or variable before classifying the schedule.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Describe patterns without treating them as guarantees", "kind": "learn", "text": "Fixed-ratio arrangements can produce high rates with pauses after reinforcement; variable-ratio arrangements often support persistent responding. Fixed-interval patterns can show increased responding near availability, while variable interval can support steadier rates. These are typical findings under relevant conditions, not an inevitable prediction for every person and activity. Motivation, reward properties and task constraints matter. A pattern alone may fit more than one interpretation unless the actual contingency is known.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Acquisition and persistence answer different questions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Continuous reinforcement supplies a consequence for each qualifying response and can support acquisition of a new action. Intermittent reinforcement occurs only for some responses and can increase persistence when reinforcement stops under relevant conditions. Extinction concerns responding after the reinforcing contingency is removed, not every pause between rewards. Ethical educational applications should encourage useful activity rather than exploit persistence. Compare schedules with equivalent tasks and a clearly stated response opportunity before attributing outcomes solely to the schedule.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Consequence and schedule reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Consequence and schedule reference</h3><p>Classification depends on effect and contingency, not moral meaning.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Added/removed or count/time</th><th>Future pattern</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Positive reinforcement</td><td>Add consequence</td><td>Target action increases</td></tr><tr><td>Negative reinforcement</td><td>Remove/avoid aversive</td><td>Target action increases</td></tr><tr><td>Positive punishment</td><td>Add aversive</td><td>Target action decreases</td></tr><tr><td>Negative punishment</td><td>Remove valued</td><td>Target action decreases</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed ratio</td><td>Fixed response count</td><td>Count-dependent</td></tr><tr><td>Variable ratio</td><td>Variable response count</td><td>Often persistent</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed interval</td><td>First response after fixed time</td><td>Time availability</td></tr><tr><td>Variable interval</td><td>First response after varying time</td><td>Time availability</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Continuous reinforces each target response. Extinction withholds the former reinforcement; shaping reinforces successive approximations.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Four schedules", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Four schedules", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Four schedules", "prompt": "Classify: reinforcement after every fifth response; after variable response counts; first eligible response after ten minutes; first eligible response after variable periods. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Every fifth response: fixed ratio.", "[1] Variable response counts: variable ratio.", "[1] First eligible response after ten minutes: fixed interval.", "[1] First eligible response after varying periods: variable interval."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-operant-q3"}, {"id": "psy12-operant-schedules-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A fictional system awards a token after every fifth completed item; another awards one after varying numbers averaging five. Classify and compare the schedules. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Every fifth item is fixed ratio: a fixed response count.", "[2] Varying counts averaging five is variable ratio: changing response requirements.", "[2] Both are response-based, unlike intervals; typical persistence/rate patterns require relevant evidence rather than certainty for every learner."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-operant-schedules-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A researcher says a fixed-interval schedule gives a reward every minute even when no response occurs. Correct the definition and contrast it with fixed ratio. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Fixed interval makes reinforcement available after the set period for the first eligible response.", "[2] Passage of time alone does not constitute a reinforced response in the stated interval account.", "[2] Fixed ratio is based on a set number of responses, not elapsed time; identify the unit and contingency clearly."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What distinguishes ratio from interval schedules?", "o": ["Ratio uses response counts; interval uses elapsed-time availability for an eligible response.", "Ratio is always unpredictable and interval always predictable.", "Interval always delivers reinforcement without any response.", "Both are defined only by whether a reward is pleasant."], "a": 0, "why": "Response requirement and time-based availability are the key distinction."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-operant-modification", "title": "Shaping, extinction and token economies", "scope": "Shaping reinforces successive approximations · Token economies require a functional contingency · Extinction and transfer need planned evaluation", "parent": "psy12-operant", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Shaping, extinction and token economies</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Shaping reinforces successive approximations</li><li>Token economies require a functional contingency</li><li>Extinction and transfer need planned evaluation</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Extinction, shaping and evaluation", "kind": "learn", "text": "In operant extinction, a previously reinforcing consequence no longer follows the target response and responding eventually declines, sometimes after a temporary increase. This differs from punishment because no new penalty is necessarily introduced. Shaping reinforces successive approximations towards a target action, making complex skills possible without requiring the full action initially. These ideas help apply learning principles, but behaviour also depends on cognition, context and competing consequences. An improvement that increases reinforcement may fail if it reinforces the wrong action or is inconsistent. Evaluation should consider measurement of the target behaviour and longer-term generalisation, not only a short-term change. Distinguish observed response rates from a claim about someone’s underlying motivation or values.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Token economies and transfer beyond tokens", "kind": "learn", "text": "A token economy gives tokens contingent on clearly defined target behaviours, with tokens later exchanged for valued outcomes. Tokens function as conditioned reinforcers through their association with those outcomes. A plan needs clear criteria, timely feedback, fair access and monitoring of both intended and unintended effects. It can increase selected behaviour, but dependence on external incentives and limited transfer beyond the programme are concerns. A strong application states the target action, how tokens are earned, exchange arrangements and how independent performance will be supported. Do not call unconditional giveaways a token economy. Evaluation should compare baseline and later behaviour and consider what happens when tokens are reduced, while preserving dignity and avoiding coercive deprivation.", "diagram": "tokens"}, {"title": "Shaping reinforces successive approximations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Shaping establishes a target action by reinforcing steps increasingly close to it, rather than waiting for the complete behaviour to occur spontaneously. For a safe educational skill, the criterion can become progressively more precise while retaining clear feedback. It differs from reinforcing every unrelated action or removing all support abruptly. Define the target, the successive criteria and the relevant evidence of improvement. Support should remain fair and accessible, with no withholding of essential needs or services.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Token economies require a functional contingency", "kind": "learn", "text": "Tokens can become conditioned reinforcers when exchangeable for valued, appropriate options. A token economy specifies the target behaviour, token delivery and exchange arrangement. Giving tokens randomly does not establish a contingent reinforcement system, and a token is not automatically a reinforcer merely because it is intended as one. Measure whether the desired action actually becomes more likely. Ethical evaluation considers consent, fairness, accessibility and whether the system produces dependence on rewards or transfers to naturally meaningful outcomes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Extinction and transfer need planned evaluation", "kind": "learn", "text": "If a formerly reinforcing consequence no longer follows an action, responding may decline through operant extinction, sometimes with initial variability or an increase. A change in one setting does not prove lasting generalisation to another. Gradual transfer to natural consequences and continued skill/understanding can matter. Evaluate retention, context and participant experience rather than only the immediate count. A classroom example is for analysing learning; support and rights should not be replaced by punishment-heavy schemes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Token economy design", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: consequences and evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Token economy design", "prompt": "Specify a fictional token economy for practice completion and explain one transfer limitation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Defines observable target completion and contingent token delivery.", "[2] Specifies how tokens are exchanged for valued outcomes, making them conditioned reinforcers.", "[2] Evaluates dependence on tokens/generalisation and plans support for independent performance as incentives fade."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-operant-q5"}, {"title": "Extended response: consequences and evidence", "prompt": "Explain operant learning, compare all four consequence types and schedules, use Thorndike/Skinner evidence and evaluate a token programme. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Correctly explains antecedent/action/consequence and future likelihood.", "[2] Distinguishes reinforcement/punishment with positive/negative addition/removal.", "[2] Separates ratio response-count and interval time rules with fixed/variable examples.", "[2] Describes correct designated study procedures and findings/contributions.", "[2] Explains tokens, contingencies and exchange in a coherent application.", "[2] Evaluates welfare/generalisation or lasting transfer with a specific improvement."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-operant-q7"}, {"id": "psy12-operant-modification-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A fictional reading programme gives tokens for clearly defined practice and allows exchange for an optional activity. Explain how to establish whether the tokens function as reinforcement and assess one ethical issue. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Define the target practice and deliver tokens contingently, with a meaningful appropriate exchange option.", "[2] Compare future practice under suitable opportunities to establish whether it increases; intention alone is insufficient.", "[2] Ensure fairness/accessibility and protect basic rights; optional rewards must not withhold essential education or pressure participation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-operant-modification-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain shaping using a safe skill and distinguish it from extinction. Include one way to assess transfer. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Reinforce successive approximations towards a defined skill, such as increasingly complete accurate steps.", "[2] Extinction removes a previously reinforcing contingency and can reduce responding; it is not the same as gradually raising a shaping criterion.", "[2] Assess the skill later in another relevant context and under natural consequences, not only during token delivery."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What defines shaping?", "o": ["Reinforcing successive approximations towards a target behaviour.", "Giving rewards independently of every action.", "Withholding essential needs until compliance.", "Presenting a CS without a UCS and calling every response operant."], "a": 0, "why": "Shaping concerns increasingly close steps in a response; it is distinct from extinction and noncontingent rewards."}, {"q": "Aversive removal increases an action. Which category?", "o": ["Negative reinforcement", "Negative punishment", "Positive punishment", "Extinction"], "a": 0, "why": "Removal plus increase defines negative reinforcement."}, {"q": "After every fifth response is which schedule?", "o": ["Fixed ratio", "Fixed interval", "Variable interval", "Continuous"], "a": 0, "why": "Ratio concerns count; fixed concerns predictability."}, {"q": "First eligible response after varying time is what?", "o": ["Variable interval", "Variable ratio", "Fixed ratio", "Only punishment"], "a": 0, "why": "Time availability differs from response count."}, {"q": "Which designated Skinner date is current here?", "o": ["1938", "Only 1948 superstition", "1961 Bobo", "1959 compliance"], "a": 0, "why": "The syllabus specifies The Behaviour of Organisms, 1938."}, {"q": "What makes a token economy contingent?", "o": ["Tokens follow defined target behaviour", "Tokens are unconditional gifts", "Only being nearby earns them", "No exchange can occur"], "a": 0, "why": "Tokens link behaviour with later valued outcomes."}], "subtitle": "13 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-social-learning-process", "title": "Bandura (1977): observational learning and motivation", "scope": "Observation is not the same as immediate performance · Vicarious reinforcement concerns observed consequences · The account extends a purely direct-consequence explanation", "parent": "psy12-social-learning", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Bandura (1977): observational learning and motivation</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Observation is not the same as immediate performance</li><li>Vicarious reinforcement concerns observed consequences</li><li>The account extends a purely direct-consequence explanation</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Bandura (1977) and observational learning", "kind": "learn", "text": "Social learning theory explains how people learn by observing models and the consequences of behaviour, with cognitive processes contributing to learning and performance. A person can acquire a response without receiving direct reinforcement for performing it. Bandura’s 1977 account identifies attention, retention, reproduction and motivation as important processes. Observing is not equivalent to automatically imitating. Someone may remember an action but lack the skill or reason to perform it. The theory adds to accounts focused only on direct consequences by considering how information about other people is processed. A scenario explanation should trace the processes and distinguish learning an action from actually carrying it out later.", "diagram": "observational"}, {"title": "Attention and retention", "kind": "learn", "text": "Attention involves noticing relevant aspects of a model’s behaviour. Features such as salience, perceived relevance, status or familiarity can affect what is observed. Retention involves maintaining a mental representation, through verbal or visual coding and rehearsal, so that the action can guide later performance. A student watching a demonstration may focus on the sequence and mentally rehearse the key steps. Mere presence in the room does not establish attention or accurate retention. If a learner misses the critical hand movement, later errors may arise even though motivation is high. Apply the terms to the actual example, describing what is selected and remembered rather than using a memorised list without explanation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reproduction and motivation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Reproduction means having and using the capacity to perform the observed action. Practice and feedback can improve performance. Motivation concerns whether the person has a reason to enact the learned response, influenced by expected outcomes and values. A student may understand a complex dance but lack the coordination to reproduce it; another may be able to do it but choose not to. These illustrate different limits. Learning and performance should therefore be distinguished. If an exam question asks why an observed action was not copied, state which process is constrained and the evidence for that conclusion. Do not assume that non-performance proves nothing was learned.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Vicarious reinforcement and modelling", "kind": "learn", "text": "Vicarious reinforcement occurs when observing a model receive a valued consequence increases the observer’s likelihood of performing a similar behaviour. The observer does not directly receive that consequence at the time of observation. Seeing a peer receive useful recognition for careful preparation can influence expectations about preparing. Observing adverse consequences can reduce performance, sometimes called vicarious punishment. Modelling involves demonstration of behaviour that others may learn. Distinguish the observer’s direct reinforcement after later action from the earlier observed consequence. Reinforcement is not a fifth necessary stage added to the current four-process list. The 2026 syllabus specifically lists attention, retention, reproduction and motivation while addressing modelling and vicarious reinforcement separately.", "diagram": "vicarious"}, {"title": "Observation is not the same as immediate performance", "kind": "learn", "text": "Bandura’s 1977 social learning account includes attention, retention, reproduction and motivation. A learner must notice the relevant model behaviour, retain a representation, have the ability/opportunity to reproduce it and be motivated to perform. Learning can occur even when performance is delayed or not immediately observed. Failing to copy once does not prove that nothing was learned: equipment, physical skill or consequences may limit performance. Explain each stage with scenario evidence instead of listing four terms without links.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Vicarious reinforcement concerns observed consequences", "kind": "learn", "text": "Seeing a model rewarded can affect the observer’s motivation to perform a learned action. This is vicarious reinforcement, distinguished from the observer personally receiving a direct consequence. A model’s similarity, status and relevance can influence attention and identification, but no single feature guarantees copying. If the observer later obtains a reward, both vicarious and direct processes may operate at different points. Name whose action and consequence are involved rather than treating all reward examples as the same learning event.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "The account extends a purely direct-consequence explanation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Observational learning helps explain acquisition of actions without the learner personally performing every step during training. It includes cognitive representation and social context. It does not imply that biological ability, direct reinforcement or individual interpretation are irrelevant. Evaluation can recognise broad application while noting the difficulty of separately measuring internal stages. A useful study operationalises both observed exposure and subsequent behaviour, with clear controls, rather than assuming a video view count proves learning.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Observed technique", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Observed technique", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Vicarious versus direct", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Vicarious versus direct", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Learning versus performance", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Observed technique", "prompt": "A learner notices a demonstration, mentally rehearses it, can perform it and expects a valued outcome. Apply Bandura’s four processes. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Attention selects the relevant demonstration.", "[1] Retention maintains a representation through rehearsal.", "[1] Reproduction uses the ability to enact it.", "[1] Motivation concerns the reason or expected outcome for performing it."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-social-learning-q1"}, {"title": "Vicarious versus direct", "prompt": "A student sees a peer praised for preparation and later prepares. Afterwards the student is praised too. Explain the two reinforcement routes. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Observing the peer’s consequence changes expectations and can illustrate vicarious reinforcement.", "[2] Praise received after the student’s own action can be direct positive reinforcement if preparation increases."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-social-learning-q3"}, {"title": "Learning versus performance", "prompt": "A student remembers a modelled action but lacks motor skill and does not perform it. Explain why non-performance does not prove no learning. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Retention can preserve the action’s representation.", "[1] Reproduction requires the capacity to enact it.", "[1] The observed motor limitation constrains performance.", "[1] Learning and performance are therefore distinct; motivation may also affect later enactment."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-social-learning-q6"}, {"id": "psy12-social-learning-process-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student watches a demonstration, recalls the steps but lacks the equipment to perform them until next week. Apply observational learning and explain why delayed performance does not prove failure to learn. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Attention and retention can occur during observation and later recall of the steps.", "[2] Reproduction requires relevant ability/equipment/opportunity, currently restricted in the scenario.", "[2] Motivation also affects performance; absence of immediate action does not establish absence of a retained representation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-social-learning-process-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A learner sees a peer praised for organising notes and later imitates the organisation before receiving any praise personally. Explain vicarious reinforcement and contrast direct reinforcement. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The learner observes a consequence for the model’s behaviour, which can support motivation to imitate.", "[2] The learner has not yet personally received the praised consequence, distinguishing vicarious from direct reinforcement.", "[2] If the learner later receives praise that increases future organisation, that later event may directly reinforce their action."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which observation best illustrates vicarious reinforcement?", "o": ["Seeing a model rewarded influences the observer’s motivation to imitate.", "The observer personally receives every reward before learning.", "A reflex occurs without any observation.", "A person cannot retain an action until performing it."], "a": 0, "why": "Vicarious reinforcement concerns another person’s consequence as observed by the learner."}], "subtitle": "18 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-social-learning-bobo", "title": "Bandura, Ross and Ross (1961): live-model evidence", "scope": "Use the 1961 live-model procedure · Specific imitation supports the learning interpretation · Coding and controls have different roles", "parent": "psy12-social-learning", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Bandura, Ross and Ross (1961): live-model evidence</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Use the 1961 live-model procedure</li><li>Specific imitation supports the learning interpretation</li><li>Coding and controls have different roles</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Bandura, Ross and Ross (1961): aim and sample", "kind": "learn", "text": "The 1961 Bobo-doll experiment investigated whether children would imitate an adult model’s aggressive behaviour in a new situation without the model present. Seventy-two children from a Stanford nursery, thirty-six boys and thirty-six girls, participated. Children were assigned to aggressive-model, non-aggressive-model or control conditions, with initial aggression ratings used in matching arrangements. Adult models differed in sex. The study examined imitation, not the long-term crime rate of all children exposed to media. The date is important: the designated 1961 procedure is distinct from later studies using filmed models or manipulated rewards. Explain the comparison groups and what behaviour was measured before discussing the learning theory.", "diagram": "bobo"}, {"title": "Bobo doll: procedure and measurement", "kind": "learn", "text": "In the aggressive condition, an adult demonstrated physical and verbal aggression towards an inflatable Bobo doll, including distinctive actions and phrases. The non-aggressive model played quietly, and control children did not receive the same model exposure. A subsequent frustration phase involved attractive toys that were then withheld. Children were later observed with aggressive and non-aggressive toys while the model was absent. Observers recorded imitative and other responses using defined categories. The distinctive copied behaviours were important evidence that observation contributed to learning. Describe these stages without claiming the 1961 study rewarded the model in different conditions; that belongs to later work. Standardised behaviour categories and observer agreement are relevant Science Inquiry features.", "diagram": "bobo"}, {"title": "Bobo doll: findings and contribution", "kind": "learn", "text": "Children exposed to the aggressive model showed more imitation of the model’s aggressive actions and words than children in the non-aggressive and control conditions. The imitation occurred after the model left, supporting the idea that an observed response could be retained and reproduced later. There were also differences associated with child and model sex, but the core conclusion should not be reduced to a stereotype about all boys or girls. The study contributed evidence for observational learning beyond direct reinforcement of the child’s copied action. It measured immediate behaviour in an artificial play setting, not lifelong aggression. Explain how the observed action was distinctive enough to connect the model exposure with later imitation.", "diagram": "bobo"}, {"title": "Bobo doll: strengths and limitations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Controlled model exposure and comparison conditions helped test a relation between observation and later responding. Matching on initial aggression addressed some participant differences, and defined observation categories supported systematic measurement. However, a narrow nursery sample limits generalisation, artificial doll play may not represent aggression towards people and children might infer that the demonstrated behaviour was permitted. Frustration and exposure to aggression raised ethical concerns about protection from harm and children’s vulnerability. Evaluate with consequences: if children treat striking the doll as acceptable play, counts may not validly measure harmful aggression in everyday settings. The findings support a learning process within these conditions rather than proving that all observed violence inevitably creates lasting antisocial behaviour.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Study questions and exact dates", "kind": "learn", "text": "A designated-study answer should identify the named study and date, state its aim, describe the relevant method, give key findings, explain its contribution and evaluate specific limitations. The 1961 Bobo procedure is not the later reward/punishment modelling study; Skinner 1938 is not the 1948 superstition paper; Craik and Tulving experiment two is not a general blend of all ten experiments. This accuracy is central to the SCSA examination advice. In a structured extended response, use the question’s scaffold to organise content and allocate space according to marks. An evaluation should connect evidence to a justified judgement rather than simply writing unethical and small sample. Explain what the limitation means for the conclusion.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Bobo-doll observation and rater agreement", "kind": "learn", "text": "Observers need clear categories to distinguish imitation of specific modelled acts from other play. Agreement between observers coding the same behaviour concerns inter-rater reliability. Blinding an observer to condition can reduce expectation-based judgement where feasible, but agreement alone does not show the category measures real-world harmful aggression. This is a distinction between reliability and validity. A study can consistently count doll strikes while the interpretation of those strikes remains limited. Evaluate both the recording method and the construct claimed. Do not confuse matching children on initial aggression with random sampling from all children or assume either procedure automatically guarantees generalisation.", "diagram": "bobo"}, {"title": "Use the 1961 live-model procedure", "kind": "learn", "text": "Bandura, Ross and Ross’s 1961 study involved 72 children from a nursery setting, with aggression ratings used in arranging comparison groups. Children observed an aggressive live adult model, a nonaggressive model or no model, then were observed in a later setting after a frustration procedure. The aggressive model displayed particular physical and verbal actions towards a Bobo doll. This is distinct from later filmed-model or model-reward/punishment experiments; using those methods would answer the wrong designated-study question.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Specific imitation supports the learning interpretation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Children exposed to aggressive models showed more matching aggressive actions than comparison conditions. The pattern supported observational acquisition and performance, with reported differences related to child/model sex and behaviour categories. These details do not prove that all children imitated equally or that exposure predicts lasting real-world violence. A doll designed to be struck, the frustrating setup and laboratory opportunities affect meaning. State the measured outcome—coded behaviour in the later task—before extending the contribution to theory.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Coding and controls have different roles", "kind": "learn", "text": "Pre-study ratings helped balance relevant baseline tendencies; defined behaviour categories and rater agreement supported more consistent measurement. These features do not automatically remove demand characteristics or make the sample representative. Child exposure to aggression and induced frustration raise ethical concerns. A good evaluation links a particular feature to internal validity, reliability or generalisation rather than using the terms interchangeably. Distinguish evidence that a behaviour was demonstrated from claims about a stable personality trait.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Designated learning studies and dates", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Designated learning studies and dates</h3><p>Keep exact designated studies separate from later experiments by the same researchers.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Study</th><th>Relevant method</th><th>Core contribution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pavlov, 1902</td><td>Signals paired with food; salivation</td><td>Classical learned association</td></tr><tr><td>Watson/Rayner, 1920</td><td>Rat/noise and infant fear</td><td>Emotion conditioning/generalisation</td></tr><tr><td>Thorndike, 1898</td><td>Puzzle-box escape trials</td><td>Law of effect</td></tr><tr><td>Skinner, 1938</td><td>Operant responses and controlled consequences</td><td>Consequence-based learning</td></tr><tr><td>Bandura/Ross/Ross, 1961</td><td>Live aggressive/non-aggressive model/control</td><td>Imitation in model’s absence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For each, prepare aim, method, findings, contribution and methodological/ethical limitations, as required by the question.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: The exact 1961 study", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: The exact 1961 study", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: A doll versus real aggression", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "The exact 1961 study", "prompt": "Describe the Bobo-doll procedure, main finding and contribution without adding later reward conditions. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Aggressive/non-aggressive adult modelling and control were followed by frustration and observation without the model.", "[2] Aggressive-model children showed greater imitation of distinctive aggressive responses.", "[2] Supports retention/reproduction from observation beyond direct reinforcement of the copied action; later reward manipulations are not this procedure."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-social-learning-q2"}, {"title": "A doll versus real aggression", "prompt": "Evaluate the claim that Bobo-doll imitation proves lifelong aggression towards people. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Recorded imitation was immediate behaviour in a specific play context.", "[1] Striking an inflatable toy may not mean the same as harming a person.", "[1] Narrow nursery sample and short observation limit generalisation/lasting conclusions.", "[1] The evidence supports observational learning under those conditions without proving the headline."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-social-learning-q4"}, {"id": "psy12-social-learning-bobo-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain the method and contribution of the 1961 live-model Bobo-doll study without substituting later filmed or rewarded-model variants. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Children observed aggressive live, nonaggressive live or no-model comparison conditions before later behaviour was observed after the specified setup.", "[2] Coding showed more matching aggressive actions following aggressive-model exposure than relevant comparisons.", "[2] This supported observational learning, but measured short-term task behaviour does not establish permanent real-world violence."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-social-learning-bobo-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Evaluate baseline aggression ratings and inter-rater agreement as two different protections, then state one remaining limitation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Baseline ratings used in group arrangement help reduce relevant pre-existing aggression differences.", "[2] Independent agreement on defined codes supports inter-rater reliability of the observation.", "[2] Demand characteristics, a narrow nursery sample, doll/task artificiality or ethical concerns remain; neither protection resolves every inference."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which method belongs to the designated 1961 study?", "o": ["Observation of a live adult model with later coded child behaviour.", "Only a filmed model receiving different rewards.", "A survey of adults’ memories of television.", "A random national sample followed for lifelong violence."], "a": 0, "why": "The syllabus specifies the live-model 1961 study, not later related variants."}], "subtitle": "19 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Memory and learning", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-social-learning-applications", "title": "Comparing learning theories and systematic desensitisation", "scope": "Match the explanation to the learning target · Systematic desensitisation combines specified elements · Evaluate improvement beyond one symptom score", "parent": "psy12-social-learning", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Comparing learning theories and systematic desensitisation</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Match the explanation to the learning target</li><li>Systematic desensitisation combines specified elements</li><li>Evaluate improvement beyond one symptom score</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Comparing three learning explanations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Classical conditioning emphasises association between stimuli and an elicited response. Operant conditioning emphasises how consequences alter future action. Observational learning emphasises acquiring information by watching models, with attention, retention, reproduction and motivation. A student may feel anxious when a previously threatening signal appears, practise more after useful feedback and learn a technique by watching a peer. These examples illustrate different mechanisms within one learning environment. Explain the relation central to the question instead of categorising every learning event by whether a reward is mentioned. An observed reward can involve vicarious reinforcement, while a reward received directly after the student acts is an operant consequence. Theory comparisons require both similarities and clear differences.", "diagram": "learningcompare"}, {"title": "Systematic desensitisation: learning application", "kind": "learn", "text": "Systematic desensitisation is studied as an application of learning principles to phobias. A trained practitioner traditionally helps a person learn relaxation, develop a graded hierarchy of feared situations and encounter manageable steps while practising a response incompatible with strong anxiety. The aim is to reduce the learned anxiety response through gradual counterconditioning and exposure-related learning. This differs from flooding, which involves intense exposure rather than the graded procedure. For an exam application, describe the hierarchy, relaxation and gradual progression with a fictional example. These course explanations are not instructions to treat yourself or expose a peer to frightening situations. Treatment requires appropriate professional judgement, consent and individual safeguards.", "diagram": "desensitisation"}, {"title": "Evaluating behaviour modification", "kind": "learn", "text": "Learning-based approaches can provide clear targets, observable change and procedures that can be evaluated. Token economies may support behaviour in structured settings, while graded desensitisation can address fear responses under professional care. Limits include individual differences, transfer beyond the setting, dependence on incentives and the role of thoughts or broader environmental influences. A programme should measure baseline behaviour, track change and evaluate outcomes after the intervention rather than relying on a favourable anecdote. Explain which learning mechanism is used and why it should affect the target. Avoid judging success solely by immediate compliance if the aim is lasting independent behaviour or wellbeing.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Match the explanation to the learning target", "kind": "learn", "text": "Classical conditioning explains elicited cue responses, operant conditioning explains changes in actions through consequences, and social learning explains acquisition/performance through observation and related processes. A person can fear a cue, avoid it because avoidance reduces distress and copy a model’s coping action. These are different targets within one context. A comparison should use the same scenario while specifying each mechanism, rather than asserting that one theory explains everything and the others are wrong.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Systematic desensitisation combines specified elements", "kind": "learn", "text": "Systematic desensitisation conventionally involves learning a relaxation response and gradually engaging with a fear hierarchy under appropriate professional conditions, using an incompatible response account to address conditioned fear. It is not simply surprising a person with their most feared stimulus or offering a prize after every exposure. The syllabus asks for application and evaluation, not personal treatment design. Explain the theoretical sequence using a fictional case and discuss consent, monitoring, suitability and professional responsibility.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluate improvement beyond one symptom score", "kind": "learn", "text": "A reduced fear rating immediately after a session does not establish long-term transfer, the mechanism responsible or the absence of other changes. Appropriate evaluation may compare baseline, follow-up and relevant functioning, with a suitable controlled design where ethical. Token economies and desensitisation target different processes and have different limits. Evidence of usefulness need not make either universally suitable. State the target, the theoretical mechanism, how improvement is measured and an important qualification.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the research and authoritative explanations", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Further evidence</h3><p>The lesson explanations provide the required student account. These links allow study details and educational health information to be checked. Read historical methods critically; they are not activities to reproduce.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Bandura/bobo.htm\">Bandura, Ross and Ross (1961), original report</a></li></ul>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Systematic desensitisation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: learning approaches", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Systematic desensitisation", "prompt": "Describe the traditional procedure in a fictional professionally supervised phobia case and distinguish flooding. (5 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Develops relaxation/incompatible calm responding.", "[1] Builds a graded hierarchy of feared situations.", "[1] Progresses through manageable steps with the trained practitioner and consent/safeguards.", "[1] Aims to reduce anxiety through counterconditioning/exposure-related learning.", "[1] Flooding uses intense rather than gradual exposure; this is theory application, not a student treatment activity."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-social-learning-q5"}, {"title": "Extended response: learning approaches", "prompt": "Compare classical, operant and observational learning. Use the 1961 study and evaluate systematic desensitisation and token-economy applications. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Distinguishes stimulus association, consequences and observation mechanisms.", "[2] Applies four observational processes to a coherent example.", "[2] Describes correct 1961 comparison and imitation finding.", "[2] Evaluates artificial behaviour, sample and child welfare.", "[2] Explains graded desensitisation and token contingency as distinct applications.", "[2] Evaluates transfer/individual differences and avoids claiming every visible change proves lasting improvement."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-social-learning-q7"}, {"id": "psy12-social-learning-applications-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "In a fictional phobia example a cue elicits fear, avoidance reduces distress and a person observes a calm model. Explain one classical, one operant and one observational contribution. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The learned cue–fear relation is a classical-conditioning account.", "[2] If distress removal increases avoidance, avoidance is negatively reinforced in an operant account.", "[2] Observing and retaining a calm model’s behaviour can support observational learning, with reproduction and motivation affecting performance."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-social-learning-applications-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Describe the conventional learning rationale of systematic desensitisation and evaluate why one lower post-session rating is insufficient evidence of lasting success. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Relaxation and a gradual fear hierarchy under suitable professional conditions apply an incompatible-response/conditioning account.", "[2] Consent, monitoring and suitability are essential; it is not abrupt forced exposure or an unsupervised student treatment.", "[2] Follow-up, relevant functioning and alternative explanations are needed; one immediate rating does not establish durable transfer or mechanism."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why can one scenario involve three learning accounts?", "o": ["Different responses and actions can arise through different interacting processes.", "The theories have identical definitions.", "Observational learning always excludes direct consequences.", "A conditioned fear automatically makes every action a reflex."], "a": 0, "why": "Identify the target and mechanism: cue response, action consequence and modelling are distinct but can coexist."}, {"q": "Watching a model receive a valued consequence is what?", "o": ["Vicarious reinforcement", "Direct reinforcement of observer already", "Extinction", "Pruning"], "a": 0, "why": "The observer learns from another person’s consequence."}, {"q": "Skill to carry out an observed action is what process?", "o": ["Reproduction", "Attention only", "Motivation only", "Reception"], "a": 0, "why": "Knowledge can be retained without the ability to perform it."}, {"q": "The 1961 Bobo study primarily compared what?", "o": ["Aggressive/non-aggressive modelling and control", "Different model rewards only", "Four sleep stages", "Different payments for lying"], "a": 0, "why": "Do not mix later model-consequence procedures into 1961."}, {"q": "Systematic desensitisation traditionally uses what?", "o": ["Graded hierarchy and relaxation under professional care", "Immediate maximal fear as its defining feature", "Only token exchange", "No consent"], "a": 0, "why": "It differs from flooding and is not a class fear-exposure activity."}, {"q": "No imitation necessarily proves what?", "o": ["Nothing definite about whether the action was learned", "Definitely no retention", "Definitely no attention", "Definitely permanent forgetting"], "a": 0, "why": "Reproduction or motivation can constrain performance."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-motivation-regulation", "title": "Self-determination: motivation and regulation styles", "scope": "Extrinsic motivation has several forms · Internal does not automatically mean intrinsic · The continuum is not a mandatory staircase", "parent": "psy12-motivation", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Self-determination: motivation and regulation styles</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Extrinsic motivation has several forms</li><li>Internal does not automatically mean intrinsic</li><li>The continuum is not a mandatory staircase</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Sources of motivation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Motivation concerns processes that initiate, direct and sustain goal-oriented behaviour. Physiological sources include bodily needs such as hunger. Cognitive sources include expectations, goals and interpretations. Emotional sources include feelings that make an activity attractive or aversive. Social sources include belonging, expectations and interactions with others. These can operate together: a student may practise because they value mastery, enjoy progress and want to participate with friends. Do not assume one source explains every action or that a physiological drive always overrides all other motives. Identify evidence in the scenario and connect the source to the behaviour. Motivation is inferred from patterns and reports, so an observer cannot reliably read every motive from one visible action.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Amotivation, extrinsic and intrinsic motivation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Self-determination theory, associated here with Deci and Ryan (2000), distinguishes amotivation, extrinsic motivation and intrinsic motivation. Amotivation involves lacking intention or a clear perceived reason or capacity to act. Extrinsic motivation involves acting for an outcome distinguishable from the activity itself. Intrinsic motivation involves doing an activity for inherent interest or enjoyment. A student reading because the subject is enjoyable is intrinsically motivated; reading to gain a qualification is extrinsic, even if the qualification is personally valued. Extrinsic does not always mean coercive. The theory examines how far regulation is autonomous rather than simply dividing good and bad motives. Specify the reason stated in the scenario.", "diagram": "sdt"}, {"title": "External and introjected regulation", "kind": "learn", "text": "External regulation is behaviour controlled by external demands or rewards, such as studying only to obtain a prize or avoid a penalty. Introjected regulation involves internal pressure, such as guilt, shame or contingent self-worth, without fully accepting the action’s value. A student who studies to avoid feeling like a failure may be under introjected pressure even when no one is currently watching. Both are relatively controlled forms of extrinsic motivation. Internal pressure is not automatically intrinsic enjoyment. An application should identify the actual reason and degree of endorsement. The continuum represents types of regulation, not fixed categories of people or a rigid path that everyone must pass through in the same order.", "diagram": "sdt"}, {"title": "Identified and integrated regulation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Identified regulation occurs when a person consciously values the importance of an action, such as revising because understanding the subject supports a chosen goal. Integrated regulation occurs when that value is consistent with broader identity and values, such as preparing responsibly as part of being the kind of learner or professional one wishes to become. Both are autonomous forms of extrinsic motivation because the action still serves a separable purpose. Intrinsic motivation is enjoyment of the activity itself. This distinction is often missed when all self-chosen behaviour is called intrinsic. Use the stated motive rather than the outward action: the same revision session can be regulated externally, through guilt, through endorsed value or through enjoyment.", "diagram": "sdt"}, {"title": "Several motives at the same time", "kind": "learn", "text": "A learner may enjoy Psychology, value a future career and worry about a grade simultaneously. These reflect intrinsic interest, endorsed extrinsic goals and possibly controlled pressure. The self-determination continuum distinguishes regulatory reasons rather than sorting each person into one permanent box. An exam vignette may emphasise one reason for classification, but an evaluation can acknowledge mixed motives. Integrated regulation remains extrinsic when the action serves a separable outcome, even though it fits identity. A programme’s quality depends on how feedback, choice and social connection are experienced, not merely whether any external reward exists. Measure the relevant motive rather than inferring it from attendance alone.", "diagram": "sdt"}, {"title": "Extrinsic motivation has several forms", "kind": "learn", "text": "Self-determination theory distinguishes amotivation, extrinsic regulation styles and intrinsic motivation. External regulation depends on external reward or pressure; introjected regulation involves internal pressure such as guilt or ego protection. Identified regulation involves personally valuing the action’s importance; integrated regulation aligns it with wider values and identity. Both identified and integrated regulation remain extrinsic when the activity serves a separable outcome. Intrinsic motivation concerns doing an activity for its inherent interest or enjoyment.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Internal does not automatically mean intrinsic", "kind": "learn", "text": "A student revises because passing supports their chosen career: the goal is valued, but studying still serves an outcome beyond the activity, fitting identified regulation. A student revises to avoid guilt may be internally pressured rather than autonomous. A student studies because exploring the ideas is enjoyable has an intrinsic reason. The same activity can have several motives, so do not force a person into one permanent category. Describe the stated reason and qualify the classification to that context.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "The continuum is not a mandatory staircase", "kind": "learn", "text": "The regulation continuum represents relative self-determination, not a guarantee that everyone moves through every style in one order. Motivation can change with context, support and competing demands. Amotivation involves limited intention or perceived value/effectiveness, not merely being tired on one occasion. Investigations need measures that separate enjoyment, value and pressure rather than counting study minutes as a complete motivation measure. An observed action does not establish the motive without suitable evidence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Self-determination continuum reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Self-determination continuum reference</h3><p>The continuum describes regulatory reasons, not fixed people or compulsory steps.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Motivation/regulation</th><th>Main reason</th><th>Autonomy distinction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Amotivation/non-regulation</td><td>No intention/value/capacity</td><td>Lacking organised intention</td></tr><tr><td>Extrinsic: external</td><td>Reward/demand</td><td>Relatively controlled</td></tr><tr><td>Extrinsic: introjected</td><td>Guilt/contingent self-worth</td><td>Internal pressure</td></tr><tr><td>Extrinsic: identified</td><td>Personally endorsed importance</td><td>More autonomous</td></tr><tr><td>Extrinsic: integrated</td><td>Coherent with wider identity</td><td>Autonomous, still separable outcome</td></tr><tr><td>Intrinsic</td><td>Interest/enjoyment of activity</td><td>Activity itself valued</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Psychological needs are autonomy, competence and relatedness. Do not equate autonomy with isolation or integrated regulation with intrinsic enjoyment.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Regulatory styles", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Regulatory styles", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Regulatory styles", "prompt": "Match studying for a prize, to avoid guilt, for an endorsed career goal, as part of personal identity and for enjoyment. Explain which remain extrinsic. (7 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Prize: external regulation.", "[1] Guilt: introjected regulation.", "[1] Endorsed goal: identified regulation.", "[1] Identity-consistent goal: integrated regulation.", "[1] Enjoyment of the activity: intrinsic motivation.", "[2] Identified and integrated remain extrinsic when the action serves a separable outcome, despite autonomy."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2], "marks": 7, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-motivation-q1"}, {"id": "psy12-motivation-regulation-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student practices because of a prize, because of guilt and because the skill supports a valued career. Classify each stated reason using self-determination theory. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Prize-contingent practice fits external regulation.", "[2] Guilt/internal pressure fits introjected regulation.", "[2] Personally valuing its career importance fits identified regulation, still extrinsic because of the separable outcome."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-motivation-regulation-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A student says a task fits their identity but finds it uninteresting. Explain integrated versus intrinsic motivation and evaluate whether the continuum predicts a required next stage. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Alignment with wider identity/values is consistent with integrated extrinsic regulation.", "[2] Intrinsic motivation requires inherent enjoyment/interest, which is not established here.", "[2] The continuum describes relative self-determination, not a compulsory sequence or automatic next-stage prediction."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which reason is identified regulation?", "o": ["I value this task because it supports an important personal goal.", "I do it only for a prize.", "I do it to avoid guilt.", "I find the activity inherently enjoyable, independent of another outcome."], "a": 0, "why": "Identified regulation values the action’s importance while retaining a separable goal."}], "subtitle": "17 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-motivation-needs", "title": "Autonomy, competence and relatedness in practice", "scope": "Autonomy is volition rather than isolation · Support needs through the task design · Evaluate the intervention and the measure", "parent": "psy12-motivation", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Autonomy, competence and relatedness in practice</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Autonomy is volition rather than isolation</li><li>Support needs through the task design</li><li>Evaluate the intervention and the measure</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Autonomy, competence and relatedness", "kind": "learn", "text": "Autonomy involves experiencing choice and endorsement in one’s actions, rather than simply working alone or having no rules. Competence involves feeling effective and able to make progress through appropriate challenges and feedback. Relatedness involves feeling connected, respected and cared for. The theory proposes that supporting these psychological needs encourages more self-determined motivation and wellbeing. A student can experience autonomy within clear expectations if meaningful choices and reasons are provided. Feedback that explains a next step can support competence; respectful collaboration can support relatedness. Explain which feature supports which need and how that relates to motivation. Giving an unmanageable task without support is not genuine competence support merely because it is challenging.", "diagram": "needs"}, {"title": "Self-determination theory: strengths and limits", "kind": "learn", "text": "The theory distinguishes the quality of motivation and explains why meaningful choice, effective feedback and connection can support engagement. It provides practical predictions that can be tested through measures and interventions. However, people can hold several motives at once, self-reports may be influenced by expectations and the expression of autonomy or belonging differs across contexts. External incentives do not have one universal effect; their meaning and arrangement matter. Evaluate a scenario using the actual evidence rather than assuming a prize always destroys interest. A research design should measure motivation and behaviour over time and distinguish reported enjoyment from performance. The model is useful for explaining patterns but does not guarantee that one classroom change produces identical outcomes for every student.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Designing a motivation investigation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A hypothetical study could compare identical revision tasks with meaningful choice of examples versus assigned examples, measuring reported autonomy and completion. Operationalise choice and the outcome, allocate participants randomly if feasible and standardise task difficulty and feedback. A higher completion rate alone does not prove intrinsic motivation because other motives may contribute. Include an appropriate self-report measure and recognise its limitations. The research should preserve voluntary participation and avoid depriving students of learning support. A conclusion should describe the measured effect in the studied sample and conditions. Connect the design to the theory by explaining why endorsed choice could support autonomy, rather than simply writing that choice is always better.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Autonomy is volition rather than isolation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Autonomy concerns experiencing actions as chosen or endorsed, not doing everything alone or rejecting all guidance. Competence concerns feeling effective and able to develop mastery, not superiority over others. Relatedness concerns connection and belonging, not popularity alone. Clear structure and supportive feedback can coexist with autonomy when people understand the rationale and have meaningful choices. A nominal choice between equally coercive options may not support autonomy. Apply the actual experience rather than counting the number of options offered.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Support needs through the task design", "kind": "learn", "text": "A learning task can offer an understandable purpose and choice of an appropriate approach, clear achievable challenges and feedback, and respectful collaboration. These features can support autonomy, competence and relatedness respectively. A difficult task is not necessarily damaging if support helps progress; praise without useful feedback may not build competence. Relatedness can involve reliable respectful relationships, even in an individual activity. A strong application connects each design feature to a need and a possible motivational consequence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluate the intervention and the measure", "kind": "learn", "text": "Self-determination theory provides a useful framework linking need support and motivation, but outcomes depend on context and measures. A questionnaire and attendance record measure different things. Comparing an autonomy-supportive class with another taught by a different teacher can confound the approach with teacher style or prior class differences. Random allocation where feasible, clear implementation and suitable baseline measures strengthen evaluation. Greater participation alone does not prove intrinsic enjoyment or that all three needs were satisfied.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Three psychological needs", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Three psychological needs", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Intrinsic is not every choice", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Choice experiment", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Three psychological needs", "prompt": "A course offers meaningful topic choice, useful achievable feedback and respectful collaboration. Apply self-determination needs. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Choice with reasons supports autonomy or endorsed action.", "[2] Effective challenge/feedback supports competence and progress.", "[2] Respectful collaboration supports relatedness and connection."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-motivation-q2"}, {"title": "Intrinsic is not every choice", "prompt": "A student voluntarily revises because a qualification supports a valued career but does not enjoy revision. Classify and justify. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] This can illustrate identified regulation.", "[1] The student consciously values the outcome.", "[1] It remains extrinsic because revision serves a separable qualification/career purpose.", "[1] Voluntary action alone does not establish intrinsic enjoyment of the activity."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-motivation-q4"}, {"title": "Choice experiment", "prompt": "Design a brief low-risk study of meaningful choice and completion, and explain why completion alone cannot prove intrinsic motivation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Manipulates specified choice versus assigned condition with equivalent task difficulty and random allocation.", "[2] Measures completion plus a relevant private motivation/autonomy report with consent and controls.", "[2] Completion can reflect multiple motives; measure limits and sample/context constrain the inference."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-motivation-q6"}, {"id": "psy12-motivation-needs-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Propose three features of a revision activity that support autonomy, competence and relatedness, explaining each link. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Provide meaningful choice/rationale so students can endorse an approach, supporting autonomy.", "[2] Use achievable challenge and specific progress feedback, supporting competence.", "[2] Provide respectful collaboration or reliable supportive contact, supporting relatedness; links must concern the experience, not labels alone."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-motivation-needs-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A class attends more after being offered choice, and a teacher concludes all students became intrinsically motivated. Evaluate the conclusion and one design improvement. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Attendance is behaviour, not a direct measure of inherent enjoyment or every student’s motive.", "[2] Choice may support autonomy, but teacher effects, novelty and baseline differences can also contribute.", "[2] Add appropriate motivation measures and a comparable controlled design/baseline where feasible; do not infer all needs or all students from one attendance change."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which statement best describes autonomy?", "o": ["Experiencing an action as voluntarily endorsed.", "Never accepting advice from anyone.", "Always working without other people.", "Being superior to classmates in every task."], "a": 0, "why": "Autonomy concerns volition; guidance and collaboration can still support it."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-motivation-maslow", "title": "Maslow (1954, 1970): needs, growth and evaluation", "scope": "Needs describe proposed motivational priorities · Distinguish the expanded 1970 presentation · Compare concepts rather than matching words", "parent": "psy12-motivation", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Maslow (1954, 1970): needs, growth and evaluation</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Needs describe proposed motivational priorities</li><li>Distinguish the expanded 1970 presentation</li><li>Compare concepts rather than matching words</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Maslow (1954): deficiency and growth needs", "kind": "learn", "text": "Maslow’s hierarchy of needs proposes that unmet needs can motivate behaviour, with foundational deficiency needs including physiological needs, safety, love and belongingness, and esteem. Growth needs concern development and self-actualisation, or realising one’s potential. The familiar five-level arrangement is a model, not a rigid biological staircase. A person can pursue learning and relationships even while some lower needs remain unmet. Physiological needs include food and rest; safety includes security; belonging includes connection; esteem includes recognition and self-respect. Explain the relevant unmet need and the action directed towards it. Avoid classifying every reward as esteem without evidence, since the same activity may serve several needs.", "diagram": "maslow"}, {"title": "Self-actualisation and characteristics", "kind": "learn", "text": "Self-actualisation refers to development and expression of capacities and potential, rather than simply being wealthy, famous or perfect. Maslow described characteristics such as acceptance, creativity, realistic appraisal, independence and commitment to meaningful goals among people he considered self-actualised. These descriptions come from a theoretical and selective historical approach, not a diagnostic checklist that students should use to rank classmates. A scenario may show someone pursuing meaningful growth despite no immediate external reward. Explain how the action expresses development rather than just reciting a list of traits. Self-actualisation is a growth concept and can coexist with ordinary difficulties. The model should be evaluated for how it was developed and how well its claims can be tested.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Maslow (1970): expanded needs", "kind": "learn", "text": "The expanded hierarchy includes cognitive needs, such as understanding and curiosity; aesthetic needs, such as order and appreciation of beauty; and transcendence needs, such as commitment beyond the self. These extend the earlier account and should not be silently substituted for the original five-level version. Cognitive needs can motivate investigation for understanding. Aesthetic needs can involve seeking harmony in an environment. Transcendence can involve contributing to a broader cause or helping others realise potential. They are theoretical categories with overlap in real situations. In a comparison, state whether you are discussing the 1954 or expanded 1970 version, then apply the relevant feature. Do not interpret transcendence as a universal religious requirement.", "diagram": "maslowexpanded"}, {"title": "Maslow: evaluation and cultural context", "kind": "learn", "text": "The hierarchy draws attention to material conditions, security, relationships and growth as influences on motivation. It can prevent explanations that blame low participation solely on laziness while ignoring unmet needs. However, evidence for a fixed universal order is limited, and cultural values can shape the meaning and priority of needs. Maslow’s selective examples and broad concepts make some claims difficult to test objectively. People can pursue belonging, meaning or learning under severe hardship, challenging a strict sequential interpretation. A balanced evaluation recognises usefulness as a framework and limitations as a universal predictive law. Compare observations with the model instead of assuming the pyramid proves that every lower need must be fully satisfied before any higher activity occurs.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Comparing Maslow and self-determination theory", "kind": "learn", "text": "Both accounts consider needs in explaining motivation, but their categories and organisation differ. Maslow arranges deficiency and growth needs hierarchically, with later expansion. Self-determination theory focuses on autonomy, competence and relatedness and the quality of regulation from controlled to autonomous forms. Belonging and relatedness overlap conceptually, while autonomy is not simply a separate step in Maslow’s basic five-level model. A student studying for a valued career can illustrate identified regulation; seeking esteem from achievement may also fit Maslow. Compare the mechanisms, not only the number of labels. An extended response can apply both to one case and evaluate whether the provided evidence distinguishes their explanations.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Needs describe proposed motivational priorities", "kind": "learn", "text": "Maslow’s 1954 account commonly presents physiological, safety, belonging/love, esteem and self-actualisation needs. Deficiency needs concern lack or unmet requirements; growth motivation concerns developing potential. Self-actualisation is not simply being famous or attaining one prize. People may pursue several needs at once, and the ordering should not be treated as a rigid rule that no higher goal is possible until every lower need is completely satisfied. The model is a proposed explanation requiring evaluation, not a universal checklist.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Distinguish the expanded 1970 presentation", "kind": "learn", "text": "The expanded account includes cognitive needs, aesthetic needs and transcendence alongside the familiar levels. Cognitive needs concern knowing and understanding; aesthetic needs concern order/beauty; transcendence extends beyond the self. A question naming 1954 should not silently receive the expanded version, while a question naming 1970 needs the added distinctions. Draw and label the version requested. The visual hierarchy simplifies relationships and does not itself provide experimental evidence for a fixed order.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Compare concepts rather than matching words", "kind": "learn", "text": "Maslow’s esteem is not identical to self-determination theory’s competence, and belonging is not a complete replacement for relatedness. Maslow organises proposed need priorities and growth, while SDT examines need support and regulation quality. Cultural context, individual variation and the difficulty of testing strict hierarchy claims are important limitations. Evaluation can acknowledge the useful attention to broader human needs while questioning universal order and operational measurement. A comparison should state both a shared concern and a substantive difference.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the research and authoritative explanations", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Further evidence</h3><p>The lesson explanations provide the required student account. These links allow study details and educational health information to be checked. Read historical methods critically; they are not activities to reproduce.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.selfdeterminationtheory.org/SDT/documents/2000_RyanDeci_SDT.pdf\">Ryan and Deci (2000), original theoretical paper</a></li></ul>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Maslow versions", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Maslow versions", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Hierarchy evaluation", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: motivation theories", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Maslow versions", "prompt": "Explain the five basic needs and identify three additions in the expanded hierarchy. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Basic deficiency needs include physiological, safety, belonging/love and esteem.", "[1] Self-actualisation is a growth need.", "[1] Expanded cognitive needs concern knowledge/understanding.", "[1] Aesthetic needs concern order/beauty.", "[1] Transcendence concerns aims beyond self; distinguish 1954 and 1970 accounts."], "allocations": [2, 1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-motivation-q3"}, {"title": "Hierarchy evaluation", "prompt": "A person pursues meaning and learning while material conditions remain difficult. Evaluate a rigid staircase interpretation of Maslow. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Higher/growth-oriented activity can occur while lower needs are not fully met.", "[1] The case challenges a strict all-or-none sequential interpretation.", "[1] Cultural/contextual priorities and overlap affect motivation.", "[1] The hierarchy can organise needs without being a universal predictive law."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-motivation-q5"}, {"title": "Extended response: motivation theories", "prompt": "Apply and compare Deci/Ryan and Maslow to a fictional learner, explaining regulation, needs and limitations. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Distinguishes amotivation, controlled/autonomous extrinsic and intrinsic motives.", "[2] Applies specific regulatory styles without calling integrated intrinsically enjoyable.", "[2] Applies autonomy, competence and relatedness to support features.", "[2] Explains deficiency/growth and expanded Maslow needs accurately.", "[2] Makes an explicit model comparison in the same case.", "[2] Evaluates self-report/multiple motives and rigid/culturally universal hierarchy claims."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-motivation-q7"}, {"id": "psy12-motivation-maslow-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student pursues a creative goal while experiencing financial insecurity. Apply Maslow and evaluate the claim that this is impossible under the hierarchy. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Financial insecurity is relevant to physiological/safety concerns; creative development can relate to growth/self-actualisation.", "[2] People can pursue multiple needs, so the example challenges a rigid complete-satisfaction-before-any-growth interpretation.", "[2] The hierarchy is a proposed motivational account with individual/cultural limits, not an absolute rule preventing higher pursuits."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-motivation-maslow-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare the 1954 and expanded 1970 presentations and state one limitation of the hierarchy’s evidence. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The familiar 1954 presentation includes physiological, safety, belonging/love, esteem and self-actualisation.", "[2] The expanded 1970 account adds cognitive, aesthetic and transcendence distinctions.", "[2] Strict universal ordering is difficult to operationalise/test and does not capture all individual/cultural variation; a labelled pyramid is a model, not proof."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which addition belongs to the expanded presentation?", "o": ["Cognitive, aesthetic and transcendence needs.", "Only the four operant consequence categories.", "An assertion that nobody can pursue two needs at once.", "A replacement of all needs with an unlimited central executive."], "a": 0, "why": "The expanded account must be distinguished from the familiar five-level version."}, {"q": "Studying for an endorsed career goal is usually what?", "o": ["Identified regulation", "Necessarily intrinsic", "Amotivation always", "Only external coercion"], "a": 0, "why": "Personally valued outcomes can remain extrinsic."}, {"q": "Acting through guilt without full endorsement is what?", "o": ["Introjected regulation", "Integrated regulation", "Sensory storage", "Pure enjoyment"], "a": 0, "why": "Internal pressure is distinct from autonomous value."}, {"q": "Autonomy means what?", "o": ["Endorsed action and meaningful choice", "Always working alone", "No rules ever", "No relationships"], "a": 0, "why": "Autonomy can exist within structured expectations."}, {"q": "Which belongs to Maslow’s expanded version?", "o": ["Transcendence", "Only salivation", "Only motor cortex", "Withdrawal rights"], "a": 0, "why": "The later account adds cognitive, aesthetic and beyond-self needs."}, {"q": "Which is a hierarchy limitation?", "o": ["A fixed universal order is not well established", "No needs exist", "Everyone must be wealthy first", "All lower needs must be perfect"], "a": 0, "why": "Motives overlap and cultural/contextual priorities vary."}], "subtitle": "19 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-wellbeing-diener", "title": "Diener (1984): life satisfaction and affect", "scope": "Life satisfaction is a judgement, not a momentary mood · Positive and negative affect need separate attention · Subjective evidence fits a subjective construct", "parent": "psy12-wellbeing", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Diener (1984): life satisfaction and affect</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Life satisfaction is a judgement, not a momentary mood</li><li>Positive and negative affect need separate attention</li><li>Subjective evidence fits a subjective construct</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Wellbeing as more than one measurement", "kind": "learn", "text": "Wellbeing concerns how people experience and function in their lives, with several theoretical approaches. Subjective wellbeing emphasises people’s evaluations and emotional experience. Psychological wellbeing emphasises aspects of positive functioning and development. These are related but not identical: a person can report short-term positive mood while lacking purpose or meaningful relationships. A model identifies selected dimensions rather than providing a complete medical assessment. In exam scenarios, use the named model’s components and evidence. Avoid treating happiness as a single universal quantity or assuming that an outward smile proves overall wellbeing. Measurement requires clear definitions, appropriate questions and awareness of cultural and contextual differences.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Diener (1984): life satisfaction", "kind": "learn", "text": "Diener’s subjective-wellbeing account includes life satisfaction, a cognitive evaluation of one’s life against personal standards. It differs from the feeling experienced in one moment. A student may feel disappointed after a difficult assessment while still judging their life positively overall. Satisfaction judgements depend on what is valued and the standards used, so identical circumstances can produce different evaluations. An exam application should state the judgement and connect it to the person’s own assessment of life. Do not classify every positive emotion as life satisfaction. A survey can ask about overall satisfaction, but responses may be influenced by current mood, social comparison or question wording, which should be considered when interpreting evidence.", "diagram": "diener"}, {"title": "Positive and negative affect and balance", "kind": "learn", "text": "Affect concerns emotional experience. Positive affect includes feelings such as enjoyment or enthusiasm; negative affect includes distress or sadness. Affective balance considers their pattern in relation to each other over a period rather than requiring a person never to experience negative emotion. Positive and negative affect are not always exact opposites on a single line: different forms can coexist or vary independently. In Diener’s model, life satisfaction and affective balance provide complementary information about subjective wellbeing. Use the scenario to identify each component and distinguish an immediate feeling from a broad judgement. A short mood diary can describe experience but may not capture all life satisfaction or functioning.", "diagram": "diener"}, {"title": "Life satisfaction is a judgement, not a momentary mood", "kind": "learn", "text": "Diener’s 1984 subjective wellbeing framework distinguishes evaluative life satisfaction from affective experience. A person may judge their life favourably while having an unpleasant day, or feel temporarily cheerful while dissatisfied with long-term circumstances. The time frame and question therefore matter. Do not infer overall satisfaction from one smile or treat one bad feeling as a complete measure of wellbeing. Ask which component is actually measured and explain its relevance to the scenario.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Positive and negative affect need separate attention", "kind": "learn", "text": "Positive affect includes pleasant emotional experiences; negative affect includes unpleasant experiences. They are not always a single perfectly opposite scale: a person can experience both during a meaningful but demanding event. Affect balance concerns their relative pattern over an appropriate period, not the elimination of every negative feeling. Frequency and intensity can be assessed differently. A questionnaire score needs a clear operational definition before comparisons, and a pleasant moment alone does not establish a stable affect profile.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Subjective evidence fits a subjective construct", "kind": "learn", "text": "Self-reported life evaluation is relevant evidence when the construct concerns how people assess their lives. Social desirability, wording, recall period and cultural interpretation can influence reports, but physiological readings cannot simply replace personal judgement. Evaluation should consider how well questions measure the intended component and whether comparisons are meaningful. A useful study can combine repeated suitable reports with contextual information, while retaining privacy and avoiding forced personal health disclosure in a classroom exercise.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Life judgement versus mood", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Life judgement versus mood", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Life judgement versus mood", "prompt": "A fictional person is disappointed today but says their life is satisfying overall. Apply Diener’s components. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Life satisfaction is the broader cognitive evaluation and may remain positive.", "[2] Current negative affect is an emotional experience; balance over a period differs from one moment."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-wellbeing-q1"}, {"id": "psy12-wellbeing-diener-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A fictional person reports high life satisfaction but feels worried during exam week. Explain why this is not necessarily contradictory in Diener’s framework. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Life satisfaction is an evaluative judgement about life, not solely a current emotion.", "[2] Worry is negative affect in a particular period and can coexist with favourable overall evaluation.", "[2] The framework distinguishes components and time frames; one week or one mood does not fully establish overall wellbeing."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-wellbeing-diener-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare positive affect, negative affect and affect balance using a rewarding but stressful project. State one measurement caution. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Enjoyment/pride illustrates positive affect; anxiety/frustration illustrates negative affect.", "[2] Both can coexist, and balance concerns their relative pattern over a stated period.", "[2] Define frequency/intensity and recall period with suitable measures; do not infer a complete profile from one visible reaction."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which is life satisfaction rather than momentary affect?", "o": ["An overall judgement that one’s life is going well.", "Feeling excited for a few minutes.", "Feeling worried during one task.", "A pulse reading with no personal evaluation."], "a": 0, "why": "Life satisfaction is evaluative; affect concerns emotional experience."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-wellbeing-ryff", "title": "Ryff (1989): six dimensions of psychological wellbeing", "scope": "The six dimensions concern functioning · Autonomy and mastery do not mean total control · Growth and purpose can vary independently", "parent": "psy12-wellbeing", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Ryff (1989): six dimensions of psychological wellbeing</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>The six dimensions concern functioning</li><li>Autonomy and mastery do not mean total control</li><li>Growth and purpose can vary independently</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Ryff (1989): six-factor psychological wellbeing", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ryff’s model describes psychological wellbeing through autonomy, environmental mastery, personal growth, positive relations with others, purpose in life and self-acceptance. These concern functioning and development rather than only pleasure. Autonomy involves acting in accordance with one’s convictions; environmental mastery involves managing circumstances and resources effectively. Personal growth concerns continued development; positive relations involve warm, trusting connections. Purpose concerns meaningful aims and direction; self-acceptance involves a balanced, accepting view of oneself and one’s history. These factors can be considered separately and interact. A scenario response should match concrete evidence to the relevant factor rather than listing all six without explaining their application.", "diagram": "ryff"}, {"title": "Autonomy and environmental mastery", "kind": "learn", "text": "Within Ryff’s model, autonomy concerns self-directed judgement and resistance to inappropriate social pressure. Environmental mastery concerns managing one’s circumstances and making effective use of available opportunities. A student choosing a personally meaningful project despite peer pressure may illustrate autonomy. Organising commitments and obtaining resources to carry it out may illustrate environmental mastery. Working alone does not automatically show autonomy, and controlling other people is not the definition of mastery. Contextual constraints can limit what people can manage, so difficulties should not be explained solely as personal failure. Compare the two factors explicitly by connecting each to different features of the case.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Personal growth and purpose in life", "kind": "learn", "text": "Personal growth involves openness to development, learning and increasing understanding of oneself. Purpose in life involves meaningful goals and a sense of direction. A person may learn a new skill as part of personal growth while connecting it to a valued long-term contribution as purpose. These are related but distinct. A goal chosen only to gain a short-term reward does not necessarily demonstrate a broad sense of purpose. An exam answer should show the developmental change for growth and meaningful direction for purpose. Avoid assuming that only paid careers or academic success provide purpose; relationships, community and other valued activities may be relevant. Apply the model without imposing a single life plan on everyone.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Positive relations and self-acceptance", "kind": "learn", "text": "Positive relations involve satisfying, trusting relationships that include care and empathy. Self-acceptance involves recognising strengths and limitations and accepting the self and past experience in a balanced way. Accepting limitations does not mean refusing to improve or endorsing harmful behaviour. A student who acknowledges a setback without defining their whole identity by it may illustrate self-acceptance, while support from trusted friends may illustrate positive relations. Feeling popular alone does not guarantee close relationships. Use evidence of quality and trust. These factors contribute to a multidimensional account of functioning, so one positive example cannot establish that every other dimension is high.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "The six dimensions concern functioning", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ryff’s 1989 model includes self-acceptance, positive relations, autonomy, environmental mastery, purpose in life and personal growth. It emphasises psychological functioning beyond pleasant feeling alone. Someone can face stress while maintaining purpose and growth. Naming six labels is not sufficient application: specify the behaviour or experience that fits each. These dimensions are not interchangeable with Maslow’s levels or SDT’s three needs even where words overlap.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Autonomy and mastery do not mean total control", "kind": "learn", "text": "Autonomy concerns self-directed judgement and resistance to inappropriate pressure; environmental mastery concerns managing everyday circumstances and making use of opportunities. A person may choose their own goals yet struggle with practical organisation, showing different patterns on these dimensions. Mastery does not imply controlling every external event. Positive relations concern warm, trusting relationships; self-acceptance includes a realistic positive attitude towards oneself, not pretending to have no limitations. Explain the construct rather than relying on a favourable-sounding adjective.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Growth and purpose can vary independently", "kind": "learn", "text": "Personal growth concerns continued development and openness to experience; purpose concerns meaningful direction and goals. A person can have a clear long-term purpose while feeling stagnant, or explore new activities while uncertain about direction. Evaluation considers how dimensions are operationalised and interpreted across contexts. A total score can conceal strengths and difficulties in specific areas. Use a fictional profile to practise explanation rather than asking classmates to disclose personal wellbeing.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Six factors in evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Six factors in evidence", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Six factors in evidence", "prompt": "A person chooses according to values, manages commitments, develops skills, has trusting relationships, pursues meaningful goals and accepts limitations. Map Ryff’s factors. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Self-directed values: autonomy.", "[1] Managing circumstances: environmental mastery.", "[1] Developing skills: personal growth.", "[1] Trusting relationships: positive relations.", "[1] Meaningful direction: purpose in life.", "[1] Balanced acceptance: self-acceptance."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-wellbeing-q2"}, {"id": "psy12-wellbeing-ryff-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A fictional student chooses their own goals, manages study resources well and feels their work serves a meaningful future. Apply three Ryff dimensions. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Self-directed choice/judgement supports autonomy.", "[2] Managing resources and circumstances supports environmental mastery.", "[2] Meaningful direction supports purpose in life; explain the evidence rather than only listing labels."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-wellbeing-ryff-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Two people have the same total wellbeing score but different six-dimension profiles. Explain why the profiles may matter and distinguish growth from purpose. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] An overall total can conceal different strengths/difficulties in functioning across dimensions.", "[2] Growth concerns ongoing development/openness; purpose concerns meaningful goals/direction.", "[2] Distinct profiles can therefore have different interpretations, with appropriate reliable/valid measures and contextual caution."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which Ryff dimension concerns meaningful direction?", "o": ["Purpose in life.", "Environmental mastery by definition.", "Only the absence of all negative emotion.", "The fixed-ratio reinforcement requirement."], "a": 0, "why": "Purpose concerns goals and meaning; other dimensions address different aspects of functioning."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-wellbeing-measurement", "title": "Comparing, measuring and evaluating wellbeing", "scope": "Compare subjective experience with functioning · Measurement quality includes interpretation · A before-and-after improvement is not a complete experiment", "parent": "psy12-wellbeing", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Comparing, measuring and evaluating wellbeing</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Compare subjective experience with functioning</li><li>Measurement quality includes interpretation</li><li>A before-and-after improvement is not a complete experiment</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Comparing Diener and Ryff", "kind": "learn", "text": "Diener emphasises subjective evaluations and affective experience, while Ryff emphasises dimensions of positive psychological functioning. Someone can have a pleasant day yet lack direction, or experience difficult emotions while pursuing a meaningful purpose. The accounts can therefore provide complementary rather than mutually exclusive information. An extended response should define the models, apply their components and compare what each reveals about the same case. Do not reduce Ryff to six types of happiness or Diener to smiling more. A comparison needs a relationship between the models, not two disconnected lists. Explain why a study measuring only mood may miss life satisfaction and aspects such as purpose or environmental mastery.", "diagram": "wellbeingcompare"}, {"title": "Connections with motivation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Autonomy, competence and relatedness in self-determination theory can support engagement and wellbeing through more endorsed action, effective progress and connection. These concepts relate to, but are not identical with, Ryff’s factors. Relatedness overlaps with positive relations; competence can contribute to mastery; autonomy appears in both accounts with their own theoretical context. A student pursuing an enjoyable and valued activity with support may report both better motivation and wellbeing. That association alone does not establish which caused the other. Use the theory to propose a mechanism and the research design to evaluate it. Keep named models and dates clear rather than blending them into one invented universal wellbeing theory.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Measuring subjective constructs", "kind": "learn", "text": "Life satisfaction and emotions are often measured through self-report scales or diaries because the person’s experience is central to the construct. Subjective measurement is not automatically invalid: it can be appropriate and reliable when well designed. However, social desirability, memory, wording and temporary context can affect responses. Structured rating questions provide comparable numbers; open-ended interviews reveal meanings and context. A physiological measure cannot replace a question about life satisfaction, since heart rate does not uniquely identify satisfaction. Mixed methods can provide complementary evidence. Explain the measure, time period and purpose. Avoid claiming that an objective number captures all wellbeing simply because it can be recorded by equipment.", "diagram": "wellbeingmeasures"}, {"title": "Cultural context and evaluation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Values and social contexts influence how autonomy, belonging, achievement and emotional expression are understood. A measure developed in one population may not have the same meaning in another. Researchers should examine translation, relevant examples and whether the same dimensions are represented, rather than assuming one scale is universally equivalent. Wellbeing is also influenced by external opportunities and constraints, so a theory should not be used to blame individuals for disadvantage. A balanced evaluation can recognise that multidimensional models help organise evidence while acknowledging self-report and cultural limitations. In an exam, link the limitation to validity or generalisation and propose a specific improvement, such as culturally informed piloting and multiple relevant measures.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A scenario-based wellbeing response", "kind": "learn", "text": "Suppose a fictional learner reports enjoyment with friends but no meaningful goals and little confidence in managing commitments. Diener’s model draws attention to positive affect and the need for a separate life-satisfaction judgement. Ryff’s model highlights positive relations alongside possible limitations in purpose and environmental mastery. Do not conclude that the person has a mental illness from these details. The answer should explain what the evidence supports and what further information is needed. A plan to evaluate a supportive programme might measure the relevant dimensions before and after, compare appropriately and protect confidentiality. A change in a score indicates change on that measure, not proof that every part of wellbeing has improved.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Before-and-after scores and causal claims", "kind": "learn", "text": "An improvement after a programme can reflect the programme, maturation, outside events, expectations or changes in who completes the follow-up. A relevant comparison condition and careful timing help evaluate alternatives, while attrition should be reported. The measure must represent the dimension claimed: a life-satisfaction score is different from every Ryff factor or immediate mood. Ethical research protects private responses and avoids presenting questionnaire categories as diagnoses. A cautious conclusion states which scores changed, for whom and under what conditions. Evaluation connects design, measurement and theory rather than simply declaring that all wellbeing improved because a mean rose.", "diagram": "wellbeingmeasures"}, {"title": "Compare subjective experience with functioning", "kind": "learn", "text": "Diener focuses on life satisfaction and affect, while Ryff emphasises six dimensions of psychological functioning. Both concern wellbeing but do not ask exactly the same questions. A person may feel satisfied while reporting little growth, or pursue meaningful growth during a difficult emotional period. A comparison should use a common example and show what each framework highlights. Avoid claiming either model includes only one feeling or that identical labels across theories have identical meaning.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Measurement quality includes interpretation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A reliable scale gives sufficiently consistent evidence under appropriate conditions; a valid interpretation fits the intended construct and use. Translation, culturally specific examples and response styles can affect comparisons. A high heart rate does not directly measure life satisfaction, while a favourable self-report can be influenced by social desirability. Appropriate mixed evidence can clarify a question but not remove all bias. Explain why the measure fits the component, what may distort it and how a targeted change could help.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A before-and-after improvement is not a complete experiment", "kind": "learn", "text": "Scores may improve because of natural change, regression towards typical values, other events or expectations. Without a suitable comparison, improvement cannot be attributed confidently to a programme alone. Different starting points and selective participation also matter. A controlled, ethical design with suitable baseline and follow-up measures supports a stronger interpretation while still limiting generalisation. Classroom practice should use fictional profiles and supplied data; personal disclosure is unnecessary for demonstrating a scientific explanation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Wellbeing framework reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Wellbeing framework reference</h3><p>Use the named model’s terminology and apply evidence to its dimensions.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Dimensions</th><th>What one measure can miss</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Diener, 1984</td><td>Life satisfaction and positive/negative affective balance</td><td>One mood moment misses broad judgement</td></tr><tr><td>Ryff, 1989</td><td>Autonomy; mastery; growth; relations; purpose; acceptance</td><td>Pleasure alone misses functioning</td></tr><tr><td>Self-determination connection</td><td>Autonomy; competence; relatedness</td><td>Motivation and wellbeing are related, not identical</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Appropriate self-report can be valid for subjective experience. Reliability, context, cultural meaning and causal design still need evaluation.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Comparing models", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Comparing models", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Measuring the construct", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Similar labels, different models", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Cultural measure validity", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: wellbeing and evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 5}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 6}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Comparing models", "prompt": "Explain why positive mood alone is insufficient to evaluate wellbeing using both Diener and Ryff. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Diener includes life satisfaction and affective balance, so one positive moment misses broader evaluation/pattern.", "[2] Ryff includes functioning such as purpose, mastery and relations rather than pleasure alone.", "[2] The models provide complementary evidence; a single mood measure does not establish all dimensions."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-wellbeing-q3"}, {"title": "Measuring the construct", "prompt": "Compare a life-satisfaction self-report with heart rate as measures of life satisfaction. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A relevant self-report directly addresses the subjective judgement, while requiring bias/reliability evaluation.", "[2] Heart rate is objective but nonspecific and does not validly replace that judgement simply because it is physiological."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-wellbeing-q4"}, {"title": "Similar labels, different models", "prompt": "Compare relatedness/competence in self-determination theory with positive relations/environmental mastery in Ryff. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Relatedness and positive relations both concern connection, within different motivational/wellbeing frameworks.", "[2] Competence concerns feeling effective; mastery concerns managing circumstances, with overlap but not identical definitions."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-wellbeing-q5"}, {"title": "Cultural measure validity", "prompt": "A translated wellbeing scale is used without checking meaning in a new context. Explain a concern and improvement. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Items may not represent the same constructs or norms, limiting validity and generalisation.", "[2] Culturally informed translation/piloting and complementary measures can test meaning rather than assuming equivalence."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-wellbeing-q6"}, {"title": "Extended response: wellbeing and evidence", "prompt": "Explain Diener and Ryff, apply a mixed wellbeing profile and design an ethical evaluation of a supportive programme. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Defines life satisfaction and positive/negative affective balance.", "[2] Explains Ryff’s six functioning factors accurately.", "[2] Applies positive and limited dimensions using distinct scenario evidence.", "[2] Specifies relevant private measures before/after with a suitable comparison.", "[2] Explains consent/confidentiality and limits of self-report/cultural meaning.", "[2] Draws a measure-specific conclusion without diagnosing or claiming every dimension improved."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-wellbeing-q7"}, {"id": "psy12-wellbeing-measurement-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A fictional participant reports high life satisfaction and positive affect but limited personal growth. Compare Diener and Ryff interpretations. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Diener’s satisfaction and affect components support a favourable subjective-wellbeing interpretation for the measured period.", "[2] Ryff’s personal-growth dimension highlights a functioning concern not resolved by positive feelings alone.", "[2] The frameworks overlap but emphasise different components; a full judgement needs the other relevant dimensions and suitable measures."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-wellbeing-measurement-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A voluntary wellbeing programme has higher average scores after four weeks. Explain two alternative causes and propose a design improvement. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Natural recovery/time or other events can alter scores without a programme effect.", "[2] Expectations, selective participation or regression towards usual levels can also contribute.", "[2] Use a suitable ethical comparison with baseline/follow-up and appropriate allocation/measurement where feasible, retaining sampling and causal limits."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What does reliable measurement establish by itself?", "o": ["Consistency under appropriate conditions, not automatic validity of every interpretation.", "That a programme necessarily caused all change.", "That a scale means the same thing in every culture.", "That personal disclosure is required for learning."], "a": 0, "why": "Reliability and validity are separate; consistency alone does not settle meaning or causation."}, {"q": "Broad cognitive evaluation of life is what?", "o": ["Life satisfaction", "One moment of positive affect", "Heart rate", "Only implicit memory"], "a": 0, "why": "Diener distinguishes judgement from emotional experience."}, {"q": "Managing circumstances in Ryff is what?", "o": ["Environmental mastery", "Only popularity", "Rehearsal", "Punishment"], "a": 0, "why": "It concerns effective use of circumstances/resources."}, {"q": "Meaningful direction is what Ryff factor?", "o": ["Purpose in life", "Only affect balance", "Receptor binding", "Compliance"], "a": 0, "why": "Purpose concerns valued aims and direction."}, {"q": "Is self-report automatically invalid?", "o": ["No; suitability and bias must be evaluated", "Yes, all reports are useless", "No measure can be reliable", "Only machines reveal satisfaction"], "a": 0, "why": "Subjective experience can require an appropriate self-report."}, {"q": "Can one positive mood establish all wellbeing?", "o": ["No, models include broader and distinct dimensions", "Yes, a smile is complete proof", "Yes, no other measure matters", "Only for every adult"], "a": 0, "why": "Life evaluation and functioning require additional evidence."}], "subtitle": "22 screens · 7 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-stress-stressors", "title": "Stress, distress, eustress and types of stressors", "scope": "Stress is a response to demands in context · Types and characteristics can overlap · Consequences depend on appraisal and resources", "parent": "psy12-stress", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Stress, distress, eustress and types of stressors</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Stress is a response to demands in context</li><li>Types and characteristics can overlap</li><li>Consequences depend on appraisal and resources</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Stress, distress and eustress", "kind": "learn", "text": "Selye’s response-based account defines stress as a non-specific bodily response to demands. Eustress describes stress experienced as constructive or energising, while distress describes stress experienced as harmful or overwhelming. These distinctions depend partly on meaning, intensity, duration and capacity to respond. Preparing for a valued challenge can involve arousal that supports action; prolonged overwhelming demands can undermine functioning. Stress is not identical to the stressor, which is the demand or circumstance contributing to the response. A scenario should distinguish the event from the bodily and psychological reaction. Do not assume all stress is harmful or that a challenge described positively can never become distressing.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Types and characteristics of stressors", "kind": "learn", "text": "Psychological stressors can involve uncertainty, perceived threat or internal conflict. Environmental stressors include noise or difficult physical conditions. Social stressors can involve conflict, exclusion or demands from relationships. Cultural stressors can involve adjustment or conflicting expectations. Categories may overlap. Source can be internal, such as a worrying interpretation, or external, such as a deadline. Duration can be acute or chronic, and intensity describes the strength of the demand. These dimensions are separate: an acute event can be intense, while a lower-level demand can persist chronically. Apply the categories to specific details rather than treating all stressors as physical dangers or using acute to mean weak.", "diagram": "stressors"}, {"title": "Stress is a response to demands in context", "kind": "learn", "text": "Stress concerns responses when demands are experienced in relation to resources and circumstances. Eustress describes stress experienced as positive or motivating; distress describes a harmful or unpleasant experience. The same event can be appraised differently by different people or at different times. Distinguish the stressor from the response: an exam is a potential demand, while worry and physiological arousal are responses. Neither the event name nor a raised pulse alone fixes whether the experience is beneficial or harmful.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Types and characteristics can overlap", "kind": "learn", "text": "Physical, psychological, social and cultural stressors describe different sources or meanings of demand. Noise and heat can be physical; evaluation and uncertainty psychological; relationship pressure social; conflicting cultural expectations cultural. An event may involve several categories. Duration, intensity, frequency and predictability affect experience, while acute and chronic exposure differ. A clear classification uses the relevant feature in the scenario rather than treating categories as mutually exclusive boxes or assuming all cultural experiences are stressful.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Consequences depend on appraisal and resources", "kind": "learn", "text": "A manageable challenge can promote engagement while persistent overwhelming demands can impair concentration, sleep and wellbeing. Support, control, preparation and recovery opportunities can alter the effect. A stress account should not blame individuals for inadequate coping where demands are structural. Research should distinguish event exposure, reported appraisal and physiological response. These are related measurements but not interchangeable, and repeated evidence is more informative than one snapshot of a person’s day.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"id": "psy12-stress-stressors-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student faces a short presentation and a prolonged noisy study environment. Distinguish two stressors and explain one characteristic that may affect responses. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Evaluation/uncertainty in the presentation is a relevant psychological/social demand.", "[2] Persistent noise is a physical environmental stressor, potentially interacting with concentration demands.", "[2] Duration, intensity, predictability or control can alter responses; an event can involve more than one category."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-stress-stressors-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Two students call the same assessment exciting and overwhelming. Explain eustress/distress and why the event alone does not determine the response. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Exciting/manageable challenge is consistent with eustress, while overwhelming unpleasant demand fits distress.", "[2] Appraisal of demands relative to resources differs between people and contexts.", "[2] Preparation, support or control can alter the experience; distinguish the assessment stressor from subjective and physiological responses."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is the stressor in an assessment example?", "o": ["The assessment demand, distinct from the person’s response.", "The person’s worry by definition of every event.", "A guaranteed identical reaction in everyone.", "A proof that the event is always harmful."], "a": 0, "why": "A stressor is a demand/source; responses and appraisals vary."}], "subtitle": "11 screens · 2 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-stress-gas", "title": "Selye’s GAS: alarm, resistance and exhaustion", "scope": "GAS explains a physiological response pattern · Alarm is more than feeling worried · Compare its strengths and boundaries", "parent": "psy12-stress", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Selye’s GAS: alarm, resistance and exhaustion</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>GAS explains a physiological response pattern</li><li>Alarm is more than feeling worried</li><li>Compare its strengths and boundaries</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "GAS as a physiological response model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Selye’s General Adaptation Syndrome, or GAS, models physiological responding over continued stress through alarm, resistance and exhaustion. The account emphasises a general pattern rather than the specific meaning of every stressor. Its value is connecting prolonged demands with physiological costs, but it does not fully explain why individuals appraise the same event differently. The stages are not exact time intervals that every person follows on a fixed schedule. Many stress responses resolve without exhaustion. In an exam explanation, identify the stage and describe the corresponding physiological pattern and demand. A qualitative graph shows a model of resistance, not a clinical measurement predicting the date of someone’s illness.", "diagram": "gas"}, {"title": "Alarm: shock and counter-shock", "kind": "learn", "text": "The alarm stage includes an initial shock response in which resistance to the stressor may fall, followed by counter-shock mobilisation. Sympathetic activity contributes to changes such as increased heart and breathing rates, supporting readiness to act. Other physiological systems also participate. A fictional person suddenly confronted with a demanding task may experience these rapid changes. Explain the two parts rather than labelling all alarm as simply panic. Counter-shock is the mobilisation phase, not immediate return to rest. The account describes a physiological model and should not be used to diagnose a person from heart rate alone, since activity, excitement and other influences can produce similar changes.", "diagram": "gas"}, {"title": "Resistance and exhaustion", "kind": "learn", "text": "In resistance, the body continues adapting to a persistent demand, often with elevated physiological resources while trying to maintain functioning. If demands are resolved, recovery can follow. If intense or prolonged demands continue and adaptive capacity is overwhelmed, the model describes exhaustion, associated with increased vulnerability and impaired functioning. Do not equate exhaustion solely with feeling tired after one busy lesson. Explain the persistence of the stressor and the continuing cost of responding. GAS highlights a possible pattern under sustained stress, but individual health and context vary. A stage label alone is insufficient: connect it to the physiological account and distinguish it from a person’s subjective appraisal.", "diagram": "gas"}, {"title": "GAS explains a physiological response pattern", "kind": "learn", "text": "Selye’s general adaptation syndrome describes alarm, resistance and exhaustion under sustained stress. Alarm includes an initial shock response and counter-shock mobilisation. Resistance involves continued adaptation while demand persists; exhaustion describes depleted adaptive capacity under prolonged strain. The model is not a mandatory three-step course of every short challenge: a person can recover before exhaustion. Explain the duration and response stage given, not merely list the names.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Alarm is more than feeling worried", "kind": "learn", "text": "Shock and counter-shock distinguish an initial disruption from mobilisation of bodily resources. Sympathetic and hormonal responses can support immediate action, but the syllabus model should not be reduced to a single pulse number. Resistance can coexist with outwardly ordinary activity while the body continues responding to demand. Prolonged exposure can increase vulnerability to difficulties. Neither a brief stress response nor a busy week proves physiological exhaustion or a diagnosis.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Compare its strengths and boundaries", "kind": "learn", "text": "GAS highlights bodily responses and the importance of prolonged demand, contributing to a biological stress explanation. Its broad response account gives less emphasis to individual appraisal, coping resources and different meanings of stressors than transactional models. Original animal research and the complexity of human experience limit direct generalisation. Evaluate the fit to the scenario: the model may explain physiological adaptation while not fully explaining why two people respond differently to the same event.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: GAS sequence", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: GAS sequence", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "GAS sequence", "prompt": "Explain alarm shock/counter-shock, resistance and exhaustion during continued demand. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Alarm involves initial lowered resistance and counter-shock physiological mobilisation such as raised heart/breathing rates.", "[2] Resistance involves continuing adaptation while the demand persists.", "[2] Exhaustion describes overwhelmed capacity under prolonged demand with increased vulnerability; it is not inevitable for every stressor."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-stress-q2"}, {"id": "psy12-stress-gas-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain alarm, resistance and exhaustion for a fictional prolonged demand, and why a brief challenge need not reach exhaustion. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Alarm involves initial disruption and counter-shock mobilisation.", "[2] Resistance involves ongoing adaptation during continued demand; exhaustion concerns reduced adaptive capacity under sustained strain.", "[2] Recovery can occur before exhaustion, so the full sequence is not inevitable for a brief event."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-stress-gas-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare one contribution and one limitation of GAS for explaining two people’s different responses to the same assessment. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] GAS explains a broad physiological mobilisation/adaptation pattern and prolonged-demand risks.", "[2] It gives less detail about personal appraisal/resources and the meaning of the assessment.", "[2] Transactional/contextual evidence can help explain differences; physiological patterns alone do not establish identical subjective stress."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which statement is accurate about GAS?", "o": ["Recovery can occur before exhaustion.", "Every short stressor inevitably causes exhaustion.", "Resistance means no bodily response remains.", "The model measures only subjective life satisfaction."], "a": 0, "why": "GAS describes a proposed response pattern under demand, not an unavoidable sequence for every event."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-stress-life-events", "title": "Holmes–Rahe (1967): life events and model comparison", "scope": "The scale treats change as a potential demand · Standard weights improve comparison and simplify experience · Compare stimulus, response and transaction", "parent": "psy12-stress", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Holmes–Rahe (1967): life events and model comparison</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>The scale treats change as a potential demand</li><li>Standard weights improve comparison and simplify experience</li><li>Compare stimulus, response and transaction</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Holmes and Rahe (1967): stress as stimulus", "kind": "learn", "text": "The Social Readjustment Rating Scale assigns life-change units to specified events, representing the adjustment demand associated with them. Events can include both positive and negative changes, since readjustment can be required in either case. A researcher can sum relevant event scores over a specified period to estimate accumulated demands and examine associations with health outcomes. This is a stimulus-based approach focusing on events rather than solely the physiological response. It is not a precise diagnosis or a guarantee that a particular total produces illness. Use supplied values in a calculation and explain what the total represents. Do not invent exact scale thresholds unless the question provides or requires the appropriate source.", "diagram": "srscale"}, {"title": "Evaluating life-event scales", "kind": "learn", "text": "A standardised life-event scale allows consistent scoring and consideration of cumulative change, making it useful for comparing patterns. However, the same event can have different meanings and effects for different people, and cultural or historical context may alter its significance. Self-report can omit events or be affected by recall. Associations with illness do not by themselves demonstrate that the event score caused illness, since other factors may contribute. A score may also miss daily hassles or ongoing conditions not adequately represented. Evaluate by linking a limitation to the measure’s interpretation and proposing a suitable improvement, such as adding an appraisal measure while retaining a clear standardised event record.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Comparing three stress models", "kind": "learn", "text": "GAS emphasises bodily response over continued demand; Holmes and Rahe emphasise the readjustment associated with stressor events; Lazarus and Folkman emphasise appraisal and coping within a person–environment transaction. The accounts can provide different perspectives on one situation. A deadline is a stimulus, raised heart rate is a response and judging resources insufficient is an appraisal. Compare each model’s focus, apply it and evaluate what it leaves less explained. A model of general physiology may miss individual meaning, while an appraisal account can be difficult to measure objectively. Extended answers should use the scaffold and marks to cover all requested models rather than giving a long description of only one.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "The scale treats change as a potential demand", "kind": "learn", "text": "Holmes and Rahe’s 1967 approach assigns life-change units to events requiring adjustment, including some events normally considered positive. The total summarises exposure to listed changes rather than a direct physiological stress reading. Adding supplied values is straightforward, but interpretation must consider the scale’s time frame and original context. A high score does not provide a certain individual diagnosis, and equal totals need not reflect equal personal meaning or resources.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Standard weights improve comparison and simplify experience", "kind": "learn", "text": "A common set of weights allows consistent scoring, but the same event can differ greatly in meaning, control, timing and support. Cultural and historical relevance also affect which events are included and how people interpret them. Retrospective reporting can be inaccurate, and associations with later illness do not by themselves prove that life events caused it. Explain both the practical summary and the missing individual appraisal rather than saying a numerical scale is automatically objective and definitive.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Compare stimulus, response and transaction", "kind": "learn", "text": "The life-events approach emphasises stressors as stimuli; GAS emphasises physiological response; Lazarus and Folkman emphasise transactions between demands, appraisal and resources. These models can illuminate different parts of one situation. A comparison should use a shared example, identify each focus and judge what remains unexplained. The fact that models coexist does not mean all claims are equally well supported. Research evidence and the particular question determine their usefulness.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Stress model comparison reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Stress model comparison reference</h3><p>The models consider different aspects of the same event.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Focus</th><th>Core limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Selye GAS, 1936/1983</td><td>Alarm, resistance, exhaustion physiology</td><td>Less explanation of individual meaning</td></tr><tr><td>Holmes/Rahe, 1967</td><td>Accumulated event readjustment</td><td>Fixed scores miss differing appraisals</td></tr><tr><td>Lazarus/Folkman, 1984</td><td>Person–environment appraisal/coping</td><td>Subjective/dynamic processes hard to measure</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Distinguish internal/external source, acute/chronic duration and intensity. Problem-focused and emotion-focused strategies are judged by context and consequences.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Life-change calculation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Three model focuses", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Life-change calculation", "prompt": "Using fictional supplied values of 40, 25 and 15 units, calculate the total and explain why it cannot diagnose illness. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Total = 40 + 25 + 15 = 80 supplied units.", "[1] It represents combined adjustment demand in the specified event measure.", "[1] Event meaning and individual/contextual resources vary.", "[1] Association with health does not establish an individual diagnosis or inevitable causal outcome."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-stress-q4"}, {"title": "Three model focuses", "prompt": "Compare stress as response, stimulus and transaction using a presentation scenario. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] GAS focuses on physiological response over sustained demand.", "[2] Stimulus approach concerns the event/readjustment demands.", "[2] Transactional approach concerns appraisal of the presentation relative to coping resources."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-stress-q5"}, {"id": "psy12-stress-life-events-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Using fictional supplied weights, an event scores 40 units and another 25 within the stated period. Calculate the total and evaluate what it does and does not establish. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Add 40 + 25 = 65 life-change units for the supplied events/period.", "[2] The total summarises listed adjustment demands under the scale’s weights.", "[2] It is not a direct stress response or certain diagnosis; appraisal, support, reporting and context can vary."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-stress-life-events-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare how a life-events scale, GAS and a transactional model would explain moving house. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A life-events scale treats the move as an adjustment-demand stimulus with an assigned weight.", "[2] GAS addresses physiological mobilisation/adaptation if the demand persists.", "[2] A transactional account examines appraisal of the move and available resources; the approaches address different aspects and have limits."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What does a life-change score primarily summarise?", "o": ["Exposure to listed adjustment demands using assigned weights.", "A confirmed diagnosis for every individual.", "A direct readout of cortical electrical activity.", "The exact appraisal shared by all people with that score."], "a": 0, "why": "The stimulus-focused summary does not replace individual appraisal or clinical evidence."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-stress-appraisal", "title": "Lazarus–Folkman (1984): appraisal, coping and reappraisal", "scope": "Primary and secondary appraisal ask different questions · Coping is classified by its target · Reappraisal updates the transaction", "parent": "psy12-stress", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Lazarus–Folkman (1984): appraisal, coping and reappraisal</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Primary and secondary appraisal ask different questions</li><li>Coping is classified by its target</li><li>Reappraisal updates the transaction</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Lazarus and Folkman (1984): transaction", "kind": "learn", "text": "The transactional theory of stress and coping considers an ongoing relationship between a person and their environment. Stress depends on appraisal of demands in relation to coping resources, rather than the event alone. Primary appraisal considers whether the situation is irrelevant, benign-positive or stressful, with stressful appraisals including harm/loss, threat or challenge. Secondary appraisal concerns available resources and options for coping. These appraisals interact and can change as new information arrives. Primary does not mean more important and secondary does not mean a final stage after stress ends. In a scenario, describe how the person interprets the demand and what resources they believe they can use.", "diagram": "appraisal"}, {"title": "Primary and secondary appraisal in context", "kind": "learn", "text": "Two students facing the same presentation may appraise it differently. One may interpret it as a challenge and identify preparation time and supportive practice as resources. Another may see threat and believe they lack time or support. The transactional model explains why an identical external demand can produce different responses. Reappraisal occurs when new information or coping changes the perceived relationship. A successful rehearsal may reduce perceived threat. Explain the evidence for each appraisal instead of assuming that confidence alone is secondary appraisal. The model is a framework for understanding subjective interpretation, so research commonly relies on reports that need valid wording, timing and evaluation.", "diagram": "appraisal"}, {"title": "Problem-focused and emotion-focused coping", "kind": "learn", "text": "Problem-focused coping acts on the demand or its source, such as planning revision, requesting clarification or changing an impractical schedule. Emotion-focused coping manages emotional responses, such as using calming strategies, acceptance or seeking emotional support. Neither is always superior. If the situation can be changed, problem-focused action may reduce the demand; if it cannot be immediately changed, emotion-focused strategies can help manage experience. Some strategies have both functions. Explain the actual target of the strategy and why it fits the scenario. Avoid classifying every conversation as emotion-focused if it is being used to obtain practical information that changes the problem.", "diagram": "coping"}, {"title": "Adaptive, maladaptive and health consequences", "kind": "learn", "text": "Adaptive coping supports functioning without creating greater longer-term problems. Maladaptive coping may offer immediate relief while worsening demands or health, such as persistent avoidance that allows work to accumulate. The usefulness of a strategy depends on context, duration and consequences, rather than a simple label attached to all instances of one action. Chronic stress can be associated with changes in sleep, attention, mood and health-related behaviours, but illness has multiple influences and associations are not a diagnosis. An exam explanation should describe the pathway between coping and consequences. A fictional learner who avoids a task experiences short-term relief but greater later workload, illustrating why an immediate benefit may not make a strategy adaptive overall.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reappraisal and changing controllability", "kind": "learn", "text": "Appraisal is ongoing. A person who first views a demand as threatening may revise that judgement after obtaining information or support. Secondary appraisal can identify a practical option, and acting on it can change both the environment and later primary appraisal. Coping can combine functions: asking for advice may provide emotional reassurance and practical information. Classify the strategy by the stated target and explain overlap if justified. An uncontrollable circumstance may call for emotional management while a changeable workload permits practical planning. Avoid claiming problem-focused coping always succeeds or emotion-focused coping is merely avoidance. Evidence about context and outcomes is central to evaluation.", "diagram": "appraisal"}, {"title": "Primary and secondary appraisal ask different questions", "kind": "learn", "text": "In Lazarus and Folkman’s 1984 model, primary appraisal concerns whether an event is irrelevant, benign-positive or stressful, including harm/loss, threat or challenge. Secondary appraisal concerns available coping resources and options. They interact and can change as new information appears. Primary does not mean only the first chronological thought forever; appraisal can recur. Identify both the event’s meaning and perceived ability to respond when explaining why similar demands affect people differently.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Coping is classified by its target", "kind": "learn", "text": "Problem-focused coping aims to alter the demand or its management, such as clarifying requirements and organising a workload. Emotion-focused coping aims to regulate the emotional response, such as appropriate support or relaxation. Both can be useful together; emotion-focused does not mean avoidance or failure, and problem-focused does not guarantee that an uncontrollable event changes. Adaptive or maladaptive describes effects in context, not simply the category name. A strategy’s short-term relief can coexist with longer-term costs.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reappraisal updates the transaction", "kind": "learn", "text": "After receiving clear instructions and support, an assessment may be reappraised from overwhelming threat towards manageable challenge. If the event cannot be changed, appropriate emotional regulation and support can still matter. Evaluation should consider whether a proposed strategy fits controllability and whether it improves functioning over time. A before-and-after rating alone does not isolate which component caused change. Student examples use fictional situations; the learning model is not a substitute for individual counselling or a requirement to disclose personal distress.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Appraisal distinction", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Appraisal distinction", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Coping target", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Coping target", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Avoidance consequences", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: stress and inquiry", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Appraisal distinction", "prompt": "A deadline is judged threatening, then the person assesses available time and support. Apply primary and secondary appraisal. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Primary appraisal concerns meaning as threat/challenge/harm or irrelevance/benign-positive.", "[2] Secondary appraisal assesses resources and coping options, such as time and support; they interact and can change."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-stress-q1"}, {"title": "Coping target", "prompt": "A student reorganises a schedule and separately uses a calming strategy for an unavoidable wait. Compare coping types. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Reorganising the schedule acts on the demand: problem-focused coping.", "[2] Calming during an unchangeable wait manages emotional response: emotion-focused coping; suitability depends on control/context."], "allocations": [2, 2], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-stress-q3"}, {"title": "Avoidance consequences", "prompt": "A student avoids revision and feels immediate relief, but later workload increases. Explain why short-term relief need not indicate adaptive coping. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Avoidance can reduce immediate uncomfortable emotion.", "[1] It leaves the original demand unresolved.", "[1] Accumulating work can increase later stress and impairment.", "[1] Evaluate longer-term/contextual consequences rather than labelling all immediate relief adaptive."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-stress-q6"}, {"title": "Extended response: stress and inquiry", "prompt": "Apply all three stress models, evaluate coping and propose an ethical study using complementary measures. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Distinguishes stressor and response with source/duration/intensity evidence.", "[2] Accurately explains GAS stages and physiological pattern.", "[2] Explains event scoring/readjustment and individual-meaning limitations.", "[2] Applies primary/secondary appraisal and context-appropriate coping.", "[2] Uses relevant private reports and physiological measures without treating heart rate as unique stress proof.", "[2] Evaluates confounds/generalisation and safeguards without deliberately creating harmful stress."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-stress-q7"}, {"id": "psy12-stress-appraisal-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student initially sees an assessment as a threat, then learns the requirements and recognises available support. Apply primary, secondary and reappraisal. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Threat interpretation is a primary appraisal of the assessment’s significance.", "[2] Evaluating information/support and coping options is secondary appraisal.", "[2] New resources can lead to reappraisal as a more manageable challenge; processes interact and can recur."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-stress-appraisal-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare problem-focused and emotion-focused coping for a partly controllable workload, and explain why neither category is automatically adaptive in every context. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Planning/clarifying tasks aims to alter or manage the demand, fitting problem-focused coping.", "[2] Appropriate emotional regulation/support addresses the response, fitting emotion-focused coping.", "[2] Suitability depends on control and consequences; strategies can complement each other, while avoidance or ineffective action can have longer-term costs."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What does secondary appraisal concern?", "o": ["Available resources and coping options.", "Only whether an event counts as a threat.", "The fixed number of reinforcement responses.", "A guarantee that the stressor can always be removed."], "a": 0, "why": "Secondary appraisal evaluates how a person may respond; primary appraisal concerns significance."}, {"q": "Primary appraisal concerns what?", "o": ["Meaning/significance of the situation", "Only resources", "A fixed heart rate", "Only sleep timing"], "a": 0, "why": "Secondary appraisal concerns coping options/resources."}, {"q": "Problem-focused coping does what?", "o": ["Acts on the demand/source", "Only suppresses every emotion", "Always avoids the task", "Only measures pulse"], "a": 0, "why": "The target is the problem rather than solely emotional response."}, {"q": "GAS mainly models what?", "o": ["Physiological responding over continued stress", "Only event counts", "Only private beliefs", "Every memory stage"], "a": 0, "why": "Its focus differs from stimulus and transaction models."}, {"q": "Can positive life change require readjustment?", "o": ["Yes", "No, only negative events", "Only if everyone becomes ill", "No events can be scored"], "a": 0, "why": "Stimulus accounts consider adjustment, not simply unpleasantness."}, {"q": "Acute versus chronic refers primarily to what?", "o": ["Duration", "Only strength", "Always source", "Moral value"], "a": 0, "why": "Intensity and internal/external source are separate dimensions."}], "subtitle": "20 screens · 6 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-sleep-rhythms", "title": "Why sleep? Circadian rhythm and the sleep–wake cycle", "scope": "Evolutionary and restorative accounts answer different questions · Circadian timing interacts with sleep pressure · Environment affects cues and opportunity", "parent": "psy12-sleep", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Why sleep? Circadian rhythm and the sleep–wake cycle</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Evolutionary and restorative accounts answer different questions</li><li>Circadian timing interacts with sleep pressure</li><li>Environment affects cues and opportunity</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Evolutionary and restorative accounts of sleep", "kind": "learn", "text": "Evolutionary accounts propose that sleep patterns can support adaptation to environmental demands, including conserving energy or reducing activity at less advantageous times. Restorative accounts emphasise recovery, maintenance and processes supporting brain and body functioning. Sleep also contributes to memory and regulation, with roles varying across stages. These accounts are not mutually exclusive and neither is fully proved by one observation. Species differ in sleep patterns, which informs evolutionary reasoning, while changes during recovery support restorative ideas. An exam comparison should state what each account explains and recognise limits. Avoid saying sleep exists only because people are tired or that its sole purpose is keeping all animals safe from predators.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Circadian rhythm and the sleep–wake cycle", "kind": "learn", "text": "A circadian rhythm is an approximately twenty-four-hour pattern in functioning. The sleep–wake cycle is influenced by internal timing and environmental cues, especially light. The brain’s timing systems coordinate patterns of alertness, while accumulating sleep pressure also contributes to sleep propensity. Melatonin is associated with signalling biological night rather than acting as a universal switch that instantly produces sleep. Irregular timing or light exposure can affect alignment between internal rhythm and required sleep times. Explain how a shift worker sleeping during daylight may face both external disturbance and timing mismatch. Circadian rhythm is different from the roughly ninety-minute cycles of stages within a night.", "diagram": "circadian"}, {"title": "Evolutionary and restorative accounts answer different questions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Evolutionary accounts consider how patterns of sleep/inactivity could support adaptation and survival in relevant environments. Restorative accounts emphasise recovery and maintenance processes. Evidence of bodily restoration is not by itself a full explanation of why species have particular timing patterns, while an adaptive timing account does not describe every restoration mechanism. Compare the focus, an example and a limitation. The accounts can be complementary, and neither should be presented as a single proven purpose covering every feature of sleep.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Circadian timing interacts with sleep pressure", "kind": "learn", "text": "Circadian rhythms are approximately 24-hour patterns coordinated with environmental cues, especially light. The suprachiasmatic nucleus contributes to central timing, while darkness-related melatonin signalling supports night-time timing rather than operating as a simple on/off sleep switch. Homeostatic sleep pressure builds with wakefulness and changes with sleep. Shift work can create a mismatch between required activity and biological timing. Explain both timing and accumulated pressure rather than treating tiredness as proof that the circadian clock has stopped.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Environment affects cues and opportunity", "kind": "learn", "text": "Light timing, irregular schedules and activity can influence the sleep–wake pattern, while noise, obligations and stress affect opportunity to sleep. A person working at night may be sleepy during work yet have difficulty sleeping during the day. This is not simply laziness or lack of motivation. Research should distinguish sleep opportunity, actual sleep and subjective alertness. A controlled comparison can support one timing explanation, but ordinary life has interacting demands and individual variation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A shift-work case", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: A shift-work case", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "A shift-work case", "prompt": "A fictional worker sleeps during noisy daylight, uses stimulating phone content and worries about work. Explain three relevant causes of sleep difficulty. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Shift timing can misalign sleep with circadian cues.", "[2] Daylight/noise and phone content can disrupt opportunity or increase arousal/delay.", "[2] Worry-related arousal can interfere with initiating/maintaining sleep; connect each to the stated details."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-sleep-q3"}, {"id": "psy12-sleep-rhythms-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A fictional night-shift worker is sleepy at work but struggles to sleep in a bright daytime room. Explain circadian mismatch and one environmental contribution. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Required night-time alertness can conflict with the usual circadian timing of sleep/wake activity.", "[2] Daytime light provides a timing cue and can make daytime sleep more difficult alongside other conditions.", "[2] Sleep pressure and opportunity also matter; the pattern is not evidence that the clock has stopped or that motivation alone explains it."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-sleep-rhythms-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare evolutionary and restorative sleep accounts, giving a distinct focus and one qualification for each. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Evolutionary accounts emphasise adaptive timing/inactivity in environments and survival-related functions.", "[2] Restorative accounts emphasise recovery/maintenance processes supported by sleep.", "[2] Timing adaptation does not explain every restoration mechanism, and restoration alone does not explain every species pattern; accounts can complement each other."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What describes a circadian rhythm?", "o": ["An approximately 24-hour pattern influenced by timing cues.", "A fixed-ratio response requirement.", "A guarantee that everyone sleeps at an identical hour.", "An on/off switch unaffected by environment."], "a": 0, "why": "Circadian rhythms concern biological timing, with environmental coordination and individual variation."}], "subtitle": "13 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-sleep-stages", "title": "NREM, REM and changing sleep architecture", "scope": "Use the current NREM classification · A cycle is not an equal set of blocks · Measurement combines different indicators", "parent": "psy12-sleep", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>NREM, REM and changing sleep architecture</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Use the current NREM classification</li><li>A cycle is not an equal set of blocks</li><li>Measurement combines different indicators</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "NREM stage 1", "kind": "learn", "text": "The current syllabus uses three NREM stages, N1, N2 and N3, plus REM, rather than the older four-NREM classification. N1 is a transition into sleep with relatively light sleep, reduced muscle activity and slow eye movements. EEG activity changes from relaxed waking patterns towards lower-frequency theta activity. A person can be easily awakened and may experience brief sensations such as falling. Heart and breathing rates generally begin to slow. These are broad patterns, not exact values for every person. Describe EEG, eye movement, muscle tension and physiological features as requested. Do not classify every dream as REM, since dreamlike experience can also occur in NREM.", "diagram": "sleepstages"}, {"title": "NREM stage 2", "kind": "learn", "text": "N2 involves more established sleep, generally slower heart activity and reduced muscle tone, with little eye movement. EEG is characterised by sleep spindles and K-complexes amid the background activity. These distinctive patterns help distinguish it from N1 and the slow-wave activity of N3. A sleep spindle is a brief burst of faster activity; a K-complex is a large transient waveform. EEG records scalp electrical patterns and does not directly show a person’s dream content. In a comparison, name the distinguishing pattern as well as the general physiological change. Avoid using relaxed waking alpha as the defining N2 feature or saying every part of the brain is inactive.", "diagram": "sleepstages"}, {"title": "NREM stage 3 and REM", "kind": "learn", "text": "N3 is deep slow-wave sleep characterised by prominent high-amplitude, low-frequency delta activity, with relatively slow heart and breathing rates and reduced muscle activity. Awakening can be difficult. REM includes rapid eye movements, relatively low-amplitude mixed-frequency EEG activity and greatly reduced skeletal-muscle tone, although essential breathing muscles remain active. Heart and breathing can become more irregular. Vivid dreaming is common in REM but not exclusive to it. The combination of active-looking EEG and low muscle tone is distinctive. Compare features directly rather than claiming REM is simply the deepest sleep or that the body is completely paralysed in every respect.", "diagram": "sleepstages"}, {"title": "Length and repetition of sleep cycles", "kind": "learn", "text": "A typical adult sleep cycle progresses through NREM and REM over roughly ninety minutes, with considerable variation often described around seventy to one hundred twenty minutes. Several cycles occur over a night. Early cycles usually contain more N3, while REM periods generally become longer later in the night. A hypnogram shows stage against time, with stage categories rather than a numerical depth measurement. The sequence is not a perfectly fixed repeated staircase and brief waking can occur. Missing the later part of a night changes access to the later-stage pattern as well as total duration. Explain the overall repetition and distribution without using a schematic diagram as exact data for an individual.", "diagram": "hypnogram"}, {"title": "Use the current NREM classification", "kind": "learn", "text": "NREM stage 1 is a light transition with reduced alertness; stage 2 includes characteristic sleep spindles and K-complexes; stage 3 is slow-wave sleep with substantial delta activity. REM includes rapid eye movements, relatively activated brain activity and reduced skeletal muscle tone, with vivid dreaming often reported. Dreaming is not exclusively REM, and muscle atonia does not mean every muscle is inactive. The current syllabus uses three NREM stages rather than treating older stages 3 and 4 as separate required categories.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A cycle is not an equal set of blocks", "kind": "learn", "text": "Sleep commonly cycles through NREM and REM across the night, with cycles often around 90 minutes but varying across people and the night. More slow-wave sleep is typical earlier, while REM periods tend to lengthen later. A schematic hypnogram represents this pattern, not a guaranteed minute-by-minute schedule. Avoid drawing stages as equal duration repeated identically or claiming everyone must follow the same number of cycles. Explain axes, stage transitions and distribution across time.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Measurement combines different indicators", "kind": "learn", "text": "EEG helps identify electrical patterns; eye-movement recording contributes to REM identification; muscle activity measures provide another relevant indicator. A report of a dream alone does not establish the precise stage. Stage comparisons should name a defining feature and its measurement where relevant. A fictional hypnogram can support interpretation practice, but exact values should only be quoted when the graph supplies them. Sleep architecture differs with age, conditions and individual circumstances.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Current sleep-stage reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Current sleep-stage reference</h3><p>Three NREM stages plus REM is the current classification. Broad features vary by individual.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>EEG pattern</th><th>Eyes and muscle/heart features</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Relaxed waking</td><td>Often alpha with eyes closed</td><td>Waking tone; not a NREM stage</td></tr><tr><td>N1</td><td>Transition/theta activity</td><td>Slow eyes, reduced tone; heart slows</td></tr><tr><td>N2</td><td>Spindles and K-complexes</td><td>Little eyes; reduced tone/heart</td></tr><tr><td>N3</td><td>Prominent delta slow waves</td><td>Little eyes; deeper reduced activity</td></tr><tr><td>REM</td><td>Low-amplitude mixed frequency</td><td>Rapid eyes, very low skeletal tone, irregular heart/breathing</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Cycles are approximately ninety minutes, with more N3 early and longer REM later. EEG patterns are schematic rather than recordings from a real participant.</p>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: NREM and REM comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: NREM and REM comparison", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Cycles across the night", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "NREM and REM comparison", "prompt": "Compare N2, N3 and REM in EEG, eye movement and muscle activity. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] N2 has spindles/K-complexes, little eye movement and reduced muscle tone.", "[2] N3 has prominent delta slow waves, little eye movement and reduced muscle activity.", "[2] REM has mixed-frequency active-looking EEG, rapid eyes and greatly reduced skeletal-muscle tone, while essential breathing continues."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-sleep-q1"}, {"title": "Cycles across the night", "prompt": "Describe typical cycle length/repetition and the changing N3/REM distribution. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Cycles are roughly ninety minutes with meaningful variation.", "[1] Several NREM/REM cycles occur during a typical night.", "[1] More N3 generally occurs early.", "[1] REM periods generally lengthen later; a schematic is not an exact individual schedule."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-sleep-q4"}, {"id": "psy12-sleep-stages-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Compare NREM stage 2, NREM stage 3 and REM using one defining feature for each and a qualification about dreaming. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] NREM 2 includes spindles/K-complexes; NREM 3 has substantial slow-wave/delta activity.", "[2] REM includes rapid eye movements and reduced skeletal muscle tone with relatively activated brain activity.", "[2] Vivid dreams are often reported in REM but dreaming is not exclusive to it; stage inference needs appropriate indicators."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-sleep-stages-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A student draws four identical 90-minute cycles with equal time in every stage. Evaluate the model and propose two corrections. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Cycle length varies and stage durations are not generally equal or identical each cycle.", "[2] Show more slow-wave sleep typically earlier and longer REM periods later.", "[2] Label a schematic time/stage pattern with appropriate limits rather than presenting exact uniform timing for every individual."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which feature is characteristic of NREM stage 2?", "o": ["Sleep spindles and K-complexes.", "The claim that dreaming can never occur.", "A required separate current NREM stage 4.", "Continuous waking-level voluntary muscle activity by definition."], "a": 0, "why": "Stage 2 has characteristic EEG features; current classification combines slow-wave sleep in NREM 3."}], "subtitle": "17 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-sleep-deprivation", "title": "Sleep deprivation, effects and sleep hygiene", "scope": "Acute and chronic sleep loss are different exposure patterns · Long-term associations need causal caution · Sleep hygiene addresses conditions, not every disorder", "parent": "psy12-sleep", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Sleep deprivation, effects and sleep hygiene</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Acute and chronic sleep loss are different exposure patterns</li><li>Long-term associations need causal caution</li><li>Sleep hygiene addresses conditions, not every disorder</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Acute and chronic sleep deprivation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Sleep deprivation involves inadequate quantity or quality of sleep for normal functioning. Acute deprivation concerns a short-term episode, which can be partial restriction or more complete loss; chronic deprivation concerns insufficient sleep recurring over an extended period. Effects depend on amount, timing, quality and individual circumstances. The 2026 syllabus uses acute and chronic terminology rather than the former acute-partial label. A person can spend time in bed without obtaining restorative sleep if it is repeatedly interrupted. In an exam response, state both the insufficiency and impact on normal functioning. Do not define chronic simply as missing one full night or assume that a single long sleep instantly reverses all effects of prolonged restriction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Causes and immediate effects of deprivation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Shift work can misalign sleep timing with circadian rhythms and create daytime noise or light exposure. Drugs can alter arousal or sleep quality. Stressors can increase pre-sleep arousal, and an unsuitable environment can disrupt continuity. Acute deprivation can affect mood, sustained attention, response or reflex speed and visual functioning. The effect can appear in tasks requiring vigilance even when a person feels they are coping. Connect each cause to a mechanism in the scenario rather than merely naming it. For example, daytime traffic noise can repeatedly interrupt sleep. These are educational explanations and do not establish the medical cause of a real person’s sleep difficulty.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Longer-term associations and wellbeing", "kind": "learn", "text": "Chronic sleep deficiency is associated with greater risk of health and psychological problems, including cardiovascular difficulties, obesity-related outcomes and anxiety, with complex two-way relationships. Insomnia can involve persistent difficulty initiating or maintaining sleep despite opportunity, and is not identical to voluntarily choosing a short night. Stress can disturb sleep, while poor sleep can make coping more difficult. Association does not mean every sleep-deprived person develops each condition or that sleep alone explains an illness. An exam answer should distinguish immediate effects from longer-term associations and describe the evidence cautiously. Clinical diagnosis or treatment requires qualified assessment and should not be inferred from a school questionnaire.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Sleep hygiene: timing, devices and environment", "kind": "learn", "text": "Sleep hygiene refers to habits and conditions that support a consistent opportunity for good sleep. Regular sleep and wake times help timing systems align. Managing electronic devices can reduce delayed bedtime, stimulating content and relevant light exposure. A suitable environment reduces avoidable light, noise and temperature discomfort. The explanation should connect the strategy with the specific barrier: moving notifications out of reach can reduce interruption, while a consistent schedule addresses irregular timing. These strategies are supportive habits, not a guarantee or complete treatment for every sleep disorder. In course activities use fictional cases or supplied data, and avoid requiring students to disclose sensitive health information or deliberately restrict sleep.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Acute and chronic sleep loss are different exposure patterns", "kind": "learn", "text": "Acute deprivation concerns short-term substantial sleep loss; chronic partial deprivation involves repeated insufficient sleep over time. Reduced opportunity, shift work, stress and irregular routines can contribute. Subjective adaptation to feeling tired does not necessarily mean objective performance has fully recovered. Attention lapses, slowed responses and mood changes are relevant immediate effects, with variation by task and person. Explain the exposure pattern and the measured effect before attributing every difficulty to sleep alone.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Long-term associations need causal caution", "kind": "learn", "text": "Persistent inadequate sleep is associated with a range of health and wellbeing difficulties, but observational relationships can reflect bidirectional effects and shared influences. Stress may impair sleep while poor sleep affects coping; underlying conditions or schedules can contribute to both. A correlation does not isolate which direction is responsible. Ethical research should not deliberately expose students to harmful deprivation. Use provided data, appropriate voluntary measurements and controlled low-risk comparisons rather than asking classmates to stay awake.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Sleep hygiene addresses conditions, not every disorder", "kind": "learn", "text": "Regular timing, suitable light and a comfortable quiet environment can support sleep opportunity. Device use can affect sleep through time displacement, arousal and light, so changing it may modify several mechanisms at once. A recommendation’s plausibility differs from proof of a specific mechanism in a trial. Persistent sleep problems can need professional support; educational guidance is not a diagnosis. An exam answer should connect each proposed condition to a plausible effect and evaluate whether evidence concerns behaviour, subjective sleep or objective measurement.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Acute and chronic effects", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Acute and chronic effects", "prompt": "Distinguish acute and chronic deprivation and give two appropriate consequences of each with caution about diagnosis. (5 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Acute is short-term insufficiency; chronic is repeated insufficient sleep over time.", "[1] Acute examples include poorer attention/mood.", "[1] Acute physiological examples include slowed responses or impaired vision.", "[1] Chronic deficiency has associations such as cardiovascular/obesity/anxiety risks.", "[1] Associations and nonspecific symptoms do not diagnose or guarantee illness."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 5, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-sleep-q5"}, {"id": "psy12-sleep-deprivation-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A fictional student sleeps too little on five consecutive nights but says they are used to it. Explain chronic partial deprivation and why the report does not prove normal performance. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Repeated insufficient sleep across nights is chronic partial deprivation rather than only one acute event.", "[2] Feeling accustomed is subjective and can differ from attention/reaction performance.", "[2] Appropriate objective and subjective measures under controlled conditions are needed; neither one report nor one task proves all functioning is normal."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-sleep-deprivation-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A survey associates late device use with poor sleep. Explain two possible mechanisms and one limitation to causal interpretation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Device use can displace sleep time and increase cognitive/emotional arousal.", "[2] Light exposure can affect timing; several mechanisms may operate together.", "[2] Poor sleepers may also use devices more, and stress/schedules may affect both, so an observational association alone does not establish direction or a single mechanism."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which exposure is chronic partial deprivation?", "o": ["Repeated nights of insufficient sleep.", "Only one moment of feeling bored.", "A guarantee that all subjective and objective effects disappear with familiarity.", "Every REM period during ordinary sleep."], "a": 0, "why": "Chronic partial deprivation concerns cumulative repeated sleep restriction, not simply any tired feeling."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-sleep-he", "title": "He et al. (2020): phone restriction and sleep evidence", "scope": "The designated trial tested a restriction package · Separate reported outcomes from universal effects · A package can work without identifying one mechanism", "parent": "psy12-sleep", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>He et al. (2020): phone restriction and sleep evidence</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>The designated trial tested a restriction package</li><li>Separate reported outcomes from universal effects</li><li>A package can work without identifying one mechanism</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "He et al. (2020): exact aim, design and measures", "kind": "learn", "text": "He and colleagues’ 2020 randomised pilot trial examined whether restricting mobile-phone use before bedtime affected sleep, pre-sleep arousal, mood and working memory. Thirty-eight university participants with poor sleep quality and bedtime phone-use habits were randomly allocated to intervention and control groups of nineteen. The intervention group avoided phone use for thirty minutes before usual bedtime for four weeks; the control group did not receive that instruction. Measures included sleep diaries, the Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index, a pre-sleep arousal scale, positive/negative affect ratings and n-back working-memory tasks. This was a restriction trial, not a survey correlating total screen time with grades. Distinguish recruitment through the university from random allocation to groups.", "diagram": "hetrial"}, {"title": "He et al. (2020): findings, contribution and limits", "kind": "learn", "text": "The intervention was associated with shorter sleep latency, longer sleep duration, better reported sleep quality, reduced pre-sleep arousal and improvements in positive affect and working-memory performance. It provided experimental evidence for a practical habit change in the studied volunteers. The small selected sample, short follow-up and motivation to change restrict generalisation and certainty about lasting effects. Participants knew their instruction and had contact supporting adherence, so expectations and monitoring are possible influences; several outcomes relied on self-report. The intervention changed phone use, not blue light alone, so the findings cannot isolate one light mechanism. Evaluation should connect those features to validity and generalisation. Read the linked original paper for exact procedure rather than substituting a different screen-time study.", "diagram": "hetrial"}, {"title": "Distinguishing trial packages and mechanisms", "kind": "learn", "text": "He et al.’s intervention altered bedtime phone use alongside instruction and adherence support. Improved outcomes are evidence about that tested arrangement, not a direct experimental isolation of screen colour, social-media content or every type of electronic device. A mechanism such as reduced arousal is plausible and measured, but should be distinguished from a uniquely established cause. A further study could compare credible conditions while preserving safe sleep opportunity, with appropriate objective and subjective measures. Short follow-up cannot show lifelong benefit. These distinctions let you use the study positively while avoiding a headline that generalises beyond the sample, time period and measured working-memory task.", "diagram": "hetrial"}, {"title": "The designated trial tested a restriction package", "kind": "learn", "text": "He and colleagues’ 2020 pilot randomised trial investigated restricting mobile-phone use before bedtime. Thirty-eight participants were allocated to a restriction group or comparison group; the intervention involved avoiding phone use for 30 minutes before bedtime for four weeks. Outcomes included sleep-related reports, pre-sleep arousal, mood and working-memory measures. The sample and pilot scale limit broad claims. State the actual exposure change and outcome measures, rather than describing an experiment that isolated blue light alone.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Separate reported outcomes from universal effects", "kind": "learn", "text": "The restriction group showed improvements on relevant sleep, arousal and cognitive outcomes in the reported trial, supporting further investigation of this practical change. Self-report, adherence and expectations remain relevant, and the sample does not represent every age or device habit. Random allocation strengthens comparison but does not make all potential bias disappear. A result for a four-week pilot does not guarantee lifelong benefit or show that every participant improved equally. Use the specified study’s comparisons and time frame.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A package can work without identifying one mechanism", "kind": "learn", "text": "Avoiding the phone changes screen light, content-related arousal and time use together. The trial therefore cannot assign the whole effect uniquely to blue-light reduction. A targeted mechanism comparison would need to vary one relevant feature while matching the others, subject to ethical and practical limits. A precise evaluation links this confounding of mechanisms to the claim, while recognising that a package trial can still assess practical usefulness. Do not confuse mechanism uncertainty with proof that the intervention had no value.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the research and authoritative explanations", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Further evidence</h3><p>The lesson explanations provide the required student account. These links allow study details and educational health information to be checked. Read historical methods critically; they are not activities to reproduce.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0228756\">He et al. (2020), original open-access paper</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep/stages-of-sleep\">NIH: current sleep stages</a></li></ul>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Exact He trial", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Exact He trial", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Trial limitations", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: sleep and evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Exact He trial", "prompt": "Describe the designated 2020 trial’s intervention, sample allocation, measures and broad finding. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Thirty-eight selected university participants were randomly allocated nineteen per group; intervention avoided phones thirty minutes before bedtime for four weeks.", "[2] Sleep diary/quality, arousal/mood scales and n-back performance measured different outcomes.", "[2] Restriction was associated with improved sleep, arousal, positive affect and working memory in the sample, without isolating blue light alone."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-sleep-q2"}, {"title": "Trial limitations", "prompt": "Evaluate two limitations of He et al. and explain why random allocation does not mean a representative sample. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Small selected motivated university sample limits broad generalisation.", "[2] Unblinded instruction/monitoring and self-report create expectation/measurement alternatives; short follow-up limits lasting claims.", "[2] Random allocation balances conditions after recruitment, whereas representative selection concerns sampling from a population."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "marks": 6, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-sleep-q6"}, {"title": "Extended response: sleep and evidence", "prompt": "Explain sleep stages/cycles, deprivation mechanisms and hygiene, then use and evaluate He et al. (2020). (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Correctly distinguishes three NREM stages plus REM and relevant EEG/physiology.", "[2] Explains circadian timing versus within-night cycles and typical changing distribution.", "[2] Applies causes and acute/chronic consequences cautiously.", "[2] Connects hygiene strategies with timing, arousal and environment barriers.", "[2] Describes exact trial sample/conditions/measures and findings.", "[2] Evaluates self-report, expectations, selected sample and limited mechanism/long-term inference."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-sleep-q7"}, {"id": "psy12-sleep-he-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain He et al.’s 2020 intervention and why it cannot establish that blue light alone caused the reported sleep differences. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The pilot randomised comparison restricted mobile-phone use for 30 minutes before bedtime over four weeks.", "[2] Restriction changes light, arousal/content and time use together, not blue light alone.", "[2] Reported group differences can support the package’s usefulness while leaving the specific mechanism unresolved."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-sleep-he-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Evaluate one strength and two limits of the 2020 trial for a claim that all teenagers should expect the same improvement. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Random allocation and a comparison support stronger inference about the intervention than an uncontrolled association.", "[2] A small pilot sample and specific participant context limit generalisation to all teenagers.", "[2] Self-report/adherence/expectations and the four-week period limit universal or long-term predictions; improvement need not be identical for each person."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What did the He trial manipulate?", "o": ["A period of mobile-phone restriction before bedtime.", "Only blue-light wavelength with all other features held identical.", "A national compulsory deprivation programme.", "The number of NREM stages in the brain."], "a": 0, "why": "The restriction package changed several aspects of bedtime phone use, so it did not isolate one mechanism."}, {"q": "Current NREM classification contains how many stages?", "o": ["Three", "Four current stages", "One only", "No stages"], "a": 0, "why": "The 2026 syllabus uses N1, N2 and N3 plus REM."}, {"q": "Spindles and K-complexes typify what?", "o": ["N2", "N3 only", "REM only", "Fully awake always"], "a": 0, "why": "They are characteristic N2 EEG features."}, {"q": "Typical cycle changes across a night include what?", "o": ["More N3 early and longer REM later", "Exactly identical cycles", "No REM until midday", "N3 only at the end"], "a": 0, "why": "The pattern varies and schematic timing is approximate."}, {"q": "He et al. restricted what?", "o": ["Phones for thirty minutes before bedtime for four weeks", "All devices all day for one year", "Only blue light experimentally", "Every participant’s sleep to four hours"], "a": 0, "why": "The actual trial was a specific bedtime-phone restriction."}, {"q": "Random allocation in He guarantees what?", "o": ["Neither universal representativeness nor lifelong effects", "All teenagers were sampled", "No expectations possible", "All memory types improved forever"], "a": 0, "why": "Allocation supports group comparison after selected recruitment."}], "subtitle": "17 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-planning", "title": "Operational hypotheses and ethical investigation planning", "scope": "Match the hypothesis to the actual evidence · An ethical design is a chain of decisions · Plan a result and a disconfirming interpretation", "parent": "psy12-inquiry-exams", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Operational hypotheses and ethical investigation planning</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Match the hypothesis to the actual evidence</li><li>An ethical design is a chain of decisions</li><li>Plan a result and a disconfirming interpretation</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Research questions and measurable predictions", "kind": "learn", "text": "A useful research question specifies the constructs and context clearly enough to guide a design. A directional hypothesis predicts a particular relationship, such as higher recognition scores following semantic rather than structural processing. A non-directional hypothesis predicts a difference without stating which condition is higher. A qualitative inquiry question can ask how participants describe an experience. Operationalise the independent condition and measured outcome, including units and timing. A study that observes naturally occurring sleep and memory measures is correlational even if the researcher predicts an effect. Design classification depends on manipulation and control, not the wording of the hypothesis. In an exam, name the actual participants and conditions rather than offering an unrelated memorised prediction.", "diagram": "variables"}, {"title": "Ethics through the entire design", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ethical design requires independent review, informed and voluntary participation, withdrawal arrangements, protection from harm, privacy, confidentiality and appropriate debriefing. Deception needs justification and safeguards rather than being treated as an automatic feature of Psychology. An inquiry on memory can use neutral material and private scores without exposing participants to shame. Sleep research must not deliberately create harmful deprivation as a student activity. Historical animal studies should be evaluated using replacement, reduction and refinement. An exam response should explain a specific breach and improvement, including how the improvement protects participants. Merely collecting a signature does not address every risk. Use provided fictional data unless an approved school procedure authorises real participation.", "diagram": "ethics"}, {"title": "Match the hypothesis to the actual evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "A directional hypothesis specifies the expected direction between operationalised conditions or variables; a non-directional hypothesis predicts a difference or relationship without a direction. A qualitative inquiry question can explore experiences or interpretations rather than forcing a numerical prediction. If a task measures recognition after a delay, a hypothesis about all intelligence over a lifetime is mismatched. Define population, conditions, outcome and time frame at the appropriate level, then keep the eventual conclusion within that scope.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "An ethical design is a chain of decisions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ethics includes recruitment, consent, the task, monitoring, data protection and reporting, not merely a paragraph at the end. A low-risk neutral learning task can test a focused hypothesis without inducing distress or collecting sensitive personal history. Participation must remain voluntary and withdrawal practicable. A comparison or blinding plan should not conceal relevant risks under the label of scientific control. Historical harmful procedures are analysed as evidence and criticism rather than proposed as student investigations.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Plan a result and a disconfirming interpretation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Specify how the data will be scored and displayed before inspecting them. Consider what pattern would support the hypothesis and what would challenge it. A null-looking mean difference can reflect small samples, insensitive measures or genuinely limited effect; it does not automatically prove no possible relationship. A descriptive practice exercise should not invent statistical significance. A reasoned conclusion combines the observed values, design features and relevant uncertainty, with appropriate acknowledgement of sources.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Study design critique", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Study design critique", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Study design critique", "prompt": "A memory intervention group receives supportive coaching and a control group receives none. The researcher credits the entire difference to the named intervention component. Evaluate. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Coaching systematically differs with condition and may be confounded with the component.", "[1] Attention, expectations or strategy instruction could explain some difference.", "[1] Use an ethically appropriate comparable-contact condition and standardise other features.", "[1] Draw a cautious conclusion about the tested package until the component is isolated."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-q3"}, {"id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-planning-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Write a directional hypothesis for an approved neutral word task comparing semantic and structural instructions, with recognition after ten minutes. Identify one ethical protection. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Predict higher correct recognition after ten minutes for the semantic instruction group than the structural group.", "[2] Operationalise the conditions and recognition scoring rather than predicting general intelligence.", "[2] Use understandable voluntary consent/withdrawal and low-risk materials with appropriate review and data protection."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-planning-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "An investigation gives similar sample means and concludes the treatment can never work for anyone. Evaluate the conclusion and specify what should be reported. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Similar descriptive means show limited observed difference under these conditions, not universal impossibility.", "[2] Sample size, measure sensitivity and design limits may affect detection/interpretation; do not invent significance.", "[2] Report the actual values, relevant comparison and limits, addressing the hypothesis without extending beyond the evidence."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which hypothesis is properly operationalised?", "o": ["Semantic-task participants will recognise more target words after ten minutes than structural-task participants.", "Everyone will become generally smarter forever.", "The study will definitely prove its theory.", "An outcome not measured anywhere in the procedure."], "a": 0, "why": "A testable hypothesis matches named conditions, an observable outcome and the relevant timing."}], "subtitle": "13 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-design", "title": "Research designs, sampling and allocation", "scope": "Research design and time structure are separate · Sampling and allocation answer different validity questions · Construct a fair comparison without overclaiming", "parent": "psy12-inquiry-exams", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Research designs, sampling and allocation</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Research design and time structure are separate</li><li>Sampling and allocation answer different validity questions</li><li>Construct a fair comparison without overclaiming</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Selecting a design for the question", "kind": "learn", "text": "Experiments manipulate a condition and compare measured outcomes with control of alternatives. Observations record behaviour without manipulating the relevant condition. Case studies provide detailed evidence about unusual individuals or cases. Correlational studies examine associations, while longitudinal and cross-sectional approaches organise observations across time or groups. These features can combine: a longitudinal investigation can be non-experimental, and an experiment can use repeated measurements. Choose the approach that answers the question ethically and explain a strength and limitation. A longitudinal association does not become causal simply because an earlier event preceded a later one. Cross-sectional group differences can reflect cohort differences. Avoid the older slide oversimplification that following people over time automatically establishes cause and effect.", "diagram": "designs"}, {"title": "Sampling, allocation and generalisation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Selection and allocation solve different problems. Convenience or snowball recruitment can limit population representation even if the recruited participants are allocated randomly to conditions. Random sampling from a suitable frame can improve representation, while proportionate stratification can ensure relevant subgroups are included. Random allocation addresses group comparability within the study. A narrow sample may allow a useful controlled comparison while restricting external validity. Describe the population claimed and show whether the sample actually supports it. A study of motivated university volunteers cannot automatically establish the same effect in all adolescents. Proposed improvements should identify how recruitment will change rather than simply saying use more people without considering diversity or bias.", "diagram": "sampling"}, {"title": "Research design and time structure are separate", "kind": "learn", "text": "Experiments manipulate conditions with a comparison; correlations measure co-variation; case studies and observations provide different kinds of non-experimental evidence. Longitudinal and cross-sectional describe when and from whom observations are collected. A longitudinal correlation can reveal change and temporal ordering without eliminating all confounds. A cross-sectional group comparison can be numerical without being an experiment. Classify the actual procedure, then state the inference it supports rather than relying on a label alone.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Sampling and allocation answer different validity questions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Random selection from an appropriate population frame addresses representativeness probabilistically; random allocation addresses systematic differences between study conditions. Stratification can improve representation of relevant subgroups, while convenience and snowball approaches have practical uses and characteristic biases. Neither a large number nor a random assignment guarantees broad external validity. Explain recruitment and group assignment separately, and identify the population to which the conclusion is intended to apply.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Construct a fair comparison without overclaiming", "kind": "learn", "text": "A controlled memory experiment can standardise exposure, materials and testing, use appropriate allocation and reduce expectancy effects. These protections strengthen internal inference while the task may still differ from real examination learning. A meaningful follow-up can test transfer, but more natural settings introduce new uncontrolled influences. An extended evaluation should connect each design feature to a consequence and propose a feasible targeted improvement, acknowledging the trade-off. A list of strengths and weaknesses without explanation is incomplete.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-design-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A study follows self-selected volunteers’ sleep and memory over six months without assigning sleep conditions. Classify the design and evaluate one causal limitation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] It is longitudinal and non-experimental/correlational when sleep and memory are measured together.", "[2] Temporal information and within-person patterns can be informative, but no assigned sleep manipulation is present.", "[2] Stress, schedules or other variables can affect both, so the relationship does not alone establish a causal effect."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-design-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare the purposes of stratified sampling and random allocation in a memory experiment across several school types. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Stratified sampling represents relevant school-type subgroups using a suitable frame and selection within strata.", "[2] Random allocation assigns recruited participants to memory conditions by chance, reducing systematic group differences on average.", "[2] Representation and condition equivalence are distinct goals; neither method guarantees perfect samples/groups or resolves all validity limits."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Can a longitudinal study still be non-experimental?", "o": ["Yes, if it follows measurements over time without manipulating conditions.", "No, every repeated measurement is a controlled experiment.", "No, collecting numbers automatically creates an IV.", "Only if it contains no participants."], "a": 0, "why": "Time structure does not itself determine experimental status or causal control."}], "subtitle": "11 screens · 2 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-controls", "title": "Confounds, blinding, reliability and validity", "scope": "Identify the alternative before choosing the control · Validity and reliability are different judgements · Improvements retain limitations", "parent": "psy12-inquiry-exams", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Confounds, blinding, reliability and validity</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Identify the alternative before choosing the control</li><li>Validity and reliability are different judgements</li><li>Improvements retain limitations</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Confounds and researcher expectations", "kind": "learn", "text": "A confound systematically covaries with the condition, creating a competing explanation. If the semantic-processing group has longer exposure than the structural group, exposure time may help explain later memory. Participant differences, environment and researcher behaviour can also influence outcomes. Random allocation, standardised instructions, equivalent materials, appropriate comparison conditions and blinding of relevant assessors reduce specific threats. A placebo can help assess expectation effects when a credible and ethical comparison is possible, but not every learning task has a suitable placebo. Demand characteristics may influence reports or performance. Explain who knows the condition and how that knowledge could change the data. Avoid claiming a double-blind design when the instructions necessarily reveal the condition to participants.", "diagram": "allocation"}, {"title": "Internal/external validity and two reliability types", "kind": "learn", "text": "Internal validity concerns whether the design supports the proposed relationship rather than alternatives. External validity concerns generalisation across people, settings and times. Test-retest reliability involves consistency on repeated measurement when the construct should be stable. Inter-rater reliability involves agreement between observers judging the same material. These concepts are distinct: increasing sample diversity can support generalisation but does not automatically remove a confound, and observer agreement does not prove that the behaviour category represents the intended construct. Explain the method, limitation and consequence. A targeted improvement might blind observers to condition and train them on defined categories for agreement, while standardising exposure addresses a different issue. Evaluation should name which property is being improved.", "diagram": "validity"}, {"title": "Identify the alternative before choosing the control", "kind": "learn", "text": "Placebo effects concern expectations about a received intervention; experimenter effects concern researcher influence on procedures or observations; demand characteristics concern cues about the expected response. They can overlap but need different protections. A scripted instruction reduces inconsistent delivery, blind scoring reduces expectation effects on judgement, and participant blinding may reduce some expectancy cues. Double blinding is not feasible for every behavioural intervention. Explain who is unaware of what and why that matters.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Validity and reliability are different judgements", "kind": "learn", "text": "Internal validity concerns whether the study supports attribution to the tested factor; external validity concerns extension to other people, settings or times. Test-retest reliability concerns consistency across suitable repeated measurements; inter-rater reliability concerns agreement across observers. A measure can be consistent but miss the intended construct, and a controlled study can have narrow generalisation. Use a specific procedure to illustrate the distinction. Avoid labelling every weakness invalid or every repeatable number objectively correct.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Improvements retain limitations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Random allocation reduces systematic group differences on average but cannot guarantee exact balance, especially in small samples. Standardisation reduces procedural variation but may reduce realism. Clear coding and independent raters improve consistency but do not automatically fix cultural interpretation or reactivity. A reasoned evaluation explains the benefit and remaining boundary. The strongest proposed change is tailored to the actual confound or measurement problem rather than adding a placebo or larger sample mechanically to every design.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-controls-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A researcher knows which group received an intervention and gives that group more encouraging instructions. Explain the validity problem and a targeted control. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Differential encouragement is a researcher effect that covaries with intervention condition.", "[2] It provides an alternative explanation of performance, reducing internal validity.", "[2] Standardise delivery and blind the relevant researcher where feasible, describing who is unaware of assignment and retaining practical limits."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-controls-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Two observers consistently agree on a biased behavioural code. Compare inter-rater reliability with validity and propose a relevant improvement. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Agreement supports inter-rater reliability under the coding procedure.", "[2] A shared biased definition may still fail to represent the intended construct, so agreement does not prove validity.", "[2] Review operational categories against appropriate evidence/context and test them, while maintaining independent coding to assess consistency."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What does inter-rater reliability assess?", "o": ["Consistency between observers’ ratings of the same relevant material.", "Whether every participant was sampled randomly.", "Whether the intervention necessarily caused an effect.", "Whether the task represents every real-world context."], "a": 0, "why": "Agreement is a measurement-consistency question, distinct from causal and generalisation judgements."}], "subtitle": "11 screens · 2 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-measures", "title": "Qualitative, quantitative and physiological measures", "scope": "Qualitative format requires more than an anecdote · Objective physiology and subjective judgement fit different questions · Mixed methods need a plan for integration", "parent": "psy12-inquiry-exams", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Qualitative, quantitative and physiological measures</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Qualitative format requires more than an anecdote</li><li>Objective physiology and subjective judgement fit different questions</li><li>Mixed methods need a plan for integration</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Choosing and evaluating measures", "kind": "learn", "text": "The measure must match the construct. A recognition test cannot be described as free recall, and a rating of motivation is not an objective count of study time. Physiological measures include heart rate, breathing rate and galvanic skin response, but these are not unique identifiers of emotion. Likert-type ratings quantify subjective responses; structured or semi-structured interviews can capture richer meanings. Focus groups allow interaction while raising conformity and confidentiality issues. Mixed methods can test whether numerical patterns align with reported experience. Explain a strength and limitation for the selected measure and what is actually concluded. Objective recording reduces some judgement but does not guarantee construct validity if the measured quantity poorly represents the question.", "diagram": "measures"}, {"title": "Qualitative format requires more than an anecdote", "kind": "learn", "text": "Structured interviews use defined questions; semi-structured interviews retain a guide with scope for relevant follow-up. Focus groups involve interaction that can generate ideas while also producing conformity or dominance. Open-ended surveys provide written qualitative responses with less opportunity for clarification. Systematic recording, coding and appropriate quotations support analysis, but privacy and contextual interpretation matter. Qualitative evidence can explain meanings that a numerical total hides without automatically representing the whole population.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Objective physiology and subjective judgement fit different questions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Heart rate, breathing rate and galvanic skin response provide physiological measures related to arousal; none uniquely identifies one emotion or life judgement. Rating scales quantify subjective experience and can be influenced by wording, recall and social desirability. A Likert-format response remains subjective even though it is numerical. Calibration improves an instrument’s technical accuracy but not every psychological interpretation. Choose the measure for the construct and explain its specific limitation rather than ranking all objective data above all subjective data.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Mixed methods need a plan for integration", "kind": "learn", "text": "A study can combine a defined physiological measure, suitable ratings and interviews to explore complementary aspects. Differences between them can be informative: a person’s reported calm and elevated pulse may reflect distinct processes or context. More measures do not guarantee a single truth or remove all bias. Explain how each addresses the research question and how contradictory evidence will be interpreted. Ethical low-risk educational tasks use fictional or appropriate non-sensitive material, not forced personal mental-health disclosure.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Extended response: evaluating a headline", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Extended response: evaluating a headline", "prompt": "A headline says one small unblinded trial proves bedtime phone restriction improves every teenager’s lifelong memory. Evaluate using aim, measures, design, sample and justified conclusion. (12 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Identifies that He tested a specified four-week restriction in selected university participants.", "[2] Distinguishes n-back working-memory performance from all forms/lifelong memory.", "[2] Recognises random allocation supports a comparison but does not ensure population representation.", "[2] Explains unblinded instructions/contact/self-report or short follow-up limitations.", "[2] States evidence for improvement in measured outcomes under the studied conditions.", "[2] Rejects universal/lifelong certainty and proposes appropriate replication/follow-up without claiming the study is useless."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 12, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-q7"}, {"id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-measures-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Compare a heart-rate measure and a subjective rating for investigating stress during a fictional neutral task. Explain one strength and one limitation of each. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Heart rate gives physiological quantitative evidence but also varies with movement, temperature and other influences.", "[2] A rating directly addresses reported experience but can be affected by wording, recall or desirability.", "[2] Neither is uniquely sufficient; matching the construct and standardising relevant conditions supports interpretation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-measures-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A focus group about a learning programme gives uniformly positive comments. Evaluate one group-process issue and propose another method that could clarify it. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Conformity, dominant speakers or concern about public criticism can shape the group discussion.", "[2] Uniform comments do not establish every participant privately agrees or that the sample represents all users.", "[2] Add appropriately confidential individual interviews/open-ended responses with clear consent, comparing themes while retaining sampling and interpretation limits."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "A numerical Likert response is necessarily what?", "o": ["Quantitative data that can still represent a subjective judgement.", "An objective physiological recording.", "Automatically free of social desirability.", "Qualitative solely because it concerns a feeling."], "a": 0, "why": "Numerical format and subjectivity are different dimensions of measurement."}], "subtitle": "11 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-analysis", "title": "Data displays, central tendency and Pearson’s r", "scope": "Use raw data with transparent working · Pearson r describes a linear relationship · Graph choice and axis design affect communication", "parent": "psy12-inquiry-exams", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Data displays, central tendency and Pearson’s r</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Use raw data with transparent working</li><li>Pearson r describes a linear relationship</li><li>Graph choice and axis design affect communication</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Mean and median from raw data", "kind": "learn", "text": "For fictional recognition scores of 4, 6, 6, 8 and 16, the mean is 40 divided by 5, or 8, while the median of the ordered scores is 6. The high value increases the mean, illustrating sensitivity to an outlier. For an even number of ordered scores, average the two middle values for the median. Report the units or meaning, such as words correctly recognised, and compare like measures. Group means do not reveal every participant’s performance or prove a statistically significant difference. The syllabus requires calculating and interpreting mean and median, and interpreting Pearson’s r; it does not require inventing significance conclusions from a mean difference alone. Show working and connect the statistic to the data.", "diagram": "data"}, {"title": "Pearson’s r and alternative explanations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Pearson’s correlation coefficient ranges from −1 to +1 and describes a linear relationship. A value of −0.82 indicates a strong negative linear association in a usual qualitative interpretation: as one measured variable increases, the other tends to decrease. The magnitude, not the sign, indicates strength. A value near zero indicates little linear association but may coexist with a curved pattern. Correlation does not establish causal direction and can reflect a third variable. If sleep difficulty and memory errors correlate, stress or another influence could contribute to both. State both variables and their relation, then a plausible alternative. Do not say a negative correlation means the study failed or that 0.82 means 82% of people followed the pattern.", "diagram": "correlation"}, {"title": "Displays and evidence-based conclusions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Use a scatterplot for paired measurements, separate columns for category comparisons, a meaningful line for time or continuous progression and a histogram for frequencies across numerical intervals. Tables should identify conditions, units and relevant sample sizes. A graph’s visual impression depends on scale, so inspect axis limits and labels. A conclusion should answer the question using the actual values and avoid claims beyond the measure or sample. A higher fictional mean recognition score supports better performance on that test under those conditions; it does not prove lifelong memory improvement. Include uncertainty and alternative explanations where relevant. Correct psychological terminology and transparent calculations help readers assess the claim.", "diagram": "datagraphs"}, {"title": "Use raw data with transparent working", "kind": "learn", "text": "Mean equals the sum of scores divided by their count; median is the middle after ordering, or the average of the two middle scores for an even count. A summary table presents appropriate condition statistics; a frequency table counts observations in categories or values. Preserve units and identify sample size. A calculation is not complete if it divides by a guessed denominator. When comparing groups also consider the distribution and context, not only which mean is larger.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Pearson r describes a linear relationship", "kind": "learn", "text": "The sign indicates direction and the magnitude indicates strength of linear association, ranging from −1 to +1. A value near zero does not rule out every nonlinear relationship. r is not a percentage caused, a regression slope or proof that changing one variable will change the other. A scatterplot helps inspect the pattern and influential points. Describe direction, strength and relevant data, then consider causal direction and third variables where the design is correlational.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Graph choice and axis design affect communication", "kind": "learn", "text": "Column graphs suit category comparisons, line graphs ordered change, histograms numerical frequency distributions and scatterplots paired variables. Label axes and units, use sensible scales and avoid misleading truncation or connecting unrelated categories as if they were a continuous trend. A graph can show a sample pattern without establishing statistical significance. In a conclusion connect the displayed evidence to the hypothesis and state design limits, rather than writing only that the graph proves the theory.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read, apply and qualify the focused model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read each label and distinguish the relationships or levels of explanation. Describe the pattern in your own words, then use the accompanying theory to explain why it matters. Apply the model to the written questions in this lesson and state its limit rather than treating an illustration as proof. Where a diagram uses fictional numbers or arbitrary units, keep those separate from the findings of a designated study.", "diagram": "nonlinear-correlation"}, {"title": "Course coverage and revision reference", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Course coverage and revision reference</h3><p>Use the syllabus alongside the modules to check definitions, applications, study details and evaluation.</p><div class=\"psy-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>Core domains</th><th>Designated studies to explain</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Biological/lifespan; complete inquiry</td><td>Harlow 1958; Ainsworth 1978; historical brain evidence</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Attitudes/identity/prejudice/influence/helping; inquiry</td><td>Festinger/Carlsmith 1959; Sherif 1954; Milgram 1963; Asch 1951; Latané/Darley smoke 1968</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Memory and learning; inquiry</td><td>Craik/Tulving experiment two 1975; Pavlov 1902; Watson/Rayner 1920; Thorndike 1898; Skinner 1938; Bandura/Ross/Ross 1961</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Motivation/wellbeing/stress/sleep; inquiry</td><td>He et al. 2020</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>School assessments and official written exams require broader performance than concept checks. The official course page provides cohort syllabus and exam documents.</p>"}, {"title": "Interactive data: linear association", "kind": "interactive", "model": "correlation"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Data and conclusion", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Data and conclusion", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Negative association", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Negative association", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Data and conclusion", "prompt": "Fictional recognition scores are 4, 6, 6, 8 and 16 words. Calculate mean and median and justify which is less influenced by the high score. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[1] Sum = 40, n = 5, mean = 8 words.", "[1] Ordered middle is 6, so median = 6 words.", "[1] The mean uses all magnitudes and rises with the outlier.", "[1] The median depends on ordered position and is less influenced here; report what the statistic measures."], "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-q1"}, {"title": "Negative association", "prompt": "A hypothetical observational study finds r = −0.82 between sleep duration and attention errors. Interpret and give two causal limits. (4 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A strong negative linear association: longer sleep tends to accompany fewer errors in the sample.", "[1] No manipulation means causal direction is not established.", "[1] A third variable such as stress could affect both; r does not mean 82% of people showed an effect."], "allocations": [2, 1, 1], "marks": 4, "practice": false, "id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-q2"}, {"id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-analysis-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Fictional scores are 3, 5, 5, 7, 10 and 18. Calculate the mean and median and explain why a distribution description still matters. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Sum 48 divided by 6 gives mean 8.", "[2] The middle ordered values are 5 and 7; median = (5+7)/2 = 6.", "[2] The high value 18 affects the mean and the scores vary; central tendency alone does not describe spread or prove a group effect."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-analysis-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A study reports r = −0.78 between reported sleep problems and a memory score. Interpret the result and evaluate a causal headline. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The sign shows a negative linear relationship: more reported problems tend to accompany lower memory scores.", "[2] Magnitude 0.78 indicates a relatively strong linear association in the sample, not 78% caused.", "[2] Direction and third variables such as stress are unresolved in a correlation, so the result alone does not prove a causal mechanism."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What does r = −0.78 indicate?", "o": ["A relatively strong negative linear association.", "That 78% of memory is caused by sleep problems.", "That the relationship must be nonlinear and absent.", "That a negative result is always weak."], "a": 0, "why": "Sign describes direction, magnitude describes linear strength, and neither alone establishes causation."}], "subtitle": "19 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Psychology motivation, wellbeing and health", "icon": "🧠", "id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-responses", "title": "Scientific sources and extended ATAR exam responses", "scope": "Answer the verb and the full scope · Use study evidence with the specified version · Plan extended answers from the supplied scaffold", "parent": "psy12-inquiry-exams", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Scientific sources and extended ATAR exam responses</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Answer the verb and the full scope</li><li>Use study evidence with the specified version</li><li>Plan extended answers from the supplied scaffold</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Scientific sources and referencing", "kind": "learn", "text": "Evaluate sources by the actual research question, sample, design, measures, transparency, analysis and whether conclusions match evidence. Peer review provides scrutiny but does not guarantee correctness. Check dates and exact studies, especially when a researcher published several related experiments. A headline can exaggerate causality or omit limitations. Original reports clarify methods, while appropriate review papers compare evidence across studies. Referencing allows readers to trace a claim to an author, year, title and source. Avoid copying unsupported commercial claims or presenting a generic infographic as empirical evidence. In a research response, distinguish the study’s findings from your inference and evaluate consistency across credible sources rather than collecting quotations without analysis.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Psychology ATAR written examination format", "kind": "learn", "text": "The current Year 12 examination design provides ten minutes reading time and three hours working time. Short-answer questions account for 70% of the examination, with a suggested 120 minutes. Extended answers account for 30%, with a suggested sixty minutes: a compulsory Part A contributes 10%, and Part B contributes 20% through one question chosen from two. Questions integrate psychological content and Science Inquiry. The concept checks in these lessons are revision extras rather than a simulation of the official format. Use the current front cover and syllabus to check requirements for your cohort. Practise short answers, data interpretation and extended responses together, rather than allocating all revision to definitions or multiple-choice recognition.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Command words and extended-response planning", "kind": "learn", "text": "Define requires a precise meaning; describe requires relevant features; explain requires connected reasons or mechanisms; compare requires relationships such as similarities and differences; evaluate requires a justified judgement using evidence and limitations. Read every part and use the marks to plan depth. For a study question, cover the exact aim, method, findings, contribution and requested critique. For an extended answer, turn the supplied scaffold into sections and connect theory, scenario and inquiry evidence. A twenty-mark response needs breadth and developed reasoning, not simply a longer introduction. Label any diagram and explain its relevance. Review model answers point by point, then rewrite an answer independently to test recall and application.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Marked response versus list of facts", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a six-mark explanation with three requested factors, plan enough space for a defined factor, a concrete scenario detail and a mechanism for each as appropriate to the question. For a comparison, place models in relation to one another rather than writing two unrelated summaries. For an evaluation, support a judgement with a limitation and its effect on confidence, while recognising relevant strengths. Suggested lesson marking points are examples, not official keys; valid equivalent wording can earn credit if it fulfils the demand. Self-mark honestly against actual written evidence, then attempt again without the model answer. A concept-check pass is supplementary evidence of key-idea recognition only.", "diagram": "examplan"}, {"title": "Answer the verb and the full scope", "kind": "learn", "text": "Identify asks for the relevant feature; describe gives characteristics; explain connects cause/process and outcome; compare states similarities and differences; evaluate gives evidence-based judgements about value or limits. These are not interchangeable instructions. Underline the target, the verb and required components before planning. A long correct list can miss marks when the question requires applied explanation. Use the scenario explicitly and organise distinct points rather than repeating a definition in several sentences.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use study evidence with the specified version", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a designated study include aim, method, key finding, contribution and relevant criticism when asked. Keep Craik and Tulving experiment two, the 1961 live-model study and the 1968 smoke-room study distinct from related variants. For a theory explain its specified features, application and strengths/limits. Original marking points in this resource are practice guidance rather than official keys. Actual exam raw marks and section weightings differ; do not assume a 10% section always contains exactly ten raw marks.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Plan extended answers from the supplied scaffold", "kind": "learn", "text": "The 2026 front cover allocates 70% to short answer and 30% to extended answer, with compulsory Part A and one of two Part B choices. It lists 134, 26 and 31 raw marks for those sections respectively, with ten minutes reading and three hours working. Future papers can differ in raw totals, so use your cohort’s official cover. Allocate attention to the actual marks and all required prompts, supporting each claim with mechanisms, application or study evidence. Reference scientific sources accurately in inquiry work and distinguish a primary study from a summary.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Extended answer Part A practice", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Extended answer Part B option 1", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Extended answer Part B option 2", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<h3>Course versions and official exam practice</h3><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective 2026, with the published 2027 version checked: The 2027 publication also refreshes the rationale, aims and General Capabilities. The prescribed knowledge and inquiry topics used here have been checked against both publications. Currency checked 8 October 2026. Check the official course page for the syllabus and examination information for your cohort.</p><p>These original learning questions and suggested marking points are ATAR-style practice, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Concept checks are extra revision: the Psychology ATAR examination uses short and extended written answers. Historical study methods are material to evaluate, not instructions to reproduce harmful research.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/psychology\">SCSA Psychology course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1230683/Psychology-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1230652/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/1265122/Psychology-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from2027.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus effective 2027</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/1232052/2025-PSY-Examination-Web-Version.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0016/1232053/2025-PSY-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1242509/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-PSY-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Narrogin research methodology, data analysis, Piaget, attachment, cognitive dissonance, social identity, obedience and prosocial behaviour PowerPoints. Earlier slide statements have been checked and re-explained.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0007/1264138/2026-PSY-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: section weights are different from raw marks. The current cover gives short answer 134 raw marks/70%, extended Part A 26 raw marks/10%, and Part B 31 raw marks/20%. Older original 10- and 20-mark exercises below are practice allocations, not claims about current official raw totals.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Extended answer Part A practice", "prompt": "A fictional study compares semantic and structural tasks with equal word exposure and tests recognition. Plan and explain the design, measures, two controls and an ethical safeguard. (10 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Identifies manipulated processing task and operational recognition count/proportion.", "[2] Explains experimental comparison/random allocation or justified within-participant counterbalanced arrangement.", "[2] Controls word difficulty/exposure/delay/instructions with two concrete examples.", "[2] Explains neutral material, consent/withdrawal and private scores as safeguards.", "[2] Chooses category comparison/table and a cautious measure-specific conclusion with a limitation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "marks": 10, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-q4"}, {"title": "Extended answer Part B option 1", "prompt": "Using exact designated studies, compare classical, operant and observational learning. Apply each to a fictional learning environment and evaluate evidence and transfer. (20 marks)", "steps": ["[4] Explains classical stimulus/response association and coherent NS/UCS/UCR/CS/CR mapping with principles.", "[4] Explains operant ABC, consequences and schedule distinctions with relevant application.", "[4] Explains observational cognitive processes and vicarious/direct reinforcement distinction with application.", "[4] Accurately uses Pavlov/Little Albert, Thorndike/Skinner and 1961 Bobo methods/findings as required by the comparison.", "[4] Makes explicit comparisons and justified evaluation of welfare, sample/task limits and generalisation beyond the learning setting."], "allocations": [4, 4, 4, 4, 4], "marks": 20, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-q5"}, {"title": "Extended answer Part B option 2", "prompt": "Apply motivation, wellbeing and stress models to a fictional student facing deadlines and disrupted sleep. Use He et al. evidence and evaluate a supportive plan. (20 marks)", "steps": ["[4] Applies regulation/needs in self-determination theory and compares relevant Maslow claims.", "[4] Distinguishes Diener’s evaluations/affect from Ryff’s functioning dimensions with scenario evidence.", "[4] Applies stressors, appraisal and coping and compares response/event perspectives.", "[4] Explains sleep timing/stages/deprivation and accurately uses He trial method/findings.", "[4] Proposes targeted supportive features and evaluates measures, sample/confounds and ethical limits without diagnosis or harmful manipulation."], "allocations": [4, 4, 4, 4, 4], "marks": 20, "practice": true, "id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-q6"}, {"id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-responses-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A six-mark prompt asks students to explain a learning mechanism and apply it to a scenario. A response lists six terms with no connections. Explain why this may be insufficient and improve its structure. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Explanation requires linked processes, not merely six accurate labels.", "[2] Application requires explicit links to the scenario’s stimuli/actions/outcomes.", "[2] Organise distinct mechanism-plus-evidence points answering the whole command and compare them with the suggested allocation, without assuming one term automatically earns one mark."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "psy12-inquiry-exams-responses-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Outline an extended response comparing two models and evaluating a designated study. Explain how you would use marks, study version and sources. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Plan clear similarities/differences and explain each model’s mechanism using the same relevant context.", "[2] Use the requested study’s aim/method/finding and specific evaluation, avoiding related variants or unsupported quotations.", "[2] Address every scaffold and allocate detail to raw marks, keeping section weights separate; acknowledge accurate primary/official sources where inquiry referencing is required."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why can a long list of correct facts lose marks?", "o": ["It may fail to explain, compare, evaluate or apply as the question requests.", "Length always guarantees full marks.", "Command words are interchangeable.", "A 10% section necessarily has ten raw marks in every paper."], "a": 0, "why": "The response must perform the specified task and cover all components; marks reward relevant reasoning rather than length alone."}, {"q": "Correlation alone establishes what?", "o": ["Association, not causal certainty", "Guaranteed direction of cause", "Every third variable removed", "A representative sample"], "a": 0, "why": "Design and alternatives matter."}, {"q": "Consistency over occasions for stable construct is what?", "o": ["Test-retest reliability", "Inter-rater only", "Only external validity", "Affect balance"], "a": 0, "why": "Repeated measurement assesses stability under suitable conditions."}, {"q": "Category comparisons usually suit which display?", "o": ["Separate columns", "Unlabelled continuous curve", "Only an anatomical scan", "Always connected scatter points"], "a": 0, "why": "Chart choice follows data structure."}, {"q": "Which official format should practice include?", "o": ["Short and extended written answers", "Only five MC items", "Only recognition flashcards", "Only oral guessing"], "a": 0, "why": "Concept checks supplement rather than simulate the ATAR written exam."}, {"q": "A study evaluation should include what?", "o": ["A specific limitation and its consequence for inference", "Only the word bad", "Only a researcher’s name", "A universal claim without evidence"], "a": 0, "why": "Explain the relevance of the critique and a suitable improvement."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}];
